COLLDATA("fazail","en",[[1,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “A time will come when groups of people will go out to fight. It will be asked, ‘Is there among you anyone who accompanied the Prophet ﷺ?’ They will reply, ‘Yes,’ and victory will be granted to them. Then another time will come, and it will be asked, ‘Is there among you anyone who accompanied the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ?’ They will reply, ‘Yes,’ and victory will be granted. Then another time will come, and it will be asked, ‘Is there among you anyone who accompanied one who accompanied the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ?’ They will reply, ‘Yes,’ and victory will be granted.”"],[2,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “A time will come upon the people when a military expedition will be dispatched from among them. They will say, ‘Look and see whether you can find among yourselves anyone who was one of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ.’ Such a man will be found, and victory will be granted to them because of him. Then a second expedition will be dispatched, and they will say, ‘Is there among them anyone who saw the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ?’ Victory will be granted to them because of him. Then a third expedition will be dispatched, and it will be said, ‘Look and see whether there is among them anyone who saw someone who had seen the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ.’ Then there will be a fourth expedition, and it will be said, ‘Look and see whether there is among them anyone who saw someone who had seen someone who had seen the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ.’ Such a man will be found, and victory will be granted to them because of him.”"],[3,"ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “The best of you are my generation, then those who follow them, and then those who follow them.” ʿImrān said: “I do not know whether the Prophet ﷺ mentioned two or three generations after his own.” The Prophet ﷺ then said: “After you will come people who betray and are not trusted, who bear witness without being asked to testify, who make vows but do not fulfil them, and among whom obesity will become widespread.”"],[4,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: A man asked the Prophet ﷺ: “Which people are the best?” He replied: “The generation in which I live, then the second, and then the third.”"],[5,"Al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The best of mankind are my generation, then those who follow them, and then those who follow those who follow them. Afterward, people will come whose oaths precede their testimony and whose testimony precedes their oaths.”"],[6,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “The best of mankind are my generation, then those who follow them, and then those who follow them. Afterward, people will come whose testimony will precede their oaths and whose oaths will precede their testimony.” Ibrāhīm said: “They used to discipline us concerning testimony and pledges.”"],[7,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I was sent from the best generations of the children of Adam, generation after generation, until I came from the generation in which I was born.”"],[8,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The best of my nation are the generation among whom I was sent, then those who follow them.” Allah knows best whether he mentioned the third generation or not. He then said: “After them will come people who love corpulence and who bear witness before being asked to testify.”"],[9,"Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “You will continue to be upon goodness so long as there remains among you someone who saw me and accompanied me. By Allah, you will continue to be upon goodness so long as there remains among you someone who saw those who saw me and accompanied those who accompanied me. By Allah, you will continue to be upon goodness so long as there remains among you someone who saw those who saw those who saw me and accompanied those who accompanied those who accompanied me.”"],[10,"Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī reported: We performed the sunset prayer with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and then said: “If only we remained seated until we could perform the night prayer with him.” So we remained seated. He came out to us and asked: “Are you still here?” We replied: “O Messenger of Allah, we performed the sunset prayer with you and then decided to remain seated until we could perform the night prayer with you.” He said: “You have done well,” or, “You have acted correctly.” He then raised his head toward the sky—and he would often raise his head toward the sky—and said: “The stars are a safeguard for the sky. When the stars disappear, that which the sky has been promised will come upon it. I am a safeguard for my Companions; when I depart, that which my Companions have been promised will come upon them. My Companions are a safeguard for my nation; when my Companions depart, that which my nation has been promised will come upon it.”"],[11,"ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out one day and performed a prayer for the martyrs of Uḥud like his prayer over the deceased. He then proceeded to the pulpit and said: “I shall precede you, and I shall be a witness over you. By Allah, I can see my Basin at this very moment. I have been given the keys to the treasures of the earth—or the keys of the earth. By Allah, I do not fear that you will associate partners with Allah after me; rather, I fear that you will compete with one another over the world.”"],[12,"Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān stood among us and said: Indeed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ once stood among us and said: “Those among the People of the Scripture who came before you divided into seventy-two religious sects. This nation will divide into seventy-three sects: seventy-two will be in the Fire and one will be in Paradise, and it is the united community.” Ibn Yaḥyā and ʿAmr added in their narrations: “Indeed, there will arise among my nation people through whom those desires will course just as rabies courses through its victim.” ʿAmr said: “Rabies spreads through its victim, leaving no vein or joint without entering it.”"],[13,"ʿAwf ibn Mālik reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The Jews divided into seventy-one sects: one will be in Paradise and seventy will be in the Fire. The Christians divided into seventy-two sects: seventy-one will be in the Fire and one will be in Paradise. By Him in whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, my nation will certainly divide into seventy-three sects: one will be in Paradise and seventy-two will be in the Fire.” It was asked: “O Messenger of Allah, who are they?” He replied: “The united community.”"],[14,"Anas reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The Children of Israel divided into seventy-one sects, and my nation will divide into seventy-two sects. All of them will be in the Fire except one, and it is the united community.”"],[15,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī reported that on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Do not light a fire at night.” Later, he said: “Light your fires and prepare your food, for no people who come after you will attain the merit of even your ṣāʿ or your mudd.”"],[16,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: During the latter part of his life, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in the night prayer one evening. After concluding the prayer, he stood and said: “Consider this night of yours: at the end of one hundred years from now, none of those presently upon the face of the earth will remain.” Ibn ʿUmar said: The people misunderstood this statement of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ because of the reports they narrated concerning one hundred years. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had only said: “None of those who are upon the face of the earth today will remain.”\nBy that, he meant that the generation then living would come to an end."],[17,"Ibn ʿUmar reported that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb delivered a sermon at al-Jābiyah and said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ once stood among us just as I now stand among you and said: “Treat my Companions well, then those who follow them, and then those who follow them. Afterward, lying will become widespread, until a man will volunteer testimony before being asked to give it. Whoever among you desires the spacious central region of Paradise must adhere to the united community, for Satan is with the solitary person and is farther away from two. None of you must be alone with a woman, for Satan will be their third. Whoever is pleased by his good deed and distressed by his evil deed is a believer.”"],[18,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Do not revile my Companions. If one of you were to spend gold equal to Mount Uḥud, it would not equal even one mudd given by one of them, nor even half of it.”"],[19,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Busr reported: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “Glad tidings to the one who saw me. Glad tidings to the one who believed in me without seeing me. Glad tidings are his, and an excellent final abode.”"],[20,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: The Companions of the Prophet ﷺ mentioned Mālik ibn al-Dukhshum in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and began speaking against and insulting him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Leave my Companions alone for my sake.” They said: “O Messenger of Allah, he is the refuge of the hypocrites and the shelter to which they resort.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Does he not testify that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah?” They replied: “Yes, but there is no good in his testimony.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “No servant sincerely testifies to that from his heart and then dies upon it except that Allah makes the Fire forbidden for him.”"],[21,"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb reported: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “The best of the Successors is a man called Uways. He has a mother and was afflicted with a whiteness of the skin. Ask him to seek forgiveness for you.”"],[22,"ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr reported: I asked ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr about the harshest thing the polytheists had done to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He replied: I saw ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ approach the Prophet ﷺ while he was praying. He placed his cloak around his neck and strangled him severely with it. Abū Bakr came and pushed him away, saying: “Would you kill a man merely because he says, ‘My Lord is Allah,’ when he has brought you clear proofs from your Lord?” [Ghāfir: 28]"],[23,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: When the Prophet ﷺ was taken by night to al-Masjid al-Aqṣā, the people began speaking about it the following morning. Some who had previously believed in and affirmed him abandoned their faith. They hurried to Abū Bakr and asked: “Have you heard about your companion? He claims that he was taken by night to Bayt al-Maqdis.” He asked: “Did he actually say that?” They replied: “Yes.” He said: “If he said it, then he has certainly spoken the truth.” They asked: “Do you truly believe him when he says that he travelled to Bayt al-Maqdis last night and returned before morning?” He replied: “Yes. I believe him concerning something even more extraordinary than that: I believe him concerning news from heaven that comes to him in the morning or evening.” For that reason, Abū Bakr was called al-Ṣiddīq—the utterly truthful affirmer."],[24,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, reported: For as long as I could remember, I had always known my parents to practise the religion, and not a day passed without the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visiting us at both ends of the day, morning and evening. When the Muslims were subjected to persecution, Abū Bakr set out as an emigrant toward Abyssinia. When he reached Bark al-Ghimād, he met Ibn al-Dughunnah, the chief of al-Qārah. Ibn al-Dughunnah asked: “Where are you going, Abū Bakr?” Abū Bakr replied: “My people have driven me out, so I intend to travel throughout the earth and worship my Lord.” Ibn al-Dughunnah said: “A man such as you neither leaves nor should be driven out. You provide for the destitute, maintain ties of kinship, support those burdened with responsibilities, honour guests and assist people when calamities befall them in the cause of truth. I grant you my protection. Return and worship your Lord in your own land.” Ibn al-Dughunnah then travelled back with Abū Bakr. He went around to the prominent disbelievers of Quraysh and said: “A man such as Abū Bakr neither leaves nor should be driven out. Would you expel a man who provides for the destitute, maintains ties of kinship, supports those burdened with responsibilities, honours guests and assists people when calamities befall them in the cause of truth?” Quraysh accepted Ibn al-Dughunnah’s protection and guaranteed Abū Bakr’s safety. They said to Ibn al-Dughunnah: “Tell Abū Bakr to worship his Lord inside his house. Let him pray and recite whatever he wishes, but he must not trouble us with it or practise it publicly, for we fear that he may tempt our sons and women away from their religion.” Ibn al-Dughunnah conveyed this to Abū Bakr. Abū Bakr therefore began worshipping his Lord within his house and did not perform his prayers or recite publicly outside it. Later, however, Abū Bakr decided to build a place of prayer in the courtyard of his house. He came out and began praying and reciting the Qur’an there. The women and children of the polytheists began crowding around him, marvelling at him and watching him. Abū Bakr was a man who wept profusely and could not restrain his tears whenever he recited the Qur’an. This alarmed the polytheist leaders of Quraysh, so they sent for Ibn al-Dughunnah. When he came to them, they said: “We granted Abū Bakr protection on the condition that he worship his Lord within his house, but he has gone beyond that. He has built a place of prayer in his courtyard and publicly performs his prayers and recites the Qur’an. We fear that he may tempt our sons and women away from their religion. Go to him. If he is willing to restrict himself to worshipping his Lord inside his house, he may do so. But if he insists on practising publicly, ask him to return your protection to you. We dislike violating the protection you have granted, but we will not allow Abū Bakr to worship publicly.” ʿĀ’ishah continued: Ibn al-Dughunnah went to Abū Bakr and said: “You know the terms upon which I made my agreement with you. Either restrict yourself to those terms or return my protection to me, for I do not want the Arabs to hear that the protection I granted to a man was violated.” Abū Bakr replied: “I return your protection to you and am content with the protection of Allah.” At that time, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was still in Makkah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “I have been shown the land to which you will emigrate. I saw a saline tract containing date palms between two fields of black volcanic rock.”\nThese were the two lava plains. After the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned this, those who emigrated went toward Madinah, and some who had emigrated to Abyssinia returned to Madinah. Abū Bakr prepared to emigrate, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him: “Wait, for I hope that permission will be granted to me.” Abū Bakr asked: “Do you truly hope for that—may my father be sacrificed for you?” He replied: “Yes.” Abū Bakr therefore remained behind for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ so that he could accompany him. For four months, he fed two riding camels that he owned with the leaves of the samur tree."],[25,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib reported: Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, came to my father at his home and purchased a camel saddle from him. He said to ʿĀzib: “Send your son to carry it with me.” So I carried it with him, while my father went out to receive its price. My father said to him: “Abū Bakr, tell me what you and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did when you travelled by night.” He replied: “Yes. We travelled throughout that night and the following day until the midday heat became intense and the road became deserted, with no one passing along it. A tall rock appeared before us with a shaded area untouched by the sun, so we stopped beside it. With my own hands, I levelled a place for the Prophet ﷺ to sleep and spread a hide there. I said, ‘Sleep, O Messenger of Allah, while I scout the area around you.’ “He fell asleep, and I went out to scout the surroundings. I then saw a shepherd approaching the rock with his sheep, seeking the same shade that we had sought. I asked him, ‘Young man, to whom do you belong?’ “He named a man from Madinah or Makkah. I asked, ‘Do your sheep have any milk?’ “He replied, ‘Yes.’ “I asked, ‘Will you milk one?’ “He replied, ‘Yes.’ “He took hold of a ewe, and I told him, ‘Clean the udder of dust, hair and dirt.’” Al-Barāʾ demonstrated by striking one hand against the other as though dusting it off. “He milked a quantity of milk into a wooden bowl. I had with me a leather vessel that I had carried for the Prophet ﷺ so that he could drink from it and perform ablution. I returned to the Prophet ﷺ and disliked waking him, but I arrived just as he awoke. I poured water over the milk until its lower portion became cool and said, ‘Drink, O Messenger of Allah.’” He drank until I was satisfied, and then asked: “Is it not time to depart?” I replied: “Yes.” We resumed our journey after the sun had passed its zenith. Surāqah ibn Mālik pursued us, so I said: “O Messenger of Allah, they have reached us!” He replied: “Do not grieve; indeed, Allah is with us.” The Prophet ﷺ supplicated against Surāqah, and his horse sank into the ground up to its belly—or so I believe—in a solid patch of earth. Zuhayr was uncertain about this wording. Surāqah said: “I believe that you have supplicated against me. Supplicate for me, and I swear by Allah that I will turn the pursuers away from you.” The Prophet ﷺ supplicated for him, and he was freed. Thereafter, whenever Surāqah met anyone, he would say: “I have taken care of this area for you.” He turned back everyone he encountered. Al-Barāʾ said: “He fulfilled his promise to us.”"],[26,"Anas reported from Abū Bakr that he said: While I was in the cave with the Prophet ﷺ, I said: “If one of them were to look beneath his feet, he would see us.” He replied: “What do you think, Abū Bakr, of two whose third is Allah?”"],[27,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī reported: The Prophet ﷺ delivered a sermon and said: “Allah gave one of His servants the choice between this world and what is with Him, and the servant chose what is with Allah.” Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, began to weep. I thought to myself: “Why is this elderly man weeping merely because Allah offered one of His servants the choice between this world and what is with Him, and the servant chose what is with Allah?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ himself was that servant, and Abū Bakr was the most knowledgeable among us. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Abū Bakr, do not weep. The person who has been the most generous toward me through his companionship and wealth is Abū Bakr. If I were to take an intimate friend from among my nation, I would take Abū Bakr, but the brotherhood and affection of Islam remain. Let no doorway leading into the mosque remain except that it be closed, apart from the doorway of Abū Bakr.”"],[28,"Abū al-Dardāʾ reported: I was sitting with the Prophet ﷺ when Abū Bakr approached, holding up the edge of his garment so that his knee was exposed. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Your companion has certainly become involved in a serious dispute.” Abū Bakr greeted him and said: “There was a disagreement between Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb and me. I spoke hastily to him, but then regretted it. I asked him to forgive me, but he refused, so I have come to you.” The Prophet ﷺ said three times: “May Allah forgive you, Abū Bakr.” Then ʿUmar regretted what had happened and went to Abū Bakr’s house. He asked: “Is Abū Bakr here?” They replied: “No.” He therefore went to the Prophet ﷺ and greeted him. The Prophet’s ﷺ face began to change with anger until Abū Bakr became fearful for ʿUmar. Abū Bakr fell to his knees and said twice: “O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, I was more at fault.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Allah sent me to you, but you said, ‘You have lied,’ whereas Abū Bakr said, ‘He has spoken the truth.’ He supported me with himself and his wealth. Will you then leave my companion alone for my sake?” He repeated it twice. Thereafter, Abū Bakr was never harmed."],[29,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “No person’s wealth has benefited us as much as the wealth of Abū Bakr.”"],[30,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “No wealth has ever benefited me as much as the wealth of Abū Bakr.” Abū Bakr wept and said: “O Messenger of Allah, are my wealth and I not entirely at your service?”"],[31,"One of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out and ascended the pulpit. He recited the testimony of faith, and after completing it, the first thing he did was seek forgiveness for the martyrs who had been killed on the day of Uḥud. He then said: “One of Allah’s servants was given the choice between this world and what is with his Lord, and he chose what is with his Lord.” Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq was the first person to understand the meaning of these words. He realised that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was referring to himself, so Abū Bakr began to weep. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: “Take it easy, Abū Bakr! Close all these doors that open into the mosque except the door of Abū Bakr, for I know of no one among the Companions whose favours toward me are greater than those of Abū Bakr.”"],[32,"ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr reported: Abū Quḥāfah said to Abū Bakr: “My son, I see you freeing weak slaves. If you are going to do this, why do you not free strong men who could protect you and stand in your defence?” Abū Bakr replied: “My father, I seek through what I am doing only Allah Almighty.” It is related that these verses were revealed concerning him: “As for the one who gives and fears Allah, and believes in the finest reward…” up to His words: “No one has any favour with him for which repayment is sought, but only seeking the Countenance of his Lord, the Most High. And he will surely be pleased.” [Sūrat al-Layl: 5–21]"],[33,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Among the people, the one who has been most generous toward me through his companionship and wealth is Abū Bakr. If I were to take an intimate friend from among my nation, I would take Abū Bakr, but there remains the intimate bond of Islam. Let no small doorway leading into the mosque remain except the doorway of Abū Bakr.”"],[34,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: During the illness from which he died, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out with his head bound in a piece of cloth. He sat upon the pulpit, praised Allah and extolled Him, and then said: “There is no one among the people who has been more generous toward me with himself and his wealth than Abū Bakr ibn Abī Quḥāfah. If I were to take an intimate friend from among the people, I would take Abū Bakr as an intimate friend, but the intimate bond of Islam is better. Close every small doorway leading into this mosque except the doorway of Abū Bakr.”"],[35,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Mulaykah reported: The people of Kūfah wrote to Ibn al-Zubayr regarding the grandfather’s share of inheritance. He replied: “As for the one about whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ‘If I were to take an intimate friend from this nation, I would take him,’ he treated him as a father.” He meant Abū Bakr."],[36,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “If I were to take an intimate friend, I would take Abū Bakr as an intimate friend. Rather, he is my brother and companion, and Allah has taken your companion as an intimate friend.”"],[37,"Jundab ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: I heard the Prophet ﷺ, five days before his death, saying: “I declare before Allah that I have no intimate friend from among you, for Allah Most High has taken me as an intimate friend just as He took Abraham as an intimate friend. If I were to take an intimate friend from among my nation, I would take Abū Bakr as an intimate friend…” to the end of the hadith."],[38,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Close every doorway leading into the mosque except the doorway of Abū Bakr. If I were to take an intimate friend, I would take Abū Bakr as an intimate friend.”"],[39,"Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim reported: A woman came to the Prophet ﷺ, and he instructed her to return to him. She asked: “What if I return and do not find you?” It was as though she meant his death. He ﷺ replied: “If you do not find me, then go to Abū Bakr.”"],[40,"ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir reported: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at a time when no one was with him except five slaves, two women and Abū Bakr."],[41,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that during his illness, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Tell Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer.” ʿĀ’ishah said: “I said, ‘When Abū Bakr stands in your place, the people will be unable to hear him because of his weeping. Tell ʿUmar to lead the prayer instead.’” He said: “Tell Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer.” ʿĀ’ishah then said to Ḥafṣah: “Tell him, ‘When Abū Bakr stands in your place, the people will be unable to hear him because of his weeping. Tell ʿUmar to lead the people in prayer instead.’” Ḥafṣah did so, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Enough! You are indeed like the women associated with Joseph. Tell Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer.” Ḥafṣah later said to ʿĀ’ishah: “I have never received any good from you.”"],[42,"Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī reported: The Prophet ﷺ became ill, and his illness grew severe. He said: “Tell Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer.” ʿĀ’ishah said: “He is a tenderhearted man. If he stands in your place, he will be unable to lead the people in prayer.” He said: “Tell Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer.” She repeated her objection, so he said: “Tell Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer, for you are like the women associated with Joseph.” The messenger went to Abū Bakr, and he led the people in prayer during the lifetime of the Prophet ﷺ."],[43,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar reported: When the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ pain became severe, he was asked about the prayer. He said: “Tell Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer.” ʿĀ’ishah said: “Abū Bakr is a tenderhearted man. When he recites, he is overcome by weeping.” He said: “Tell him to lead the prayer.” She raised the matter with him again, so he said: “Tell him to lead the prayer. You are like the women associated with Joseph.”"],[44,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Zamʿah ibn al-Aswad ibn al-Muṭṭalib ibn Asad reported: When the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ illness became overwhelming, I was with him among a group of Muslims. Bilāl called for the prayer, so the Prophet ﷺ said: “Tell someone to lead the people in prayer.” I went out and found ʿUmar among the people, while Abū Bakr was absent. I said: “Stand, ʿUmar, and lead the people in prayer.” He stood, and when ʿUmar pronounced the opening takbīr, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard his voice, for ʿUmar was a man with a loud voice. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Where is Abū Bakr? Allah and the Muslims refuse this! Allah and the Muslims refuse this!” He then sent for Abū Bakr. Abū Bakr arrived after ʿUmar had completed that prayer and thereafter led the people in prayer. ʿAbdullāh ibn Zamʿah continued: ʿUmar said to me: “Woe to you! What have you done to me, Ibn Zamʿah? By Allah, when you instructed me, I assumed only that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had ordered you to do so. Had it not been for that, I would never have led the people in prayer.” I replied: “By Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not order me to do so. However, when I did not see Abū Bakr, I considered you the person present who was most entitled to lead the prayer.”"],[45,"Sahl ibn Saʿd reported: There was fighting among Banū ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf. When news of it reached the Prophet ﷺ, he went to them after the noon prayer to reconcile them. He said to Bilāl: “If the time for the afternoon prayer arrives and I have not returned to you, then tell Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer…” The remainder of the hadith follows."],[46,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “Who among you began this day fasting?” Abū Bakr replied: “I did.” He asked: “Who among you followed a funeral procession today?” Abū Bakr replied: “I did.” He asked: “Who among you fed a poor person today?” Abū Bakr replied: “I did.” He asked: “Who among you visited a sick person today?” Abū Bakr replied: “I did.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “These qualities are never combined in a person except that he enters Paradise.”"],[47,"Abū Hurayrah reported: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “Whoever spends two of any kind of property in the path of Allah will be called from the gates—meaning the gates of Paradise—‘O servant of Allah, this is good!’ Whoever was devoted to prayer will be called from the Gate of Prayer. Whoever was devoted to jihad will be called from the Gate of Jihad. Whoever was devoted to charity will be called from the Gate of Charity. Whoever was devoted to fasting will be called from the Gate of Fasting, the Gate of al-Rayyān.” Abū Bakr said: “A person called from any of those gates will have no cause for distress. Will anyone be called from all of them, O Messenger of Allah?” He replied: “Yes, and I hope that you will be among them, Abū Bakr.”"],[48,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: During his illness, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “Call Abū Bakr and your brother for me so that I may write a document, for I fear that someone may aspire to the position and another may say, ‘I am more entitled to it,’ whereas Allah and the believers will accept none but Abū Bakr.”"],[49,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on one of his journeys… The hadith contains the account of ʿĀ’ishah’s necklace being lost, because of which the people remained without water and could not perform ablution. Abū Bakr reproached ʿĀ’ishah and prodded her. Allah then revealed the verse concerning dry ablution, and the people performed dry ablution. Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr said: “O family of Abū Bakr, this is not the first blessing that has come through you.” ʿĀ’ishah said: “We then made the camel upon which I had been riding stand up, and we found the necklace beneath it.”"],[50,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever drags his garment out of arrogance, Allah will not look at him on the Day of Resurrection.” Abū Bakr said: “One side of my garment slips down unless I continually attend to it.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “You are not doing that out of arrogance.” Mūsā said: “I asked Sālim, ‘Did ʿAbdullāh specifically mention, “Whoever drags his lower garment”?’” He replied: “I heard him mention only ‘his garment.’”"],[51,"Aslam reported: I heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, say: One day, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed us to give charity. This coincided with a time when I had wealth, so I said, “Today I will surpass Abū Bakr, if ever I am to surpass him.” I brought half of my wealth. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “What have you left for your family?” I replied, “The same amount.” Then Abū Bakr came with everything he possessed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him: “What have you left for your family?” He replied, “I have left Allah and His Messenger for them.” I said, “I will never compete with you in anything again.”"],[52,"ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr reported: Abū Bakr’s name was ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUthmān. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: “You are the one whom Allah has set free from the Fire.” He was therefore called ʿAtīq."],[53,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, came out while ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, was speaking to the people. Abū Bakr said, “Sit down,” but ʿUmar refused. He again said, “Sit down,” but he refused. Abū Bakr then recited the testimony of faith, so the people turned toward him and left ʿUmar. He said: “To proceed: Whoever among you worshipped Muḥammad ﷺ, then Muḥammad ﷺ has died. But whoever worships Allah, then Allah is Ever-Living and never dies.” Allah Most High says: “Muḥammad is no more than a messenger; other messengers have passed away before him. If he were to die or be killed, would you turn back on your heels? Whoever turns back on his heels will not harm Allah in the least, and Allah will reward the grateful.” [Āl ʿImrān 3:144] By Allah, it was as though the people had not known that Allah had revealed this verse until Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, recited it. The people received it from him, and no one could be heard except reciting it."],[54,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died while Abū Bakr was at al-Sunḥ. She then related the hadith, in which it states: Abū Bakr then came out and said: “O you who are swearing, take it easy.” When Abū Bakr began speaking, ʿUmar sat down. Abū Bakr praised and extolled Allah and then said: “Take heed! Whoever worshipped Muḥammad ﷺ, then Muḥammad has died. But whoever worships Allah, then Allah is Ever-Living and never dies.” He then recited: “Indeed, you will die, and they too will die.” [Al-Zumar 39:30] He also recited: “Muḥammad is no more than a messenger; other messengers have passed away before him. If he were to die or be killed, would you turn back on your heels? Whoever turns back on his heels will not harm Allah in the least, and Allah will reward the grateful.” [Āl ʿImrān 3:144] The people then began sobbing and weeping. The Anṣār gathered around Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah in the portico of Banū Sāʿidah. They said: “Let there be a leader from among us and a leader from among you.” Abū Bakr, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb and Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ went to them. ʿUmar began to speak, but Abū Bakr silenced him. ʿUmar used to say: “By Allah, I intended nothing by that except that I had prepared words which pleased me, and I feared that Abū Bakr might not express them as effectively.” Abū Bakr then spoke, and he was the most eloquent of the people. Among the things he said was: “We are the rulers, and you are the ministers.” Al-Ḥubāb ibn al-Mundhir said: “No, by Allah, we will not accept that. There must be a leader from among us and a leader from among you.” Abū Bakr replied: “No. We are the rulers, and you are the ministers. They are the Arabs’ most central tribe by territory and the noblest of them by lineage. Therefore, pledge allegiance to ʿUmar or Abū ʿUbaydah.” ʿUmar said: “Rather, we shall pledge allegiance to you. You are our leader, the best among us and the most beloved of us to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” ʿUmar took Abū Bakr’s hand and pledged allegiance to him, and the people also pledged allegiance to him. Someone said: “You have killed Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah!” ʿUmar replied: “Allah has killed him.”"],[55,"Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb reported: A delegation from Buzākhah, consisting of members of Asad and Ghaṭafān, came to Abū Bakr seeking peace. He gave them a choice between a war that would drive them from their homes and a humiliating peace. They said: “We understand the war that drives people from their homes, but what is the humiliating peace?” He replied: “We will take your weapons and riding animals from you. We will retain as spoils whatever we seized from you, while you must return whatever you seized from us. You must pay blood money for those of us whom you killed, while your dead will be in the Fire. You must also leave some people to follow the tails of camels until Allah shows the caliph of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the Emigrants something by which they may excuse you.” Abū Bakr presented his proposal to the people. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb stood and said: “You have formed an opinion, and we shall offer you our counsel. As for what you said about a war that drives them from their homes and a humiliating peace, what you said is excellent. As for retaining whatever we seized from them while they return whatever they seized from us, what you said is excellent. But as for your statement that they must pay blood money for our dead while their dead will be in the Fire—our dead fought and were killed in obedience to Allah’s command. Their rewards are with Allah, and no blood money is due for them.” The people then successively agreed with what ʿUmar had said."],[56,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī reported: They set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey and divided into separate groups, one group accompanying one person and another accompanying someone else. I joined Abū Bakr’s group. With us was a Bedouin, and we stopped near a Bedouin household in which there was a pregnant woman. The Bedouin said to her: “Would you like to give birth to a boy? If you give me a sheep, you will give birth to a boy.” She gave him a sheep, and he recited rhyming phrases for her. He then slaughtered the sheep. When the people sat down to eat, a man asked: “Do you know the story of this sheep?” He told them what had happened. I then saw Abū Bakr relieving himself, seeking stones for cleansing, and vomiting."],[57,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: When the Prophet ﷺ arrived, none of his Companions had graying hair except Abū Bakr, so he dyed it with henna and katam."],[58,"Abū Hurayrah reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died, Abū Bakr succeeded him as caliph and those Arabs who fell into disbelief did so, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb said to Abū Bakr: “How can you fight the people when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ‘I have been commanded to fight the people until they say, “There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah.” Whoever says, “There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah,” has protected his property and life from me, except where it is lawfully due, and his reckoning is with Allah’?” Abū Bakr replied: “By Allah, I will certainly fight anyone who separates prayer from zakāh, for zakāh is the right due upon wealth. By Allah, if they withheld from me even a rope which they used to give to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, I would fight them for withholding it.” ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb said: “By Allah, once I saw that Allah Almighty had opened Abū Bakr’s heart to fighting them, I knew that it was the truth.”"],[59,"Ibn ʿUmar reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Allah, strengthen Islam through whichever of these two men is more beloved to You: Abū Jahl or ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.” The more beloved of the two to Him was ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb."],[60,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Allah, strengthen Islam specifically through ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.”"],[61,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: While ʿUmar was at home in fear, al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wā’il al-Sahmī, Abū ʿAmr, came to him. He was wearing an embroidered cloak and a shirt edged with silk. He belonged to Banū Sahm, who had been our allies during the pre-Islamic period. He asked ʿUmar: “What is the matter with you?” ʿUmar replied: “Your people claim that they will kill me if I embrace Islam.” Al-ʿĀṣ said: “No one can reach you.” After he said this, I felt safe. Al-ʿĀṣ went out and encountered a crowd of people so numerous that they filled and flowed through the valley. He asked: “Where are you going?” They replied: “We are going after this Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb who has abandoned his religion.” He said: “No one can reach him.” The people then turned back."],[62,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Hishām reported: We were with the Prophet ﷺ while he was holding ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb by the hand. ʿUmar said to him: “O Messenger of Allah, you are certainly more beloved to me than everything except myself.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “No, by the One in Whose hand is my soul, not until I am more beloved to you than your own self.” ʿUmar then said: “Now, by Allah, you are more beloved to me than my own self.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Now, O ʿUmar.”"],[63,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Among the nations before you were people who were divinely inspired. If there is anyone of this kind in my nation, it is ʿUmar.”"],[64,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to say: “There were divinely inspired people among the nations before you. If there is anyone among my nation who is one of them, it is ʿUmar, for ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb is one of them.”"],[65,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “While I was sleeping, I saw the people being presented before me wearing shirts. Some of them reached their chests, while others were shorter than that. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb passed before me wearing a shirt which he was dragging.” They asked: “How did you interpret it, O Messenger of Allah?” He replied: “The religion.”"],[66,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar reported: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “While I was sleeping, I was brought a cup of milk. I drank from it until I could see the signs of satiation emerging through my fingernails. I then gave what remained of it to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.” They asked: “How did you interpret it, O Messenger of Allah?” He replied: “Knowledge.”"],[67,"Ibn ʿUmar reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I saw in a dream that I was given a large vessel filled with milk. I drank until I was completely filled, to the point that I saw it flowing through my veins between my skin and flesh. Some remained, so I gave it to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb. Interpret it.” They said: “O Prophet of Allah, this is knowledge which Allah gave you and with which He filled you. Some of it remained, so you gave it to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.” He said: “You are correct.”"],[68,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ reported: ʿUmar sought permission to enter upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while some women of Quraysh were with him, speaking to him, asking him for more and raising their voices over his voice. When ʿUmar sought permission to enter, they hurried to conceal themselves behind the curtain. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ permitted him to enter while laughing. ʿUmar said: “May Allah keep you smiling, O Messenger of Allah.” He said: “I was astonished by these women who were with me. As soon as they heard your voice, they hurried behind the curtain.” ʿUmar said: “You, O Messenger of Allah, were more deserving of being held in awe by them.” He then addressed the women: “O enemies of your own souls! Do you fear me but do not fear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” They replied: “Yes, for you are harsher and sterner than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “By the One in Whose hand is my soul, Satan has never encountered you travelling along a mountain pass except that he took a different pass from yours.”"],[69,"Abū Hurayrah reported: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while some women were with him and had raised their voices in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When ʿUmar sought permission to enter, they hurried behind the curtain. He then related a report similar to the preceding hadith of Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ."],[70,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sitting when we heard a commotion and the voices of children. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up and saw an Abyssinian woman dancing while the children were gathered around her. He said: “O ʿĀ’ishah, come and watch.” I came and placed my chin upon the shoulder of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I began watching her from the space between his shoulder and his head. He asked me: “Have you not had enough? Have you not had enough?” I continued saying, “No,” because I wanted to see my standing with him. Then ʿUmar appeared, and the people scattered from around her. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I can indeed see the devils among humankind and the jinn fleeing from ʿUmar.” She said: “I then returned.”"],[71,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “ʿUmar never travelled along a mountain pass except that Satan took a different pass.”"],[72,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “I saw myself entering Paradise, where I encountered al-Rumayṣāʾ, the wife of Abū Ṭalḥah. I heard the sound of footsteps and asked, ‘Who is this?’ He replied, ‘This is Bilāl.’ I also saw a palace with a young woman in its courtyard. I asked, ‘To whom does this belong?’ He replied, ‘To ʿUmar.’ I wanted to enter it and look inside, but I remembered your jealousy.” ʿUmar said: “May my father and mother be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah! Would I be jealous concerning you?”"],[73,"Abū Hurayrah reported: While we were sitting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he said: “While I was sleeping, I saw myself in Paradise. There was a woman performing ablution beside a palace. I asked, ‘To whom does this belong?’ They replied, ‘It belongs to ʿUmar.’ I remembered his jealousy, so I turned away.” ʿUmar, who was present in the gathering, wept and said: “Would I be jealous concerning you, O Messenger of Allah?”"],[74,"Anas reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I entered Paradise and found myself before a palace made of gold. I asked, ‘To whom does this palace belong?’ They replied, ‘To a young man from Quraysh.’ I thought that I was that man, so I asked, ‘To whom does it belong?’ They replied, ‘To ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.’” He continued: “Had it not been for what I knew of your jealousy, I would have entered it.” ʿUmar said: “Would I be jealous concerning you, O Messenger of Allah?”"],[75,"Buraydah ibn al-Ḥuṣayb reported: One morning, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned Bilāl and said: “O Bilāl, by what deed did you precede me into Paradise? I have never entered Paradise without hearing the sound of your footsteps before me. Last night, I entered Paradise and heard your footsteps. I then came upon a lofty, elevated palace made of gold. I asked, ‘To whom does this palace belong?’ They replied, ‘To an Arab man.’ I said, ‘I am an Arab. To whom does this palace belong?’ They replied, ‘To a Muslim man from the nation of Muḥammad.’ I said, ‘I am Muḥammad. To whom does this palace belong?’ They replied, ‘To ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.’” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “Had it not been for your jealousy, O ʿUmar, I would have entered the palace.” ʿUmar said: “O Messenger of Allah, I would never be jealous concerning you.” He then asked Bilāl: “By what deed did you precede me into Paradise?” Bilāl replied: “Whenever I entered a state of minor ritual impurity, I performed ablution and prayed two rakʿahs.”\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “It was because of this.”"],[76,"Anas reported that ʿUmar said: “My opinion corresponded with my Lord’s revelation on three occasions. I said, ‘O Messenger of Allah, if only we were to take the Station of Abraham as a place of prayer.’ Then the following was revealed: ‘Take the Station of Abraham as a place of prayer.’ [Al-Baqarah 2:125] Regarding the verse of the veil, I said: ‘O Messenger of Allah, if only you would command your wives to observe the veil, for both the righteous and the wicked speak to them.’ Then the verse of the veil was revealed. The wives of the Prophet ﷺ also united in their jealousy concerning him, so I said to them: ‘Perhaps, if he divorces you, his Lord will replace you with wives better than you.’ [Al-Taḥrīm 66:5] Then this verse was revealed.”"],[77,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, reported: Sawdah went out to attend to her needs after the command concerning the veil had been revealed. She was a large woman and could not remain unrecognized by anyone who knew her. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb saw her and said: “O Sawdah, by Allah, you cannot remain unrecognized by us, so consider carefully how you go out.” She turned back while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in my house, eating his evening meal and holding a bone with meat upon it. She entered and said: “O Messenger of Allah, I went out to attend to some of my needs, and ʿUmar said such-and-such to me.” Allah then sent revelation to him. When the state of revelation had passed, the bone was still in his hand, and he had not put it down. He said: “You have been granted permission to go out to attend to your needs.”"],[78,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: When ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl died, his son, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh, came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked him to give him his shirt so that he could shroud his father in it. He gave it to him. He then asked him to perform the funeral prayer over his father. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood to perform the prayer over him, but ʿUmar stood and took hold of the garment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saying: “O Messenger of Allah, will you pray over him when Allah has forbidden you to pray over him?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “Allah has only given me a choice, for He said: ‘Ask forgiveness for them, or do not ask forgiveness for them. Even if you ask forgiveness for them seventy times…’ [Al-Tawbah 9:80]—and I shall ask more than seventy times.” ʿUmar said: “He is a hypocrite.” Nevertheless, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed the funeral prayer over him. Allah Almighty then revealed: “Never perform the funeral prayer over any of them who dies, nor stand at his grave.” [Al-Tawbah 9:84]"],[79,"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb reported: When ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl died, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was called to perform the funeral prayer over him. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood to do so, I sprang toward him and said: “O Messenger of Allah, will you pray over Ibn Ubayy when he said such-and-such on such-and-such a day?” I began recounting his statements to him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ smiled and said: “Stand back from me, O ʿUmar.” When I persisted, he said: “I was given a choice, and I have chosen. If I knew that he would be forgiven if I asked more than seventy times, I would ask more than seventy times.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then performed the funeral prayer over him and departed. Only a short time passed before the two verses from Sūrat Barā’ah were revealed: “Never perform the funeral prayer over any of them who dies…” through His words: “…while they were defiantly disobedient.” [Al-Tawbah 9:84] ʿUmar said: “Afterward, I was astonished at my boldness toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Allah and His Messenger know best.”"],[80,"Ibn ʿUmar reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Allah Almighty has placed the truth in ʿUmar’s heart and upon his tongue.” Ibn ʿUmar also said: “Whenever an issue arose among the people and they expressed an opinion concerning it, and ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb also expressed his opinion—or whenever ʿUmar expressed an opinion—the Qur’an was revealed in accordance with what ʿUmar had said.”"],[81,"Ghuḍayf ibn al-Ḥārith reported that he passed by ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, who said: “What an excellent young man Ghuḍayf is!” Ghuḍayf later met Abū Dharr, who said to him: “My brother, ask Allah to forgive me.” Ghuḍayf replied: “You are a Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and you are more deserving of asking forgiveness for me!” Abū Dharr said: “I heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb say, ‘What an excellent young man Ghuḍayf is!’ And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘Allah has firmly established the truth upon ʿUmar’s tongue and in his heart.’”"],[82,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Allah has placed the truth upon ʿUmar’s tongue and in his heart.”"],[83,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “There has never been a prophet except that within his nation there was one man—or two men—who were divinely instructed. If there is anyone of this kind in my nation, it is ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb. Indeed, the truth is upon ʿUmar’s tongue and in his heart.”"],[84,"Ḥudhayfah reported: We were with ʿUmar when he asked: “Which of you has preserved the hadith of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning tribulation exactly as he stated it?” I replied: “I have.” He said: “You are certainly bold! How did he state it?” I replied: “I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘A man’s trials concerning his family, wealth, self, children, and neighbor are expiated by fasting, prayer, charity, enjoining what is right, and forbidding what is wrong.’” ʿUmar said: “That is not what I mean. I mean the tribulation that will surge like the waves of the sea.” I said: “Why should you be concerned about it, O Commander of the Believers? Between you and it is a closed door.” He asked: “Will the door be broken or opened?” I replied: “No, it will be broken.” He said: “Then it is more likely never to be closed again.” We later asked Ḥudhayfah: “Did ʿUmar know who the door was?” He replied: “Yes, just as he knew that tonight comes before tomorrow. I narrated to him a report containing no errors or riddles.”\nWe were too apprehensive to ask Ḥudhayfah who the door was, so we said to Masrūq: “Ask him.” Masrūq asked him, and he replied: “ʿUmar.”"],[85,"Abū Burdah ibn Abī Mūsā al-Ashʿarī reported: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar asked me: “Do you know what my father said to your father?” I replied: “No.” He said: “My father said to your father: ‘O Abū Mūsā, would it please you if our acceptance of Islam with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, our migration with him, our striving alongside him, and all our deeds with him were secured for us, while we escaped from every deed we performed after him neither rewarded nor punished—simply even, with nothing for us and nothing against us?’ “My father replied: ‘No, by Allah! After the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, we fought in Allah’s cause, prayed, fasted, performed much good, and many people accepted Islam at our hands. We certainly hope to be rewarded for that.’ “Then my father said: ʿAs for me, by the One in Whose hand is ʿUmar’s soul, I wish that what we did with him had been secured for us and that we escaped from everything we did afterward simply even—with nothing for us and nothing against us.’” I said: “By Allah, your father was better than mine.”"],[86,"Saʿīd ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl said in the mosque of Kūfah: “By Allah, I remember when ʿUmar had bound me because of my acceptance of Islam, before he himself became Muslim. If Mount Uḥud had collapsed because of what you did to ʿUthmān, it would have been justified.”"],[87,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd said: “We have remained strong and honored ever since ʿUmar accepted Islam.”"],[88,"Abū ʿUthmān reported: I heard Ibn ʿUmar become angry whenever it was said to him: “He migrated before his father.” He explained: “ʿUmar and I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and found him taking his midday rest, so we returned home. ʿUmar then sent me, saying: ‘Go and see whether he has awakened.’ “I went to him, entered upon him, and pledged allegiance to him. I then went to ʿUmar and informed him that the Prophet ﷺ had awakened. We hurried to him at a brisk pace. ʿUmar entered and pledged allegiance to him, and then I pledged allegiance to him.”"],[89,"Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān reported: ʿUmar was invited to attend a funeral. As he went out to attend it—or was about to do so—I took hold of him and said: “Remain seated, O Commander of the Believers, for the deceased is one of those people.” ʿUmar said: “I adjure you by Allah: am I one of them?” Ḥudhayfah replied: “No, but I will not declare anyone after you free of suspicion.”"],[90,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: Whenever ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb finished a prayer, he would sit with the people so that anyone who had a need could speak to him. If no one had a need, he would rise and enter his residence. On several occasions, however, he prayed without sitting with the people afterward. Ibn ʿAbbās said: “I came to his door and asked, ‘O Yarfaʾ, is the Commander of the Believers ill?’ “He replied, ‘There is nothing wrong with the Commander of the Believers.’ “I sat down, and then ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān arrived and sat as well. Yarfaʾ came out and said, ‘Rise, Ibn ʿAffān; rise, Ibn ʿAbbās.’ “We entered upon ʿUmar and found piles of wealth before him, with a shoulder blade placed upon each pile. ʿUmar said: “‘I have considered the people of Madinah and found that you have two of its largest clans. Take this wealth and divide it between yourselves. If anything remains, return it.’ “ʿUthmān began scooping up the wealth, but I knelt upon my knees and asked: “‘If it falls short, will you give us more?’ “He replied: “‘A trait from Akhshan—a stone from a mountain! Was this wealth not with Allah when Muḥammad ﷺ and his Companions were eating dried strips of hide?’ “I said: “‘Yes, by Allah, this wealth was with Allah Almighty while Muḥammad ﷺ was alive. Had it been granted to him, he would have dealt with it differently from the way you are dealing with it.’ “ʿUmar became angry and said: “‘Tell me, what would he have done?’ “I replied: “‘He would have eaten from it and fed us as well.’ “ʿUmar then sobbed so intensely that his ribs shook, and he said: “‘I wish I could leave this matter simply even—with nothing for me and nothing against me.’”"],[91,"Zayd ibn Aslam reported from his father: Ibn ʿUmar asked me about some of his father ʿUmar’s affairs, so I informed him. He then said: “Since the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, I have never seen anyone work with greater diligence and generosity, persevering until he completed what he undertook, than ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.”"],[92,"ʿAmr ibn Maymūn reported: A few days before ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, was attacked, I saw him in Madinah standing with Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān and ʿUthmān ibn Ḥunayf. He asked them: “How have you proceeded? Do you fear that you may have imposed upon the land more than it can bear?” They replied: “We have imposed upon it something it is capable of bearing, and there is no great excess in it.” He said: “Make certain that you have not imposed upon the land more than it can bear.” They replied: “No.” ʿUmar then said: “If Allah preserves me, I shall certainly leave the widows of Iraq in such a condition that they will never need to depend upon any man after me.” Only four days passed before he was attacked. On the morning when he was attacked, I was standing with no one between him and me except ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās. Whenever ʿUmar passed between the rows, he would say, “Straighten your rows.” When he saw no gap in them, he would step forward and pronounce the takbīr. He would sometimes recite Sūrat Yūsuf, Sūrat al-Naḥl, or something similar during the first rakʿah so that the people could gather. He had scarcely pronounced the takbīr when I heard him say: “The dog has killed me—or devoured me!” This was when the attacker stabbed him. The non-Arab assailant fled with a double-edged knife, stabbing everyone he passed on his right or left, until he had stabbed thirteen men, seven of whom died. When a Muslim man saw this, he threw a cloak over him. Realizing that he had been captured, the assailant cut his own throat. ʿUmar took the hand of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf and moved him forward to lead the prayer. Those close to ʿUmar saw what I saw, but those in the other parts of the mosque did not know what had happened. They only noticed that ʿUmar’s voice was absent, and they kept saying, “Subḥān Allah! Subḥān Allah!” ʿAbd al-Raḥmān therefore led them in a brief prayer. When they finished, ʿUmar said: “O Ibn ʿAbbās, find out who attacked me.” He went around for a while and then returned, saying: “It was al-Mughīrah’s slave.” ʿUmar asked: “The skilled craftsman?” He replied: “Yes.” ʿUmar said: “May Allah destroy him! I had commanded that he be treated well. Praise be to Allah, who did not cause my death to come at the hands of a man claiming Islam. You and your father used to favor increasing the number of non-Arabs in Madinah.” Most of them were slaves. Ibn ʿAbbās said: “If you wish, I will do it,” meaning: “If you wish, we will kill them.” ʿUmar replied: “You are mistaken! How could that be done after they have spoken your language, prayed toward your qiblah, and performed the pilgrimage as you do?” ʿUmar was carried to his home, and we went with him. It was as though the people had never suffered a calamity before that day. Some said, “There is nothing seriously wrong with him,” while others said, “I fear for him.” He was brought nabīdh and drank it, but it came out through his abdomen. He was then brought milk and drank it, but it emerged from his wound. They therefore knew that he was dying. We entered upon him, and people came praising him. A young man came and said: “Rejoice, O Commander of the Believers, in the glad tidings Allah has prepared for you: the companionship of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, your early precedence in Islam, which you know well, then your appointment as ruler and your governing with justice, and now martyrdom.” ʿUmar replied: “I wish that all of that would leave me even—with nothing against me and nothing in my favor.” When the young man turned away, his lower garment was dragging along the ground. ʿUmar said: “Bring the young man back to me.” He then told him: “O son of my brother, raise your garment, for that will preserve your garment and is more mindful of your Lord.” He then said: “O ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, determine how much debt I owe.” They calculated it and found it to be approximately eighty-six thousand dirhams. ʿUmar said: “If the wealth of the family of ʿUmar is sufficient to repay it, then repay it from their wealth. Otherwise, ask Banū ʿAdī ibn Kaʿb. If their wealth is insufficient, ask Quraysh, but do not go beyond them to anyone else. Repay this debt on my behalf.” He then said: “Go to ʿĀ’ishah, the Mother of the Believers, and say: ‘ʿUmar sends you greetings of peace.’ Do not say, ‘The Commander of the Believers,’ for today I am no longer the commander of the believers. Say: ‘ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb requests permission to be buried beside his two companions.’” ʿAbdullāh went, conveyed the greeting, and requested permission. He entered and found her sitting and weeping. He said: “ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb sends you greetings of peace and requests permission to be buried beside his two companions.” She replied: “I had wished that place for myself, but today I shall give him preference over myself.” When ʿAbdullāh returned, someone said: “ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar has arrived.” ʿUmar said: “Raise me.” A man supported him, and ʿUmar asked: “What news do you have?” He replied: “What you wished for, O Commander of the Believers. She has granted permission.” ʿUmar said: “Praise be to Allah. Nothing was more important to me than that. When I die, carry me there, then convey greetings of peace and say: ‘ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb requests permission to enter.’ If she grants me permission, take me inside; but if she refuses me, return me to the Muslim cemetery.” Ḥafṣah, the Mother of the Believers, then came accompanied by other women. When we saw her, we stood up. She entered upon him and wept beside him for a while. The men then requested permission to enter, so she went into an inner room, and we could hear her weeping from inside. They said: “Give your final instructions, O Commander of the Believers, and appoint a successor.” He replied: “I find no one more entitled to this matter than these men—or this group—with whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was pleased when he died.” He named ʿAlī, ʿUthmān, al-Zubayr, Ṭalḥah, Saʿd, and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. He said: “ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar shall attend your deliberations, but he shall have no share in the matter,” as a means of consoling him. “If authority falls to Saʿd, then so be it. Otherwise, whoever among you is appointed should seek his assistance, for I did not remove him because of incompetence or dishonesty.” He continued: “I instruct the caliph after me to honor the rights of the earliest Emigrants and preserve their sanctity. I instruct him to treat the Anṣār well—those who established their home and embraced the faith before them—to accept the good deeds of their virtuous members and pardon those who fall into error. “I instruct him to treat the inhabitants of the provinces well, for they are the support of Islam, the collectors of its wealth, and a source of rage to the enemy. Nothing should be taken from them beyond their surplus, and only with their consent. “I instruct him to treat the Bedouins well, for they are the origin of the Arabs and the strength of Islam. Zakāh should be taken from the outer portions of their wealth and returned to their poor. “I instruct him concerning those protected by the covenant of Allah Almighty and the covenant of His Messenger ﷺ: their covenant must be fulfilled, their enemies must be fought in their defense, and they must not be burdened beyond their capacity.” When ʿUmar passed away, we carried him out and proceeded with him. ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar conveyed greetings of peace and said: “ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb requests permission to enter.” ʿĀ’ishah said: “Bring him in.” He was brought inside and laid beside his two companions. When his burial was completed, the appointed group assembled. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: “Reduce the matter to three among you.” Al-Zubayr said: “I give my authority to ʿAlī.” Ṭalḥah said: “I give my authority to ʿUthmān.” Saʿd said: “I give my authority to ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf.” ʿAbd al-Raḥmān then said to ʿAlī and ʿUthmān: “Which of you will withdraw from this matter so that we may entrust the decision to him? Allah and Islam shall be his witnesses that he must consider which of the two is better in his judgment.” The two elders remained silent. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: “Will you entrust the matter to me? By Allah, I shall spare no effort in selecting the better of you.” They replied: “Yes.” He took one of them by the hand and said: “You possess kinship with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the precedence in Islam that you know. I adjure you by Allah: if I appoint you, you must rule with justice; and if I appoint ʿUthmān, you must listen and obey.” He then spoke privately to the other and said the same to him. After securing their covenant, he said: “Raise your hand, O ʿUthmān.” ʿAbd al-Raḥmān pledged allegiance to him, then ʿAlī pledged allegiance to him, and the people of the house entered and pledged allegiance to him."],[93,"Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah reported: When ʿUmar was stabbed, he began to suffer pain. Ibn ʿAbbās said to him, as though seeking to console him: “O Commander of the Believers, even if this has happened, you accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and were an excellent companion to him. Then you parted from him while he was pleased with you. You accompanied Abū Bakr and were an excellent companion to him. Then you parted from him while he was pleased with you. You then accompanied their Companions and treated them well. If you now part from them, you will certainly leave them while they are pleased with you.” ʿUmar replied: “As for what you mentioned concerning my companionship with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his pleasure with me, that was only a favor Allah Almighty bestowed upon me. As for what you mentioned concerning my companionship with Abū Bakr and his pleasure with me, that too was only a favor Allah—Majestic is His remembrance—bestowed upon me. “As for the distress you see in me, it is on account of you and your companions. By Allah, if I possessed enough gold to fill the earth, I would use it to ransom myself from the punishment of Allah Almighty before seeing it.”"],[94,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: When Abū Luʾluʾah stabbed ʿUmar, he stabbed him twice. ʿUmar feared that he might have committed some wrong against the people of which he was unaware. He therefore summoned Ibn ʿAbbās—whom he loved, kept close, and listened to—and said: “I want you to find out from the gatherings of the people whether this happened because of something they held against me.” Ibn ʿAbbās went out, and he did not pass any gathering of people without finding them weeping. He returned and said: “O Commander of the Believers, I did not come upon any gathering of Muslims without finding them weeping as though they had lost the firstborn of their children today.” ʿUmar asked: “Who attacked me?” He replied: “Abū Luʾluʾah the Magian, the slave of al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah.” Ibn ʿAbbās said: “I saw joy appear upon his face.” ʿUmar said: “Praise be to Allah, who did not test me through someone who could dispute with me by saying, ‘There is no deity except Allah.’ I had forbidden you from bringing any of these non-Arabs among us, but you disobeyed me.” He then said: “Call my brothers for me.” They asked: “Who are they?” He replied: “ʿUthmān, ʿAlī, Ṭalḥah, al-Zubayr, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, and Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ.” They sent for them, and ʿUmar placed his head in my lap. When they arrived, I said: “They are all present.” He said: “Yes. I have considered the Muslims’ affair and found that you six are the leaders and commanders of the people. This authority will belong only to you. So long as you remain upright, the people’s affairs will remain upright; but if disagreement arises, it will arise among you.” When I heard him mention disagreement and division, I became convinced that it would occur, for he rarely said anything without my later seeing it come to pass. He then began losing blood. The six men whispered among themselves until I feared that they might pledge allegiance to one of them. I said: “The Commander of the Believers is still alive, and there cannot be two caliphs looking at one another.” ʿUmar said: “Carry me.” So we carried him. He said: “Consult one another for three days, and let Ṣuhayb lead the people in prayer.” They asked: “Whom should we consult, O Commander of the Believers?” He replied: “Consult the Emigrants, the Anṣār, and the prominent commanders of the armies who are present here.” He then called for a cup of milk and drank it. The whiteness of the milk emerged from both wounds, so he knew that death had come. He said: “Now, if I possessed the entire world, I would use it to ransom myself from the terror of what lies ahead. Yet, praise be to Allah, I do not believe that I have seen anything but good.” Ibn ʿAbbās said: “Even if you say that, may Allah reward you with goodness! Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ not supplicate that Allah strengthen the religion and the Muslims through you when they were living in fear in Makkah? When you accepted Islam, your Islam was a source of strength, and through you Islam, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and his Companions became publicly manifest. “You migrated to Madinah, and your migration was a victory. You were never absent from any battle in which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fought the polytheists, on such-and-such a day and such-and-such a day. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away while pleased with you. “You supported the caliph after him upon the way of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. You confronted those who turned away with the help of those who advanced, until people entered Islam willingly or unwillingly. Then that caliph passed away while pleased with you. “You were then entrusted with authority in the best manner in which anyone has governed the people. Through you, Allah established the garrison cities, collected wealth, and drove back the enemy. Through you, Allah brought into every household an expansion in their religion and an increase in their provision. Then He concluded your life with martyrdom. So congratulations to you!” ʿUmar replied: “By Allah, the truly deceived person is the one whom you cause to become deceived by such praise.” He then asked: “Will you testify to this for me before Allah on the Day of Resurrection, O ʿAbdullāh?” Ibn ʿAbbās replied: “Yes.” ʿUmar said: “O Allah, to You belongs all praise. Place my cheek upon the ground, O ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās.” I lowered his head from my thigh to my shin. He again said: “Place my cheek upon the ground.” He allowed his beard and cheek to rest upon the earth and said: “Woe to you—and woe to your mother, O ʿUmar—if Allah does not forgive you!” He then passed away, may Allah have mercy upon him. After his death, they sent for ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar. He said: “I will not come to you unless you carry out his command to consult the Emigrants, the Anṣār, and the prominent commanders of the armies who are present here.” When al-Ḥasan was told about ʿUmar’s conduct at the time of his death and his fear of his Lord, he said: “Such is the believer: he combines righteous deeds with fear and apprehension. The hypocrite combines wrongdoing with deluded confidence. By Allah, I have never found—among those of the past or those who remain—a servant who increased in righteousness without also increasing in fear and apprehension of Him. Nor have I found a servant who increased in wrongdoing without also increasing in deluded confidence.”"],[95,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was reclining in my house with his thighs—or his lower legs—uncovered. Abū Bakr sought permission to enter, and he permitted him while remaining in that state, and they spoke. Then ʿUmar sought permission to enter, and he permitted him while remaining in the same state, and they spoke. Then ʿUthmān sought permission to enter. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat up and adjusted his clothes. Muḥammad said: “I do not say that all of this occurred on the same day.” ʿUthmān entered and spoke with him. After he left, ʿĀ’ishah said: “Abū Bakr entered, yet you did not become especially animated or concerned about his entrance. Then ʿUmar entered, and you likewise did not. But when ʿUthmān entered, you sat up and adjusted your clothes.” He ﷺ said: “Should I not feel shy before a man before whom the angels feel shy?”"],[96,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, and ʿUthmān narrated that Abū Bakr sought permission to enter upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was reclining on his bed, wearing the outer garment of ʿĀ’ishah. The Prophet ﷺ admitted Abū Bakr while remaining in that state. Abū Bakr fulfilled the purpose for which he had come and then departed. After that, ʿUmar sought permission to enter. The Prophet ﷺ admitted him while remaining in the same state. He fulfilled his need and then departed. ʿUthmān said: Then I sought permission to enter. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat upright and said to ʿĀ’ishah: “Gather your garments around yourself.” I fulfilled my need and then left. ʿĀ’ishah asked: “O Messenger of Allah, why did I not see you become concerned for Abū Bakr and ʿUmar as you did for ʿUthmān?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “Indeed, ʿUthmān is a man of great modesty, and I feared that if I admitted him while I was in that condition, he would not be able to express the purpose for which he had come.”"],[97,"Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ʿUthmān was absent from the Battle of Badr only because the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who was his wife, was ill. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: “You shall have the reward of a man who participated in the Battle of Badr, as well as his share of the spoils.”"],[98,"ʿUthmān ibn Mawhab narrated: A man from the people of Egypt came to perform Ḥajj. He saw a group of people sitting together and asked, “Who are these people?” They replied, “They are Quraysh.” He asked, “Who is the elder among them?” They answered, “ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar.” The man said, “O Ibn ʿUmar, I wish to ask you about something, so tell me. Do you know that ʿUthmān fled on the Day of Uḥud?” He replied, “Yes.” The man asked, “Do you know that he was absent from Badr and did not participate?” He replied, “Yes.” The man asked, “Do you know that he was absent from the Pledge of al-Riḍwān and did not attend it?” He replied, “Yes.” The man exclaimed, “Allāhu Akbar!” Ibn ʿUmar said: “Come, and I will explain it to you. As for his retreat on the Day of Uḥud, I testify that Allah pardoned him and forgave him. As for his absence from Badr, it was because the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who was his wife, was ill. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: ‘You shall have the reward of a man who participated in Badr, along with his share of the spoils.’ As for his absence from the Pledge of al-Riḍwān, if there had been anyone in the heart of Makkah more honored than ʿUthmān, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would have sent him instead. However, he sent ʿUthmān. The Pledge of al-Riḍwān took place after ʿUthmān had gone to Makkah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took his right hand and said: ‘This is the hand of ʿUthmān.’ Then he struck it upon his other hand and said: ‘This is on behalf of ʿUthmān.’” Ibn ʿUmar then said to the man: “Now take this answer with you.”"],[99,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Samurah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān came to the Prophet ﷺ carrying one thousand dinars wrapped in his garment when the Prophet ﷺ was preparing the Army of Hardship. He poured them into the lap of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ began turning the coins over with his hand while repeatedly saying: “Nothing that Ibn ʿAffān does after this day will harm him.” He repeated these words several times."],[100,"Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated: When ʿUthmān was under siege, he addressed the people from above and said: “I adjure you by Allah—and I ask only the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ— Do you not know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘Whoever develops the Well of Rūmah shall have Paradise,’ and I was the one who developed it? Do you not know that he also said: ‘Whoever equips the Army of Hardship shall have Paradise,’ and I was the one who equipped it?” The people acknowledged that what he had said was true."],[101,"Saʿīd ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl said in the Mosque of Kūfah: “By Allah, I remember the time when ʿUmar had me bound because I had accepted Islam, before he himself embraced Islam. If Mount Uḥud had collapsed because of what you have done to ʿUthmān, it would have been justified.” Another wording states: “If you had seen me when ʿUmar had both me and his own sister bound because of Islam before he embraced Islam! If Mount Uḥud had collapsed because of what you have done to ʿUthmān, it would certainly have had every right to collapse.”"],[102,"ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAdī ibn al-Khiyār reported that al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aswad ibn ʿAbd Yaghūth said to him: “Why do you not speak to your maternal uncle, ʿUthmān, concerning his brother al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah? The people have spoken much about what happened regarding him.” ʿUbaydullāh said: I stood waiting for ʿUthmān as he came out for the prayer and said, “I have something to say to you as sincere advice.” He replied, “O man, I seek refuge in Allah from you.” So I withdrew. After the prayer I sat with al-Miswar and Ibn ʿAbd Yaghūth and informed them of what had occurred. They said: “You have fulfilled your duty.” While I was sitting with them, a messenger from ʿUthmān came to summon me. They said: “Allah has now put you to the test.” I entered upon ʿUthmān, who asked: “What was the advice you mentioned earlier?” I praised Allah and testified to the faith, then said: “Allah sent Muḥammad ﷺ with the truth and revealed the Book to him. You were among those who responded to Allah and His Messenger, believed in him, migrated the two migrations, accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and witnessed his guidance. The people have spoken much regarding al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah, so it is your duty to carry out the prescribed punishment upon him.” ʿUthmān asked: “My nephew, did you meet the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” I replied: “No, but knowledge of him has reached me just as surely as it reaches a secluded virgin behind her curtain.” ʿUthmān then praised Allah and said: “Allah sent Muḥammad ﷺ with the truth and revealed the Book to him. I responded to Allah and His Messenger, believed in what Muḥammad ﷺ was sent with, made the two migrations as you have mentioned, accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and pledged allegiance to him. By Allah, I never disobeyed him nor betrayed him until Allah took him in death. Then Allah appointed Abū Bakr as Caliph, and by Allah, I neither disobeyed him nor betrayed him. Then ʿUmar became Caliph, and by Allah, I neither disobeyed him nor betrayed him. Then I became Caliph. Do I not deserve from you the same loyalty that they deserved from me?” They replied: “Indeed.” He continued: “Then what are these reports that reach me from among you? As for what you have mentioned concerning al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah, we shall, Allah willing, deal with him according to the truth.” The narrator said: Al-Walīd was then flogged with forty lashes, and ʿUthmān instructed ʿAlī to carry out the punishment, and ʿAlī himself administered the lashes."],[103,"Saʿd ibn ʿUbaydah said: A man came to Ibn ʿUmar and asked him about ʿUthmān. Ibn ʿUmar mentioned his good qualities and virtuous deeds. He then said: “Perhaps that displeases you?” The man replied: “Yes.” Ibn ʿUmar said: “May Allah humble you.” Then the man asked him about ʿAlī. Ibn ʿUmar mentioned his virtues and good qualities and then pointed toward his house, saying: “That is his house, situated among the houses of the Prophet ﷺ.” He then asked: “Perhaps that displeases you?” The man replied: “Yes.” Ibn ʿUmar said: “May Allah humble you. Go and do whatever you can against me.”"],[104,"Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ once mentioned a coming trial (fitnah). As he was speaking, a man passed by. The Prophet ﷺ said: “This veiled man will be killed during that trial while being wronged.” Ibn ʿUmar said: I looked, and it was ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān."],[105,"Abū al-Ashʿath al-Ṣanʿānī narrated: A group of speakers addressed the people in Syria, among whom were several Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The last to stand was a man named Murrah ibn Kaʿb. He said: “Had I not heard a statement from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, I would not have stood to speak.” He then mentioned the future trials and said they were drawing near. At that moment, a man passed by with his face covered by his garment. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “This man, on that day, will be upon true guidance.” I went toward him and looked at his face. It was ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān. I turned him toward me and asked: “This man?” He replied: “Yes.”"],[106,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent for ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān. When ʿUthmān came, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke privately with him. When we saw this, we turned our attention away from them toward one another. Among the last things the Prophet ﷺ said to him was that he struck him gently on the shoulder and said: “O ʿUthmān, perhaps Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, will clothe you with a garment. If the hypocrites seek to make you remove it, then do not remove it until you meet me. O ʿUthmān, perhaps Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, will clothe you with a garment. If the hypocrites seek to make you remove it, then do not remove it until you meet me.” He repeated this three times. The narrator said: I asked her, “O Mother of the Believers, where was this narration all this time?” She replied: “By Allah, I had forgotten it, so I never mentioned it.” The narrator said: I informed Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān of this narration, but he was not satisfied with merely hearing it from me. Instead, he wrote to the Mother of the Believers requesting that she write it to him herself. So she wrote it to him in a letter."],[107,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: During his illness, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I wish that one of my companions were with me.” We said: “O Messenger of Allah, shall we call Abū Bakr for you?” He remained silent. We said: “Shall we call ʿUmar?” Again, he remained silent. We said: “Shall we call ʿUthmān?” He replied: “Yes.” So ʿUthmān came, and the Prophet ﷺ spoke privately with him. As the Messenger of Allah ﷺ continued speaking, the expression on ʿUthmān’s face began to change."],[108,"Abū Sahlah, the freed servant of ʿUthmān, narrated that on the day his house was surrounded, ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān said: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entrusted me with a matter, and I shall remain patient upon it.” Qays said: “They understood that this was what he was referring to on that day.”"],[109,"Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥanafiyyah said: Had ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) ever wished to speak ill of ʿUthmān, he would have done so on the day when some people came to him complaining about ʿUthmān’s zakāh collectors. Instead, ʿAlī said to me: “Go to ʿUthmān and inform him that this is the instruction of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ regarding the collection of charity. Tell him to instruct his collectors to act according to it.” I brought it to ʿUthmān. He said: “We have no need of it.” So I returned to ʿAlī and informed him. He replied: “Put it back where you took it from.”"],[110,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān asked Saʿd, “What prevents you from reviling Abū Turāb (ʿAlī)?” Saʿd replied: “As long as I remember three statements that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said about him, I will never revile him. Possessing even one of those virtues would be more beloved to me than red camels. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say to him when he left him behind during one of his military expeditions. ʿAlī said: ‘O Messenger of Allah, are you leaving me behind with the women and children?’ The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: ‘Are you not pleased that your position to me is like the position of Hārūn (Aaron) to Mūsā (Moses), except that there will be no prophet after me?’ I also heard him say on the Day of Khaybar: ‘Tomorrow I will certainly give the banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger, and whom Allah and His Messenger love.’ We all hoped it would be given to us. Then he said: ‘Call ʿAlī for me.’ He was brought while suffering from an eye ailment. The Prophet ﷺ applied his saliva to his eyes and handed him the banner, and Allah granted victory through him. And when the following verse was revealed: “Say, ‘Come, let us call our sons and your sons…’” (Āl ʿImrān 3:61), the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called ʿAlī, Fāṭimah, al-Ḥasan, and al-Ḥusayn, and said: “O Allah, these are my family.”"],[111,"Yazīd ibn Ḥayyān said: I, Ḥuṣayn ibn Sabrah, and ʿUmar ibn Muslim went to Zayd ibn Arqam. After we sat with him, Ḥuṣayn said: “O Zayd, you have been blessed with tremendous good. You saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, heard his narrations, fought alongside him, and prayed behind him. Tell us what you heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” Zayd replied: “My nephew, by Allah, I have become old, much time has passed, and I have forgotten some of what I once preserved from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Whatever I narrate to you, accept it, and whatever I do not narrate, do not burden me concerning it.” He then said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ once stood among us delivering a sermon at a watering place called Khumm, between Makkah and Madinah. After praising Allah, glorifying Him, exhorting, and reminding the people, he said: “Indeed, I am only a human being. Soon the messenger of my Lord will come to me, and I will answer His call. I am leaving among you two weighty matters. The first of them is the Book of Allah, in which there is guidance and light. Therefore, hold firmly to the Book of Allah and cling to it.” He strongly encouraged adherence to the Book of Allah. Then he said: “And my household. I remind you by Allah concerning my household. I remind you by Allah concerning my household. I remind you by Allah concerning my household.” Ḥuṣayn then asked: “Who are the members of his household, O Zayd? Are not his wives included among his household?” Zayd replied: “His wives are indeed among his household. However, those specifically intended here are those for whom charity became unlawful after him.” He asked: “Who are they?” Zayd replied: “They are the family of ʿAlī, the family of ʿAqīl, the family of Jaʿfar, and the family of al-ʿAbbās.” He asked: “Was charity forbidden for all of them?” Zayd replied: “Yes.”"],[112,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ departed for the expedition of Tabūk and appointed ʿAlī as his deputy in Madinah. ʿAlī said: “Are you leaving me behind with the women and the children?” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Are you not pleased that your position in relation to me is like the position of Hārūn (Aaron) in relation to Mūsā (Moses), except that there is no prophet after me?”"],[113,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said to ʿAlī: “You are to me in the position of Hārūn (Aaron) in relation to Mūsā (Moses), except that there is no prophet after me.”"],[114,"Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to ʿAlī: “You are to me in the position of Hārūn (Aaron) in relation to Mūsā (Moses), except that there will be no prophet after me.”"],[115,"Sahl ibn Saʿd (may Allah be pleased with him) said: A man from the family of Marwān was appointed governor over Madinah. He summoned Sahl ibn Saʿd and ordered him to revile ʿAlī. Sahl refused. The governor said: “If you refuse, then at least say: ‘May Allah curse Abū Turāb.’” Sahl replied: “There was no name more beloved to ʿAlī than Abū Turāb. In fact, he would become happy whenever he was called by it.” They asked him: “Tell us the story. Why was he called Abū Turāb?” He replied: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to the house of Fāṭimah but did not find ʿAlī there. He asked: “Where is your cousin?” She replied: “Something occurred between us. He became upset with me and left without taking his midday rest here.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to someone: “Go and see where he is.” The man returned and said: “O Messenger of Allah, he is lying down in the mosque.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to him and found him reclining. His cloak had slipped from one side of his body, and dust had settled upon him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ began brushing the dust off him while saying: “Get up, O Abū Turāb. Get up, O Abū Turāb.”"],[116,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ʿAlī had remained behind during the expedition to Khaybar because he was suffering from an eye inflammation. He said: “Should I remain behind while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ goes forth?” So ʿAlī set out and caught up with the Prophet ﷺ. On the evening before Allah granted the conquest the following morning, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Tomorrow I will surely give the banner—or tomorrow the banner will be taken—by a man whom Allah and His Messenger love, or he said, who loves Allah and His Messenger. Through him Allah will grant victory.” We looked around, not expecting ʿAlī because of his condition. Then it was said: “Here is ʿAlī.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ handed him the banner, and Allah granted victory through him."],[117,"Sahl ibn Saʿd (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that on the Day of Khaybar the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I will surely give this banner to a man through whose hands Allah will grant victory. He loves Allah and His Messenger, and Allah and His Messenger love him.” The people spent that night discussing among themselves, each hoping that he would be the one to receive it. The next morning they all came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ hoping it would be given to them. He asked: “Where is ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib?” They replied: “O Messenger of Allah, he is suffering from pain in his eyes.” He said: “Send for him.” ʿAlī was brought before him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ applied his blessed saliva to ʿAlī’s eyes and supplicated for him. He was immediately cured, as though he had never suffered any pain. The Prophet ﷺ then gave him the banner. ʿAlī asked: “O Messenger of Allah, should I fight them until they become like us?” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Proceed calmly until you reach their territory. Then invite them to Islam and inform them of the rights Allah has made obligatory upon them. By Allah, if Allah guides even one man through you, it is better for you than possessing red camels.”"],[118,"Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that on the Day of Khaybar the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I will surely give this banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger. Allah will grant victory through his hands.” ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb said: “I never desired leadership except on that day.” He continued: “I stretched myself forward, hoping that I would be called.” Instead, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib and gave him the banner, saying: “Go, and do not turn back until Allah grants you victory.” ʿAlī walked a short distance, then stopped without turning around and called out: “O Messenger of Allah, upon what should I fight the people?” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Fight them until they testify that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah. If they do so, they have protected from you their lives and their property except by the right of Islam, and their reckoning rests with Allah.”"],[119,"ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “I will surely give the banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger”—or he said—“whom Allah and His Messenger love.” He then called ʿAlī, who was suffering from an eye inflammation, and Allah granted victory through his hands."],[120,"Buraydah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I participated with ʿAlī in the expedition to Yemen, and I experienced some harshness from him. When I returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, I mentioned ʿAlī and spoke critically of him. I saw the expression on the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ face change, and he said: “O Buraydah, am I not closer to the believers than they are to themselves?” I replied: “Yes, O Messenger of Allah.” He then said: “Whoever I am his Mawlā, then ʿAlī is his Mawlā.”"],[121,"Abū Surayḥah Ḥudhayfah ibn Usayd—or, according to another narration, Zayd ibn Arqam—reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Whoever I am his Mawlā, then ʿAlī is his Mawlā.”"],[122,"Saʿīd ibn Wahb said: ʿAlī called upon the people to testify, whereupon five or six of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ stood and bore witness that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said: “Whoever I am his Mawlā, then ʿAlī is his Mawlā.”"],[123,"Rabāḥ ibn al-Ḥārith reported: A group of men came to ʿAlī at al-Raḥbah and said: “Peace be upon you, our Mawlā.” He said: “How can I be your Mawlā when you are Arab men?” They replied: “We heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say on the day of Ghadīr Khumm: ‘Whoever I am his Mawlā, then this man is his Mawlā.’” Rabāḥ said: After they left, I followed them and asked: “Who are these men?” They replied: “A group from the Anṣār, among whom was Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī.”"],[124,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh (may Allah be pleased with him) said: We were at al-Juḥfah, at Ghadīr Khumm, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us. He took ʿAlī by the hand and said: “Whoever I am his Mawlā, then ʿAlī is his Mawlā.”"],[125,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever I am his Mawlā, then ʿAlī is his Mawlā.”"],[126,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: We were sitting, waiting for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he came out to us from one of his wives’ houses. We stood and accompanied him. On the way, one of his sandals broke, so ʿAlī stayed behind to repair it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ continued walking, and we walked with him. Then he stopped and waited for ʿAlī, and we waited with him. The Prophet ﷺ then said: “Indeed, among you is one who will fight for the correct interpretation of this Qur’an just as I fought for its revelation.” We looked toward one another, hoping it referred to one of us. Among those present were Abū Bakr and ʿUmar. The Prophet ﷺ said: “No. Rather, it is the one repairing the sandal.” Abū Saʿīd said: We went to give ʿAlī the glad tidings, but it was as though he had already heard it."],[127,"Abū Sinān Yazīd ibn Umayyah al-Duʾalī said: ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib became gravely ill until he was near death, and we feared for him. Then Allah cured him, and he recovered. We said: “Congratulations, O Abū al-Ḥasan! Praise be to Allah who has restored your health. We truly feared for you.” ʿAlī replied: “But I was never afraid for myself. The truthful one, whose truth is always confirmed, informed me that I will not die until I am struck here”— and he pointed to the front left side of his head— “and this”— while taking hold of his beard— “will be dyed with blood from it.” Then he said: “The most wretched man of this nation will kill you, just as the most wretched man of Thamūd hamstrung the she-camel of Allah.” The narrator said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened his killer to the most wretched man among the people of Thamūd."],[128,"ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: “Judge cases as you have always judged them, for I dislike disagreement until the people become united, or until I die as my companions died.” The narrator added: Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn held the view that most of what was attributed to ʿAlī was fabricated."],[129,"Saʿd ibn ʿUbaydah narrated: A man came to Ibn ʿUmar and asked him about ʿUthmān. Ibn ʿUmar mentioned the good qualities and virtuous deeds of ʿUthmān. He then asked the man: “Perhaps that displeases you?” The man replied: “Yes.” Ibn ʿUmar responded: “May Allah humble you.” The man then asked him about ʿAlī. Ibn ʿUmar mentioned the virtues and good qualities of ʿAlī and said: “There is his house, situated among the houses of the Prophet ﷺ.” He then asked: “Perhaps that displeases you?” The man replied: “Yes.” Ibn ʿUmar said: “May Allah humble you. Go and do your utmost against me.”"],[130,"ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿIyāḍ ibn ʿAmr al-Qārī narrated: ʿAbdullāh ibn Shaddād returned from Iraq a few nights after the killing of ʿAlī and visited ʿĀ’ishah while we were sitting with her. She asked him: “O ʿAbdullāh ibn Shaddād, will you answer me truthfully? Tell me about the people whom ʿAlī fought and killed.” He replied: “Why would I not tell you the truth?” She said: “Then tell me their story.” He explained that after ʿAlī and Muʿāwiyah agreed to arbitration, about eight thousand Qur’an reciters broke away from ʿAlī and settled at Ḥarūrāʾ near Kūfah. They objected to him, saying: “You have stripped off a garment that Allah Almighty clothed you with and a name by which Allah Almighty named you, then you went and appointed arbiters in the religion of Allah. Judgment belongs only to Allah Almighty.”\nWhen ʿAlī learned of their objections, he gathered the scholars of the Qur’an before him and brought forth a large muṣḥaf. He placed it before them, struck it with his hand, and said: “O Muṣḥaf, speak to the people!” They replied that it was only ink upon paper and that people must speak according to what they had learned from it. ʿAlī then demonstrated from the Qur’an that arbitration is permitted, citing Allah’s command regarding marital disputes: “If you fear a breach between them, appoint an arbiter from his family and an arbiter from hers…” (Qur’an 4:35) He argued that if arbitration is permitted to reconcile a husband and wife, then reconciling the bloodshed of the Muslim community deserves it even more.\nHe also answered their objection to his writing to Muʿāwiyah under the name “ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib.” He recalled that Suhayl ibn ʿAmr came to them at al-Ḥudaybiyah while they were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he made peace with Quraysh. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote: “In the Name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Especially Merciful.” Suhayl said: “Do not write ‘In the Name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Especially Merciful’; rather write: ‘In Your Name, O Allah.’” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “Write: Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah.” Suhayl replied: “If I knew that you were the Messenger of Allah, I would not have opposed you.” So it was written: “This is what Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh has agreed upon in peace with Quraysh.” Allah Almighty says: “Indeed, in the Messenger of Allah you have an excellent example.” (Qur’an 33:21).\nʿAlī then sent ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās to them, and he went out with him. When they reached the middle of their camp, Ibn al-Kawwāʾ stood to address the people and said: “O bearers of the Qur’an, this is ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās. Whoever does not know him, I know him from the Book of Allah. He is among those about whom, and about whose people, was revealed: ‘They are a contentious people.’ (Qur’an 43:58). Send him back to his companion and do not debate the Book of Allah with him.” Their speakers then stood and said: “By Allah, we will indeed debate the Book of Allah with him. If he brings the truth, we will recognize it and follow it; and if he brings falsehood, we will refute him with his falsehood.” He then debated them with the Qur’an for three days.\nFor three days Ibn ʿAbbās discussed the matter with them until four thousand repented and returned, including Ibn al-Kawwāʾ. ʿAlī then addressed those who remained, saying that they were free to stay apart provided they did not shed unlawful blood, terrorize travelers, or violate the rights of protected non-Muslims. If they committed those crimes, war would become unavoidable. ʿĀ’ishah asked: “So he eventually fought them?” He replied: “By Allah, he did not send forces against them until they began attacking travelers, shedding innocent blood, and violating the rights of protected people.”\nShe asked him to swear by Allah, and he confirmed it. She then asked about the reports circulating in Iraq concerning Dhu al-Thudayyah (the man with the deformed arm). He replied that he personally saw him among those killed and witnessed ʿAlī asking people whether they recognized him. Many testified that they had seen him praying in various mosques, but no further reliable information about him was established. She then asked: “What did ʿAlī say when he stood over him, as the people of Iraq claim?”\nHe answered: “I heard him say only: ‘Allah and His Messenger have spoken the truth.’” She asked: “Did you hear him say anything else?” He replied: “No, by Allah.” ʿĀ’ishah said: “Indeed, Allah and His Messenger have spoken the truth. May Allah have mercy upon ʿAlī. Whenever he witnessed something that amazed him, he would simply say: ‘Allah and His Messenger have spoken the truth.’ But the people of Iraq attributed false statements to him and added things to the narration.”"],[131,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Some people complained about ʿAlī. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then stood before us and delivered a sermon. I heard him say: “O people, do not complain about ʿAlī. By Allah, he is indeed uncompromising in the cause of Allah”—or he said—“in the path of Allah.”"],[132,"Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Ten men from Quraysh are in Paradise: Abū Bakr is in Paradise. ʿUmar is in Paradise. ʿUthmān is in Paradise. ʿAlī is in Paradise. Ṭalḥah is in Paradise. Al-Zubayr is in Paradise. Saʿd is in Paradise. Saʿīd is in Paradise. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf is in Paradise. Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ is in Paradise.”"],[133,"Al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām (may Allah be pleased with him) said: On the Day of Uḥud, the Prophet ﷺ was wearing two coats of armor. He attempted to climb onto a large rock but was unable to do so. Ṭalḥah sat beneath him, allowing the Prophet ﷺ to step upon him until he stood firmly upon the rock. I then heard the Prophet ﷺ say: “Ṭalḥah has secured Paradise.”"],[134,"ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “Ṭalḥah has secured Paradise.” This was because of what Ṭalḥah did in defense of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. During the Battle of Uḥud, the people fled from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Some retreated as far as al-Manqā before al-Aʿwaṣ. Among those who fled were ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, ʿUqbah ibn ʿUthmān, and Saʿd ibn ʿUthmān—two men from the Anṣār belonging to Banū Zurayq. They reached al-Jalʿab, a mountain near Madinah, where they remained for three days before returning to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to them: “You went very far indeed.”"],[135,"Anas narrated from Abū Ṭalḥah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I was among those overcome by drowsiness on the Day of Uḥud, to the extent that my sword repeatedly fell from my hand. It would fall, I would pick it up, then it would fall again, and I would pick it up once more."],[136,"Ṭalḥah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to an unlearned Bedouin: “Ask him who it is that has fulfilled his vow.” They themselves were too reverent and respectful to ask the Prophet ﷺ directly. The Bedouin asked him, but the Prophet ﷺ turned away. He asked again, and again the Prophet ﷺ turned away. He asked a third time, and the Prophet ﷺ again turned away. Then I entered through the door of the mosque wearing green garments. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw me, he said: “Where is the one asking about the person who has fulfilled his vow?” The Bedouin replied: “I am, O Messenger of Allah.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “This is one of those who has fulfilled his vow.”"],[137,"Al-Sā’ib ibn Yazīd said: I accompanied ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydullāh, al-Miqdād, and Saʿd (may Allah be pleased with them all). I never heard any of them narrate from the Prophet ﷺ, except that I heard Ṭalḥah narrate about the Day of Uḥud."],[138,"Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim said: I saw Ṭalḥah’s hand paralyzed. He had used it to shield the Prophet ﷺ on the Day of Uḥud."],[139,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh (may Allah be pleased with him) said: On the Day of Uḥud, when the people had retreated, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on one side of the battlefield with twelve men from the Anṣār, among whom was Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydullāh. The polytheists caught up with them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned and said: “Who will face these people?” Ṭalḥah replied: “I will.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Remain where you are.” One of the Anṣār then said: “I will, O Messenger of Allah.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “You.” The man fought until he was martyred. Again the Prophet ﷺ turned and saw the polytheists approaching and asked: “Who will face these people?” Ṭalḥah again said: “I will.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Remain where you are.” Another man from the Anṣār volunteered, and the Prophet ﷺ permitted him to fight. He fought until he was martyred. This continued, with each Anṣārī going forward one after another and fighting until he was killed, until only the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Ṭalḥah remained. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then asked: “Who will face these people?” Ṭalḥah replied: “I will.” Ṭalḥah then fought with the bravery of all eleven men combined until his hand was struck and his fingers were severed. He exclaimed: “Ḥas!” (an exclamation of pain) The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Had you said, ‘Bismillāh (In the Name of Allah),’ the angels would have raised you while the people watched.” Then Allah drove the polytheists back."],[140,"Abū ʿUthmān said: During one of the days on which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was fighting, no one remained with him except Ṭalḥah and Saʿd. This was narrated on the authority of both of them."],[141,"ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr said: On the Day of al-Aḥzāb (the Battle of the Trench), I and ʿUmar ibn Abī Salamah were placed with the women. I looked out and saw al-Zubayr riding repeatedly toward Banū Qurayẓah two or three times. When he returned, I said: “Father, I saw you going back and forth.” He asked: “Did you really see me, my son?” I replied: “Yes.” He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said: “Who will go to Banū Qurayẓah and bring me news of them?” So I went. When I returned, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ invoked both his father and mother for me, saying: “May my father and my mother be sacrificed for you.”"],[142,"Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: The Prophet ﷺ said: “Who will bring me news of the people on the Day of al-Aḥzāb?” Al-Zubayr replied: “I will.” The Prophet ﷺ asked again: “Who will bring me news of the people?” Al-Zubayr again replied: “I will.” The Prophet ﷺ then said: “Every prophet has a close disciple (ḥawārī), and my close disciple is al-Zubayr.”"],[143,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Every prophet has a close disciple, and my close disciple is al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām.”"],[144,"Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) heard a man say: “O son of the close disciple of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ!” He replied: “If you are addressing someone from the family of al-Zubayr, then yes; otherwise, no.”"],[145,"ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr said: ʿĀ’ishah said to me: “By Allah, your two fathers were among those who responded to Allah and the Messenger after they had been wounded.” Another wording adds: “She meant Abū Bakr and al-Zubayr.”"],[146,"Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam said: During the year known as “the Year of the Nosebleed,” ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān suffered a severe nosebleed that prevented him from performing Ḥajj. Thinking death might be near, he made his will. A man from Quraysh entered and said: “Appoint a successor.” ʿUthmān asked: “Have the people said this?” He replied: “Yes.” ʿUthmān asked: “Whom do they suggest?” The man remained silent. Later another man—Marwān thought it was al-Ḥārith—entered and also urged him to appoint a successor. ʿUthmān again asked: “Have the people said this?” He replied: “Yes.” ʿUthmān asked: “Whom?” The man remained silent. ʿUthmān then said: “Perhaps they have suggested al-Zubayr?” He replied: “Yes.” ʿUthmān said: “By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, as far as I know, he is the best of them, as far as I know, and he was certainly the most beloved of them to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”"],[147,"ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr said: When al-Zubayr stood on the Day of the Battle of the Camel, he called me to him, and I stood beside him. He said: “My son, today no one will be killed except one who is either an oppressor or one who has been wronged. I believe that today I will be killed as one who has been wronged. One of my greatest concerns is my debt. Do you think anything of our wealth will remain after my debts are paid?” Then he said: “My son, sell our property and pay off my debts.” He also bequeathed one-third of his estate, and one-third of that one-third for the children of ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr. Hishām said: At that time, some of ʿAbdullāh’s children were already grown, including Khubayb and ʿAbbād. He then had nine sons and nine daughters. ʿAbdullāh continued: He kept reminding me about his debts, saying: “My son, if you are ever unable to pay any portion of it, seek help from my Mawlā.” I did not understand what he meant until I asked: “Father, who is your Mawlā?” He replied: “Allah.” By Allah, whenever I later faced difficulty in paying one of his debts, I would say: “O Mawlā of al-Zubayr, settle his debt.” And Allah would make it possible for it to be paid. Al-Zubayr was then killed, leaving behind neither gold nor silver coins. Rather, he left landed properties, including al-Ghābah, eleven houses in Madinah, two houses in Baṣrah, one in Kūfah, and one in Egypt. ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr continued: The reason my father had accumulated debt was that people would bring him money for safekeeping. Whenever someone entrusted him with wealth, al-Zubayr would say: “No, let it be considered a loan instead, for I fear it might be lost while in my possession.” He never held the office of governor, never collected land tax, and never occupied a position of public financial authority. His service was only in military expeditions alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, and ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with them all). I calculated the amount of his debt and found that it totaled 2,200,000. Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām met me and asked: “My nephew, how much debt did my brother leave?” I concealed the true amount and replied: “One hundred thousand.”\nḤakīm said: “By Allah, I do not think your wealth will be sufficient even for that.” I then asked him: “What if it were 2,200,000?” He replied: “I do not think you can manage that. If you are unable to repay any part of it, seek my assistance.” Al-Zubayr had purchased al-Ghābah for 170,000, and I later sold it for 1,600,000. Then I stood and announced: “Whoever has a claim against al-Zubayr should come to us at al-Ghābah.” ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar came, for al-Zubayr owed him 400,000.\nHe said to me: “If you wish, I will forgo the debt.” I replied: “No.” He said: “Then, if you wish, defer it until later.” I replied: “No.” He then said: “Then allocate a portion of the land to me.” So I marked out a section of the property for him. I continued selling portions of the land until all of my father’s debts had been paid in full. Afterward, four and a half shares of the estate still remained. I then went to Muʿāwiyah, in whose company were ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān, al-Mundhir ibn al-Zubayr, and Ibn Zamʿah.\nMuʿāwiyah asked: “At what value have you assessed al-Ghābah?” I replied: “Each share is worth one hundred thousand.” He asked: “How many shares remain?” I answered: “Four and a half.” Al-Mundhir ibn al-Zubayr said: “I have purchased one share for one hundred thousand.” ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān said: “I have also purchased one share for one hundred thousand.” Ibn Zamʿah likewise said: “I have purchased one share for one hundred thousand.” Muʿāwiyah then asked: “How much remains?” I replied: “One and a half shares.” He said: “I will purchase them for one hundred and fifty thousand.” Later, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar sold his share to Muʿāwiyah for 600,000. After I had completely settled my father’s debts, the children of al-Zubayr said: “Distribute our inheritance among us.” I replied: “No, by Allah. I will not divide it until I announce during the pilgrimage season for four consecutive years: ‘Whoever is owed anything by al-Zubayr should come to us so that we may repay it.’” He continued making this announcement every year during the pilgrimage season. When four years had passed, he distributed the estate. Al-Zubayr had four wives. After setting aside the one-third bequest, each wife received 1,200,000, and the total value of his estate amounted to 50,200,000."],[148,"ʿUrwah said: Al-Zubayr had three sword wounds. One of them was on his shoulder, and it was so deep that I used to put my fingers into it. Two of those wounds were inflicted on the Day of Badr, and one at the Battle of al-Yarmūk. ʿUrwah further said: After ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr was killed, ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Marwān asked me: “Do you recognize al-Zubayr’s sword?” I replied: “Yes.” He asked: “What distinguishes it?” I said: “It has a notch that it received on the Day of Badr.” He replied: “You have spoken the truth.” Then he recited: “Its notches were made by the clash of battalions.” He then returned the sword to ʿUrwah. Hishām said: We valued it among ourselves at three thousand, and one of us eventually acquired it. I wish that I had been the one who obtained it."],[149,"ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated that the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to al-Zubayr on the Day of al-Yarmūk: “Will you charge the enemy so that we may charge with you?” Al-Zubayr replied: “If I charge, you will not keep up with me.” They said: “No, we will not fail you.” So he launched his attack and drove straight through the enemy ranks until he passed completely beyond them, with no one remaining alongside him. He then turned back toward his own army. As he returned, the enemy seized the reins of his horse and struck him twice on his shoulder, in addition to the wound he had previously received at the Battle of Badr. ʿUrwah said: When I was a small child, I used to put my fingers into those wounds while playing. He also said: On that day, ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr was with him, and he was only ten years old. Al-Zubayr placed him upon a horse and entrusted him to the care of another man."],[150,"ʿUrwah said: The sword of al-Zubayr was adorned with silver. Hishām added: The sword of ʿUrwah was likewise adorned with silver."],[151,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: One night the Prophet ﷺ was unable to sleep. He said: “If only a righteous man from among my Companions would guard me tonight.” Then we heard the sound of weapons. The Prophet ﷺ asked: “Who is this?” The man replied: “It is Saʿd, O Messenger of Allah. I have come to guard you.” After that, the Prophet ﷺ slept until we heard him snoring. Another narration states: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained awake one night shortly after arriving in Madinah. While they were in that state, they heard the clattering of weapons. He asked: “Who is this?” The reply came: “Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “What has brought you here?” Saʿd replied: “I became concerned for the safety of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so I came to guard you.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then supplicated for him before going to sleep."],[152,"ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never invoked both his father and mother as a ransom for anyone except Saʿd ibn Mālik. On the Day of Uḥud he repeatedly said to him: “Shoot, O Saʿd! May my father and my mother be sacrificed for you.”"],[153,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ invoked both his father and his mother as a ransom for me on the Day of Uḥud. Another narration states: The Prophet ﷺ handed me his quiver on the Day of Uḥud and said: “Shoot! May my father and my mother be sacrificed for you.” Another wording says: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ invoked both his father and mother for me on the Day of Uḥud when he said: “May my father and my mother be sacrificed for you,” while I was fighting. Another narration in Muslim adds: A man from among the polytheists was causing great harm to the Muslims. The Prophet ﷺ said to me: “Shoot! May my father and my mother be sacrificed for you.” So I shot an arrow without an iron head. It struck him in his side, causing him to fall. His private parts became exposed, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed until I could see his molar teeth."],[154,"Muṣʿab ibn Saʿd narrated from his father: Several verses of the Qur’an were revealed concerning me. My mother swore that she would never speak to me until I abandoned my religion. She also vowed that she would neither eat nor drink. She said: “You claim that Allah has commanded you to be dutiful to your parents. I am your mother, and I command you to do this.” I remained steadfast for three days until she collapsed from exhaustion. One of her sons, named ʿUmārah, gave her water to drink. Afterward she began supplicating against me. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: “And We have enjoined upon man kindness to his parents…” (Qur’an 29:8) And: “But if they strive to make you associate with Me that of which you have no knowledge…” (Qur’an 31:15) Including His statement: ”…Yet accompany them in this world with kindness.” (Qur’an 31:15) The Messenger of Allah ﷺ acquired a large amount of war booty, among which was a sword. I took the sword and brought it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saying: “Grant this sword to me as an extra share, for you know my service and what I have done.”\nHe replied: “Return it to where you took it from.” So I went away. But when I was about to place it back with the collected spoils, I reproached myself and returned to him, saying: “Give it to me.” He raised his voice to me and said: “Return it to where you took it from.” Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: “They ask you concerning the spoils of war…” (Qur’an 8:1) Later I became ill and sent for the Prophet ﷺ. He came to visit me. I said: “Allow me to distribute my wealth as I wish.”\nHe refused. I asked: “Then half of it?” He refused. I asked: “Then one-third?” He remained silent, and thereafter one-third became the permissible limit for making a bequest. I once passed by a group of the Anṣār and the Muhājirūn, and they said: “Come and eat with us, and drink some wine.” This was before wine had been prohibited. I joined them in a garden where there was a roasted camel’s head and a skin filled with wine. I ate and drank with them.\nDuring the gathering, the Anṣār and the Muhājirūn were mentioned. I said: “The Muhājirūn are better than the Anṣār.” A man then took one of the jawbones of the roasted camel’s head and struck me with it, injuring my nose. I went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him of what had happened. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed concerning me—that is, regarding the matter of wine— “Indeed, intoxicants, gambling, stone altars, and divining arrows are nothing but filth from the work of Satan…” (Qur’an 5:90)"],[155,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ (may Allah be pleased with him) said: There were six of us with the Prophet ﷺ when the polytheists said to him: “Drive these people away so that they will not presume equality with us.” Those present were myself, Ibn Masʿūd, a man from Hudhayl, Bilāl, and two others whose names I will not mention. A thought crossed the mind of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as Allah willed that it should. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: “And do not drive away those who call upon their Lord morning and evening, seeking His Face. You are not accountable for them in the least…” (Qur’an 6:52)"],[156,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ (may Allah be pleased with him) said: No one embraced Islam before the day on which I embraced Islam. For seven days, I was one-third of the Muslim community."],[157,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I was the first Arab to shoot an arrow in the cause of Allah. I remember that we used to go out on military expeditions while having no food except the leaves of the ḥablah plant and the samar tree. One of us would pass stool just as a sheep does, with nothing mixed into it because of the lack of proper food. And now Banū Asad are teaching me the laws of Islam! If that were so, then I would indeed have failed and all my efforts would have been in vain."],[158,"ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd said: Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ was tending his camels when his son ʿUmar came to him. When Saʿd saw him approaching, he said: “I seek refuge in Allah from the evil of this rider.” His son dismounted and said: “You remain here with your camels and sheep while the people are disputing among themselves over political authority?” Saʿd struck him on the chest and said: “Be quiet. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘Indeed, Allah loves the servant who is God-fearing, self-sufficient, and unobtrusive.’”"],[159,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Prophet ﷺ visited him in Makkah while he was ill. Saʿd began to weep. The Prophet ﷺ asked: “What is making you weep?” He replied: “I fear that I may die in the land from which I emigrated, just as Saʿd ibn Khawlah died.” The Prophet ﷺ supplicated three times: “O Allah, cure Saʿd. O Allah, cure Saʿd. O Allah, cure Saʿd.” Saʿd then said: “O Messenger of Allah, I possess abundant wealth, and only my daughter will inherit from me. May I bequeath all of my wealth?” He replied: “No.” Saʿd asked: “Then two-thirds?” He replied: “No.” Saʿd asked: “Then half?” He replied: “No.” Saʿd asked: “Then one-third?” The Prophet ﷺ said: “One-third, and even one-third is much. Your charity from your wealth is charity, your spending on your family is charity, and whatever your wife eats from your wealth is charity. It is better for you to leave your family well provided—or he said: well off—than to leave them dependent upon people, begging from them.” He then gestured with his hand."],[160,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited me during the Farewell Pilgrimage while I was suffering from an illness that had brought me close to death. I said: “O Messenger of Allah, you see the severity of my illness. I possess wealth, and only one daughter will inherit from me. May I give two-thirds of my wealth in charity?” He replied: “No.” I asked: “Then half?” He said: “No. One-third, and even one-third is much. It is better to leave your heirs wealthy than to leave them poor and dependent upon people. You will not spend anything seeking the Face of Allah except that you will be rewarded for it—even the morsel of food that you place into your wife’s mouth.” I then asked: “O Messenger of Allah, will I remain behind after my companions?” He replied: “You will not remain behind and perform any deed seeking the Face of Allah except that it will increase you in rank and honor. Perhaps you will live on so that through you some people will benefit and others will be harmed. O Allah, complete the migration of my Companions and do not cause them to turn back on their heels. But pitiable is Saʿd ibn Khawlah.” Saʿd explained: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ expressed sorrow because Saʿd ibn Khawlah had died in Makkah."],[161,"Abū Mūsā (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Saʿd became ill in Makkah, and the Prophet ﷺ came to visit him. Saʿd asked: “O Messenger of Allah, do you not dislike that a man should die in the land from which he emigrated?” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Yes. But perhaps Allah, Blessed and Exalted, will raise your rank, and through you He will harm some people and benefit others.”"],[162,"ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ narrated that Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ said during the illness from which he eventually died: “Dig a niche grave (laḥd) for me, and set the bricks over me upright, just as was done for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”"],[163,"Jābir ibn Samurah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The people of Kūfah complained about Saʿd to ʿUmar. As a result, ʿUmar removed him from office and appointed ʿAmmār as governor over them. They continued making complaints until they even claimed that Saʿd did not know how to lead the prayer properly. ʿUmar summoned him and said: “O Abū Isḥāq, these people claim that you do not perform the prayer correctly.” Abū Isḥāq (Saʿd) replied: “By Allah, I used to lead them exactly as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed, without falling short in any way. I would prolong the first two rakʿahs of the ʿIshāʾ prayer and shorten the last two.” ʿUmar said: “That is exactly what I expected of you, O Abū Isḥāq.” He then sent one man—or several men—with Saʿd to Kūfah to investigate. They visited every mosque in the city, asking the people about him, and everywhere they went the people praised him. Finally they entered a mosque belonging to Banū ʿAbs. A man named Usāmah ibn Qatādah, whose kunyah was Abū Saʿdah, stood up and said: “Since you have placed us under oath, I will speak. Saʿd did not personally accompany military expeditions, he did not distribute wealth equally, and he was not just in his judgments.” Saʿd then said: “By Allah, I shall supplicate with three prayers: O Allah, if this servant of Yours is lying and has spoken only for showing off and seeking fame, then prolong his life, prolong his poverty, and expose him to trials.” Afterward, whenever that man was asked about his condition, he would say: “I am an old man afflicted by trials. The supplication of Saʿd has overtaken me.” ʿAbd al-Malik said: I later saw him myself. His eyebrows had drooped over his eyes from extreme old age, yet he still exposed himself to temptation by pursuing young women in the streets and winking at them."],[164,"ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd narrated that Saʿd rode to his residence at al-ʿAqīq, where he found a slave cutting trees—or striking them to knock off their leaves. Saʿd confiscated what the slave had with him. When Saʿd returned, the slave’s family came to him and asked him to return to their servant—or to them—what he had taken. Saʿd replied: “Allah forbid that I should return something which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted to me as lawful spoils.” He refused to return it to them."],[165,"Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him) said: We were sitting with the Prophet ﷺ when Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ approached. The Prophet ﷺ said: “This is my maternal uncle. Let each person show me his maternal uncle!”"],[166,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (may Allah be pleased with him) said: My name was ʿAbd ʿAmr, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ renamed me ʿAbd al-Raḥmān."],[167,"Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: When ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf arrived in Madinah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ established a bond of brotherhood between him and Saʿd ibn al-Rabīʿ. Saʿd was a man of great wealth and said: “The Anṣār know that I am among the wealthiest of them. I will divide my wealth equally between you and me. I also have two wives; look to see which one pleases you most, and I will divorce her so that, after her waiting period is complete, you may marry her.” ʿAbd al-Raḥmān replied: “May Allah bless your family and your wealth.” That very day, he went to the marketplace and returned having earned some clarified butter and dried yogurt. Not long afterward, he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with traces of yellow perfume upon him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “What is the matter?” He replied: “I have married a woman from the Anṣār.” The Prophet ﷺ asked: “What did you give her as her bridal gift (mahr)?” He replied: “The weight of a date-stone of gold.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Hold a wedding feast, even if only with one sheep.”"],[168,"Al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: We came to the people while they had already begun the prayer. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf was leading them, and they had already completed one rakʿah. When he realized that the Prophet ﷺ had arrived, he tried to step back, but the Prophet ﷺ motioned for him to remain where he was. After ʿAbd al-Raḥmān concluded the prayer with salām, the Prophet ﷺ and I stood and performed the rakʿah that we had missed."],[169,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came while he was leading the people in prayer. He attempted to step back, but the Prophet ﷺ signaled to him: “Remain where you are.” So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ completed the prayer behind ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf."],[170,"Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf came to her and said: “O my mother, I fear that the abundance of my wealth may destroy me. I am the wealthiest man among Quraysh.” She replied: “My son, spend generously, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘Among my Companions are those who will not see me after I depart from them.’” ʿAbd al-Raḥmān then left and met ʿUmar, informing him of what Umm Salamah had said. ʿUmar came to her and asked: “By Allah, am I one of them?” She replied: “No, and I will not declare anyone else free from that after you.”"],[171,"Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf narrated from ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say to his wives: “Your welfare is one of the matters that concerns me after my death, and none will treat you with patience and care except those who are truly patient.” Thereafter, ʿĀ’ishah would say: “May Allah give your father to drink from the Salsabīl spring of Paradise,” referring to ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, because he had provided financial support to the wives of the Prophet ﷺ. It is said that the property he gave them was sold for forty thousand."],[172,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I entered into a written agreement with Umayyah ibn Khalaf: he would protect my property in Makkah, and I would protect his property in Madinah. When I wrote my name as ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, he said: “I do not recognize ‘al-Raḥmān.’ Write to me using the name you had during the pre-Islamic period.” So I wrote ʿAbd ʿAmr. On the Day of Badr, when the people had gone to sleep, I went with Umayyah toward a mountain to keep him safe. Bilāl saw him and cried out: “Umayyah ibn Khalaf! May I not survive if Umayyah survives!” He then called a group of the Anṣār, and they pursued us. When I feared they would catch us, I left Umayyah’s son behind for them in hopes of delaying them, but they killed him and continued after us. Umayyah was a heavy man. When they caught up to us, I said to him: “Kneel down.” He knelt, and I threw myself over him in an attempt to shield him. However, they thrust their swords beneath me until they killed him. One of their swords struck my foot, leaving a scar. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf used to show us that scar on the top of his foot."],[173,"Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Every nation has a trustworthy guardian, and the trustworthy guardian of this nation is Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ.”"],[174,"Ḥudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: The people of Najrān came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “Send us a trustworthy man.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “I will certainly send you a man who is truly trustworthy.” The people eagerly looked on to see whom he would choose. He then sent Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ."],[175,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: The al-ʿĀqib and al-Sayyid, the two leaders of Najrān, came intending to invoke mutual imprecation with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. One of them said to the other: “Do not invoke mutual imprecation with him. By Allah, if he is truly a prophet and we do so, neither we nor our descendants will ever succeed.” So they came to him and said: “We will not invoke mutual imprecation with you. Rather, we will give you what you requested. Send with us a trustworthy man.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “I will surely send with you a man who is truly trustworthy—truly trustworthy.” The Companions eagerly anticipated being chosen. The Prophet ﷺ then said: “Stand up, O Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ.” When he stood, the Prophet ﷺ said: “This is the trustworthy guardian of this nation.”"],[176,"Khālid ibn al-Walīd (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “Every nation has a trustworthy guardian, and the trustworthy guardian of this nation is Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ.”"],[177,"Saʿīd ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl (may Allah be pleased with him) said in the mosque of Kūfah: “By Allah, I remember when ʿUmar had me bound because of my Islam before he himself embraced Islam. If Mount Uḥud had collapsed because of what you did to ʿUthmān, it would have been justified.”"],[178,"ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad narrated that his father narrated from Saʿīd ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl (may Allah be pleased with him): A woman named Arwā disputed with him over a portion of his property. Saʿīd said: “Leave her and her claim, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘Whoever wrongfully takes even a handspan of land will be burdened with it from the depths of seven earths on the Day of Resurrection.’” Then Saʿīd supplicated: “O Allah, if she is lying, then take away her eyesight and make her grave within her own house.” The narrator said: Later I saw her blind, feeling her way along the walls, saying: “The supplication of Saʿīd ibn Zayd has struck me.” Then, while she was walking within her property, she passed by a well in the courtyard, fell into it, and it became her grave."],[179,"Abū Ḥumayd al-Sāʿidī narrated that the Companions asked: “O Messenger of Allah, how should we invoke Allah’s blessings upon you?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “Say: O Allah, bestow Your blessings upon Muḥammad, his wives, and his descendants, just as You bestowed Your blessings upon the family of Ibrāhīm. And bless Muḥammad, his wives, and his descendants, just as You blessed the family of Ibrāhīm. Indeed, You are the Praiseworthy, the Glorious.”"],[180,"Zayd ibn Arqam narrated: One day, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood among us to deliver a sermon at a watering place called Khumm, situated between Makkah and Madinah. He praised and extolled Allah, admonished us, and reminded us. He then said: “To proceed: O people, I am only a human being. The messenger of my Lord may soon come to me, and I will answer his call. I am leaving among you two weighty things. The first of them is the Book of Allah, in which there is guidance and light. So hold firmly to the Book of Allah and adhere to it.” He strongly encouraged them to adhere to the Book of Allah and urged them toward it. Then he said: “And the members of my household. I remind you of Allah regarding the members of my household. I remind you of Allah regarding the members of my household. I remind you of Allah regarding the members of my household.” Ḥuṣayn asked Zayd: “Who are the members of his household, O Zayd? Are his wives not among the members of his household?” He replied: “His wives are among the members of his household. However, the members of his household referred to here are those for whom charity remained forbidden after him.” Ḥuṣayn asked: “Who are they?” He replied: “They are the family of ʿAlī, the family of ʿAqīl, the family of Jaʿfar, and the family of al-ʿAbbās.” He asked: “Was charity forbidden to all of them?” Zayd replied: “Yes.”"],[181,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “By the One in whose hand is my soul, no man hates us, the People of the Household, except that Allah will admit him into the Fire.”"],[182,"ʿAlī ibn Rabīʿah al-Asadī al-Wālibī narrated: I met Zayd ibn Arqam while he was intending to enter upon al-Mukhtār. I said to him: “A hadith has reached me from you.” He asked: “What is it?” I said: “Did you hear the Prophet ﷺ say: ‘I am leaving among you two weighty things: the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and my close family’?” He replied: “Yes.”"],[183,"Yazīd ibn Ḥayyān narrated from Zayd ibn Arqam: We entered upon him and said: “You have witnessed much good. You accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and prayed behind him.” He then related the hadith with wording similar to that of Abū Ḥayyān, except that he said that the Prophet ﷺ stated: “Indeed, I am leaving among you two weighty things. One of them is the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic. It is the rope of Allah. Whoever follows it will remain upon guidance, and whoever abandons it will be upon misguidance.” The narration also states: We asked: “Who are the members of his household? Are they his wives?” Zayd replied: “No, by Allah. A woman may remain with a man for a long period of time, and then he may divorce her, whereupon she returns to her father and her people. The members of his household are his blood relatives and paternal kin, for whom charity was forbidden after him.”"],[184,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “I was shown in a dream that I was drawing water from a well with a bucket attached to a pulley. Abū Bakr came and drew one or two buckets, and there was some weakness in his drawing—may Allah forgive him. Then ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb came, and the bucket transformed into a large one. I have never seen a remarkably strong man perform as he did, until the people had drunk their fill and brought their camels to their resting places.”"],[185,"Abū Hurayrah narrated: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: “While I was sleeping, I saw myself beside a well with a bucket upon it. I drew from it for as long as Allah willed. Then the son of Abū Quḥāfah took it and drew one or two buckets, and there was some weakness in his drawing—may Allah forgive his weakness. Then it transformed into a large bucket, and Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb took it. I have never seen any remarkably strong man draw water as ʿUmar did, until the people brought their camels to their resting places.”"],[186,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “While I was sleeping, I was shown that I was drawing water at my basin and giving the people drink. Abū Bakr came and took the bucket from my hand to relieve me. He drew two buckets, and there was some weakness in his drawing—may Allah forgive him. Then Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb came and took it from him. I have never seen any man draw more powerfully than he did, until the people departed while the basin was full and overflowing.”"],[187,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb was placed upon his bier, and the people gathered around him, supplicating for him, praising him, and invoking blessings upon him before he was carried away. I was among them. Suddenly, a man placed his hand upon my shoulder from behind. I turned and saw that it was ʿAlī. He prayed for mercy upon ʿUmar and said: “You have left behind no one whose deeds I would more dearly wish to meet Allah with than yours. By Allah, I have always believed that Allah would join you with your two companions, for I frequently heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘I came with Abū Bakr and ʿUmar; I entered with Abū Bakr and ʿUmar; and I went out with Abū Bakr and ʿUmar.’ Therefore, I used to hope—or believe—that Allah would join you with them.”"],[188,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “While a man was driving a cow upon which he had placed a load, the cow turned toward him and said: ‘I was not created for this. I was created only for plowing.’” The people exclaimed: “Glory be to Allah!” They were amazed and alarmed, saying: “A cow speaks?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I believe it, and so do Abū Bakr and ʿUmar.” Abū Hurayrah also narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “While a shepherd was tending his sheep, a wolf attacked and seized one of them. The shepherd pursued it until he rescued the sheep from it. The wolf then turned toward him and said: ‘Who will care for it on the Day of the Beast, when it will have no shepherd other than me?’”\nThe people exclaimed: “Glory be to Allah!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I believe that, and so do Abū Bakr and ʿUmar.”"],[189,"Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥanafiyyah narrated: I asked my father, ʿAlī: “Who is the best of people after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” He replied: “Abū Bakr.” I asked: “Then who?” He replied: “Then ʿUmar.” I feared that he would next say ʿUthmān, so I asked: “Then you?” He replied: “I am only one man among the Muslims.”"],[190,"Ḥudhayfah narrated: We were sitting with the Prophet ﷺ when he said: “I do not know how much longer I will remain among you. Therefore, follow the two who will come after me,” and he pointed toward Abū Bakr and ʿUmar. He continued: “Follow the guidance of ʿAmmār, and believe whatever Ibn Masʿūd narrates to you.”"],[191,"Abū Juḥayfah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Abū Bakr and ʿUmar are the leaders of the mature men of Paradise, from the earlier and later generations, except for the prophets and messengers.”"],[192,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said regarding Abū Bakr and ʿUmar: “These two are the leaders of the mature men of Paradise.”"],[193,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated: I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and no one else was with us, when Abū Bakr and ʿUmar approached, each holding the other’s hand. The Prophet ﷺ said: “These two are the leaders of the mature men of Paradise, from the earlier and later generations, except for the prophets and messengers. Do not tell them, O ʿAlī.” So I did not tell them. Had they still been alive, I would not have narrated it."],[194,"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb narrated: One day, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us to give charity. At that time, I happened to possess some wealth, so I said to myself: “Today I will surpass Abū Bakr, if I am ever able to surpass him.” I brought half of my wealth. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “What have you left for your family?” I replied: “An equal amount.” Then Abū Bakr came with everything he possessed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him: “What have you left for your family?” He replied: “I have left Allah and His Messenger for them.” I then said: “I will never compete with you in anything again.”"],[195,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “A man from the inhabitants of ʿIlliyyīn will look down upon the people of Paradise like a brilliant star, and Abū Bakr and ʿUmar are among them—and what excellent men they are.”"],[196,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The inhabitants of ʿIlliyyīn will be seen by those below them just as a brilliant star is seen upon the horizon of the sky. Abū Bakr and ʿUmar are indeed among them—and what excellent men they are.”"],[197,"Ibn ʿUmar narrated: During the lifetime of the Prophet ﷺ, we used to rank the people in virtue. We gave precedence to Abū Bakr, then ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, and then ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with them all."],[198,"Ibn ʿUmar narrated: During the lifetime of the Prophet ﷺ, we regarded no one as equal to Abū Bakr, then came ʿUmar, then ʿUthmān. After them, we would leave the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ without ranking one above another."],[199,"Anas narrated that the Prophet ﷺ ascended Mount Uḥud accompanied by Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, and ʿUthmān. The mountain shook beneath them, so the Prophet ﷺ said: “Be firm, Uḥud, for upon you are only a prophet, a truthful one, and two martyrs.”"],[200,"Abū Mūsā narrated: I performed ablution in my house and then went out, saying to myself: “I will remain close to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and spend this entire day with him.” I went to the mosque and asked where the Prophet ﷺ was. The people said: “He went out and headed in that direction.” I followed his path, asking about him, until he entered the garden containing the Well of Arīs. I sat at its gate, which was made of palm branches, until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ relieved himself and performed ablution. I then went to him and found him sitting upon the stone ledge surrounding the Well of Arīs. He sat in the middle of it, uncovered his lower legs, and let them hang into the well. I greeted him and then returned to sit at the gate, saying: “Today I will serve as the gatekeeper of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” Abū Bakr came and pushed the gate. I asked: “Who is it?” He replied: “Abū Bakr.” I said: “Wait there.” I went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah, Abū Bakr is requesting permission to enter.” He said: “Admit him and give him the glad tidings of Paradise.” I returned and said to Abū Bakr: “Enter, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gives you the glad tidings of Paradise.” Abū Bakr entered and sat to the Prophet’s right upon the stone ledge. He uncovered his lower legs and let them hang into the well, just as the Prophet ﷺ had done. I then returned to my place. I had left my brother performing ablution, intending to join me, so I thought: “Should Allah intend good for him, He will bring him here.” Then someone moved the gate. I asked: “Who is it?” He replied: “ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.” I said: “Wait there.” I went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, greeted him, and said: “ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb is requesting permission to enter.” He said: “Admit him and give him the glad tidings of Paradise.” I returned and said: “Enter, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gives you the glad tidings of Paradise.” He entered and sat to the Prophet’s left upon the stone ledge, and he let his legs hang into the well. I returned to the gate and again thought: “Should Allah intend good for my brother, He will bring him here.” Then someone came and moved the gate. I asked: “Who is it?” He replied: “ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān.” I said: “Wait there.” I went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him. He said: “Admit him and give him the glad tidings of Paradise, along with a trial that will afflict him.” I returned and said: “Enter, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gives you the glad tidings of Paradise, along with a trial that will afflict you.” He entered and found that the stone ledge was full, so he sat facing them on the opposite side. Sharīk said that Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib stated: “I interpreted this as referring to their graves.”"],[201,"Abū Mūsā narrated: I was with the Prophet ﷺ in one of the gardens of Madinah when a man came seeking permission to enter. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Open the gate for him and give him the glad tidings of Paradise.” I opened it, and it was Abū Bakr. I informed him of what the Prophet ﷺ had said, and he praised Allah. Then another man came requesting permission to enter. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Open the gate for him and give him the glad tidings of Paradise.” I opened it, and it was ʿUmar. I informed him of what the Prophet ﷺ had said, and he praised Allah. Then another man requested permission to enter. The Prophet ﷺ said to me: “Open the gate for him and give him the glad tidings of Paradise, along with a trial that will befall him.” It was ʿUthmān. I informed him of what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said. He praised Allah and then said: “Allah is the One whose help is sought.”"],[202,"Abū Mūsā narrated: The Prophet ﷺ entered a garden and instructed me to guard its gate. A man came requesting permission to enter. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Let him enter and give him the glad tidings of Paradise.” It was Abū Bakr. Then another man came requesting permission. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Let him enter and give him the glad tidings of Paradise.” It was ʿUmar. Then another man came requesting permission. The Prophet ﷺ remained silent for a short while before saying: “Let him enter and give him the glad tidings of Paradise together with a trial that will befall him.” It was ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān. Ḥammād said: ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal and ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥakam narrated to us that they heard Abū ʿUthmān narrating from Abū Mūsā a similar report. ʿĀṣim added: “The Prophet ﷺ was sitting in a place containing water, and his knees—or one of his knees—were uncovered. When ʿUthmān entered, he covered them.”"],[203,"Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī narrated: One day the Prophet ﷺ went out to one of the gardens of Madinah to relieve himself, and I followed him. When he entered the garden, I sat at its gate and said to myself: “Today I will serve as the gatekeeper of the Prophet ﷺ,” even though he had not instructed me to do so. The Prophet ﷺ relieved himself and then sat upon the stone ledge surrounding the well. He uncovered his lower legs and let them hang into the well. Abū Bakr then came seeking permission to enter. I said: “Please wait until I ask permission for you.” He waited. I went to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “O Prophet of Allah, Abū Bakr is asking permission to enter.” He replied: “Let him enter and give him the glad tidings of Paradise.” Abū Bakr entered and sat on the Prophet’s right. He uncovered his lower legs and let them hang into the well. Then ʿUmar came. I said: “Please wait until I ask permission for you.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Let him enter and give him the glad tidings of Paradise.” He entered, sat on the Prophet’s left, uncovered his lower legs, and let them hang into the well until the stone ledge became completely occupied. Then ʿUthmān came. I said: “Please wait until I ask permission for you.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Let him enter and give him the glad tidings of Paradise together with a trial that will befall him.” He entered but found no place to sit with them, so he moved to the opposite edge of the well, uncovered his lower legs, and let them hang into the well. Meanwhile, I continued hoping that my brother would arrive and kept praying to Allah that he would come. Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib said: “I interpreted this as symbolizing their graves: Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, and the Prophet ﷺ would be buried together, while ʿUthmān would be buried separately.”"],[204,"ʿĀ’ishah narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was reclining in my house with his thighs—or his lower legs—uncovered. Abū Bakr sought permission to enter, and the Prophet ﷺ admitted him while remaining in that state. They spoke together. Then ʿUmar sought permission to enter, and the Prophet ﷺ admitted him while remaining in the same state. They spoke together. Then ʿUthmān sought permission to enter. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat upright and adjusted his clothing. Muḥammad said: “I do not say that all of this occurred on the same day.” ʿUthmān entered and spoke with him. After he left, ʿĀ’ishah said: “Abū Bakr entered, yet you did not become particularly animated or concerned by his entrance. Then ʿUmar entered, and you did not become particularly animated or concerned by his entrance. But when ʿUthmān entered, you sat upright and adjusted your clothing.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Should I not feel modest before a man before whom the angels feel modest?”"],[205,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, and ʿUthmān narrated: Abū Bakr sought permission to enter upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was reclining upon his bed and wearing ʿĀ’ishah’s woolen garment. The Prophet ﷺ permitted Abū Bakr to enter while remaining in that state. Abū Bakr presented his need and then departed. Then ʿUmar sought permission to enter. The Prophet ﷺ admitted him while remaining in the same state. ʿUmar presented his need and then departed. ʿUthmān said: Then I sought permission to enter. The Prophet ﷺ sat upright and said to ʿĀ’ishah: “Gather your garments around yourself.” I presented my need to him and then departed. ʿĀ’ishah asked: “O Messenger of Allah, why did I not see you become concerned about Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, as you became concerned about ʿUthmān?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “ʿUthmān is an exceedingly modest man, and I feared that if I admitted him while I was in that state, he would not fully express his need to me.”"],[206,"Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn narrated: A man in Kūfah testified that ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, had been killed as a martyr. Some harsh guards seized him and brought him before ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him. They said: “Were it not that you have forbidden us—or had forbidden us—from killing anyone, we would have killed him. He claims to testify that ʿUthmān was killed as a martyr.” The man said to ʿAlī: “You also bear witness to this. Do you remember that I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked him, and he gave to me; then I came to Abū Bakr and asked him, and he gave to me; then I came to ʿUmar and asked him, and he gave to me; and then I came to ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, and asked him, and he gave to me?” He continued: I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah to bless me.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “How could you not be blessed when a prophet, a truthful one, and two martyrs have given to you? A prophet, a truthful one, and two martyrs have given to you. A prophet, a truthful one, and two martyrs have given to you.”"],[207,"Al-Aswad ibn Hilāl narrated from a man of his people who used to say during the caliphate of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb: “ʿUthmān will not die until he becomes caliph.” We asked him: “How do you know that?” He replied: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “Last night, I saw in a dream that three of my Companions were weighed. Abū Bakr was weighed and proved weighty. Then ʿUmar was weighed and proved weighty. Then ʿUthmān was weighed, and our companion fell short, though he is righteous.”"],[208,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr narrated: I was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when Abū Bakr came and sought permission to enter. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Admit him and give him the glad tidings of Paradise.” Then ʿUmar came and sought permission to enter. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Admit him and give him the glad tidings of Paradise.” Then ʿUthmān came and sought permission to enter. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Admit him and give him the glad tidings of Paradise.” I asked: “Then where will I be?” He replied: “You will be with your father.”"],[209,"Buraydah ibn al-Ḥuṣayb narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sitting upon Mount Ḥirāʾ with Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, and ʿUthmān when the mountain moved. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Be firm, Ḥirāʾ, for upon you there is only a prophet, a truthful one, or a martyr.”"],[210,"Sahl ibn Saʿd narrated: Mount Uḥud shook while the Prophet ﷺ, Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, and ʿUthmān were upon it. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Be firm, Uḥud. Upon you are only a prophet, a truthful one, and two martyrs.”"],[211,"Abū Bakrah narrated: One day, the Prophet ﷺ asked: “Who among you has seen a dream?” A man replied: “I have. I saw as though a scale descended from heaven. You and Abū Bakr were weighed, and you outweighed Abū Bakr. Then Abū Bakr and ʿUmar were weighed, and Abū Bakr outweighed ʿUmar. Then ʿUmar and ʿUthmān were weighed, and ʿUmar outweighed ʿUthmān. Then the scale was raised.” We then saw displeasure upon the face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[212,"Samurah ibn Jundub narrated that a man said: “O Messenger of Allah, I saw in a dream that a bucket was lowered from the sky. Abū Bakr came, took hold of its crosspieces, and drank a small amount. Then ʿUmar came, took hold of its crosspieces, and drank until he was fully satisfied. Then ʿUthmān came, took hold of its crosspieces, and drank until he was fully satisfied. Then ʿAlī came and took hold of its crosspieces, but the bucket was suddenly pulled upward, and some of its water splashed upon him.”"],[213,"Abū Hurayrah narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was upon Mount Ḥirāʾ together with Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, ʿUthmān, ʿAlī, Ṭalḥah, and al-Zubayr. The rock moved beneath them, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Be still, for upon you there is only a prophet, a truthful one, or a martyr.”"],[214,"Saʿīd ibn Zayd said: I bear witness concerning the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to that which my ears heard and my heart retained from him. I would never narrate a lie concerning him for which he might question me when I meet him. He said: “Abū Bakr is in Paradise, ʿUmar is in Paradise, ʿAlī is in Paradise, ʿUthmān is in Paradise, Ṭalḥah is in Paradise, al-Zubayr is in Paradise, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān is in Paradise, Saʿd ibn Mālik is in Paradise, and the ninth believer is in Paradise.” Saʿīd added: “Were I to wish to name him, I could do so.” The people in the mosque raised their voices and implored him: “O Companion of the Messenger of Allah, who is the ninth?” He replied: “You have adjured me by Allah—and Allah is Most Great—I am the ninth believer, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is the tenth.” He then followed this with an oath, saying: “By Allah, a single battle attended by a man, in which his face became covered with dust alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, is better than the lifetime deeds of any one of you, even were he granted the lifespan of Nūḥ, peace be upon him.”"],[215,"Ibn ʿUmar narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Ten men from Quraysh are in Paradise: Abū Bakr is in Paradise, ʿUmar is in Paradise, ʿUthmān is in Paradise, ʿAlī is in Paradise, Ṭalḥah is in Paradise, al-Zubayr is in Paradise, Saʿd and Saʿīd are in Paradise, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf is in Paradise, and Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ is in Paradise.”"],[216,"ʿĀ’ishah was asked: “Whom would the Messenger of Allah ﷺ have appointed as his successor, had he appointed one?” She replied: “Abū Bakr.” She was asked: “Then who after Abū Bakr?” She replied: “ʿUmar.” She was then asked: “Who after ʿUmar?” She replied: “Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ.” She went no further than that."],[217,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Shaqīq narrated: I asked ʿĀ’ishah: “Which of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was most beloved to him?” She replied: “Abū Bakr.” I asked: “Then who?” She replied: “ʿUmar.” I asked: “Then who?” She replied: “Then Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ.” I asked: “Then who?” The narrator said: She remained silent."],[218,"ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ narrated: The Prophet ﷺ appointed me commander of the army sent on the expedition of Dhāt al-Salāsil. I came to him and asked: “Who among the people is most beloved to you?” He replied: “ʿĀ’ishah.” I asked: “Among the men?” He replied: “Her father.” I asked: “Then who?” He replied: “Then ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.” He then named a number of other men."],[219,"Buraydah ibn al-Ḥuṣayb narrated: The most beloved of women to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was Fāṭimah, and the most beloved of men was ʿAlī."],[220,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, narrated: The Prophet ﷺ went out one morning wearing a striped cloak made of black hair. Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī came, and the Prophet ﷺ brought him beneath it. Then al-Ḥusayn came and entered beneath it with him. Then Fāṭimah came, and he brought her beneath it. Then ʿAlī came, and he brought him beneath it. He then recited: “Allah only intends to remove impurity from you, O People of the Household, and to purify you completely.” [Al-Aḥzāb 33:33]"],[221,"Umm Salamah narrated: The Prophet ﷺ was in her house when Fāṭimah brought him a cooking pot containing a dish of meat and flour. She entered with it, and he said to her: “Call your husband and your two sons.” She said: ʿAlī, al-Ḥusayn, and al-Ḥasan came and entered upon him. They sat and ate from that dish while the Prophet ﷺ was upon a sleeping mat placed on a raised platform, beneath which was a Khaybarī cloak. She continued: I was praying in the room when Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed this verse: “Allah only intends to remove impurity from you, O People of the Household, and to purify you completely.” The Prophet ﷺ took the remaining part of the cloak and covered them with it. He then extended his hand and raised it toward the sky, saying: “O Allah, these are the members of my household and my closest ones. Remove impurity from them and purify them completely. O Allah, these are the members of my household and my closest ones. Remove impurity from them and purify them completely.” She said: I placed my head inside the room and asked: “Am I with you, O Messenger of Allah?” He replied: “You are upon goodness. You are upon goodness.”"],[222,"ʿUmar ibn Abī Salamah, the stepson of the Prophet ﷺ, narrated: This verse was revealed to the Prophet ﷺ in the house of Umm Salamah: “Allah only intends to remove impurity from you, O People of the Household, and to purify you completely.” The Prophet ﷺ called Fāṭimah, al-Ḥasan, and al-Ḥusayn and covered them with a cloak. ʿAlī was behind him, so he covered him with the cloak as well. Then he said: “O Allah, these are the members of my household. Remove impurity from them and purify them completely.” Umm Salamah asked: “Am I with them, O Messenger of Allah?” He replied: “You shall remain in your place, and you are upon goodness.”"],[223,"Shaddād Abū ʿAmmār narrated: I entered upon Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ while a group of people were with him. They mentioned ʿAlī. After they had left, he said to me: “Shall I tell you what I witnessed from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” I replied: “Yes.” He said: I went to Fāṭimah, may Allah be pleased with her, to ask about ʿAlī. She said: “He has gone to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” I sat and waited for him until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived accompanied by ʿAlī, al-Ḥasan, and al-Ḥusayn, holding each of the two boys by the hand. When he entered, he drew ʿAlī and Fāṭimah close and seated them before him. He seated al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn, one upon each thigh. He then wrapped his garment—or he said, a cloak—around them and recited: “Allah only intends to remove impurity from you, O People of the Household, and to purify you completely.” He then said: “O Allah, these are the members of my household, and the members of my household are the most entitled.”"],[224,"ʿAlī narrated: The Prophet ﷺ went to a cloak that had been folded, and he sat upon it together with Fāṭimah, ʿAlī, al-Ḥasan, and al-Ḥusayn. The Prophet ﷺ then gathered its edges and wrapped it around them, saying: “O Allah, be pleased with them just as I am pleased with them.”"],[225,"ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār narrated that a man informed him that he saw the Prophet ﷺ embracing al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn and saying: “O Allah, I love them both, so love them both.”"],[226,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn: “O Allah, I love them both, so love them both.”"],[227,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said concerning al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn: “O Allah, I love them both, so love them both. Whoever loves them has indeed loved me.”"],[228,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever loves al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn has loved me, and whoever hates them has hated me.”"],[229,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying, and whenever he prostrated, al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn would leap upon his back. When the people attempted to prevent them, he gestured to them to leave the two boys alone. When he completed the prayer, he placed them in his lap and said: “Whoever loves me should love these two.”"],[230,"Buraydah ibn al-Ḥuṣayb narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was delivering a sermon to us when al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn came wearing two red shirts, walking and stumbling. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ descended from the pulpit, carried them, and placed them before him. He then said: “Allah has spoken the truth: ‘Your wealth and your children are only a trial.’ I looked at these two boys walking and stumbling, and I could not remain patient until I interrupted my speech and lifted them up.”"],[231,"Khālid ibn Maʿdān narrated: Al-Miqdām ibn Maʿdīkarib, ʿAmr ibn al-Aswad, and a man from Banū Asad who lived in Qinnasrīn came as a delegation to Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān. Muʿāwiyah said to al-Miqdām: “Have you learned that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī has died?” Al-Miqdām began reciting the words said when a calamity occurs. A man asked him: “Do you regard it as a calamity?” He replied: “Why should I not regard it as a calamity when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed him in his lap and said: ‘This one is from me, and Ḥusayn is from ʿAlī’?”"],[232,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar was asked about a person in the state of iḥrām. Shuʿbah said: “I think he was asked whether such a person could kill flies.” Ibn ʿUmar replied: “The people of Iraq ask about flies, although they killed the son of the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ!” The Prophet ﷺ said: “They are my two fragrant flowers in this world.”"],[233,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ narrated: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn were playing upon his stomach. I asked: “O Messenger of Allah, do you love them?” He replied: “Why should I not love them? They are my two fragrant flowers.”"],[234,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the leaders of the youth of Paradise.”"],[235,"Ḥudhayfah narrated: My mother asked me: “When was the last time you met him?” She meant the Prophet ﷺ. I replied: “I have not met him for such-and-such a period.” She reproached me, so I said to her: “Let me go to the Prophet ﷺ, pray Maghrib with him, and ask him to seek forgiveness for you and me.” I went to the Prophet ﷺ and prayed Maghrib with him. He continued praying until he prayed ʿIshāʾ. Then he departed, and I followed him. He heard my footsteps and said: “Who is this? Is it Ḥudhayfah?” I replied: “Yes.” He asked: “What do you need? May Allah forgive you and your mother.” He then said: “This is an angel who had never descended to the earth before this night. He sought permission from his Lord to greet me and give me the glad tidings that Fāṭimah is the leader of the women of Paradise, and that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the leaders of the youth of Paradise.”"],[236,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the leaders of the youth of Paradise, and Fāṭimah is the leader of the women of Paradise, except for Maryam, the daughter of ʿImrān.”"],[237,"Abū Mūsā narrated: I was with the Prophet ﷺ while he was staying at al-Jiʿrānah, between Makkah and Madinah, and Bilāl was with him. A Bedouin came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “Will you not fulfill what you promised me?” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Receive glad tidings.” The man said: “You have given me ‘glad tidings’ too many times.” The Prophet ﷺ then turned toward Abū Mūsā and Bilāl, appearing somewhat displeased, and said: “He has rejected the glad tidings, so you two accept them.” They both replied: “We accept them.” The Prophet ﷺ then called for a vessel containing water. He washed his hands and face in it, rinsed his mouth into it, and then said: “Drink from it, pour some of it over your faces and chests, and receive glad tidings.” They took the vessel and did as they were instructed. Umm Salamah then called out from behind the curtain: “Leave some for your mother.” So they left a portion of it for her."],[238,"Anas narrated: Two men departed from the Prophet ﷺ on a very dark night, and a light appeared before them. When they separated, the light also separated with them. Maʿmar narrated from Thābit, from Anas, that they were Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr and another man from the Anṣār. Ḥammād narrated from Thābit, from Anas, that the two men were Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr and ʿAbbād ibn Bishr."],[239,"ʿĀʾidh ibn ʿAmr narrated: Abū Sufyān passed by Salmān, Ṣuhayb, Bilāl, and a group of others. They said: “By Allah, the swords of Allah have not yet taken their full due from the neck of the enemy of Allah.” Abū Bakr said to them: “Do you say this to the elder of Quraysh and their chief?” He then went to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him. The Prophet ﷺ said: “O Abū Bakr, perhaps you have offended them. If you have indeed offended them, then you have offended your Lord.” Abū Bakr went back to them and said: “My brothers, have I offended you?” They replied: “No. May Allah forgive you, our brother.”"],[240,"Al-Barāʾ narrated: The Prophet ﷺ performed ʿUmrah during the month of Dhū al-Qaʿdah… The narration includes the story of the daughter of Ḥamzah, who followed them as they were leaving Makkah. ʿAlī took her by the hand and said to Fāṭimah: “Take charge of your cousin.” Fāṭimah carried her. Then ʿAlī, Zayd, and Jaʿfar disputed over who had the greater right to care for her. ʿAlī said: “I have the greatest right to her, for she is my cousin.” Jaʿfar said: “She is my cousin, and her maternal aunt is my wife.” Zayd said: “She is the daughter of my brother.” The Prophet ﷺ awarded custody to her maternal aunt and said: “The maternal aunt is in the position of the mother.” He then said to ʿAlī: “You are from me, and I am from you.” He said to Jaʿfar: “You resemble me in both appearance and character.” And he said to Zayd: “You are our brother and our freedman.”"],[241,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated: When we departed from Makkah, the daughter of Ḥamzah followed us, calling out: “O my uncle! O my uncle!” I took her by the hand and entrusted her to Fāṭimah, saying: “Take charge of your cousin.” When we arrived in Madinah, Jaʿfar, Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, and I disputed over her custody. Jaʿfar said: “She is my cousin, and her maternal aunt—Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays—is with me.” Zayd said: “She is the daughter of my brother.” I said: “I took responsibility for her, and she is my cousin.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “As for you, Jaʿfar, you resemble me in both appearance and character. As for you, ʿAlī, you are from me, and I am from you. As for you, Zayd, you are our brother and our freedman. The girl shall remain with her maternal aunt, for the maternal aunt is like a mother.” I then asked: “O Messenger of Allah, will you not marry her?” He replied: “She is the daughter of my foster brother.”"],[242,"Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated: Jaʿfar, ʿAlī, and Zayd ibn Ḥārithah gathered together. Jaʿfar said: “I am the most beloved of you to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” ʿAlī said: “I am the most beloved of you to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” Zayd said: “I am the most beloved of you to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” They then said: “Let us go to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and ask him.” Usāmah ibn Zayd said: They came seeking permission to enter. The Prophet ﷺ said to me: “Go out and see who they are.” I replied: “They are Jaʿfar, ʿAlī, and Zayd”—I did not say, “my father.” He said: “Permit them to enter.” They entered and asked: “Who is most beloved to you?” He replied: “Fāṭimah.” They said: “We are asking you about the men.” He said: “As for you, Jaʿfar, your character resembles my character, and your appearance resembles my appearance. You are from me and from my family tree. As for you, ʿAlī, you are my son-in-law and the father of my children. I am from you, and you are from me. As for you, Zayd, you are my freedman, from me and close to me, and the most beloved of these men to me.”"],[243,"Khālid ibn Sumayr narrated: ʿAbdullāh ibn Rabāḥ came to us, and I found a group of people gathered around him. He said: Abū Qatādah, the horseman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, narrated to us: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ dispatched the army of commanders and said: “Zayd ibn Ḥārithah shall be in command over you. If Zayd is struck down, then Jaʿfar shall take command. If Jaʿfar is struck down, then ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah al-Anṣārī shall take command.” Jaʿfar sprang up and said: “May my father and mother be sacrificed for you, O Prophet of Allah. I never expected that you would appoint Zayd over me.” He replied: “Proceed, for you do not know which of the two situations is better.” The army departed and remained away for as long as Allah willed. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ascended the pulpit and ordered that the call be made: “Gather for prayer.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “News has come—or good news has returned; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān was uncertain—which I shall tell you concerning this army of yours that went out to fight. They proceeded until they met the enemy. Zayd was struck down as a martyr, so seek forgiveness for him.” The people sought forgiveness for him. He continued: “Then Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib took the banner and charged against the enemy until he was killed as a martyr. I bear witness that he is a martyr, so seek forgiveness for him. Then ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah took the banner and stood his ground until he was struck down as a martyr, so seek forgiveness for him. Then Khālid ibn al-Walīd took the banner. He had not been one of the appointed commanders, but he assumed command himself.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised two of his fingers and said: “O Allah, he is a sword from among Your swords, so grant him victory.” On another occasion, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated the wording: “So grant victory through him.” From that day onward, Khālid was called the Sword of Allah. The Prophet ﷺ then said: “Go forth and reinforce your brothers, and let no one remain behind.” The people set out in intense heat, some on foot and others mounted."],[244,"Anas narrated: Four men collected the Qur’an during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, all of whom were from the Anṣār: Muʿādh ibn Jabal, Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, Zayd ibn Thābit, and Abū Zayd. Qatādah said: I asked Anas: “Who was Abū Zayd?” He replied: “One of my paternal uncles.”"],[245,"Masrūq narrated: Ibn Masʿūd was mentioned in the presence of ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, and he said: “He is a man whom I shall always continue to love after hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘Learn the Qur’an from four: Ibn Masʿūd, Sālim the freedman of Abū Ḥudhayfah, Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, and Muʿādh ibn Jabal.’”"],[246,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “What an excellent man Abū Bakr is. What an excellent man ʿUmar is. What an excellent man Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ is. What an excellent man Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr is. What an excellent man Thābit ibn Qays ibn Shammās is. What an excellent man Muʿādh ibn Jabal is. What an excellent man Muʿādh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Jamūḥ is.”"],[247,"Abū Mūsā narrated: When the Prophet ﷺ had finished with the Battle of Ḥunayn, he sent Abū ʿĀmir at the head of an army toward Awṭās. He encountered Durayd ibn al-Ṣimmah. Durayd was killed, and Allah defeated his companions. Abū Mūsā said: The Prophet ﷺ sent me with Abū ʿĀmir. Abū ʿĀmir was struck in the knee by an arrow shot by a man from Jusham, and the arrow lodged in his knee. I reached him and said: “O uncle, who shot you?” Abū ʿĀmir pointed and said: “That is the man who killed me, the one who shot me.” I went after him and caught up with him. When he saw me, he turned and fled. I pursued him, saying: “Are you not ashamed? Will you not stand your ground?” He stopped, and we exchanged two sword blows. I killed him. I then returned to Abū ʿĀmir and said: “Allah has killed your attacker.” He said: “Pull this arrow out.” I removed it, and water began to flow from the wound. He then said: “O son of my brother, convey my greetings of peace to the Prophet ﷺ and ask him to seek forgiveness for me.” Abū ʿĀmir appointed me to lead the people after him. He remained alive for only a short time and then died. I returned and entered upon the Prophet ﷺ in his house. He was lying upon a woven bed with a mattress upon it, and the cords of the bed had left marks upon his back and sides. I informed him of what had happened to us and of what had happened to Abū ʿĀmir, and I told him that Abū ʿĀmir had asked him to seek forgiveness for him. The Prophet ﷺ called for water, performed ablution, then raised his hands and said: “O Allah, forgive ʿUbayd Abū ʿĀmir.” I could see the whiteness of his armpits. He then said: “O Allah, place him on the Day of Resurrection above many of the people You created.” I said: “And seek forgiveness for me as well.” He said: “O Allah, forgive the sin of ʿAbdullāh ibn Qays and admit him on the Day of Resurrection through an honorable entrance.” Abū Burdah said: One supplication was for Abū ʿĀmir, and the other was for Abū Mūsā."],[248,"Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated that the Prophet ﷺ would take him and al-Ḥasan together and say: “O Allah, love them both, for I love them both.”"],[249,"ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ narrated: The people in Madinah were suddenly alarmed while the Prophet ﷺ was among them, and they scattered. I saw Sālim, the freedman of Abū Ḥudhayfah, sitting in the mosque with his knees drawn up, using the straps of his sword. When I saw him, I did the same as he had done. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out and saw Sālim and me, while the rest of the people were arriving. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O people, should not your place of refuge be with Allah and His Messenger? Why did you not do as these two believing men did?”"],[250,"ʿAlqamah narrated: I came to Syria and prayed two rakʿahs. Then I said: “O Allah, make a righteous companion easy for me.” I went to a group of people and sat with them. An elderly man came and sat beside me. I asked: “Who is this?” They replied: “Abū al-Dardāʾ.” I said to him: “I asked Allah to make a righteous companion easy for me, and He has made you available to me.” He asked: “Where are you from?” I replied: “From Kūfah.” He said: “Do you not have among you Ibn Umm ʿAbd, the keeper of the sandals, the cushion, and the water vessel? Is there not among you the man whom Allah protected from Satan through the words of His Prophet ﷺ? And is there not among you the keeper of the Prophet’s ﷺ secret, which no one else knows?” He then asked: “How does ʿAbdullāh recite: ‘By the night when it covers’?” I recited to him: “By the night when it covers, and by the day when it appears, and by the male and the female.” He said: “By Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught me this recitation directly from his mouth to mine.”"],[251,"Khaythamah ibn Abī Sabrah narrated: I came to Madinah and asked Allah to make a righteous companion easy for me. He made Abū Hurayrah available to me, so I sat with him. I said: “I asked Allah to make a righteous companion easy for me, and I was guided to you.” He asked: “Where are you from?” I replied: “From Kūfah. I came seeking goodness and pursuing it.” He said: “Do you not have among you Saʿd ibn Mālik, whose supplications are answered; Ibn Masʿūd, the keeper of the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ purification water and sandals; Ḥudhayfah, the keeper of the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ secret; ʿAmmār, whom Allah protected from Satan through the words of His Prophet; and Salmān, the possessor of the two scriptures?” Qatādah said: The two scriptures were the Gospel and the Qur’an."],[252,"Abū Mūsā narrated: News of the departure of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached us while we were in Yemen, so we set out as emigrants to join him. I was accompanied by two of my brothers, and I was the youngest of the three. One of them was Abū Burdah and the other was Abū Ruhm. There were either a little over fifty men from my people, or fifty-three, or fifty-two—the narrator was uncertain. We boarded a ship, but our ship carried us to al-Najāshī in Abyssinia. There we met Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib and his companions. Jaʿfar said: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent us here and ordered us to remain, so stay with us.” We remained with him until we all arrived together. We reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at the time when he conquered Khaybar. He assigned us a share from its spoils—or the narrator said that he gave us some of it. He did not give any share from Khaybar to anyone who had been absent from its conquest except those who had actually been present with him, with the exception of the people of our ship who had come with Jaʿfar and his companions. He gave them shares along with the others. Some people would say to us—that is, to the people of the ship: “We preceded you in migration.” Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays, who was among those who had arrived with us, visited Ḥafṣah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ. Asmāʾ had been among those who migrated to al-Najāshī. ʿUmar entered upon Ḥafṣah while Asmāʾ was with her. When he saw Asmāʾ, he asked: “Who is this?” Ḥafṣah replied: “Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays.” ʿUmar said: “Is this the Abyssinian woman? Is this the woman who came by sea?”\nShe replied: “Yes.” ʿUmar said: “We preceded you in migration, so we have a greater right to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ than you do.” Asmāʾ became angry and said: “You are mistaken, O ʿUmar! No, by Allah! You were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he fed your hungry and instructed your ignorant. We, meanwhile, were in the land—or the territory—of distant and hostile people in Abyssinia, and that was for the sake of Allah and His Messenger. By Allah, I will neither eat food nor drink anything until I mention to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what you have said. We used to suffer harm and live in fear. I will mention this to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and ask him about it. By Allah, I will neither lie, deviate from the truth, nor add anything to what you said.” When the Prophet ﷺ arrived, she said: “O Prophet of Allah, ʿUmar said such-and-such.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “He and his companions have no greater right to me than you. He and his companions have one migration, while you, the people of the ship, have two migrations.” Asmāʾ said: I saw Abū Mūsā and the other people of the ship coming to me in successive groups and asking me about this hadith. Nothing in the world brought them greater happiness or held greater importance in their hearts than what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said to them.\nAbū Burdah said: Asmāʾ said: “I saw Abū Mūsā repeatedly asking me to narrate this hadith to him again.”"],[253,"Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥubulī narrated: I heard ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ when a man asked him: “Are we not among the poor Emigrants?” ʿAbdullāh asked him: “Do you have a wife with whom you find shelter?” He replied: “Yes.” He asked: “Do you have a house in which you live?” He replied: “Yes.” ʿAbdullāh said: “Then you are among the wealthy.” The man said: “I also have a servant.” He replied: “Then you are among kings.” Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān continued: Three men came to ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ while I was with him and said: “O Abū Muḥammad, by Allah, we possess nothing—no provisions, no riding animal, and no belongings.”\nHe said to them: “You may choose as you wish. If you wish, return to us and we will give you whatever Allah makes easy for us to provide. If you wish, we will present your situation to the ruler. And if you wish, remain patient, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘The poor Emigrants will enter Paradise forty years before the wealthy on the Day of Resurrection.’” They replied: “We will remain patient and ask no one for anything.”"],[254,"Thawbān, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, narrated: I was standing beside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a Jewish rabbi came and said: “Peace be upon you, O Muḥammad.” I pushed him with such force that he nearly fell. He asked: “Why did you push me?” I replied: “Why did you not say, ‘O Messenger of Allah’?” The Jew answered: “We call him only by the name given to him by his family.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “My name is indeed Muḥammad, the name given to me by my family.” The Jew said: “I have come to ask you some questions.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “Will it benefit you if I answer you?” He replied: “I shall listen with my own ears.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ scratched the ground with a stick he was holding and said: “Ask.” The Jew asked: “Where will the people be when the earth is changed into another earth and the heavens as well?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “They will be in darkness before the Bridge.” He asked: “Who will be the first people permitted to cross?” He replied: “The poor Emigrants.” He asked: “What will be their first hospitality when they enter Paradise?” He replied: “The extra lobe of the fish’s liver.” He asked: “What will they eat after that?” He replied: “The bull of Paradise, which used to graze along its edges, will be slaughtered for them.” He asked: “What will they drink with it?” He replied: “From a spring in Paradise called Salsabīl.” The Jew said: “You have spoken the truth.” He then said: “I have also come to ask you about something that no one on earth knows except a prophet, or perhaps one or two men.” The Prophet ﷺ asked: “Will it benefit you if I tell you?” He replied: “I shall listen with my own ears.” He said: “I have come to ask you about the child.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “The fluid of the man is white, and the fluid of the woman is yellow. When they come together, if the man’s fluid prevails over the woman’s fluid, the child will be male by Allah’s permission. If the woman’s fluid prevails over the man’s fluid, the child will be female by Allah’s permission.” The Jew said: “You have spoken the truth. Indeed, you are a prophet.” He then departed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “He asked me about matters of which I had no knowledge until Allah revealed them to me.”"],[255,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Do you know who will be the first of Allah’s creation to enter Paradise?” They replied: “Allah and His Messenger know best.” He said: “The first of Allah’s creation to enter Paradise will be the poor Emigrants, through whom the frontiers are guarded and by whom hardships are warded off. One of them dies while still carrying an unfulfilled need in his heart that he was unable to satisfy. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, will say to whichever of His angels He wills: ‘Go to them and greet them.’ The angels will say: ‘We are the inhabitants of Your heavens and the chosen among Your creation. Do You command us to go and greet these people?’ Allah will say: ‘They were servants who worshipped Me and associated nothing with Me. Through them the frontiers were guarded, by them hardships were repelled, and one of them died while carrying an unfulfilled need in his heart which he was unable to fulfill.’ Then the angels will come to them and enter upon them through every gate, saying: ‘Peace be upon you for what you patiently endured. How excellent is the final home!’” (Qur’an 13:24)"],[256,"Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ narrated: I was among the People of the Ṣuffah. I remember that not one of us possessed a complete garment. Sweat had mixed with the dust and dirt upon our skin. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us and said: “Glad tidings to the poor Emigrants.” A man wearing fine clothing then approached. Whenever the Prophet ﷺ spoke, the man would deliberately try to speak in a manner that rose above the Prophet’s words. When he departed, the Prophet ﷺ said: “Indeed, Allah does not love this type of person. They twist their tongues before people just as a cow twists its tongue around pasture. Likewise, Allah will twist their tongues and their faces in the Fire.”"],[257,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ spent ten years visiting the camps of the pilgrims during the pilgrimage season, at Majannah, at ʿUkāẓ, and at their encampments in Minā, saying: “Who will give me shelter? Who will support me so that I may convey the messages of my Lord? Paradise shall be his.” Yet he found no one who would support or shelter him. A man would travel from Muḍar or from Yemen to visit his relatives, and his people would warn him: “Beware of the young man of Quraysh lest he lead you astray.” Meanwhile, the Prophet ﷺ walked among their camps calling them to Allah, while they pointed at him with their fingers. Then Allah sent supporters for him from Yathrib. A man would come to him, embrace Islam, learn the Qur’an from him, and return to his family, who would accept Islam through him, until there remained no household in Yathrib except that it contained Muslims openly practicing their faith. Then Allah sent us forth, and we conferred together. Seventy of us gathered and said: “How long shall we leave the Messenger of Allah ﷺ being driven through the mountains of Makkah and living in fear?” We traveled until we met him during the pilgrimage season at the valley of al-ʿAqabah. His uncle al-ʿAbbās said: “O son of my brother, I do not know these people who have come to you. I know the people of Yathrib well.” We approached him one or two at a time. When al-ʿAbbās looked at our faces, he said: “These are people I do not recognize. They are young men.” We asked: “O Messenger of Allah, upon what shall we pledge allegiance to you?” He replied: “You pledge to hear and obey whether energetic or weary; to spend in ease and hardship; to enjoin what is right and forbid what is wrong; to speak the truth for Allah without fearing the blame of any critic; and to support me when I come to Yathrib, protecting me as you protect yourselves, your wives, and your children. In return, Paradise will be yours.” We stood to pledge our allegiance. Asʿad ibn Zurārah, the youngest of the seventy, took hold of the Prophet’s hand and said: “Take your time, O people of Yathrib. We have not exhausted our riding camels in coming here except because we know that he is truly the Messenger of Allah. Taking him away today means declaring yourselves against all the Arabs, the killing of your finest men, and that swords will strike you. If you are prepared to endure that patiently, then take him, and your reward is with Allah. But if you fear that you will fail, then leave him now, for that will be a better excuse before Allah.” They replied: “O Asʿad ibn Zurārah, remove your hand. By Allah, we will never abandon this pledge nor seek release from it.” Then we pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one by one. He accepted our pledge upon the conditions mentioned by al-ʿAbbās and promised us Paradise in return."],[258,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married Fāṭimah to me, he sent with her a velvet covering, a leather pillow stuffed with palm fiber, two hand mills, a waterskin, and two earthenware jars. One day I said to Fāṭimah: “By Allah, I have drawn water until my chest has become sore. Allah has brought your father captives, so go and ask him for a servant.” She replied: “By Allah, I have ground grain until my hands have become blistered.” She went to the Prophet ﷺ, who asked: “What has brought you here, my dear daughter?” She replied: “I came to greet you.” She felt too shy to ask him and returned. I asked her: “What happened?” She replied: “I was too shy to ask him.” So we both went to him together. I said: “O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, I have drawn water until my chest has become sore.” Fāṭimah said: “I have ground grain until my hands have become blistered. Allah has granted you captives and abundance, so provide us with a servant.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “By Allah, I will not give you a servant while leaving the People of the Ṣuffah with their stomachs empty. I have nothing else to spend on them. Rather, I will sell these captives and spend their price on them.” They returned home. Later, the Prophet ﷺ came to them after they had wrapped themselves in a blanket. Whenever they covered their heads, their feet became uncovered, and whenever they covered their feet, their heads became exposed. They rose, but he said: “Stay where you are.” Then he said: “Shall I not tell you something better than what you asked for?” They replied: “Yes.” He said: “These are words Jibrīl taught me: after every prayer, glorify Allah ten times, praise Him ten times, and proclaim His greatness ten times. When you go to bed, glorify Allah thirty-three times, praise Him thirty-three times, and proclaim His greatness thirty-four times.” ʿAlī said: “By Allah, I have never abandoned these words since the Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught them to me.” Ibn al-Kawwāʾ asked him: “Not even on the night of Ṣiffīn?” He replied: “May Allah fight you, O people of Iraq! No—not even on the night of Ṣiffīn.”"],[259,"ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Rāfiʿ, the scribe of ʿAlī, narrated: I heard ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me, al-Zubayr, and al-Miqdād, saying: “Go to Rawḍat Khākh. There you will find a woman carrying a letter. Take it from her.” We rode swiftly until we found the woman. We said: “Produce the letter.” She replied: “I do not have any letter.” We said: “You will either produce the letter or remove your clothing.” She then took the letter out from the braids of her hair. We brought it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and it was found to be from Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah to some of the idolaters of Makkah, informing them about part of the Prophet’s plans. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “O Ḥāṭib, what is this?” Ḥāṭib replied: “O Messenger of Allah, do not be hasty in judging me. I was a man attached to Quraysh”—Sufyān explained that he was their ally but not one of them by lineage—“while the other Emigrants had relatives there who protected their families. Since I had no such relatives, I wished to establish a favor with them so they would protect my family. I did not do this out of disbelief, apostasy, or approval of disbelief after embracing Islam.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “He has spoken the truth.” ʿUmar said: “O Messenger of Allah, allow me to strike the neck of this hypocrite.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “He participated in Badr. And what makes you know? Perhaps Allah looked upon the people of Badr and said: ‘Do whatever you wish, for I have forgiven you.’” Then Allah revealed: “O you who believe! Do not take My enemies and your enemies as allies…” (Qur’an 60:1)"],[260,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Allah looked upon the people of Badr and said: ‘Do whatever you wish, for I have forgiven you.’”"],[261,"Muʿādh ibn Rifāʿah ibn Rāfiʿ al-Zuraqī narrated from his father—who was one of the participants of Badr—that Jibrīl (Gabriel) came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked: “How do you regard those among you who took part in the Battle of Badr?” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “They are among the best of the Muslims,” or words to that effect. Jibrīl then said: “And likewise, among us, those angels who witnessed Badr are the best.”"],[262,"Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj narrated: Jibrīl—or an angel—came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked: “How do you regard those among you who participated in Badr?” They replied: “They are the best among us.” He said: “In the same way, among us, those angels who took part in Badr are our best.”"],[263,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated: On the Day of Badr, while the Prophet ﷺ was inside a small tent, he supplicated: “O Allah, I implore You by Your covenant and Your promise. O Allah, if You will, You will not be worshipped after today.” Abū Bakr took hold of his hand and said: “That is enough, O Messenger of Allah. You have earnestly and repeatedly called upon your Lord.” The Prophet ﷺ then emerged wearing his coat of armor while reciting: “Their assembly will be defeated, and they will turn their backs in flight. Rather, the Hour is their appointed time, and the Hour is more disastrous and more bitter.” (Qur’an 54:45–46)"],[264,"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb narrated: On the Day of Badr, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at the army of the polytheists, who numbered one thousand, while his own companions numbered only three hundred and nineteen men. The Prophet ﷺ then faced the qiblah, raised his hands, and continuously called upon his Lord, saying: “O Allah, fulfill for me what You have promised me. O Allah, grant me what You have promised me. O Allah, if this small group of the people of Islam is destroyed today, You will not be worshipped on earth.” Thereupon Allah reinforced him with the angels. (The remainder of the narration follows in the original source.)"],[265,"ʿAlī narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ spent the night supplicating to his Lord, the Mighty and Majestic, saying: “O Allah, if this group is destroyed, You will not be worshipped.”"],[266,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: Representatives of Riʿl, Dhakwān, ʿUṣayyah, and Banū Liḥyān came to the Prophet ﷺ claiming that they had accepted Islam and asked him to send assistance against their own people. The Prophet ﷺ sent with them seventy men from the Anṣār. Anas said: “We used to call them the Reciters (al-Qurrāʾ). During the day they gathered firewood, and during the night they devoted themselves to prayer.” When they reached Bi’r Maʿūnah, those tribes betrayed them and massacred them. The Prophet ﷺ then supplicated against Riʿl, Dhakwān, and Banū Liḥyān during the qunūt prayer for an entire month. Qatādah narrated from Anas that a Qur’anic passage was revealed concerning them which the Muslims recited: “Convey to our people that we have met our Lord, and He is pleased with us and has made us pleased.” Later, the recitation of this verse was abrogated."],[267,"Anas ibn Mālik al-Anṣārī narrated: On the Day of Uḥud, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to supplicate: “O Allah, if You will, You will not be worshipped on earth.”"],[268,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “When your brothers were martyred at Uḥud, Allah placed their souls inside green birds. They roam through the rivers of Paradise, eat from its fruits, and take refuge in lanterns of gold suspended beneath the Shade of the Throne. When they experienced the excellence of their food, drink, and resting place, they said: ‘Who will convey to our brothers that we are alive in Paradise, receiving provision, so that they will not lose interest in striving in Allah’s cause nor become reluctant in battle?’ Allah, the Exalted, said: ‘I shall convey it to them on your behalf.’ Then Allah revealed: ‘And never think of those who have been killed in the way of Allah as dead…’ (Qur’an 3:169), continuing to the end of the passage."],[269,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated: Umm Mubashshir informed me that she heard the Prophet ﷺ say in the presence of Ḥafṣah: “None of those who pledged allegiance beneath the Tree will enter the Fire, Allah willing.” Ḥafṣah said: “Yes, O Messenger of Allah, they will.” He rebuked her, so she recited: “There is none among you except that he will come to it.” (Qur’an 19:71) The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has also said: ‘Then We will save those who were mindful of Allah and leave the wrongdoers therein on their knees.’” (Qur’an 19:72)"],[270,"Jābir narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “No one who pledged allegiance beneath the Tree will enter the Fire.”"],[271,"Jābir narrated: On the day of al-Ḥudaybiyah, we numbered fourteen hundred. The Prophet ﷺ said to us: “You are the best people on earth today.” Jābir also said: “Had I still been able to see, I would have shown you the location of the Tree.”"],[272,"Umm Mubashshir, the wife of Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in the house of Ḥafṣah when he said: “No one who witnessed Badr and al-Ḥudaybiyah will enter the Fire.” Ḥafṣah asked: “Does Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, not say: ‘There is none among you except that he will come to it’?” (Qur’an 19:71) The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “Stop there. Then Allah says: ‘Then We will save those who were mindful of Allah and leave the wrongdoers therein on their knees.’”\n(Qur’an 19:72)"],[273,"Umm Mubashshir narrated: A servant of Ḥāṭib came and said: “By Allah, Ḥāṭib will not enter Paradise.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “You have lied. He witnessed Badr and al-Ḥudaybiyah.”"],[274,"Ḥafṣah narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I truly hope that no one who witnessed Badr and al-Ḥudaybiyah will enter the Fire, Allah willing.” I said: “Does Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, not say: ‘There is none among you except that he will come to it’?” (Qur’an 19:71) She said: I then heard him recite: “Then We will save those who were mindful of Allah and leave the wrongdoers therein on their knees.” (Qur’an 19:72)"],[275,"Jābir narrated: A servant of Ḥāṭib came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ complaining about Ḥāṭib and said: “O Messenger of Allah, Ḥāṭib will certainly enter the Fire.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “You have lied. He will not enter it, for he witnessed Badr and al-Ḥudaybiyah.”"],[276,"Jābir narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “No man who witnessed Badr and al-Ḥudaybiyah will enter the Fire.”"],[277,"Ibn Abī Awfā narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “No one who witnessed Badr and al-Ḥudaybiyah will enter the Fire.”"],[278,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: Among the supplications of the Prophet ﷺ on the day of Ḥunayn was: “O Allah, if You will, You will not be worshipped after this day.”"],[279,"Umm Ḥarām narrated that she heard the Prophet ﷺ say: “The first army from my nation to undertake a naval expedition will have secured Paradise.” Umm Ḥarām said: I asked: “O Messenger of Allah, will I be among them?” He replied: “You will be among them.” Then the Prophet ﷺ said: “The first army from my nation to invade the city of Caesar will be forgiven.” I asked: “O Messenger of Allah, will I be among them?” He replied: “No.”"],[280,"Thawbān, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Two groups from my nation have been protected by Allah from the Fire: a group that will campaign in India, and a group that will be with ʿĪsā ibn Maryam.”"],[281,"Anas narrated: When the mother of Ibrāhīm gave birth to Ibrāhīm, it appeared that something concerning him entered the heart of the Prophet ﷺ, until Jibrīl came and said: “Peace be upon you, O Abū Ibrāhīm.”"],[282,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: I never saw anyone more merciful toward children than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Ibrāhīm was being nursed for him in the upper districts of Madinah. The Prophet ﷺ would go there while we accompanied him. He would enter the house, which was filled with smoke because Ibrāhīm’s foster father was a blacksmith. He would take Ibrāhīm, kiss him, and then return."],[283,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib narrated: When Ibrāhīm died, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “He has a wet nurse in Paradise.”"],[284,"Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Awfā narrated: I asked Ibn Abī Awfā: “Did you see Ibrāhīm, the son of the Prophet ﷺ?” He replied: “He died while still young. Had it been decreed that there would be a prophet after Muḥammad ﷺ, his son would have lived. However, there is no prophet after him.”"],[285,"Anas narrated: I saw Ibrāhīm struggling in the throes of death before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The eyes of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ filled with tears, and he said: “The eyes shed tears, the heart grieves, and we say only what pleases our Lord. By Allah, O Ibrāhīm, we are truly grieved by your departure.”"],[286,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: The Prophet ﷺ said to Ubayy: “Allah has commanded me to recite to you: ‘Those who disbelieved were not…’ (Qur’an 98:1)” Ubayy asked: “Did He mention me by name?” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Yes.” Thereupon Ubayy wept."],[287,"Ubayy ibn Kaʿb narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Allah has commanded me to recite the Qur’an to you.” He then recited: “Those who disbelieved among the People of the Book…” (Qur’an 98:1) And within the recitation he recited: “If the son of Adam were to ask for a valley filled with wealth and were given it, he would ask for a second. If he were given a second, he would ask for a third. Nothing fills the belly of the son of Adam except dust. Allah accepts the repentance of whoever repents. The religion with Allah is the upright religion, neither polytheism, Judaism, nor Christianity, and whoever does good will never have it denied.”"],[288,"Ubayy ibn Kaʿb narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked me: “O Abū al-Mundhir, do you know which verse of the Book of Allah that you possess is the greatest?” I replied: “Allah and His Messenger know best.” He again asked: “O Abū al-Mundhir, do you know which verse of the Book of Allah that you possess is the greatest?” I replied: “Allah—there is no deity except Him, the Ever-Living, the Sustainer of all.” (Qur’an 2:255) He struck me gently on the chest and said: “By Allah, may knowledge bring you joy, O Abū al-Mundhir.”"],[289,"ʿĀ’ishah narrated: The affair of the Makhzūmī woman greatly concerned Quraysh, so they said: “Who would dare speak to him about her except Usāmah ibn Zayd, the beloved of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?”"],[290,"Fāṭimah bint Qays narrated, in the context of the ḥadīth of al-Jassāsah: After I became unmarried, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf proposed to me along with several other Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ proposed that I marry his freedman, Usāmah ibn Zayd. I had already been informed that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever loves me should love Usāmah.”"],[291,"ʿĀ’ishah narrated: The Prophet ﷺ intended to wipe mucus from Usāmah’s nose. I said: “Allow me to do it.” He replied: “O ʿĀ’ishah, love him, for indeed I love him.”"],[292,"Ibn ʿUmar narrated: The Prophet ﷺ dispatched an expedition and appointed Usāmah ibn Zayd as its commander. Some people criticized his appointment, so the Prophet ﷺ said: “If you criticize his leadership, you also criticized the leadership of his father before him. By Allah, his father was truly worthy of command and was among the most beloved people to me. And this son of his is among the most beloved people to me after him.”"],[293,"Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated that the Prophet ﷺ would take him and al-Ḥasan and say: “O Allah, love them both, for indeed I love them both.”"],[294,"Ḥarmalah, the freedman of Usāmah ibn Zayd, narrated: While I was with ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, al-Ḥajjāj ibn Ayman entered and performed a prayer in which he did not complete his bowing or prostration properly. Ibn ʿUmar said to him: “Repeat the prayer.” When he turned away, Ibn ʿUmar asked me: “Who is this?” I replied: “He is al-Ḥajjāj ibn Ayman, the son of Umm Ayman.” Ibn ʿUmar said: “Had the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seen him, he would have loved him.” He then mentioned the Prophet’s ﷺ love for Umm Ayman and for those to whom she gave birth."],[295,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār narrated: One day, while Ibn ʿUmar was in the mosque, he saw a man dragging his garments in one corner of the mosque. He said: “Find out who this is. I wish he were here beside me.” A man said to him: “Do you not recognize him, O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān? This is Muḥammad ibn Usāmah.” Ibn ʿUmar lowered his head and tapped the ground with his hands, then said: “Had the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seen him, he would have loved him.”"],[296,"Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated: When the illness of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ became severe, I came down to Madinah, and the people also came down to the city. I entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was silent and unable to speak. He began placing his hands upon me and then raising them, and I understood that he was supplicating for me."],[297,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib narrated: A man recited Sūrat al-Kahf while an animal was in the house, and the animal began to bolt and behave restlessly. He looked and saw something like a mist or cloud covering him. He later mentioned this to the Prophet ﷺ, who said: “Continue reciting, so-and-so, for that was the tranquility descending because of the Qur’an—or descending for the Qur’an.”"],[298,"Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr narrated: One night, while he was reciting in his threshing floor, his horse became agitated. He continued reciting, and it became agitated again. He recited once more, and the horse again moved restlessly. Usayd said: “I feared that it might trample Yaḥyā, so I went toward it. I then saw something resembling a canopy above my head, containing objects like lamps. It ascended into the sky until I could no longer see it.” The next morning, I went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah, last night, in the middle of the night, while I was reciting in my threshing floor, my horse became agitated.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Continue reciting, O Ibn Ḥuḍayr.” I said: “I continued reciting, and it became agitated again.” He said: “Continue reciting, O Ibn Ḥuḍayr.” I said: “I recited again, and it became agitated once more.” He said: “Continue reciting, O Ibn Ḥuḍayr.” I said: “I then stopped, because Yaḥyā was close to the horse and I feared that it might trample him. I saw something resembling a canopy containing objects like lamps, which ascended into the sky until I could no longer see it.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Those were angels listening to your recitation. Had you continued reciting, the people would have seen them in the morning, and they would not have hidden themselves from them.”"],[299,"Kaʿb ibn Mālik narrated: Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr had a beautiful voice when reciting the Qur’an. He came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “While I was reciting on the roof of my house, with my wife inside the room and the horse tied at the room’s door, something resembling a cloud covered me. I feared that the horse might break loose, frightening my wife and causing her to fall, so I stopped reciting.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Continue reciting, O Usayd. That was an angel listening to the Qur’an.”"],[300,"Anas narrated: Two men left the presence of the Prophet ﷺ on a dark night, and a light appeared before them. When they separated and each went his own way, the light also divided and accompanied each of them."],[301,"Anas narrated from Umm Sulaym that she said: “O Messenger of Allah, Anas is your servant, so supplicate to Allah for him.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “O Allah, increase his wealth and children, and bless him in what You have given him.”"],[302,"Anas narrated: The Prophet ﷺ entered upon us, and there was no one present except me, my mother, and my maternal aunt Umm Ḥarām. My mother said: “O Messenger of Allah, this is your little servant, so supplicate to Allah for him.” He supplicated for every form of goodness for me, and among the final things for which he supplicated was: “O Allah, increase his wealth and children, and bless him in them.”"],[303,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by, and my mother, Umm Sulaym, heard his voice. She said: “May my father and mother be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah—here is little Anas.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then made three supplications for me. I have already seen two of them fulfilled in this world, and I hope for the third in the Hereafter."],[304,"Anas narrated: The Prophet ﷺ entered upon Umm Sulaym, and she brought him dates and clarified butter. He said: “Return your clarified butter to its waterskin and your dates to their container, for I am fasting.” He then stood in a corner of the house and performed a voluntary prayer. Afterward, he supplicated for Umm Sulaym and the members of her household. Umm Sulaym said: “O Messenger of Allah, I have a special request.” He asked: “What is it?” She replied: “Your servant Anas.” He did not leave any good of this world or the Hereafter without supplicating for me concerning it. Among his supplications was: “O Allah, grant him wealth and children, and bless him in them.” I became one of the wealthiest men among the Anṣār. My daughter Umaynah informed me that, by the time al-Ḥajjāj arrived in Baṣrah, more than one hundred and twenty of my direct descendants had been buried."],[305,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “My dear son.”"],[306,"Anas narrated: “No one remains among those who prayed toward both qiblahs except me.”"],[307,"Anas narrated: The Prophet ﷺ entrusted me with a secret, and I never disclosed it to anyone after him. Even when Umm Sulaym asked me about it, I did not tell her."],[308,"Sahl ibn al-Ḥanẓaliyyah narrated that, during a lengthy report concerning the day of Ḥunayn, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “Who will guard us tonight?” Anas ibn Abī Marthad al-Ghanawī replied: “I will, O Messenger of Allah.” The narration states that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ later said to him: “You have made Paradise obligatory for yourself, so there will be no blame upon you even if you perform no deed after this.”"],[309,"Anas narrated: Al-Rubayyiʿ, the daughter of al-Naḍr, broke the front tooth of a young woman. Her family requested monetary compensation and asked the injured party to pardon her, but they refused. They brought the matter before the Prophet ﷺ, and he ordered legal retaliation. Anas ibn al-Naḍr said: “O Messenger of Allah, will the front tooth of al-Rubayyiʿ be broken? No, by the One who sent you with the truth, her tooth will not be broken.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “O Anas, the ruling in the Book of Allah is legal retaliation.” The injured party then became satisfied and granted pardon. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Among the servants of Allah are those who, were they to swear by Allah that something would happen, Allah would fulfill their oath.”"],[310,"Anas narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “How many a person there is whose hair is disheveled, who is covered with dust, and who wears two worn-out garments, to whom people pay no attention, yet if he were to swear by Allah, Allah would fulfill his oath. Among them is al-Barāʾ ibn Mālik.”"],[311,"Bishr ibn ʿAqrabah narrated: My father was martyred alongside the Prophet ﷺ in one of his military expeditions. The Prophet ﷺ passed by me while I was crying and said: “Do not cry. Would you not be pleased for me to be your father and ʿĀ’ishah to be your mother?” I replied: “Yes indeed. May my father and mother be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah.”"],[312,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that, at the time of the dawn prayer, the Prophet ﷺ said to Bilāl: “O Bilāl, tell me about the deed you have performed in Islam for which you have the greatest hope, for I heard the sound of your sandals ahead of me in Paradise.” Bilāl replied: “I have not performed any deed for which I have greater hope than this: whenever I performed ablution at any hour of the night or day, I prayed with that ablution as much as had been decreed for me to pray.” Abū ʿAbdullāh said: The phrase “the sound of your sandals” means the sound of their movement."],[313,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I was shown Paradise, and I saw the wife of Abū Ṭalḥah. Then I heard the sound of footsteps before me, and it was Bilāl.”"],[314,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated: ʿUmar used to say: “Abū Bakr is our master, and he set our master free,” meaning Bilāl."],[315,"Qays narrated that Bilāl said to Abū Bakr: “If you purchased me only for yourself, then keep me with you. But if you purchased me for the sake of Allah, then leave me to perform deeds for Allah.”"],[316,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: The Prophet ﷺ noticed that Thābit ibn Qays was absent. A man said: “O Messenger of Allah, I will find out what has happened to him.” He went to Thābit and found him sitting in his house with his head lowered. He asked: “What is the matter with you?” Thābit replied: “It is terrible. I used to raise my voice above the voice of the Prophet ﷺ. My deeds have therefore become worthless, and I am among the people of the Fire.” The man returned and informed the Prophet ﷺ of what Thābit had said. Mūsā ibn Anas said: The man returned to Thābit a second time bearing tremendous good news. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Go to him and tell him: You are not among the people of the Fire. Rather, you are among the people of Paradise.”"],[317,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: When the following verse was revealed: “O you who believe, do not raise your voices above the voice of the Prophet…” continuing to His saying: “…while you are unaware.” (Qur’an 49:2) Thābit ibn Qays said: “By Allah, I am the one who used to raise my voice in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I fear that I am among the people of the Fire.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Rather, he is among the people of Paradise.” Anas said: “We thereafter saw him walking among us as a man from the people of Paradise,” or words to that effect."],[318,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: We used to see him—meaning Thābit ibn Qays—walking among us while we knew that he was one of the people of Paradise. On the day of al-Yamāmah, some of our ranks began to fall back. Thābit ibn Qays ibn Shammās came forward after applying embalming perfume to himself and putting on his burial shroud. He said: “What an evil habit you are teaching your opponents!” He then fought them until he was killed."],[319,"Thawbān, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Who will guarantee for me that he will not ask people for anything, and I will guarantee Paradise for him?” Thawbān replied: “I will.” Thereafter, he would never ask anyone for anything."],[320,"Jābir ibn Samurah narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would deliver the sermon while standing, then sit, and then stand again to deliver the sermon. Whoever tells you that he used to deliver the sermon while seated has lied. By Allah, I prayed with him more than two thousand times."],[321,"Jābir narrated: We were traveling with the Prophet ﷺ when my riding camel fell behind. He then related the remainder of the narration and said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prodded it, then said to me: “Mount in the name of Allah.” The narration also adds: He continued increasing the price for me while saying: “May Allah forgive you.”"],[322,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “If the wealth of Bahrain were to arrive, I would give you this much, this much, and this much,” indicating three handfuls. The wealth of Bahrain did not arrive until after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had passed away. When it arrived during the caliphate of Abū Bakr, Abū Bakr ordered a caller to announce: “Whoever is owed a debt or has been promised something by the Prophet ﷺ should come to me.” Jābir said: I went to Abū Bakr and informed him that the Prophet ﷺ had said: “If the wealth of Bahrain arrives, I will give you this much, this much, and this much,” three times. So Abū Bakr gave me something. Jābir continued: I met Abū Bakr later and asked him, but he did not give me anything. Then I came to him again, but he did not give me anything. Then I came to him a third time, but he still did not give me anything. I said: “I came to you, but you did not give me anything. Then I came to you again, and you did not give me anything. Then I came to you a third time, and you did not give me anything. Either give me something or admit that you are withholding from me out of miserliness.” Abū Bakr said: “Did you say that I was being miserly toward you? What disease is worse than miserliness?” He repeated this three times. He then said: “Each time I withheld from you, I had intended to give you.” ʿAmr narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī that he heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh say: I came to Abū Bakr, and he said to me: “Count it.” I counted it and found it to be five hundred. He then said: “Take twice that amount as well.”"],[323,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated: I went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a military expedition, but my camel became slow and exhausted, so I fell behind. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came upon me and said: “O Jābir.” I replied: “Yes.” He asked: “What is the matter with you?” I said: “My camel has become slow and exhausted, so I have fallen behind.” He dismounted, struck it with his crooked staff, and then said: “Mount.” I mounted it, and afterward I found myself holding it back so that it would not overtake the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He asked: “Have you married?” I replied: “Yes.” He asked: “A virgin or a previously married woman?” I replied: “A previously married woman.” He said: “Why did you not marry a young virgin with whom you could play and who could play with you?” I said: “I have sisters, so I wished to marry a woman who could gather them together, comb their hair, and care for them.” He said: “You are about to arrive home, so when you arrive, act wisely and thoughtfully.” Then he asked: “Will you sell me your camel?” I replied: “Yes.” He purchased it from me for one uqiyyah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ later arrived in Madinah, and I arrived the following morning. I went to the mosque and found him at the mosque’s entrance. He asked: “Have you only just arrived?” I replied: “Yes.” He said: “Leave your camel and enter the mosque to pray two rakʿahs.” I entered and prayed. When I returned, he instructed Bilāl to weigh one uqiyyah for me. Bilāl weighed it and gave me more than the exact amount. I departed, but when I turned away, the Prophet ﷺ said: “Call Jābir back to me.” I was summoned and thought: “Now he is going to return the camel to me,” although nothing had become more disliked to me than that camel. He then said: “Take your camel, and keep its price as well.”"],[324,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated: I set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the expedition of Dhāt al-Riqāʿ, riding a weak camel of mine. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ began the return journey, the rest of the caravan moved ahead while I continued falling behind until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ caught up with me. He asked: “What is the matter with you, O Jābir?” I replied: “O Messenger of Allah, this camel of mine has become slow.” He said: “Make it kneel.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then dismounted and said: “Hand me the stick you are carrying.” —or, according to another wording— “Cut a stick for me from a tree.” I did so. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took the stick and gently prodded the camel several times before saying: “Mount.” I mounted it, and by the One Who sent him with the truth, it began keeping pace with the Prophet’s she-camel. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then spoke with me and asked: “O Jābir, will you sell me this camel?” I replied: “O Messenger of Allah, I would rather give it to you as a gift.” He said: “No, but sell it to me.” I said: “Then name your price.” He first said: “I will buy it for one dirham.” I replied: “No, then you would be underpaying me, O Messenger of Allah.” He said: “Then two dirhams?” I replied: “No.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ continued increasing the price until it reached one uqiyyah. I said: “I am satisfied.” He asked: “You are satisfied?” I replied: “Yes.” He said: “Then I have bought it.” He then asked me: “O Jābir, have you married?” I replied: “Yes, O Messenger of Allah.” He asked: “A previously married woman or a virgin?” I replied: “A previously married woman.” He said: “Why did you not marry a young virgin who could play with you and with whom you could play?” I replied: “O Messenger of Allah, my father was martyred on the Day of Uḥud, leaving behind seven daughters. Therefore, I married an experienced woman who could gather them together and take care of them.”\nHe said: “You made the right choice, if Allah wills.” He then said: “When we arrive at Ṣirār, we shall slaughter a camel, remain there for the day, and your wife will hear of our arrival and prepare her cushions.” I replied: “By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, we have no cushions.” He said: “You will have them in time. So when you arrive home, conduct yourself with wisdom and gentleness.” When we reached Ṣirār, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered that a camel be slaughtered, and we remained there for the rest of the day.\nWhen evening came, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered Madinah, and we entered as well. I informed my wife of everything the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said to me. She replied: “Then do exactly as he instructed. I hear and obey.” The following morning I took hold of the camel and brought it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, making it kneel at the entrance of his house. I then sat nearby in the mosque. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out, saw the camel, and asked: “What is this?”\nThey replied: “O Messenger of Allah, this is the camel Jābir has brought.” He asked: “Where is Jābir?” I was called, and he said: “Come here, my nephew. Take hold of your camel—it is yours.” He then called Bilāl and said: “Take Jābir and give him one uqiyyah.” Bilāl went with me, gave me one uqiyyah, and added a little extra. Jābir said: “By Allah, that blessing continued to grow among us, and we continued to see its effects until it was lost along with what the people lost on the Day of al-Ḥarrah.”"],[325,"Jābir narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ purchased a camel from me for two uqiyyahs and one dirham—or, according to another narration, two dirhams. When we reached Ṣirār, he ordered that a cow be slaughtered, and everyone ate from it. When we arrived in Madinah, he instructed me to go to the mosque and pray two rakʿahs. He then weighed out the price of the camel for me and gave me more than the agreed amount."],[326,"Jarīr ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated: From the day I embraced Islam, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ never prevented me from entering to see him, and whenever he saw me, he would smile."],[327,"Jarīr ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Bajalī narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “Will you not relieve me of Dhū al-Khalaṣah?” It was a shrine belonging to the tribe of Khathʿam and was known as the Kaʿbah of Yemen. Jarīr said: I set out with one hundred and fifty horsemen from Aḥmas, who were skilled cavalrymen. I was unable to remain steady upon a horse, so the Prophet ﷺ struck me on the chest until I could see the marks of his fingers upon it, and he said: “O Allah, make him firm, and make him one who guides others and is rightly guided.” I went to Dhū al-Khalaṣah, destroyed it, and burned it. I then sent someone to inform the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Jarīr’s messenger said: “By the One who sent you with the truth, I did not come to you until I had left it resembling a hollow or diseased camel.” The Prophet ﷺ then invoked Allah’s blessings upon the horses and men of Aḥmas five times."],[328,"Jarīr ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated: When I drew near to Madinah, I made my riding animal kneel, opened my travel bag, put on my fine garments, and then entered. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was delivering a sermon, and the people began staring at me. I said to the man sitting beside me: “O servant of Allah, did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mention me?” He replied: “Yes. He mentioned you a short while ago in the finest manner. While delivering his sermon, he paused and said: ‘A man from among the finest people of Yemen will enter upon you through this door—or through this mountain pass. Indeed, upon his face is the mark of an angel.’” Jarīr said: I praised Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for the favor with which He had blessed me."],[329,"Al-Barāʾ narrated: The Prophet ﷺ performed ʿumrah during the month of Dhū al-Qaʿdah. The narration includes the story of the daughter of Ḥamzah. When the Muslims were leaving Makkah, she followed them. ʿAlī reached out, took her by the hand, and said to Fāṭimah: “Take your cousin’s daughter.” Fāṭimah then carried her. ʿAlī, Zayd, and Jaʿfar disputed over who should care for her. ʿAlī said: “I have the greatest right to her, for she is the daughter of my paternal uncle.” Jaʿfar said: “She is the daughter of my paternal uncle, and her maternal aunt is my wife.” Zayd said: “She is the daughter of my brother.” The Prophet ﷺ awarded her custody to her maternal aunt and said: “The maternal aunt holds the same position as the mother.” He said to ʿAlī: “You are from me, and I am from you.” He said to Jaʿfar: “You resemble me in both my appearance and my character.” He said to Zayd: “You are our brother and our freedman.”"],[330,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated: You should have seen me, Qutham, and ʿUbaydullāh, the two sons of al-ʿAbbās, when we were young boys playing. The Prophet ﷺ passed by while riding an animal and said: “Lift this boy up to me.” They lifted me up, and he seated me in front of him. He then said regarding Qutham: “Lift this boy up to me.” He placed him behind him. ʿUbaydullāh was more beloved to al-ʿAbbās than Qutham, yet the Prophet ﷺ did not refrain, out of consideration for his uncle, from carrying Qutham and leaving ʿUbaydullāh behind. He then passed his hand over my head three times, saying each time: “O Allah, succeed Jaʿfar in caring for his children.” The narrator said: I asked ʿAbdullāh: “What happened to Qutham?” He replied: “He was martyred.” I said: “Allah knows best where goodness lies, and His Messenger knew best.” He replied: “Yes.”"],[331,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I saw Jaʿfar flying in Paradise with the angels.”"],[332,"Al-Shaʿbī narrated: Whenever Ibn ʿUmar greeted the son of Jaʿfar, he would say: “Peace be upon you, O son of the possessor of two wings.”"],[333,"Abū Hurayrah narrated: People used to say: “Abū Hurayrah narrates excessively.” However, I remained constantly in the company of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ merely to satisfy my hunger. At that time, I did not eat leavened bread, wear decorated garments, or have any man or woman serving me. Because of hunger, I would press my stomach against the pebbles. I would sometimes ask a man to recite a verse to me, even though I already knew it, hoping that he would take me home and feed me. The best of all people toward the poor was Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib. He would take us home and feed us whatever he had in his house. Sometimes he would bring out an empty leather container that had once held clarified butter. We would cut it open and lick whatever traces remained inside it."],[334,"Abū Barzah narrated: The Prophet ﷺ was on one of his military expeditions, and Allah granted him victory and spoils. He asked his Companions: “Are you missing anyone?” They replied: “Yes, so-and-so, so-and-so, and so-and-so.” He asked again: “Are you missing anyone?” They replied: “Yes, so-and-so, so-and-so, and so-and-so.” He asked a third time: “Are you missing anyone?” They replied: “No.” He said: “But I am missing Julaybīb. Search for him.” They searched among the slain and found him beside seven enemy soldiers whom he had killed before they killed him. The Prophet ﷺ came and stood over him, saying: “He killed seven, and then they killed him. He is from me, and I am from him. He is from me, and I am from him.” The Prophet ﷺ then carried him upon his forearms, and Julaybīb had no bier other than the forearms of the Prophet ﷺ. A grave was dug for him, and he was placed within it. The narrator did not mention that he was washed."],[335,"Abū Barzah al-Aslamī narrated: Julaybīb was a man who would pass by women and speak playfully with them. I said to my wife: “Do not allow Julaybīb to enter upon you, for if he enters, I will certainly do such-and-such.” Among the Anṣār, whenever a man had an unmarried daughter, he would not marry her off until he had first learned whether the Prophet ﷺ wished to marry her. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to a man from the Anṣār: “Give me your daughter in marriage.” The man replied: “Yes, with honor and delight, O Messenger of Allah. It would be a joy to me.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “I do not want her for myself.” The man asked: “For whom, then, O Messenger of Allah?” He replied: “For Julaybīb.” The man said: “O Messenger of Allah, I will consult her mother.” He went to her mother and said: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ is seeking your daughter’s hand in marriage.” She replied: “Yes, with honor and delight.” He said: “He is not seeking her for himself. He is seeking her for Julaybīb.” She exclaimed: “Julaybīb? Julaybīb? Julaybīb? No, by Allah, we will not marry her to him!” When the father was about to leave to inform the Messenger of Allah ﷺ of what her mother had said, the young woman asked: “Who has proposed to you for me?” Her mother informed her. The young woman said: “Will you reject the command of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? Give me to him in marriage, for he will never cause me to be lost.” Her father went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Proceed with her.” He then married her to Julaybīb. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ later set out on a military expedition. After Allah granted him victory, he asked his Companions: “Are you missing anyone?” They replied: “We are missing so-and-so and so-and-so.” He asked: “Look again. Are you missing anyone?” They replied: “No.” He said: “But I am missing Julaybīb.” He then said: “Search for him among the slain.” They searched and found him beside seven enemy soldiers whom he had killed before they killed him.\nThey said: “O Messenger of Allah, here he is beside seven men whom he killed before they killed him.” The Prophet ﷺ came to him, stood over him, and said: “He killed seven, and then they killed him. He is from me, and I am from him. He is from me, and I am from him.” He repeated this two or three times. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then carried him upon his forearms. Julaybīb had no bier except the forearms of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. A grave was dug for him, and he was placed within it. The narrator did not mention that he was washed. Thābit said: There was no previously unmarried or widowed woman among the Anṣār who possessed greater wealth than she did. Isḥāq ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah informed Thābit and asked: “Do you know what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for her?” He said: “O Allah, pour goodness upon her in abundance, and do not make her life one of hardship and toil.” There was no woman among the Anṣār who possessed greater wealth than she did."],[336,"Anas narrated: There was a man among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named Julaybīb, whose appearance was considered unattractive. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ proposed marriage to him. Julaybīb said: “Then you will find me unwanted.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Yet before Allah, you are not unwanted.”"],[337,"Anas narrated: The Prophet ﷺ proposed on behalf of Julaybīb to the father of a woman from the Anṣār. Her father said: “I must first seek her mother’s approval.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “That is appropriate.” The man went to his wife and informed her. She said: “No, by Allah! Could the Messenger of Allah ﷺ find no one but Julaybīb, when we have already refused her to so-and-so and so-and-so?” The young woman was listening from behind her curtain. When her father prepared to leave and inform the Prophet ﷺ of their refusal, she said: “Do you intend to reject the decision of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? Since he has approved him for you, then marry me to him.” It was as though she removed all hesitation from her parents, and they said: “You have spoken the truth.” Her father went to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “Since you have approved him, we too approve him.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “I have indeed approved him.” He then married her to Julaybīb. The people of Madinah were later summoned to battle, and Julaybīb rode out with them. He was found slain, with a number of polytheists around him whom he had killed. Anas said: “I later saw his wife, and she was among the wealthiest previously married women in Madinah.”"],[338,"Abū Qatādah narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed us and said: “You will travel during this evening and throughout the night, and, if Allah wills, you will reach water tomorrow.” The people set out, and no one turned aside to wait for another. Abū Qatādah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ continued traveling until the night became very dark, and I was beside him. He became drowsy and leaned to one side of his riding animal. I came close and supported him without waking him until he sat upright again. He then continued traveling until the night became even darker. Again he leaned from his riding animal, so I supported him without waking him until he became upright. He continued onward until the final part of the night, when he leaned much more severely than on the previous two occasions and nearly fell. I came to him and supported him. He raised his head and asked: “Who is this?” I replied: “Abū Qatādah.” He asked: “How long have you been traveling beside me like this?” I replied: “I have remained beside you throughout the night.” He said: “May Allah protect you because of the way you protected His Prophet.” The narration continues."],[339,"Ḥumayd narrated: I heard Anas say: Ḥārithah was killed on the Day of Badr while he was still a young boy. His mother came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah, you know the place Ḥārithah held in my heart. If he is in Paradise, I will remain patient and seek reward from Allah. But if it is otherwise, then you will see what I will do.” He said: “Woe to you! Have you lost your mind? Is there only one garden? There are many gardens, and he is in the Garden of al-Firdaws.”"],[340,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “While I was asleep and saw myself in Paradise, I heard the voice of a reciter. I asked: ‘Who is this?’ They replied: ‘This is Ḥārithah ibn al-Nuʿmān.’ Such is the reward of dutifulness. Such is the reward of dutifulness. Such is the reward of dutifulness.” He was among the most dutiful of people."],[341,"Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf narrated from the man who passed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was speaking privately with Jibrīl (Gabriel), peace be upon him. Abū Salamah said that the man refrained from approaching the Messenger of Allah ﷺ because he feared overhearing their private conversation. The following morning, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him: “What prevented you from greeting me when you passed by me last night?” The man replied: “I saw you conversing privately with another man, and I feared that you might dislike my approaching the two of you.” The Prophet ﷺ asked: “Do you know who that man was?” He replied: “No.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “That was Jibrīl. Had you greeted us, he would have returned your greeting.” The narrator added: I heard from someone other than Abū Salamah that the man was Ḥārithah ibn al-Nuʿmān."],[342,"ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Rāfiʿ—the scribe of ʿAlī—narrated: I heard ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me, al-Zubayr, and al-Miqdād, saying: “Proceed until you reach Rawḍat Khākh. There you will find a woman carrying a letter. Take it from her.” We rode swiftly until we reached the place. There we found the woman, and we said: “Bring out the letter.” She replied: “I do not have any letter.” We said: “Either you produce the letter, or we will take off your clothes.” She then removed it from the braids of her hair. We brought it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. It was a letter from Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah addressed to some of the polytheists in Makkah, informing them of part of the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ plans. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “O Ḥāṭib, what is this?” Ḥāṭib replied: “O Messenger of Allah, do not be hasty in judging me. I was a man affiliated with Quraysh as an ally, but I was not one of them by lineage. The Emigrants who are with you have relatives in Makkah who protect their families and property. Since I did not possess such family ties, I wanted to establish a favor with them so they would protect my relatives. I did not do this out of abandoning my religion, nor because I was pleased with disbelief after embracing Islam.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “He has spoken the truth.” ʿUmar said: “O Messenger of Allah, allow me to strike the neck of this hypocrite.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “He participated in Badr. And what makes you know? Perhaps Allah looked upon those who fought at Badr and said: ‘Do whatever you wish, for I have already forgiven you.’” Allah then revealed the opening verse of Sūrat al-Mumtaḥanah: “O you who believe, do not take My enemies and your enemies as allies, showing them affection while they have disbelieved in the truth that has come to you. They have driven out the Messenger and yourselves because you believe in Allah, your Lord. If you have gone forth striving in My cause and seeking My pleasure, do not secretly show them affection. I know best what you conceal and what you reveal. Whoever among you does that has certainly strayed from the straight path.” (Qur’an 60:1)"],[343,"Jābir narrated: A servant belonging to Ḥāṭib came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ complaining about him and said: “O Messenger of Allah, Ḥāṭib will certainly enter the Fire.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “You have spoken falsely. He will not enter it, for he participated in the Battle of Badr and the Treaty of al-Ḥudaybiyah.”"],[344,"Umm Mubashshir narrated: A servant of Ḥāṭib said: “By Allah, Ḥāṭib will never enter Paradise.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “You have spoken falsely. He participated in the Battle of Badr and the Treaty of al-Ḥudaybiyah.”"],[345,"ʿĀ’ishah narrated: On the Day of Uḥud, the polytheists suffered a decisive defeat. Then Satan cried out: “O servants of Allah, those behind you!” The Muslims at the front turned back toward those behind them, and the rear ranks began fighting. Ḥudhayfah looked and saw that his own father was among them, so he cried out: “O servants of Allah! My father! My father!” ʿĀ’ishah said: By Allah, they did not stop until they had killed him. Ḥudhayfah then said: “May Allah forgive you.” Hishām narrated from his father, ʿUrwah: By Allah, because of those words, goodness continued to remain with Ḥudhayfah until he met Allah, the Mighty and Majestic."],[346,"Yazīd ibn Sharīk al-Taymī narrated: We were sitting with Ḥudhayfah when a man said: “Had I lived during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, I would have fought alongside him and distinguished myself.” Ḥudhayfah replied: “Would you really have done that? I was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the night of the Battle of al-Aḥzāb (the Confederates). A fierce wind and bitter cold overtook us. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘Is there anyone who will bring me news of the enemy? Allah will place him with me on the Day of Resurrection.’ We remained silent, and none of us answered him. He asked a second time: ‘Is there anyone who will bring us news of the enemy? Allah will place him with me on the Day of Resurrection.’ Again we remained silent, and no one answered. He asked a third time: ‘Is there anyone who will bring us news of the enemy? Allah will place him with me on the Day of Resurrection.’ Once more we remained silent. Then he said: ‘Stand up, O Ḥudhayfah, and bring us news of the enemy.’” When he called me by name, I had no choice but to stand. He said: “Go and bring me news of the enemy, but do not alarm them against me.” As I departed from him, it was as though I were walking in a warm bath, until I reached the enemy. There I saw Abū Sufyān warming his back by the fire. I placed an arrow in the middle of my bow and was about to shoot him, but I remembered the words of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: “Do not alarm them against me.” Had I released the arrow, I would certainly have struck him. I returned, still feeling as though I were walking in a warm bath. When I reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him of the enemy’s situation, the warmth left me and I began shivering from the cold. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrapped me in the extra portion of a cloak that he had been praying in. I remained asleep until morning. When morning came, he said: “Get up, O sleepy one.”"],[347,"Ḥudhayfah narrated: I came to the Prophet ﷺ and prayed the Maghrib prayer with him. He continued praying until he performed the ʿIshāʾ prayer. Then he left, and I followed him. When he heard my footsteps, he asked: “Who is this? Is it Ḥudhayfah?” I replied: “Yes.” He said: “What brings you here? May Allah forgive you and your mother.” Then he said: “This is an angel who had never descended to the earth before this night. He sought permission from his Lord to greet me and to give me the glad tidings that Fāṭimah is the leader of the women of Paradise, and that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the leaders of the young men of Paradise.”"],[348,"Ibn ʿUmar narrated: I was with the Prophet ﷺ when Ḥarmalah ibn Zayd came and sat before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: “O Messenger of Allah, faith is here,” and he pointed to his tongue. “And hypocrisy is here,” and he pointed to his chest. “And he remembers Allah only a little.” The Prophet ﷺ remained silent. Ḥarmalah repeated what he had said, and the Prophet ﷺ again remained silent. Then the Prophet ﷺ took hold of the tip of Ḥarmalah’s tongue and supplicated: “O Allah, grant him a truthful tongue, a grateful heart, provide him with love for me and love for those who love me, and direct all of his affairs toward goodness.” Ḥarmalah then said: “O Messenger of Allah, I have companions who are hypocrites, and I used to be one of their leaders. Shall I identify them for you?” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “No. Whoever comes to us as you have come, we seek Allah’s forgiveness for him just as we have sought forgiveness for you. Whoever persists in his sin, Allah is more deserving to deal with him. Do not tear away the veil that covers anyone.”"],[349,"Abū Hurayrah narrated: ʿUmar once passed by Ḥassān while he was reciting poetry in the mosque. ʿUmar looked at him disapprovingly. Ḥassān said: “I used to recite poetry here while one who was better than you was present.” He then turned to Abū Hurayrah and said: “I ask you by Allah, did you hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘Answer on my behalf. O Allah, support him with the Holy Spirit (Jibrīl).’?” Abū Hurayrah replied: “Yes, by Allah.”"],[350,"Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf narrated: I heard Ḥassān ibn Thābit al-Anṣārī asking Abū Hurayrah to testify: “I ask you by Allah, did you hear the Prophet ﷺ say: ‘O Ḥassān, defend the Messenger of Allah. O Allah, support him with the Holy Spirit (Jibrīl).’?” Abū Hurayrah replied: “Yes.”"],[351,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: Ḥassān sought the Prophet’s ﷺ permission to satirize the polytheists. The Prophet ﷺ asked: “What about my lineage?” Ḥassān replied: “I will separate you from them just as a hair is drawn out of dough.” And from his father, who said: I began speaking against Ḥassān in the presence of ʿĀ’ishah, but she said: “Do not speak against him, for he used to defend the Prophet ﷺ.”"],[352,"Masrūq narrated: I entered upon ʿĀ’ishah while Ḥassān ibn Thābit was with her, reciting poetry that included the lines: A chaste and dignified woman, never suspected of wrongdoing, Who goes hungry rather than consume the flesh of the heedless. ʿĀ’ishah said to him: “But you are not like that.” Masrūq said: I asked her: “Why do you allow him to enter upon you when Allah has said: ‘And the one who took upon himself the greater share of it—for him is a tremendous punishment.’ (Qur’an 24:11)?” She replied: “What punishment is greater than blindness? Besides, he used to defend—or satirize in defense of—the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”"],[353,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib narrated: The Prophet ﷺ said to Ḥassān: “Satirize them—or wage verbal battle against them—and Jibrīl (Gabriel) is with you.”"],[354,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Satirize Quraysh, for it is more severe upon them than being showered with arrows.” He sent for Ibn Rawāḥah and said: “Satirize them.” He satirized them, but it did not satisfy the intended purpose. So he sent for Kaʿb ibn Mālik, and then he sent for Ḥassān ibn Thābit. When Ḥassān entered, he said: “It is time for you to send for this lion striking with his tail.” Then he thrust out his tongue and began moving it, and said: “By the One who sent you with the truth, I shall tear them with my tongue just as leather is torn.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Do not be hasty. Abū Bakr is the most knowledgeable of Quraysh concerning their genealogies, and I share lineage with them. Let him distinguish my lineage for you.” Ḥassān went to Abū Bakr, then returned and said: “O Messenger of Allah, he has distinguished your lineage for me. By the One who sent you with the truth, I will separate you from them just as a hair is drawn from dough.” ʿĀ’ishah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say to Ḥassān: “The Holy Spirit will continue to support you so long as you defend Allah and His Messenger.” And she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “Ḥassān satirized them, thereby bringing relief and satisfying the believers.” Ḥassān said: You satirized Muḥammad, so I answered in his defense,  And with Allah lies the reward for that. You satirized Muḥammad, righteous and upright,  The Messenger of Allah, whose character is faithfulness. Indeed, my father, his father, and my honor  Are a shield protecting Muḥammad’s honor from you. May my daughter lose me if you do not see our horses  Raising clouds of dust from the two sides of Kadāʾ. They race upward, straining against their reins,  While thirsting spears rest upon their shoulders. Our horses continue charging forward,  While women strike their faces with their head-coverings. If you turn away from us, we shall perform ʿUmrah,  And victory will come and the veil will be removed. Otherwise, then endure the blows of a day  On which Allah will grant honor to whomever He wills. Allah has said: “I have sent a servant  Who speaks the truth, in which there is no obscurity.”\nAnd Allah has said: “I have prepared an army—  They are the Anṣār, whose readiness is for battle.” Every day we have with Maʿadd  Either an exchange of insults, fighting, or satire. The one among you who satirizes the Messenger of Allah, and the one who praises him and supports him, are the same. And Jibrīl, the messenger of Allah among us,  Is the Holy Spirit; none can equal him."],[355,"Al-Ḥasan (al-Baṣrī) narrated: By Allah, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī advanced toward Muʿāwiyah with armies like mountains. ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ said: “I see armies that will not retreat until they have destroyed one another.” Muʿāwiyah—who, by Allah, was the better of the two men in this matter—said: “O ʿAmr, if these kill those, and those kill these, who will look after the affairs of the people? Who will care for their women? Who will care for their properties?” He then sent two men from Quraysh, of Banū ʿAbd Shams—ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Samurah and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿĀmir ibn Kurayz—and instructed them: “Go to this man, present my offer to him, negotiate with him, and seek reconciliation.” They came to al-Ḥasan, entered upon him, spoke with him, and presented Muʿāwiyah’s proposal. Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī said: “We, the family of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, have already received wealth from this treasury, and this nation has shed far too much of its own blood.” They replied: “He is offering you such-and-such and is asking you to accept peace.” Al-Ḥasan asked: “Will you guarantee that for me?” They replied: “We guarantee it.” Whatever he requested from them, they answered: “We guarantee it.” So al-Ḥasan concluded a peace treaty with Muʿāwiyah. Al-Ḥasan then said: I heard Abū Bakrah say: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon the pulpit while al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī was beside him. The Prophet ﷺ would look at the people, then at al-Ḥasan, and say: “This son of mine is a leader, and perhaps Allah will bring about reconciliation through him between two great groups of the Muslims.” Abū ʿAbdullāh (al-Bukhārī) said: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdullāh told me: “The only established narration proving that al-Ḥasan heard directly from Abū Bakrah is this hadith.”"],[356,"Anas narrated: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivering a sermon while al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī was sitting upon his thigh. He said: “I truly hope that this son of mine will be a leader, and I hope that Allah will bring about reconciliation through him between two groups from my nation.”"],[357,"Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī narrated: We were with Abū Hurayrah when al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī came and greeted us with salām. The people returned his greeting, and he continued on his way, while Abū Hurayrah had not realized who it was. Someone said to him: “That was al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī giving salām.” Abū Hurayrah hurried after him and said: “And peace be upon you, my master.” Someone asked him: “You call him ‘my master’?” He replied: “I bear witness that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘Indeed, he is a leader (sayyid).’”"],[358,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib narrated: I saw the Prophet ﷺ carrying al-Ḥasan upon his shoulder, saying: “O Allah, I love him, so love him.”"],[359,"Abū Hurayrah narrated: I went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during part of the day. Neither did he speak to me nor did I speak to him, until we reached the marketplace of Banū Qaynuqāʿ. He then returned and came to the tent of Fāṭimah and asked: “Is the little one here? Is the little one here?” He meant al-Ḥasan. We thought that his mother was keeping him briefly in order to wash him and adorn him with a necklace. Before long, al-Ḥasan came running. The Prophet ﷺ and al-Ḥasan embraced one another. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Allah, I love him, so love him, and love whoever loves him.”"],[360,"Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated that the Prophet ﷺ would take him together with al-Ḥasan and say: “O Allah, love them both, for indeed I love them both.”"],[361,"Zuhayr ibn al-Aqmar narrated: While al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī was delivering a sermon after the martyrdom of ʿAlī, a tall, dark-complexioned man from the tribe of Azd stood up and said: “I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ holding al-Ḥasan in his lap and saying: ‘Whoever loves me should love him. Let those who are present convey this to those who are absent.’” He then added: “Had it not been for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ charging us to convey it, I would not have related it to you.”"],[362,"Muʿāwiyah narrated: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ suck the tongue—or, as he said, the lips—of al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī. Muʿāwiyah added: “A tongue or lips that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has sucked will never be punished.”"],[363,"Anas narrated: No one resembled the Prophet ﷺ more than al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī."],[364,"Abū Juḥayfah narrated: I saw the Prophet ﷺ, and al-Ḥasan closely resembled him."],[365,"ʿUqbah ibn al-Ḥārith narrated: I saw Abū Bakr carrying al-Ḥasan while saying: “May my father be sacrificed for him! He resembles the Prophet ﷺ and does not resemble ʿAlī.” ʿAlī was present, smiling. In another wording recorded by al-Bukhārī: After leading the ʿAṣr prayer, Abū Bakr went out walking. He saw al-Ḥasan playing with the children, so he lifted him onto his shoulders."],[366,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated: I proposed marriage to Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Prophet ﷺ. ʿAlī sold a coat of mail that he owned, along with some of his belongings, and the total came to four hundred and eighty dirhams. The Prophet ﷺ instructed that two-thirds of the money be spent on perfume and one-third on clothing. He then spat into a vessel of water and instructed them to bathe with it. He also instructed Fāṭimah not to nurse her child before bringing him to him. She nursed al-Ḥusayn before bringing him. As for al-Ḥasan, the Prophet ﷺ did something with his mouth that we do not know exactly what it was. As a result, al-Ḥasan became the more knowledgeable of the two brothers."],[367,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivering a sermon from the pulpit. Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī came out wearing a garment around his neck that trailed behind him. He stumbled over it and fell on his face. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ immediately came down from the pulpit to go to him. When the people saw this, they picked up the child and brought him to the Prophet ﷺ. He carried him and said: “May Allah defeat Satan. Indeed, children are a trial. By Allah, I was not even aware that I had come down from the pulpit until he was brought to me.”"],[368,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever would be pleased to look at a man from the people of Paradise should look at al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī.” Jābir said: “I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say this.”"],[369,"Yaʿlā ibn Murrah narrated: We went out with the Prophet ﷺ to a meal to which he had been invited. Along the way, al-Ḥusayn was playing in the street. The Prophet ﷺ went ahead of the people and stretched out his arms toward him. The child ran this way and that, playfully, while the Prophet ﷺ smiled and played with him until he caught him. He placed one hand beneath al-Ḥusayn’s chin and the other behind his head, then kissed him and said: “Ḥusayn is from me, and I am from Ḥusayn. May Allah love whoever loves Ḥusayn. Ḥusayn is a branch among the great branches.”"],[370,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: The head of al-Ḥusayn, peace be upon him, was brought to ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyād and placed in a basin. He began striking it with a stick and made a remark about al-Ḥusayn’s appearance. Anas said: “He was the one who most closely resembled the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and his hair had been dyed with wasmah (a dark herbal dye).”"],[371,"ʿAbdullāh al-Bahī, the freedman of al-Zubayr, narrated: I was in the mosque while Marwān was delivering a sermon. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr said: “By Allah, he did not appoint one of his own family as successor.” Marwān replied: “You are the one concerning whom the verse was revealed: ʿBut the one who says to his parents, “Fie upon you both…”’ (Qur’an 46:17).” ʿAbd al-Raḥmān responded: “You have lied. Rather, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed your father.”"],[372,"Al-Shaʿbī narrated: I heard ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr, while leaning against the Kaʿbah, say: “By the Lord of this Kaʿbah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed so-and-so and those born from his lineage.” In another narration: “Allah cursed al-Ḥakam and his offspring upon the tongue of His Prophet ﷺ.”"],[373,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr narrated: We were sitting with the Prophet ﷺ while ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ had gone to put on his clothes so that he could join us. While we were sitting there, the Prophet ﷺ said: “A cursed man is about to enter upon you.” I remained anxious, watching everyone who entered and left, until that man entered—the narrator explained that it was al-Ḥakam."],[374,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The best of the martyrs in the sight of Allah is Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.”"],[375,"Anas narrated: The Prophet ﷺ informed the people of the deaths of Zayd, Jaʿfar, and Ibn Rawāḥah before news of their martyrdom had reached them. He said: “Zayd took the banner and was martyred. Then Jaʿfar took it and was martyred. Then Ibn Rawāḥah took it and was martyred.” His eyes were shedding tears. Then he said: “Then it was taken up by one of the swords of Allah, and Allah granted victory through him.”"],[376,"Abū Qatādah, the cavalryman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, narrated that during the account of the Battle of Muʾtah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Then Khālid ibn al-Walīd took up the banner, although he had not been appointed as one of the commanders; he assumed command himself.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then raised his two fingers and supplicated: “O Allah, he is one of Your swords, so grant him victory.”"],[377,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ dispatched an army and appointed Zayd ibn Ḥārithah as its commander, saying: “If Zayd is killed—or martyred—then your commander will be Jaʿfar. If Jaʿfar is killed—or martyred—then your commander will be ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah.” When they met the enemy, Zayd carried the banner and fought until he was martyred. Then Jaʿfar took the banner and fought until he was martyred. Then ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah took it and fought until he was martyred. After that, Khālid ibn al-Walīd took the banner, and Allah granted victory through him. When news reached the Prophet ﷺ, he came out to the people, praised Allah, and said: “Your brothers met the enemy. Zayd took the banner and fought until he was martyred. Then Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib took it and fought until he was martyred. Then ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah took it and fought until he was martyred. Then the banner was taken by one of the swords of Allah—Khālid ibn al-Walīd—and Allah granted victory through him.”"],[378,"Abū al-ʿAjfāʾ narrated: It was said to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb: “O Commander of the Believers, why do you not appoint a successor?” ʿUmar replied: “Had Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ still been alive, I would have appointed him. Then, when I stood before my Lord and He asked me, ‘Why did you appoint him over the nation of Muḥammad?’ I would have replied: ʿI heard Your servant and beloved say: “Every nation has a trustworthy guardian, and the trustworthy guardian of this nation is Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ.”’ He continued: “And if Khālid ibn al-Walīd had still been alive, I would have appointed him. Then, when I stood before my Lord and He asked me, ‘Whom did you appoint over the nation of Muḥammad?’ I would have replied: ʿI heard Your servant and beloved say to Khālid: “He is one of the swords of Allah, unsheathed by Allah against the polytheists.”’”"],[379,"Abū Hurayrah narrated: We stopped at a campsite with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. As people passed by, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would ask: “Who is this, O Abū Hurayrah?” I would reply: “So-and-so.” Sometimes he would say: “What an excellent servant of Allah he is.” At other times he would ask about someone else, and after I identified him, he would say: “What an evil servant of Allah he is.” Eventually Khālid ibn al-Walīd passed by. The Prophet ﷺ asked: “Who is this?” I replied: “This is Khālid ibn al-Walīd.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “What an excellent servant of Allah Khālid ibn al-Walīd is—a sword from among the swords of Allah.”"],[380,"Abū al-Dardāʾ narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Khuraym al-Asadī is an excellent man, were it not for the length of his hair and the fact that he lets his garment hang below the proper length.” When this reached Khuraym, he immediately took a razor and cut his hair until it reached the middle of his ears. He also raised his lower garment so that it reached the middle of his shins. The narrator said: My father told me: Later, I entered upon Muʿāwiyah and saw an elderly man with his hair ending above his ears and his garment reaching the middle of his shins.\nI asked who he was, and they replied: “This is Khuraym al-Asadī.”"],[381,"Zayd ibn Thābit narrated: When I was copying the written sheets of the Qur’an into the compiled muṣḥafs, I found that one verse from Sūrat al-Aḥzāb was missing. I had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recite it, but I did not find it with anyone except Khuzaymah ibn Thābit al-Anṣārī—the Companion whose testimony the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had declared to be equal to the testimony of two men. The verse was: “Among the believers are men who have been true to what they pledged to Allah.” (Qur’an 33:23)"],[382,"ʿUmārah ibn Khuzaymah narrated from his uncle, who was one of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ: The Prophet ﷺ purchased a horse from a Bedouin and asked him to accompany him so that he could pay its price. The Prophet ﷺ walked ahead at a quicker pace, while the Bedouin lagged behind. Along the way, people approached the Bedouin and began offering to buy the horse, unaware that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had already purchased it. The Bedouin called out to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: “If you are buying this horse, then buy it. Otherwise I will sell it.” When the Prophet ﷺ heard him, he said: “Have you not already sold it to me?” The Bedouin replied: “No, by Allah, I have not sold it to you.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Rather, you have sold it to me.” The Bedouin then repeatedly demanded: “Bring a witness.” Khuzaymah ibn Thābit stepped forward and said: “I testify that you sold it to him.” The Prophet ﷺ turned to Khuzaymah and asked: “On what basis do you testify, when you were not present?” Khuzaymah replied: “Because I believe you, O Messenger of Allah.” Because of this, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ accepted the testimony of Khuzaymah as being equal to the testimony of two men."],[383,"Khuzaymah ibn Thābit narrated: He passed by the Prophet ﷺ after he had purchased a horse from a Bedouin. The Bedouin denied making the sale and said: “I never sold it to you.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “You did indeed sell it to me.” Khuzaymah ibn Thābit happened to pass by and heard their discussion. He said: “I testify that you sold it to him.” The Prophet ﷺ asked him: “How do you know that, when you were not present with us?” Khuzaymah answered: “Because we have believed and testified to something far greater than this.” Accordingly, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ accepted Khuzaymah’s testimony as equal to the testimony of two men, and this remained his distinction until he passed away."],[384,"Jābir narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The Prophets were shown to me. Mūsā (Moses) was a strongly built man, resembling the men of Shanūʾah. I saw ʿĪsā ibn Maryam (Jesus, son of Mary), peace be upon them both, and the person who most closely resembled him was ʿUrwah ibn Masʿūd. I also saw Ibrāhīm (Abraham), peace be upon him, and the one who most closely resembled him was your companion”—meaning himself ﷺ—“and I saw Jibrīl (Gabriel), peace be upon him, and the one who most closely resembled him was Diḥyah.” In the narration of Ibn Rumḥ, it reads: “Diḥyah ibn Khalīfah.”"],[385,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated: Jibrīl (Gabriel), peace be upon him, would come to the Prophet ﷺ in the appearance of Diḥyah."],[386,"The wife of Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj narrated: Rāfiʿ was struck by an arrow while fighting alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the Day of Uḥud—or the Day of Khaybar. The arrow lodged in the upper part of his chest. He came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah, remove the arrow.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “O Rāfiʿ, if you wish, I will remove both the arrow and the embedded arrowhead. Or, if you wish, I will remove only the shaft of the arrow, leave the arrowhead where it is, and testify on your behalf on the Day of Resurrection that you are a martyr.” Rāfiʿ said: “O Messenger of Allah, remove the arrow, leave the arrowhead in place, and testify for me on the Day of Resurrection that I am a martyr.” So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ removed the shaft of the arrow while leaving the arrowhead embedded. Another narration adds: He lived with it until the caliphate of Muʿāwiyah. Then the wound suddenly became inflamed again, and he died after the ʿAṣr prayer."],[387,"Rabīʿah al-Aslamī narrated: I used to serve the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. One day he said to me, “O Rabīʿah, will you not get married?” I replied, “By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, I do not wish to marry. I have nothing with which I can support a wife, and I do not want anything to distract me from serving you.” The Prophet ﷺ turned away from me. After some time, he asked me again, “O Rabīʿah, will you not get married?” I gave him the same answer: “I do not wish to marry. I have nothing by which I can support a wife, and I do not want anything to distract me from you.” Again, he turned away from me. Then I reflected to myself and said, “By Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ knows better than I do what is best for me in this world and the Hereafter. If he asks me again to marry, I will certainly say, ‘Yes, O Messenger of Allah. Command me as you wish.’” So he asked me a third time, “O Rabīʿah, will you not get married?” I answered, “Yes. Command me, O Messenger of Allah.” He said, “Go to such-and-such a family from among the Anṣār and tell them: ‘The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has sent me to you, commanding you to marry me to such-and-such a woman from among you.’” So I went to them and conveyed the Prophet’s message. They replied, “Welcome to the Messenger of Allah, and welcome to the messenger of the Messenger of Allah. By Allah, the messenger of the Messenger of Allah will not return without having his request fulfilled.” They married her to me, treated me with great kindness and generosity, and did not ask me for any proof. Rabīʿah said: I returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ feeling saddened. He asked me, “What is the matter with you, O Rabīʿah?” I replied, “O Messenger of Allah, I went to a people of great generosity. They married me to their daughter, honored me, and treated me kindly. They did not ask me for any proof, but I do not possess anything to give as her bridal gift (mahr).” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said, “O Buraydah al-Aslamī, collect for him the weight of a date-stone in gold.” So they gathered for me gold equal in weight to a date-stone. I took what they had collected and brought it to the Prophet ﷺ. He said, “Take this to them and say: ‘This is her bridal gift.’” So I went to them and said, “This is her bridal gift.” They accepted it with pleasure and said, “It is a generous and excellent gift.” Rabīʿah continued: Then I returned to the Prophet ﷺ once again feeling distressed. He asked, “O Rabīʿah, why are you still saddened?” I replied, “O Messenger of Allah, I have never seen a people more generous than them. They accepted what I brought them graciously, treated me with the utmost kindness, and said, ‘It is a generous and excellent gift.’ But I have nothing with which to prepare a wedding feast.” The Prophet ﷺ then said, “O Buraydah, collect a sheep for him.” Rabīʿah said: So they gathered for me a large, fat ram. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me, “Go to ʿĀ’ishah and tell her to send the basket that contains the food.” I went to her and conveyed the Prophet’s instruction. She said, “This is the basket. It contains nine sāʿ of barley. By Allah, this is the only food we have left this morning. Take it.” So I took it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him of what ʿĀ’ishah had said. He replied, “Take this to them and tell them to prepare your wedding meal with it.” So I brought them the ram and the basket. They said, “As for the barley, we will bake it into bread, and as for the ram, we will slaughter it.” Rabīʿah said: We slaughtered it, skinned it, and cooked it. By the next morning we had bread and meat. Then I held the wedding feast (walīmah) and invited the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Rabīʿah continued: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted me a piece of land, and Abū Bakr also granted me a piece of land. As worldly possessions became more plentiful, a dispute arose between us regarding the boundary of a single date-palm tree. I said, “It lies within my boundary,” while Abū Bakr said, “Rather, it lies within my boundary.”\nSome words passed between us. During the exchange, Abū Bakr said something that he immediately disliked and regretted. He then said to me, “O Rabīʿah, say the same words back to me so that we will be even.” I replied, “I will not do that.” Abū Bakr said, “Either you will say it back to me, or I will take the matter to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” I answered, “I will not do so.” He then left the land and went to the Prophet ﷺ, and I followed after him. Some men from the tribe of Aslam came to me and said, “May Allah have mercy on Abū Bakr! Why is he taking a complaint against you to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he was the one who spoke to you as he did?” I replied, “Do you know who this is? This is Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq. He is the second of the two in the cave, and he is one of the most honored elders among the Muslims. Beware that he should turn around and see you supporting me against him. If he becomes angry, he may go to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who would become angry because of his anger. Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, would become angry because of their anger, and Rabīʿah would be ruined.” They asked, “Then what do you command us to do?” I said, “Go back.” Abū Bakr went on to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I followed him alone until he reached the Prophet ﷺ. Abū Bakr related the entire incident exactly as it had happened. The Prophet ﷺ then looked at me and said, “O Rabīʿah, what has happened between you and al-Ṣiddīq?” I replied, “O Messenger of Allah, this and this happened. He spoke a word to me which he regretted, and then asked me to respond with the same words so that we would be even, but I refused.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Indeed. Do not return the insult to him. Instead, say: ‘May Allah forgive you, O Abū Bakr.’” So I said, “May Allah forgive you, O Abū Bakr.” Al-Ḥasan said: Abū Bakr turned away weeping."],[388,"Narrated from Anas: Anas reported that there was a man from the desert named Zāhir. He used to bring gifts from the desert to the Prophet ﷺ, and whenever he wished to depart, the Prophet ﷺ would provide him with provisions for his journey. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Indeed, Zāhir is our representative from the desert, and we are his people in the town.” The Prophet ﷺ loved him, although he was not physically handsome. One day, while Zāhir was selling his goods in the marketplace, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came up behind him and embraced him. Zāhir could not see who it was and said, “Let me go! Who is this?” When he turned and recognized the Prophet ﷺ, he began pressing his back against the Prophet’s chest as closely as he could out of love. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then playfully called out: “Who will buy this servant?” Zāhir replied: “O Messenger of Allah, then by Allah you will find me to be of little value.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “But in the sight of Allah you are not of little value.” Or he said: “Rather, in the sight of Allah you are precious.”"],[389,"Narrated from Zāhir ibn Ḥarām: Zāhir ibn Ḥarām narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him in the marketplace of Madinah selling some merchandise. The Prophet ﷺ had not come to him at that time, so he embraced him from behind by his shoulders. Zāhir turned and saw that it was the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he kissed his hand. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Who will buy this servant?” Zāhir replied: “O Messenger of Allah, then you will find me to be of little worth.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Rather, with Allah you are profitable.” Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Every town has its people of the desert, and the people of the desert for the family of Muhammad are Zāhir ibn Ḥarām.”"],[390,"Zayd ibn Arqam reported: I was taking part in a military expedition when I heard ʿAbd Allah ibn Ubayy say: “Do not spend anything upon those who are with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until they disperse from around him. And when we return to Madinah, the more honorable will surely expel the more contemptible from it.” I mentioned this to my uncle—or to ʿUmar—and he reported it to the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ called me, and I related to him what I had heard. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then summoned ʿAbd Allah ibn Ubayy and his companions. They swore that they had not said it, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ accepted their statement and regarded my report as untrue. I was overcome by distress unlike anything I had ever experienced before. I remained sitting at home, and my uncle said to me: “What did you gain except that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ regarded your statement as false and became displeased with you?” Then Allah Most High revealed: “When the hypocrites come to you…” [Al-Munāfiqūn 63:1] The Prophet ﷺ sent for me, recited the sūrah, and said: “Indeed, Allah has confirmed that you spoke the truth, O Zayd.”"],[391,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: I grieved deeply over those who were killed at al-Ḥarrah. When Zayd ibn Arqam learned of the severity of my grief, he wrote to me, mentioning that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “O Allah, forgive the Anṣār and the children of the Anṣār.” Ibn al-Faḍl was uncertain whether the narration also included: “And the children of the children of the Anṣār.” One of those who was present with Anas asked him about Zayd ibn Arqam, and Anas said: “He is the one concerning whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘This is the one whose hearing Allah vindicated.’”"],[392,"Zayd ibn Arqam reported: I suffered from inflammation of the eyes, so the Prophet ﷺ visited me. When I recovered, I went out. Zayd said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then asked me: “Tell me, had your eyes remained in the condition they were in, what would you have done?” I replied: “Had my eyes remained as they were, I would have remained patient and sought the reward from Allah.” He said: “Had your eyes remained in that condition, and you then remained patient and sought Allah’s reward, you would have met Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, without any sin upon you.” Ismāʿīl narrated the wording: “Then, had you remained patient and sought Allah’s reward, Allah Most High would have guaranteed Paradise for you.”"],[393,"ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿUmar reported: The Prophet ﷺ dispatched an expedition and appointed Usāmah ibn Zayd as its commander. Some people criticized his appointment, so the Prophet ﷺ said: “If you criticize his leadership, you had previously criticized the leadership of his father. By Allah, his father was certainly worthy of command, and he was among the people most beloved to me. And this man is among the people most beloved to me after him.”"],[394,"ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿUmar reported that he used to say: We did not call Zayd ibn Ḥārithah by any name other than Zayd ibn Muḥammad until the following was revealed in the Qur’an: “Call them by the names of their fathers; that is more just in the sight of Allah.” [Al-Aḥzāb 33:5]"],[395,"Jabalah ibn Ḥārithah, the brother of Zayd, reported: I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah, send my brother Zayd with me.” He replied: “He is here. Should he choose to leave with you, I will not prevent him.” Zayd said: “O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, I would never choose anyone over you.” Jabalah said: I then realized that my brother’s judgment was better than my own."],[396,"Buraydah ibn al-Ḥuṣayb reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Last night I entered Paradise, and there I saw a young woman. I asked her, ‘To whom do you belong?’ She replied, ‘To Zayd ibn Ḥārithah.’” The Prophet ﷺ then gave Zayd the glad tidings of her when morning came."],[397,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ reported: I fought alongside the Prophet ﷺ in seven military expeditions, and Zayd ibn Ḥārithah was appointed as our commander."],[398,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never dispatched Zayd ibn Ḥārithah with an army except that he appointed him as its commander. Had Zayd lived after him, he would have appointed him as his successor."],[399,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: One night during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, I returned late after the ʿIshāʾ prayer. He asked me, “Where have you been?” I replied, “I was listening to the recitation of one of your Companions. I have never heard anyone with a recitation or voice like his.” She said: The Prophet ﷺ stood up, and I stood with him until he listened to the man’s recitation. Then he turned to me and said, “This is Sālim, the freed slave of Abū Ḥudhayfah. All praise belongs to Allah, who has placed among my Ummah someone like this.”"],[400,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar reported: When the first Muhājirūn arrived at al-ʿUṣbah—a place near Qubāʾ—before the arrival of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Sālim, the freed slave of Abū Ḥudhayfah, used to lead them in prayer because he was the one among them who knew the Qur’an best."],[401,"Al-Sā’ib ibn Yazīd reported: My maternal aunt took me to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “O Messenger of Allah, my nephew is ill.” The Prophet ﷺ wiped my head, supplicated that I be blessed, then performed ablution. I drank from the water of his ablution. Afterwards I stood behind him and saw the Seal of Prophethood between his shoulders, resembling the button of a bridal canopy."],[402,"Al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah reported: Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah said, “If I were to see a man with my wife, I would strike him with the edge of the sword, not its flat side.” When this reached the Prophet ﷺ, he said: “Are you amazed at Saʿd’s sense of protective jealousy? I possess more protective jealousy than he does, and Allah possesses more protective jealousy than I do.”"],[403,"Abū Hurayrah reported: Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah said, “O Messenger of Allah, if I found a man with my wife, would I leave him until I could bring four witnesses?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “Yes.” Saʿd said, “No! By the One Who sent you with the truth, I would strike him with the sword before that.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “Listen to what your leader is saying. Indeed, he possesses protective jealousy, and I possess more protective jealousy than he does, and Allah possesses more protective jealousy than I do.”"],[404,"Al-Barāʾ reported: A silk garment was presented to the Prophet ﷺ. His Companions began touching it and marveling at how soft it was. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Are you amazed by the softness of this? The handkerchiefs of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh in Paradise are better than this—or even softer.”"],[405,"Anas reported: A cloak made of fine silk was presented to the Prophet ﷺ, even though he used to forbid wearing silk. The people were amazed by it, so he said: “By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad, the handkerchiefs of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh in Paradise are more beautiful than this.”"],[406,"ʿAṭārid ibn Ḥājib reported: A brocade garment that Kisrā (the Persian emperor) had gifted was presented to the Prophet ﷺ. His Companions came in and said, “Has this been sent down to you from heaven?” He replied: “Why are you amazed by this? A single handkerchief of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh in Paradise is better than this.” He then said: “Young man, take it to Abū Jahm ibn Ḥudhayfah and tell him to send me the plain woolen cloak.”"],[407,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī reported: The people of Banū Qurayẓah agreed to accept the judgment of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent for Saʿd, and he came riding on a donkey. When he approached near the mosque, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to the Anṣār: “Stand up for your leader,” or, according to another wording, “Stand up for the best among you.” He then said: “These people have accepted your judgment.” Saʿd ruled: “Their fighting men are to be executed, and their children are to be taken captive.” The Prophet ﷺ then said: “You have judged according to the judgment of Allah.” In another wording he said: “You have judged according to the judgment of the King (Allah).”"],[408,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Anṣārī reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The Throne of the Most Merciful shook at the death of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh.”"],[409,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said, while the funeral of Saʿd was laid before him: “The Throne of the Most Merciful shook for him.”"],[410,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: Saʿd ibn Muʿādh was struck by an arrow in the vein of his forearm. The Prophet ﷺ cauterized the wound with his own hand using a spearhead. When it swelled again, he cauterized it a second time."],[411,"Jābir reported: On the Day of al-Aḥzāb (the Battle of the Trench), Saʿd ibn Muʿādh was struck by an arrow which severed the upper vein—or, according to another wording, the lower vein—of his forearm. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ cauterized the wound with fire, but his hand swelled, so he left it. The wound then began bleeding again, so he cauterized it a second time, but it swelled once more. When Saʿd saw this, he supplicated: “O Allah, do not take my soul until You allow my eyes to find satisfaction through the fate of Banū Qurayẓah.” The bleeding then stopped completely, and not a single drop flowed until Banū Qurayẓah accepted the judgment of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh. He was brought to give his verdict, and he ruled that their fighting men should be executed and that their women should be taken captive, from whom the Muslims would benefit. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “You have reached the judgment of Allah concerning them.” They numbered four hundred men. When their execution had been completed, Saʿd’s wound burst open again, and he passed away."],[412,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: Saʿd was wounded on the Day of the Trench. A man from Quraysh named Ḥibbān ibn al-ʿAriqah shot him in the forearm vein. The Prophet ﷺ pitched a tent for him inside the mosque so that he could visit him easily. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from the Battle of the Trench, he laid down his weapons and bathed. Then Jibrīl (Gabriel), peace be upon him, came to him while shaking the dust from his head and said: “Have you laid down your weapons? By Allah, I have not laid mine down. Go out against them.” The Prophet ﷺ asked: “Where?” Jibrīl pointed toward Banū Qurayẓah. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ marched against them. They agreed to accept his judgment, and he referred the matter to Saʿd. Saʿd ruled: “The fighting men are to be executed, the women and children are to be taken captive, and their wealth is to be divided.” Hishām said: My father narrated to me from ʿĀ’ishah that Saʿd supplicated: “O Allah, You know that there is no people whom I love to fight for Your sake more than those who denied Your Messenger ﷺ and drove him out. O Allah, I believe that You have now brought the war between us and them to an end. If any fighting with Quraysh still remains, then keep me alive so that I may strive against them for Your sake. But if You have ended the war, then cause my wound to burst open and let my death come through it.”\nHis wound then burst open from his upper chest, and the people were alarmed as blood flowed from his tent into the nearby tent of Banū Ghifār within the mosque. They cried out: “People of the tent! What is this that is coming to us from your direction?” They found that Saʿd’s wound was pouring forth blood, and he died from it."],[413,"Al-ʿAbbās reported: I was present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the Day of Ḥunayn. Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib and I remained at the side of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and never left him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was riding a white mule that had been presented to him by Farwah ibn Nufāthah al-Judhāmī. When the Muslims and the disbelievers met in battle and the Muslims turned back in retreat, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ began urging his mule forward toward the enemy. Al-ʿAbbās said: “I was holding the bridle of the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ mule, restraining it so that it would not rush forward too quickly, while Abū Sufyān was holding onto the stirrup of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ…” The remainder of the hadith is lengthy."],[414,"Abū Ḥabbah al-Badrī reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith is the best of my family.”"],[415,"Abū Hurayrah reported: We were sitting with the Prophet ﷺ when Sūrat al-Jumuʿah was revealed to him. When he recited: “And [He has sent him to] others among them who have not yet joined them.” [Qur’an 62:3] A man asked, “O Messenger of Allah, who are these people?” The Prophet ﷺ did not answer him until he asked once, twice, or three times. Abū Hurayrah said: Salmān al-Fārisī was sitting among us. The Prophet ﷺ placed his hand upon Salmān and then said: “If faith were at the Pleiades, men from these people would surely attain it.”"],[416,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “If the religion were at the Pleiades, a man from Persia—or he said: from the sons of Persia—would go after it until he attained it.”"],[417,"Qays ibn Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “If faith were suspended at the Pleiades, men from among the sons of Persia would surely attain it.”"],[418,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās reported: Salmān al-Fārisī narrated his own story to me directly. He said: I was a Persian man from Iṣfahān, from a village called Jayy. My father was the chief landowner of our village, and I was the dearest of Allah’s creation to him. His love for me became so great that he kept me confined in the house just as a young girl would be confined. I devoted myself to the Magian religion until I became the keeper of the sacred fire, ensuring that it never went out for even a single moment. He continued: My father owned a large estate. One day he became occupied with construction work and said to me, “My son, I am busy today with this building project and cannot inspect my estate. Go and look after it for me,” giving me instructions regarding what he wanted done. So I set out for the estate. On my way I passed one of the churches of the Christians. I heard their voices as they prayed inside. Because my father had always kept me confined at home, I knew nothing about the religions of other people. When I heard them, I went inside to see what they were doing. When I observed them, I was deeply impressed by their worship. I became attracted to their religion and said to myself: “By Allah, this religion is better than the one we follow.” By Allah, I remained with them until sunset and never reached my father’s estate. I asked them, “Where did this religion originate?” They replied, “In al-Shām (Greater Syria).” I then returned to my father, who had already sent people searching for me, and because of me all of his work had been interrupted. When I came to him, he asked, “My son, where have you been? Did I not entrust you with a task?” I replied, “My father, I passed by some people praying in their church. I was amazed by their religion, and by Allah I remained with them until sunset.” He said, “My son, there is no good in that religion. Your religion and the religion of your forefathers are better.” I answered, “No, by Allah! It is better than our religion.” Fearing that I would leave his religion, he placed shackles on my feet and confined me in his house. Salmān continued: I sent word to the Christians, saying, “When a caravan of Christian merchants arrives from Syria, let me know.” When a caravan from Syria arrived, they informed me. I told them, “When they finish their business and prepare to return to their land, inform me.” When they were about to depart, they notified me. I removed the shackles from my feet and left with them until we arrived in Syria. After reaching Syria, I asked, “Who is the most knowledgeable and righteous follower of this religion?” They replied, “The bishop in the church.” So I went to him and said, “I have become interested in this religion and wish to remain with you, serving you in your church, learning from you, and praying alongside you.” He said, “Come in.” So I stayed with him. However, he was an evil man. He would encourage people to give charity and urge them to do so, but whenever they brought their charity to him, he hoarded it for himself and gave nothing to the poor. Eventually he accumulated seven large jars filled with gold and silver.\nI came to hate him intensely because of what I saw him doing. When he died, the Christians gathered to bury him. I said to them, “This was an evil man. He used to command you to give charity and encourage you to do so, but whenever you brought it to him, he hoarded it for himself and never gave any of it to the poor.” They asked, “How do you know that?” I replied, “I can show you where his treasure is.” They said, “Show us.” So I led them to its location. They uncovered seven large jars filled with gold and silver. When they saw them, they declared, “By Allah, we will never bury him.” So they crucified him and then stoned him. Then they brought another man and appointed him in his place. Salmān said: I never saw a man—although he did not perform the five daily prayers—whom I considered better than him. No one was more detached from this world, more eager for the Hereafter, or more devoted in worship day and night than he was. I loved him more than I had ever loved anyone before him. I remained with him for some time until death approached him. I said: “So-and-so, I stayed with you and came to love you more than anyone before you. Now the decree of Allah has come upon you. To whom do you entrust me, and what do you command me to do?” He replied: “My son, by Allah, I do not know anyone today who remains upon what I am upon. The people have perished, altered their religion, and abandoned most of what they once followed, except for a man in al-Mawṣil (Mosul). He remains upon what I follow, so go to him.” When he died and was buried, I traveled to the man in Mosul and said: “So-and-so, before his death, so-and-so instructed me to come to you and told me that you follow the same way as he did.”\nHe said: “Stay with me.” So I stayed with him and found him to be an excellent man, firmly following the same path as his predecessor. Before long, however, he too passed away. As death approached him, I said: “So-and-so, so-and-so entrusted me to you and instructed me to come to you. Now the decree of Allah has come upon you. To whom do you entrust me, and what do you command me to do?” He replied: “My son, by Allah, I know no one who remains upon what we follow except a man in Naṣībīn. He is upon the same path, so go to him.” When he died and was buried, I traveled to the man in Naṣībīn and informed him of my story and of the instructions given to me by my former teacher. He said: “Stay with me.” So I remained with him and found that he too faithfully followed the same path as his predecessor. I stayed with another righteous man, but before long death came to him as well. As he lay dying, I asked: “So-and-so, so-and-so entrusted me to another, who then entrusted me to you. To whom do you now entrust me, and what do you command me to do?” He replied: “My son, by Allah, we know of no one remaining upon our way except a man in ʿAmmūriyyah (Amorium). He is upon the same path as we are. If you wish, then go to him.” Salmān continued: When he died and was buried, I traveled to the man in ʿAmmūriyyah (Amorium) and told him my story. He said: “Stay with me.” So I remained with him. He followed the same guidance and way as his predecessors. During my stay with him, I worked until I acquired some cows and a flock of sheep. Then Allah decreed that his death should come. As he lay dying, I said: “So-and-so, I stayed with so-and-so, who entrusted me to another, then another entrusted me to another, and finally I was sent to you. Now to whom do you entrust me, and what do you command me to do?” He replied: “My son, by Allah, I know of no one left upon what we follow to whom I can direct you. However, the time of a Prophet has now drawn near. He will be sent with the religion of Ibrāhīm (Abraham). He will emerge in the land of the Arabs and migrate to a land situated between two lava fields, with date palms growing between them. He has unmistakable signs: he accepts gifts but does not eat charity, and between his shoulders is the Seal of Prophethood. If you are able to reach that land, then do so.”\nSalmān said: He then died and was buried. I remained in ʿAmmūriyyah for as long as Allah willed. Later, a group of merchants from the tribe of Kalb passed by. I said to them: “Will you take me to the land of the Arabs if I give you these cows and this flock of sheep?” They replied: “Yes.” So I gave them everything I owned, and they took me with them. But when we reached Wādī al-Qurā, they betrayed me and sold me as a slave to a Jewish man. I stayed with him. When I saw the date palms, I hoped that this might be the land my companion had described, but I was not yet certain.\nSalmān continued: While I was with that Jewish man, a cousin of his from Madinah, belonging to Banū Qurayẓah, came to him. He purchased me from him and took me to Madinah. By Allah, as soon as I saw it, I recognized it from the description my companion had given me. So I remained there. Then Allah sent His Messenger ﷺ. He remained in Makkah for as long as Allah willed, but because I was occupied with slavery, I heard nothing about him. Then he migrated to Madinah. By Allah, one day I was at the top of a date palm working for my master while he was sitting below. A cousin of his came and said to him: “May Allah fight Banū Qaylah! By Allah, they are gathered right now at Qubāʾ around a man who arrived today from Makkah, claiming to be a Prophet.” The moment I heard those words, I began trembling so violently that I thought I would fall from the tree onto my master. I quickly climbed down and kept asking the man, “What did you say? What did you say?” My master became angry, struck me a hard blow, and said, “What concern is this of yours? Get back to your work!” I replied, “Nothing. I only wanted to make sure of what he said.” I had some food that I had been saving. When evening came, I took it and went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was at Qubāʾ. I entered and said: “I have heard that you are a righteous man, and that you have companions who are strangers and in need. I had this set aside as charity, and I thought that you were more deserving of it than anyone else.” I placed it before him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to his Companions: “Eat.” But he himself kept his hand back and did not eat. I said to myself: “This is one sign.” Salmān continued: I then left him, gathered something else, and after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had moved to Madinah, I brought it to him and said: “I noticed that you do not eat charity. This is a gift that I have brought to honor you.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ate from it and instructed his Companions to eat with him, and they all ate together.\nI said to myself: “Now there are two signs.” Then I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was at Baqīʿ al-Gharqad after attending the funeral of one of his Companions. He was sitting among his Companions wearing two cloaks. I greeted him with salām, then walked around behind him, looking at his back to see whether I could find the Seal of Prophethood that my companion had described to me. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ noticed me moving behind him, he realized that I was trying to verify something that had previously been described to me. So he uncovered his back by moving his cloak aside. I looked at the Seal of Prophethood, recognized it immediately, then threw myself toward him, kissing it and weeping. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “Come around and sit.” So I came around, and I related my entire story to him exactly as I have related it to you, O Ibn ʿAbbās. Salmān said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was pleased that his Companions were able to hear my story. Salmān said: My slavery kept me occupied, and because of it I missed being with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at the Battles of Badr and Uḥud. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “Arrange a contract of emancipation (mukātabah), O Salmān.” So I made an agreement with my master that I would plant three hundred date palms, ensuring each one took root successfully, and pay him forty uqiyyahs of silver. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said to his Companions: “Help your brother.” So the Companions helped me with the date palms. One man contributed thirty young palm trees, another twenty, another fifteen, another ten—each according to what he was able to give—until I had gathered three hundred young date palms.\nThen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “Go, O Salmān, and prepare the holes for them. When you have finished, come to me, and I will plant them with my own hands.” I prepared the holes, and my Companions helped me until everything was ready. Then I went to inform the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He came out with me. We handed him each young palm one by one, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ planted every single one with his own hands until all three hundred had been planted. Salmān said: By the One in Whose Hand is Salmān’s soul, not a single one of those trees died. Only the monetary payment remained. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ received a piece of gold, about the size of an egg, from the spoils of one of the military expeditions. He asked: “Where is the Persian who has the emancipation contract?” I was called to him. He said: “Take this and pay off what you owe, O Salmān.” I said: “O Messenger of Allah, how could this possibly be enough to cover what I owe?” He replied: “Take it, for Allah will settle your debt with it.” So I took it and weighed it out for them. By the One in Whose Hand is Salmān’s soul, it weighed forty uqiyyahs, exactly what I owed. I paid them in full, and I was set free. After that, I took part with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the Battle of the Trench (al-Khandaq), and from then on I never missed any campaign alongside him."],[419,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: Salmān became ill, and Saʿd came to visit him. He found him weeping and said: “Why are you crying, my brother? Did you not accompany the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? Did you not? Did you not?” Salmān replied: “I am not crying for either of two reasons. I am not crying out of love for this world, nor out of dislike for the Hereafter. Rather, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entrusted me with an instruction, and I fear that I have gone beyond it.” Saʿd asked: “What instruction did he give you?” Salmān replied: “He instructed me: ‘For one of you, provisions equal to those of a traveler are sufficient.’ Yet I fear that I have exceeded that.” Then he said to Saʿd: “As for you, O Saʿd, fear Allah when you judge, when you distribute, and when you resolve upon a matter.” Thābit said: “It reached me that when Salmān died, he left behind no more than a little over twenty dirhams from a small amount of savings that he possessed.”"],[420,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ reported: Zinbāʿ al-Judhāmī had a slave named Sandar. He found him kissing one of his slave girls, so he mutilated him by cutting off his male organ and severing his nose. Sandar went to the Prophet ﷺ, who summoned Zinbāʿ and said: “Do not burden them with what they cannot bear. Feed them from what you yourselves eat, clothe them with what you yourselves wear. If you are pleased with them, then keep them; and if you dislike them, then sell them. Do not torture the creation of Allah. Whoever is mutilated or burned with fire becomes free. He is the freed servant of Allah and the freed servant of His Messenger ﷺ.” Sandar was therefore emancipated. He then said: “O Messenger of Allah, entrust me to someone’s care.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “I entrust you to the care of every Muslim.” After the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, Sandar went to Abū Bakr and said: “Honor the instruction of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning me.” Abū Bakr continued providing for him until his own death. Then Sandar went to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb and said: “Honor the instruction of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning me.” ʿUmar replied: “Certainly. If you wish to remain with me, I will continue providing for you just as Abū Bakr did. Otherwise, choose a place that you would like to live, and I will write a letter on your behalf.” Sandar replied: “Egypt, for it is a fertile land.” So ʿUmar wrote to ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ: “Honor the instruction of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning him.” When Sandar arrived in Egypt, ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ granted him a spacious tract of land and a house, and he lived there supported from the public treasury until he passed away. After his death, the property was reclaimed."],[421,"Yazīd ibn Abī ʿUbayd said: I saw the scar of a wound on Salamah’s leg, so I asked, “O Abū Muslim, what is this wound?” He replied: “It is a wound that I received on the Day of Khaybar. The people said, ‘Salamah has been wounded.’ So I came to the Prophet ﷺ, and he blew lightly upon it three times. From that day until this very moment, it has never caused me any pain.”"],[422,"Sunayn Abū Jamīlah said: I met the Prophet ﷺ and accompanied him during the year of the Conquest of Makkah."],[423,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: Ḍimād came to Makkah. He belonged to the tribe of Azd Shanūʾah and was known for treating people through ruqyah for what was believed to be madness or possession by jinn. He heard the foolish people of Makkah saying, “Muḥammad is insane.” Ḍimād said to himself: “If only I could meet this man, perhaps Allah would cure him through me.” He met the Prophet ﷺ and said: “O Muḥammad, I perform ruqyah for this affliction, and Allah grants healing through my hands to whom He wills. Would you like me to treat you?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “Indeed, all praise belongs to Allah. We praise Him and seek His help. Whomever Allah guides, none can lead astray, and whomever He leaves astray, none can guide. I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah alone, without partner, and that Muḥammad is His servant and His Messenger. To proceed…” Ḍimād said: “Repeat those words to me.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ repeated them three times. Ḍimād then said: “I have heard the words of soothsayers, magicians, and poets, but I have never heard speech like these words. They have reached the very depths of the sea.” He then said: “Give me your hand so that I may pledge allegiance to you upon Islam.” So he pledged allegiance. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “And on behalf of your people as well?” Ḍimād replied: “And on behalf of my people.” Later, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ dispatched a military expedition that passed by the people of Ḍimād. The commander asked: “Did you take anything from these people?” One of the soldiers replied: “I took a water vessel from them.” The commander said: “Return it, for these are the people of Ḍimād.”"],[424,"Ḍamrah ibn Thaʿlabah reported: He came to the Prophet ﷺ wearing two garments from Yemen. The Prophet ﷺ said: “O Ḍamrah, do you think these two garments will admit you into Paradise?” Ḍamrah replied: “O Messenger of Allah, if you seek Allah’s forgiveness for me, I will not sit down until I take them off.” The Prophet ﷺ supplicated: “O Allah, forgive Ḍamrah ibn Thaʿlabah.” Ḍamrah immediately hurried away and removed the two garments."],[425,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ reported: We set out with the Prophet ﷺ on the expedition to Khaybar, traveling during the night. A man from among the people said to ʿĀmir: “O ʿĀmir, won’t you entertain us with some of your verses?” ʿĀmir was known for chanting to encourage travelers, so he began reciting: O Allah, were it not for You, we would never have been guided; Nor would we have given charity or offered prayer. So forgive us—we would sacrifice ourselves for You—for what we have committed. Make our feet firm when we meet the enemy. Send tranquility down upon us. Whenever we are called to battle, we respond. And they have relied upon shouting against us. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “Who is this camel-driver?” They replied: “ʿĀmir ibn al-Akwaʿ.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “May Allah have mercy upon him.” One of the Companions remarked: “It has become certain, O Messenger of Allah. If only you could let us benefit from him a little longer.” (The narration then continues:) When the two armies met, ʿĀmir’s sword was short. He struck at the leg of a Jewish fighter, but the blade rebounded and struck his own knee, causing the wound from which he later died.\nSalamah said: When the army was returning, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw me while holding my hand and asked: “What is the matter with you?” I replied: “May my father and mother be sacrificed for you. They claim that ʿĀmir’s deeds have become void.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Whoever said that has lied. He will receive two rewards.” He joined two of his fingers together and added: “He was truly a striving warrior in Allah’s cause. Few Arabs have ever walked the earth equal to him.”"],[426,"Abū Hurayrah reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed ʿUmar to collect the zakāh. It was said: “Ibn Jamīl, Khālid ibn al-Walīd, and al-ʿAbbās, the uncle of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, have withheld their zakāh.” The narration continues, and in it the Prophet ﷺ said: “O ʿUmar, do you not know that a man’s uncle is like his father?”"],[427,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said regarding al-ʿAbbās: “This is al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib—the most generous of Quraysh in giving, and the one who maintains family ties better than any of them.”"],[428,"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb reported: A letter was sent to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said to ʿAbdullāh ibn Arqam: “Reply to these people.” ʿAbdullāh ibn Arqam wrote the reply and then presented it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ said: “You have done well.” ʿUmar said: Those words remained with me until I became Caliph, so I appointed him in charge of the Bayt al-Māl (Public Treasury)."],[429,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Thaʿlabah ibn Ṣuʿayr—upon whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had passed his hand—reported: He saw Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ perform the Witr prayer with a single rakʿah."],[430,"Ibn Abī Mulaykah reported: Ibn al-Zubayr said to Ibn Jaʿfar: “Do you remember when you, Ibn ʿAbbās, and I went out to meet the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” He replied: “Yes. He picked us up to ride with him and left you.”"],[431,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar reported: Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from a journey, the children of his household would go out to welcome him. On one such occasion, I reached him first, so he seated me in front of him on his mount. Then one of Fāṭimah’s two sons was brought to him, and he seated him behind him. Thus, the three of us entered Madinah together on a single riding animal."],[432,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar reported: One day, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seated me behind him on his riding animal and confided to me something privately that I have never disclosed to anyone."],[433,"Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr reported: When I was pregnant with ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr, I left Makkah near the end of my pregnancy and came to Madinah. I stayed at Qubāʾ, where I gave birth to him. I then brought him to the Prophet ﷺ, who placed him in his lap. He asked for a date, chewed it, and then put some of it into the infant’s mouth. Thus, the very first thing to enter his stomach was the saliva of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He then performed taḥnīk by rubbing the chewed date against the roof of his mouth, supplicated for him, invoked blessings upon him, and he became the first child born to the Muslims after the Hijrah."],[434,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: The first child born to the Muslims after the Hijrah was ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr. They brought him to the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ took a date, chewed it thoroughly, then placed it into the infant’s mouth. Thus, the very first thing that entered his stomach was the saliva of the Prophet ﷺ."],[435,"Ibn Abī Mulaykah narrated that Ibn ʿAbbās said, during the disagreement that occurred between him and Ibn al-Zubayr: “His father is al-Zubayr, his mother is Asmāʾ, his maternal aunt is ʿĀ’ishah, his grandfather is Abū Bakr, and his grandmother is Ṣafiyyah.”"],[436,"Ibn Abī Mulaykah reported: There was a dispute between Ibn ʿAbbās and Ibn al-Zubayr. I went to Ibn ʿAbbās and said: “Do you intend to fight Ibn al-Zubayr and thereby violate the sacred sanctuary of Allah?” He replied: “Allah forbid! Allah has decreed that Ibn al-Zubayr and Banū Umayyah will be the ones involved in making it lawful to fight there, but by Allah, I will never be the one to violate it.” He continued: “People said to me, ‘Pledge allegiance to Ibn al-Zubayr.’ I replied, ‘How could anyone compare with him in this matter? His father was the disciple of the Prophet ﷺ’—meaning al-Zubayr. ‘His grandfather was the Companion of the Cave’—meaning Abū Bakr. ‘His mother was Dhāt al-Niṭāqayn’—meaning Asmāʾ. ‘His maternal aunt was the Mother of the Believers’—meaning ʿĀ’ishah. ‘His paternal aunt was the wife of the Prophet ﷺ’—meaning Khadījah. ‘The Prophet’s paternal aunt was his grandmother’—meaning Ṣafiyyah. ‘Furthermore, he is chaste in Islam and a reciter of the Qur’an.’ By Allah, if my relatives honor me, then it is my own close relatives honoring me; and if they raise me in rank, they are honorable equals doing so. Yet they preferred over me members of Banū Asad—Banū Tuwayt, Banū Usāmah, and Banū Ḥumayd.” He then said: “The son of Abī al-ʿĀṣ”—meaning ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Marwān—“has come forward walking proudly, while Ibn al-Zubayr has withdrawn.”"],[437,"Ibn Abī Mulaykah reported: We entered upon Ibn ʿAbbās, and he said: “Are you not amazed by Ibn al-Zubayr and what he has undertaken?” He continued: “I examined myself concerning him more carefully than I ever examined myself regarding Abū Bakr or ʿUmar, even though those two were more deserving of every good than he was. I reflected that he is the son of the Prophet’s paternal aunt, the son of al-Zubayr, the grandson of Abū Bakr, the nephew of Khadījah, and the nephew of ʿĀ’ishah. Yet he behaved proudly toward me and did not desire reconciliation. I never imagined that I would humble myself in this way only for him to reject it. I do not think that he intends good. Nevertheless, if I must be humbled, I would rather it be at the hands of my own relatives than at the hands of others.”"],[438,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: When ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām heard of the arrival of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he was working in his orchard. He came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “I am going to ask you about three matters that no one knows except a Prophet: What is the first sign of the Hour? What will be the first food of the people of Paradise? And what causes a child to resemble his father or his mother?” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Jibrīl has just informed me of them.” He asked: “Jibrīl?” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Yes.” ʿAbdullāh said: “He is the enemy of the Jews among the angels.” The Prophet ﷺ then recited: ‘Say: Whoever is an enemy to Jibrīl—for indeed he has brought it down upon your heart by the permission of Allah…’ (Qur’an 2:97) The Prophet ﷺ then said: “As for the first sign of the Hour, it is a fire that will gather the people from the east to the west. As for the first food of the people of Paradise, it will be the extra lobe of a whale’s liver. If the man’s fluid precedes the woman’s fluid, the child will resemble his father; and if the woman’s fluid precedes the man’s fluid, the child will resemble his mother.” ʿAbdullāh said: “I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah, and I bear witness that you are the Messenger of Allah. O Messenger of Allah, the Jews are a people who slander others. If they learn of my accepting Islam before you ask them about me, they will falsely accuse me.” The Jews then came. The Prophet ﷺ asked them: “What kind of man is ʿAbdullāh among you?” They replied: “He is the best of us, the son of the best of us, our leader and the son of our leader.” The Prophet ﷺ asked: “What if ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām were to embrace Islam?” They replied: “May Allah protect him from such a thing!” Then ʿAbdullāh came out and declared: “I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah.” Immediately they said: “He is the worst of us and the son of the worst of us,” and they began speaking ill of him. ʿAbdullāh then said: “O Messenger of Allah, this is exactly what I feared.”"],[439,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ said: I never heard the Prophet ﷺ say about anyone walking upon the earth, “He is one of the people of Paradise,” except regarding ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām. He also said: Concerning him, the following verse was revealed: “And a witness from the Children of Israel has testified to its truth…” (Qur’an 46:10) Mālik said: “I am not certain whether the statement about the verse was part of the hadith itself or the narrator’s own comment.”"],[440,"Qays ibn ʿAbbād said: I was in Madinah among some people, among whom were some of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ. Then a man came whose face bore the marks of humility and devotion. Some of the people said: “This is a man from the people of Paradise.” He prayed two rakʿahs, making them brief, then left. I followed him until he entered his house, and I entered as well. We talked together, and when he became at ease with me, I said to him: “When you entered earlier, a man said such-and-such about you.” He said: “Subḥān Allah! It is not appropriate for anyone to say what he does not know. I will tell you why that is.” He continued: “I saw a dream during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I related it to him. I saw myself in a garden”—and he mentioned its vastness, its grass, and its greenery—“and in the middle of the garden was a pillar of iron, its base in the earth and its top in the sky. At its top was a handhold. It was said to me, ‘Climb it.’ I said, ‘I cannot.’ Then a helper came to me.” Ibn ʿAwn said: “The helper was a servant.” “He took hold of my garments from behind”—and he described that he lifted him from behind with his hand—“and I climbed until I reached the top of the pillar. I grasped the handhold, and it was said to me: ‘Hold on firmly.’ When I awoke, it was still in my hand. I related it to the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: ‘That garden is Islam. That pillar is the pillar of Islam. That handhold is the Firmest Handhold. And you will remain upon Islam until you die.’”\nHe said: “The man was ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām.”"],[441,"Kharashah ibn al-Ḥurr said: I was sitting in a gathering in the Mosque of Madinah. In it was an elderly man of fine appearance, and he was ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām. He began relating good narrations to them. When he stood to leave, the people said: “Whoever wishes to look at a man from the people of Paradise, let him look at this man.” I said: “By Allah, I will follow him until I learn where his house is.” So I followed him. He continued until he nearly left Madinah, then entered his house. I sought permission to enter, and he gave me permission. He said: “What is your need, my nephew?” I said: “I heard the people say, when you stood up, ‘Whoever wishes to look at a man from the people of Paradise, let him look at this man,’ so I wished to be with you.” He said: “Allah knows best who the people of Paradise are. I will tell you why they said that. While I was sleeping, a man came to me and said, ‘Get up.’ He took me by the hand, and I went with him. Then I saw paths on my left. I was about to take them, but he said to me: ‘Do not take them, for they are the paths of the people of the left.’ Then I saw a clear path on my right, and he said to me: ‘Take this way.’ He brought me to a mountain and said: ‘Climb.’ Whenever I tried to climb, I fell upon my backside. This happened repeatedly. Then he took me until he brought me to a pillar whose top was in the sky and whose base was in the earth. At its top was a ring. He said to me: ‘Climb above this.’ I said: ‘How can I climb it when its top is in the sky?’ He took me by the hand and lifted me up forcefully. Suddenly I was holding onto the ring. Then he struck the pillar and it fell, while I remained holding onto the ring until morning. I then came to the Prophet ﷺ and related it to him.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “As for the paths that you saw on your left, they are the paths of the people of the left. As for the paths on your right, they are the paths of the people of the right. As for the mountain, it is the station of the martyrs, and you will not attain it. As for the pillar, it is the pillar of Islam. As for the ring, it is the handhold of Islam. You will continue holding firmly to it until you die.”"],[442,"Yazīd ibn ʿUmayrah said: When death approached Muʿādh ibn Jabal, it was said to him: “O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, advise us.” He said: “Seat me.” Then he said: “Knowledge and faith remain in their place. Whoever seeks them will find them.” He repeated that three times. Then he said: “Seek knowledge from four men: from ʿUwaymir Abū al-Dardāʾ, from Salmān al-Fārisī, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, and from ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām, who was a Jew and then accepted Islam. For I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘Indeed, he is the tenth of ten in Paradise.’”"],[443,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ reported: A bowl of tharīd was brought to the Prophet ﷺ. He ate from it, and some food remained. He said: “A man from the people of Paradise will enter through this mountain pass and eat what remains of this food.” Saʿd said: I had left my brother, ʿUmayr ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, preparing to come to the Prophet ﷺ, so I hoped that it would be him. Then ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām came and ate it."],[444,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: The Prophet ﷺ went to relieve himself, and I placed water for his ablution. When he came out, he said: “Who placed this?” In another wording: “They said: ‘Ibn ʿAbbās.’” He said: “O Allah, grant him understanding.”"],[445,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ embraced me and said: “O Allah, teach him the Book.”"],[446,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed his hand upon my shoulder—or upon my shoulder; Saʿīd was uncertain—and then said: “O Allah, grant him understanding of the religion and teach him the interpretation.”"],[447,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿĀbis said: Ibn ʿAbbās was asked: “Did you attend the ʿĪd prayer with the Prophet ﷺ?” He replied: “Yes. Had it not been for my closeness to him, I would not have attended because I was so young. He came to the landmark near the house of Kathīr ibn al-Ṣalt and led the prayer. Then he delivered the sermon. He made no mention of an adhān or an iqāmah. Then he commanded that charity be given. The women began pointing to their earrings and their necklaces. He instructed Bilāl, who went to them. Then he returned to the Prophet ﷺ.”"],[448,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: I was with my father in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and there was a man with him speaking to him privately. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ seemed as though he was occupied from giving attention to my father. When we left him, my father said to me: “My son, did you not notice that your cousin seemed occupied from me?” I said: “My father, there was a man with him speaking privately to him.” So we returned to the Prophet ﷺ. My father said: “O Messenger of Allah, I said such-and-such to ʿAbdullāh, and he told me that there was a man with you speaking privately to you. Was there someone with you?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Did you see him, O ʿAbdullāh?” I replied: “Yes.” He said: “That was Jibrīl, and he was the one who occupied me from giving attention to you.”"],[449,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar said: I saw in a dream as though there was a piece of silk in my hand. Whenever I lowered it toward any place in Paradise, it carried me flying to that place. I related it to Ḥafṣah, and Ḥafṣah related it to the Prophet ﷺ. He said: “Indeed, your brother is a righteous man.” Or he said: “Indeed, ʿAbdullāh is a righteous man.”"],[450,"Nāfiʿ reported that Ibn ʿUmar said: Some men among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to see dreams during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and they would relate them to him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say concerning them whatever Allah willed. At that time I was a young boy, and before I married, my home was the mosque. I said to myself: “If there is any good in you, you will see something like what these people see.” When I lay down one night, I said: “O Allah, if You know that there is any good in me, then show me a dream.” While I was in that state, two angels came to me. In the hand of each of them was an iron mace. They took me toward Hell, while I was between them saying: “O Allah, I seek refuge in You from Hell.” Then an angel met me, carrying an iron mace in his hand, and he said: “Do not be afraid. You are a good man, if only you would pray more.” They took me until they stopped at the edge of Hell. It was folded like a folded well. It had side-posts like the side-posts of a well, and between every two side-posts was an angel holding an iron mace. I saw in it men hanging by chains with their heads downward. I recognized among them some men from Quraysh. Then they took me away toward the right. I related the dream to Ḥafṣah, and Ḥafṣah related it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Indeed, ʿAbdullāh is a righteous man.” Nāfiʿ said: After that, he never ceased increasing his prayer."],[451,"Mujāhid said: Ibn ʿUmar attended the Conquest while he was twenty years old. He had with him a spirited horse and a heavy spear. Ibn ʿUmar went to cut fodder for his horse. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Indeed, ʿAbdullāh, indeed, ʿAbdullāh.”"],[452,"Mujāhid said: We were traveling with Ibn ʿUmar. He passed by a place and turned away from it. He was asked: “Why did you do that?” He replied: “I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do this, so I did it.”"],[453,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh said: My father was brought on the Day of Uḥud after his body had been mutilated. He was placed before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he had been covered with a cloth. I went, intending to uncover him, but my people stopped me. Then I went again to uncover him, but my people stopped me. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave the order, and he was lifted. Then he heard the voice of a woman crying. He said: “Who is this?” They replied: “The daughter of ʿAmr,” or, “the sister of ʿAmr.” He said: “Why is she weeping? The angels continued shading him with their wings until he was lifted.”"],[454,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ met me and said: “O Jābir, why do I see you broken with grief?” I said: “O Messenger of Allah, my father was martyred, and he left behind dependents and debt.” He said: “Shall I not give you glad tidings of how Allah received your father?” I said: “Certainly, O Messenger of Allah.” He said: “Allah has never spoken to anyone except from behind a veil, but He brought your father to life and spoke to him directly, face to face. He said: ‘My servant, wish from Me and I will give to you.’ He said: ‘My Lord, return me to life so that I may be killed in Your cause a second time.’ The Lord, the Mighty and Majestic, said: ‘It has already been decreed by Me that they shall not return.’ Then this verse was revealed: ‘And never think of those who have been killed in the cause of Allah as dead…’ [Āl ʿImrān 3:169]."],[455,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out from Madinah to fight the polytheists. My father, ʿAbdullāh, said to me: “O Jābir, it is your duty to remain among those watching over the people of Madinah until you learn what becomes of us. For by Allah, were it not that I would leave daughters behind after me, I would have loved for you to be killed before me.” He said: While I was among those keeping watch, my paternal aunt came with my father, and my maternal uncle. They had placed them equally upon a camel used for drawing water. She entered Madinah with them so that she might bury them in our graveyards. Then a man caught up with them, calling out: “Indeed, the Prophet ﷺ commands you to return with those who have been killed and bury them where they were killed.” So we returned with them and buried them where they had been killed. Then, during the caliphate of Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān, a man came to me and said: “O Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, by Allah, the workers of Muʿāwiyah have uncovered your father.”\nPart of his body became exposed. So I went to him and found him in the same condition in which I had buried him. Nothing had changed except what the killing—or the slain body—had left unchanged. So I buried him again. He said: My father left behind a debt that was to be paid in dates. Some of his creditors became severe with me in demanding repayment. So I went to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “O Prophet of Allah, my father was killed on such-and-such a day, and he left behind a debt to be paid in dates. Some of his creditors have become severe with me in demanding repayment. I would like you to help me with them, so that perhaps they may defer part of the payment until the coming date harvest.”\nHe said: “Yes. I shall come to you, if Allah wills, around the middle of the day.” His close companions came with him. He sought permission to enter, and he entered. I had already said to my wife: “The Prophet ﷺ is coming to me today around the middle of the day. I do not want him to see you. Do not do anything in my house that may offend the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and do not speak to him.” He entered, and I spread out bedding and a pillow for him.\nHe placed his head down and went to sleep. I said to one of my servants: “Slaughter this young she-goat—it is a fat domesticated one—and quickly, quickly, finish preparing it before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wakes up, and I will help you.” We continued preparing it until we had finished, while he was still asleep. I said to him: “When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wakes up, he will ask for water for purification. I fear that once he finishes, he will stand up. So do not let him complete his ablution until you have placed the young goat before him.” When he got up, he said: “O Jābir, bring me water for purification.” Before he had finished his purification, I placed the young goat before him. He looked at me and said: “It is as though you knew of our love for meat. Call Abū Bakr for me.” He then called those of his close companions who were with him, and they entered. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ struck his hands together and said: “In the Name of Allah. Eat.” They ate until they were satisfied, and a great amount of the meat remained. He said: By Allah, the gathering-place of Banū Salamah kept looking toward it. It was more beloved to them than their own eyes, yet none of them came near it for fear of causing inconvenience. When they had finished, the Prophet ﷺ stood up, and his companions also stood up. They went out ahead of him. He used to say: “Leave my back for the angels.” I followed them until they reached the threshold of the door. My wife uncovered her face from behind a screen in the house and said: “O Messenger of Allah, pray for me and for my husband. May Allah send blessings upon you.”\nHe said: “O Allah, send blessings upon her and upon her husband.” Then he said: “Call so-and-so for me,” meaning my creditor who had become severe in demanding payment from me. He came, and the Prophet ﷺ said: “Give my neighbor, ʿAbdullāh”—meaning until he is able to pay—“a postponement for part of the debt that his father owes you until this coming date harvest.” He replied: “I will not do that.” He offered an excuse and said: “It is only the property of orphans.”\nThe Prophet ﷺ said: “Where is Jābir?” I replied: “Here I am, O Messenger of Allah.” He said: “Measure out for him, for Allah will surely repay it in full.” I looked toward the sky, and the sun had begun to decline. He said: “The prayer, O Abū Bakr.” They then proceeded to the mosque. I said: “Bring your containers near.” So I measured out the ʿajwah dates for him. Allah repaid him in full, and there remained for us such-and-such an amount of dates. Then I hurried to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in his mosque, as though I were a spark. I found that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had already prayed. I said: “O Messenger of Allah, have you not seen that I measured out the dates for my creditor, Allah repaid him in full, and there remained for us such-and-such an amount of dates?” He said: “Where is ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb?” He came running. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Ask Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh about his creditor and his dates.” ʿUmar replied: “I will not ask him. I already know that Allah will repay him in full, since you informed us that Allah would repay him in full.” He repeated this statement to him three times. Each time, ʿUmar said: “I will not ask him.” He would not answer him again after the third time. Then the Prophet ﷺ said: “O Jābir, what happened with your creditor and your dates?” I said: “Allah repaid him in full, and there remained for us such-and-such an amount of dates.” Then he returned to his wife and said: “Did I not forbid you from speaking to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” She replied: “Did you think that Allah would bring the Messenger of Allah ﷺ into my house and then let him leave before I asked him to pray for me and for my husband?”"],[456,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ said: I used to fast every day and recite the Qur’an every night. He said: Either news of that reached the Prophet ﷺ, or he sent for me, and I came to him. He said to me: “Have I not been informed that you fast every day and recite the Qur’an every night?” I said: “Yes, O Prophet of Allah, and I intended nothing by that except good.” He said: “Then fast three days.” I said: “O Prophet of Allah, I am able to do more than that.” He said: “Your wife has a right over you, your visitor has a right over you, and your body has a right over you.” He said: “Then observe the fast of Dāwūd, the Prophet of Allah, for he was the most devoted of people in worship.” I said: “O Prophet of Allah, what was the fast of Dāwūd?” He said: “He would fast one day and break the fast the next day.” He said: “Recite the Qur’an once every month.” I said: “O Prophet of Allah, I am able to do more than that.” He said: “Then recite it once every twenty days.” I said: “O Prophet of Allah, I am able to do more than that.” He said: “Then recite it once every ten days.” I said: “O Prophet of Allah, I am able to do more than that.” He said: “Then recite it once every seven days, and do not exceed that. Your wife has a right over you, your visitor has a right over you, and your body has a right over you.” He said: I insisted on doing more, so it was made stricter for me. The Prophet ﷺ also said to me: “You do not know; perhaps your life will be long.” He said: So I eventually followed what the Prophet ﷺ had told me. When I grew old, I wished that I had accepted the concession of the Prophet of Allah ﷺ."],[457,"Abū Hurayrah said: There was none among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ who narrated more ḥadīths from him than I did, except for ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, for he used to write, whereas I did not write."],[458,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd said: When this verse was revealed: “There is no blame upon those who believe and do righteous deeds for what they have consumed, provided they fear Allah, believe…” until the end of the verse. (Sūrat al-Mā’idah 5:93) The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “You are among them.”"],[459,"Qays ibn Marwān narrated that he came to ʿUmar and said: “O Commander of the Believers, I have come from Kūfah, where I left a man dictating copies of the Qur’an entirely from memory.” ʿUmar became angry, and his anger swelled until it seemed to fill the space between the two sides of his saddle. He said: “Who is he? Woe to you!” The man replied: “ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd.” Then ʿUmar’s anger gradually subsided until he returned to his normal state. He then said: “Woe to you! By Allah, I do not know of anyone still living who is more deserving of that than him. Let me tell you why.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would often spend part of the night with Abū Bakr discussing matters concerning the Muslims. One night he stayed with him, and I was with them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then left, and we left with him. There was a man standing in the mosque praying. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood listening to his recitation. When we were about to recognize who he was, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever wishes to recite the Qur’an fresh as it was revealed, let him recite according to the recitation of Ibn Umm ʿAbd.” He said: The man then sat making supplication. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ kept saying to him: “Ask, and you will be given. Ask, and you will be given.” ʿUmar said: “By Allah, I will go to him in the morning and give him this glad tiding.” He said: I went to him the next morning to give him the good news, but I found that Abū Bakr had reached him before me and had already informed him. By Allah, I never raced Abū Bakr to any good deed except that he reached it before me."],[460,"ʿAlī said: I was with the Prophet ﷺ, and with him were Abū Bakr and whomever Allah willed of his Companions. We passed by ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd while he was praying. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Who is this?” It was said: “ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd.” He said: “Indeed, ʿAbdullāh recites the Qur’an fresh as it was revealed.” Then ʿAbdullāh praised his Lord and extolled Him, and he excelled in praising his Lord. After that, he supplicated, making his request in the finest manner. He asked his Lord with one of the most beautiful supplications ever made by a servant. Then he said: “O Allah, I ask You for faith that never turns back, everlasting bliss, and the companionship of Your Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ in the highest ranks of ʿIlliyyīn in the Gardens of Eternity.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ kept saying: “Ask, and you will be given. Ask, and you will be given.” He repeated it twice. I then went to give him the glad tidings, but I found that Abū Bakr had reached him before me. Abū Bakr was always the first to perform good deeds."],[461,"ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever loves to recite the Qur’an fresh as it was revealed should recite it according to the recitation of Ibn Umm ʿAbd.”"],[462,"Qurrah ibn Iyās narrated: ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd climbed a tree to gather twigs for using as siwāk. As he climbed, his legs became visible. The Companions of the Prophet ﷺ laughed because of how thin his shins were. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to them: “They are heavier on the Scale than Mount Uḥud.”"],[463,"Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī said: My brother and I came from Yemen. We stayed for some time, and we thought that ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd was one of the members of the Prophet’s ﷺ household because we so often saw him and his mother entering upon the Prophet ﷺ."],[464,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out on the day of al-Aḍḥā or al-Fiṭr to the prayer place. After finishing, he turned to the people, admonished them, and instructed them to give charity, saying: “O people, give charity.” He then passed by the women and said: “O gathering of women, give charity, for I have seen that you make up the majority of the people of the Fire.” They asked: “Why is that, O Messenger of Allah?” He replied: “You frequently curse, and you are ungrateful to your husbands. I have not seen anyone more capable of taking away the understanding of a resolute man than one of you, despite being deficient in intellect and religion, O gathering of women.” He then departed. When he reached his house, Zaynab, the wife of Ibn Masʿūd, came seeking permission to enter. It was said: “O Messenger of Allah, this is Zaynab.” He asked: “Which Zaynab?” They replied: “The wife of Ibn Masʿūd.” He said: “Yes, give her permission.” She entered and said: “O Prophet of Allah, today you commanded us to give charity. I had some jewelry of my own and wanted to give it in charity, but Ibn Masʿūd claims that he and his children have a greater right to receive my charity.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Ibn Masʿūd has spoken the truth. Your husband and your children have a greater right to receive your charity.”"],[465,"Abū al-Aḥwaṣ said: We were in the house of Abū Mūsā with a group of the companions of ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, and they were looking through a copy of the Qur’an. Then ʿAbdullāh stood up. Abū Masʿūd said: “I do not know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ left behind anyone more knowledgeable about what Allah revealed than this man who has just stood up.” Abū Mūsā said: “If you say that, then indeed he used to be granted permission to enter when we were kept outside, and he would be present when we were absent.”"],[466,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd said: “By Him besides Whom there is no deity worthy of worship, there is no sūrah in the Book of Allah except that I know where it was revealed, and there is no verse except that I know concerning what it was revealed. If I knew of anyone more knowledgeable than I am regarding the Book of Allah, and the camels could reach him, I would ride to him.”"],[467,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd said regarding the verse: “And whoever betrays, will come with what he betrayed on the Day of Resurrection.” (Sūrat Āl ʿImrān 3:161) Then he said: “According to whose recitation do you command me to recite? I recited more than seventy sūrahs directly to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ know that I am the most knowledgeable among them regarding the Book of Allah. If I knew that anyone was more knowledgeable than I am, I would travel to him.” Shaqīq said: I sat in the study circles of the Companions of Muḥammad ﷺ, and I did not hear any one of them reject what he said or criticize him for it."],[468,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd said: We asked Ḥudhayfah about a man whose manner, guidance, and conduct most closely resembled that of the Prophet ﷺ so that we could learn from him. He said: “I do not know anyone whose manner, guidance, and conduct more closely resemble those of the Prophet ﷺ than Ibn Umm ʿAbd.”"],[469,"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb narrated: During the lifetime of the Prophet ﷺ there was a man named ʿAbdullāh, who was nicknamed al-Ḥimār (the Donkey). He used to make the Messenger of Allah ﷺ laugh. The Prophet ﷺ had previously punished him for drinking alcohol. One day he was brought again, and the Prophet ﷺ ordered that he be punished. A man among the people said: “O Allah, curse him! How often he is brought for this!” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Do not curse him. For by Allah, all that I know of him is that he loves Allah and His Messenger.”"],[470,"ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said about a man known as Dhū al-Bijādayn: “Indeed, he is awwāh (deeply humble, frequently supplicating, and devoted to Allah).” This was because he was a man who remembered Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, abundantly while reciting the Qur’an, and he would raise his voice in supplication."],[471,"ʿAttāb ibn Usayd said: “From the office to which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed me, I gained nothing except two striped garments, which my freedman Kaysān clothed me with.”"],[472,"ʿUthmān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ al-Thaqafī narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said to him: “Lead your people in prayer.” He said: “O Messenger of Allah, I feel something within myself.” He said: “Come near.” He seated me in front of him, then placed his hand upon my chest between my breasts. Then he said: “Turn around.” So he placed it upon my back between my shoulder blades. Then he said: “Lead your people in prayer. Whoever leads people in prayer should make it light, for among them are the elderly, the sick, the weak, and those who have needs. But when one of you prays alone, let him pray as long as he wishes.”"],[473,"ʿUthmān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ narrated that he came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah, Satan comes between me and my prayer and my recitation, confusing me.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “That is a devil called Khinzab. Whenever you sense his presence, seek refuge in Allah from him and spit lightly to your left three times.” He said: I did that, and Allah removed him from me."],[474,"ʿUthmān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed me over Ṭā’if, something began to come over me during my prayer until I no longer knew what I was praying. When I saw that happening, I traveled to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: “Ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ?” I replied: “Yes, O Messenger of Allah.” He asked: “What has brought you here?” I said: “O Messenger of Allah, something comes over me during my prayers until I no longer know what I am praying.” He said: “That is Satan. Come near.” I came close to him and sat on the fronts of my feet. He struck my chest with his hand, spat lightly into my mouth, and said: “Come out, O enemy of Allah!” He did this three times. Then he said: “Return to your work.” ʿUthmān said: By my life, I do not think that it ever troubled me again after that."],[475,"Umm al-ʿAlāʾ, one of the women who had pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, said: When the Anṣār drew lots to determine which of them would host the Muhājirūn, ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn came to stay with us. He fell ill, and we cared for him until he passed away. After he died, we wrapped him in his garments. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to us, and I said: “May the mercy of Allah be upon you, O Abū al-Sā’ib. My testimony concerning you is that Allah has surely honored you.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “How do you know that?” I replied: “By Allah, I do not know.” He said: “As for him, certainty (death) has now come to him, and I hope for good for him from Allah. But by Allah, although I am the Messenger of Allah, I do not know what will be done with me or with you.” Umm al-ʿAlāʾ said: By Allah, after that I never declared anyone to be certainly righteous. She said: I later saw in a dream that a flowing spring was running for ʿUthmān. I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and told him about it. He said: “That is his deeds continuing to flow for him.”"],[476,"ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim said: While I was with the Prophet ﷺ, a man came to him complaining of poverty. Then another man came complaining that the roads had become unsafe because of bandits. The Prophet ﷺ said: “O ʿAdī, have you seen al-Ḥīrah?” I replied: “I have not seen it, though I have heard about it.” He said: “If you live long enough, you will surely see a woman traveling from al-Ḥīrah until she circumambulates the Kaʿbah, fearing no one except Allah.” I said to myself: “Then where will the bandits of Ṭayyiʾ, who have spread corruption throughout the land, be?” He continued: “And if you live long enough, the treasures of Kisrā will surely be opened.” I asked: “Kisrā, the son of Hurmuz?” He replied: “Kisrā, the son of Hurmuz. And if you live long enough, you will surely see a man holding a handful of gold or silver, searching for someone to accept it from him, yet he will find no one to accept it. Every one of you will meet Allah on the Day he meets Him, with no interpreter between himself and Allah. Allah will say: ‘Did I not send you a messenger to convey My message to you?’ He will reply: ‘Yes.’ Allah will say: ‘Did I not give you wealth and children and bestow My favor upon you?’ He will reply: ‘Yes.’ Then he will look to his right and see nothing but Hellfire, and he will look to his left and see nothing but Hellfire.” ʿAdī said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: “Protect yourselves from the Fire, even if only with half of a date. And whoever cannot find even half a date, then with a good word.” ʿAdī said: I indeed saw a woman travel from al-Ḥīrah until she circumambulated the Kaʿbah fearing none but Allah. I was among those who conquered the treasures of Kisrā ibn Hurmuz. And if you live long enough, you too will witness what Abū al-Qāsim ﷺ said: ‘A man will come out holding a handful (of wealth).’"],[477,"ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim said: We came to ʿUmar as part of a delegation. He began calling each man one by one, mentioning them by name. I said: “Do you not recognize me, O Commander of the Believers?” He replied: “Indeed I do. You accepted Islam when others disbelieved. You came forward when others turned away. You remained faithful when others betrayed. You recognized the truth when others denied it.” ʿAdī said: “Then after that, I do not mind.”"],[478,"Ḥuṣayn ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: I was with Saʿīd ibn Jubayr when he asked: “Which of you saw the shooting star that fell last night?” I replied: “I did.” Then I added: “But I was not praying at the time. Rather, I had been stung.” He asked: “What did you do?” I said: “I sought ruqyah (treatment through supplication).” He asked: “What led you to do that?” I replied: “A ḥadīth that al-Shaʿbī narrated to us.” He asked: “And what did al-Shaʿbī narrate to you?” I said: He narrated to us from Buraydah ibn al-Ḥuṣayb al-Aslamī that he said: “There is no ruqyah except for the evil eye or a venomous sting.” Saʿīd said: “Whoever acts according to what he has heard has done well. However, Ibn ʿAbbās narrated to us from the Prophet ﷺ that he said:” “The nations were presented before me. I saw a prophet with only a small group following him, another prophet with one or two men, and another prophet with no followers at all. Then a huge multitude was raised before me, and I thought they were my nation. But I was told: ‘This is Mūsā and his people. Rather, look toward the horizon.’ I looked and saw a great multitude. Then I was told: ‘Look toward the other horizon.’ There was another great multitude. Then I was told: ‘This is your nation, and among them are seventy thousand who will enter Paradise without reckoning and without punishment.’” Then the Prophet ﷺ stood up and entered his house. The people began discussing who those people would be who would enter Paradise without reckoning or punishment. Some said: “Perhaps they are those who accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” Others said: “Perhaps they are those who were born into Islam and never associated anything with Allah.” They mentioned various possibilities. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out and said: “What are you discussing?” They informed him. He said: “They are those who do not perform ruqyah, do not seek ruqyah, do not believe in evil omens, and place their trust entirely in their Lord.” Then ʿUkkāshah ibn Miḥṣan stood and said: “Supplicate to Allah that He makes me one of them.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “You are one of them.” Then another man stood and said: “Supplicate to Allah that He makes me one of them.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “ʿUkkāshah has preceded you in that.”"],[479,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd said: We were talking with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one night until the conversation became prolonged. Then we returned to our homes. When morning came, we went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Last night the prophets were shown to me along with the followers from their nations. A prophet came with three of his people, another prophet came with a group from his people, another prophet came with only a few of his people, and another prophet came with no one from his people. Then Mūsā ibn ʿImrān came before me with a great multitude from the Children of Israel. When I saw them, I was impressed, so I said: ‘My Lord, who are these?’ He said: ‘This is your brother Mūsā ibn ʿImrān.’ I said: ‘My Lord, where is my nation?’ He said: ‘Look to your right, toward the hills.’” He said: I looked, and the hills of Makkah were filled with people. I said: “My Lord, who are these?” He said: “They are your nation.” He said: “Then it was said to me: ‘Are you pleased?’ I replied: ‘My Lord, I am pleased. My Lord, I am pleased.’” He said: “Then I was told: ‘Among them are seventy thousand who will enter Paradise with no reckoning upon them.’” Then ʿUkkāshah ibn Miḥṣan, from Banū Asad ibn Khuzaymah, stood up and said: “O Prophet of Allah, supplicate to your Lord that He makes me one of them.”\nHe said: “O Allah, make him one of them.” Then another man stood and said: “O Prophet of Allah, supplicate to your Lord that He makes me one of them.” He replied: “ʿUkkāshah has preceded you in that.” Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “May my father and mother be sacrificed for you. If you are able to be among those seventy thousand, then be among them. If you fall short of that, then be among the people of the hills. If you fall short of that, then be among the people of the horizon, for I saw there people fighting one another in great numbers.”\nThen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I truly hope that those who follow me from my nation will make up one quarter of the people of Paradise.” He said: We proclaimed Allāhu Akbar. Then he said: “I truly hope that they will make up one third.” So we proclaimed Allāhu Akbar. Then he said: “I truly hope that they will make up one half.” So we proclaimed Allāhu Akbar. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited: “A multitude of the former peoples, and a multitude of the later peoples.” (Sūrat al-Wāqiʿah 56:39–40) He said: The Muslims then discussed those seventy thousand, saying: “We think they are people who were born into Islam and continued practicing it until they died.” Their discussion reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: “It is not like that. Rather, they are those who do not seek ruqyah, do not cauterize themselves, do not believe in evil omens, and upon their Lord they place their trust.”"],[480,"Abū Hurayrah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “A group of seventy thousand from my nation will enter Paradise, their faces shining with the brightness of the moon.” Then ʿUkkāshah ibn Miḥṣan al-Asadī, wearing a striped cloak, stood up and said: “O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah that He makes me one of them.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “O Allah, make him one of them.” Then a man from the Anṣār stood and said: “O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah that He makes me one of them.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “ʿUkkāshah has preceded you in that.”"],[481,"ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Seventy thousand from my nation will enter Paradise without reckoning.” They asked: “Who are they, O Messenger of Allah?” He replied: “They are those who do not cauterize themselves, do not seek ruqyah, and upon their Lord they place their trust.” Then ʿUkkāshah stood and said: “Supplicate to Allah that He makes me one of them.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “You are one of them.” Then another man stood and said: “O Prophet of Allah, supplicate to Allah that He makes me one of them.” He replied: “ʿUkkāshah has preceded you in that.”"],[482,"Hānīʾ ibn Hānīʾ said: ʿAmmār entered upon ʿAlī. ʿAlī said: “Welcome to the pure one who has been made pure.” Then he said: “I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘ʿAmmār has been filled with faith up to his very bones.’”"],[483,"A man from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “ʿAmmār has been filled with faith up to his very bones.”"],[484,"Bilāl ibn Yaḥyā al-ʿAbsī said: When death approached Ḥudhayfah, and he lived only forty nights after the killing of ʿUthmān, it was said to him: “O Abū ʿAbdullāh, this man has been killed”—meaning ʿUthmān—“so what do you think?” He replied: “If you insist, then seat me.” So they supported him by leaning him against a man’s chest. He then said: “I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘Abū al-Yaqẓān (ʿAmmār) is upon the fiṭrah. He will never abandon it until he dies, unless old age causes him to forget.’”"],[485,"ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār said: A man came and spoke ill of ʿAlī and ʿAmmār in the presence of ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with them). She said: “As for ʿAlī, I will not say anything to you concerning him. But as for ʿAmmār, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘Whenever he is given a choice between two matters, he chooses the one that is more rightly guided.’”"],[486,"ʿIkrimah narrated that Ibn ʿAbbās said to him and to ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdullāh: “Go to Abū Saʿīd and listen to his narrations.” So we went to him while he and his brother were watering their orchard. When he saw us, he came, wrapped his garment around himself, and sat down. He said: We used to carry the bricks for building the mosque, one brick at a time. But ʿAmmār would carry two bricks at a time. The Prophet ﷺ passed by him, wiped the dust from his head, and said: “Woe to ʿAmmār! The rebellious group will kill him. ʿAmmār calls them to Allah, while they call him to the Fire.”"],[487,"Umm Salamah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to ʿAmmār: “The rebellious group will kill you.”"],[488,"Abū Qatādah al-Anṣārī narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to ʿAmmār while he was digging the trench, as the Prophet ﷺ wiped the dust from his head: “Alas for the son of Sumayyah! A rebellious group will kill you.”"],[489,"Abū Hurayrah said: He used to say: “Tell me about a man who entered Paradise without ever having performed a single prayer.” If the people did not know who he was, they would ask: “Who was he?” He would reply: “The little warrior (al-Uṣayrim) of Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal, ʿAmr ibn Thābit ibn Waqsh.” Al-Ḥuṣayn said: I asked Maḥmūd ibn Labīd: “What was the story of al-Uṣayrim?” He said: He had refused to accept Islam while his people had accepted it.\nWhen the Day of Uḥud came and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to Uḥud, Allah opened his heart to Islam, so he embraced Islam. He took up his sword and went out until he reached the battlefield. He entered into the midst of the fighting and continued fighting until he was overwhelmed by his wounds. He said: While the men of Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal were searching the battlefield for their slain, they came upon him. They said: “This is al-Uṣayrim! Why did he come? We left him while he was rejecting this matter. Ask him what brought him here.” They asked: “O ʿAmr, what brought you here? Was it out of loyalty and affection for your people, or out of a desire for Islam?” He replied: “Rather, it was out of a desire for Islam. I believed in Allah and His Messenger and accepted Islam. Then I took my sword and went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and fought until I was afflicted with what you see.” He did not live much longer after that and died in their hands. They mentioned his case to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: “Indeed, he is among the people of Paradise.”"],[490,"Jābir narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Who is the chief of Banū Salamah?” We replied: “Jadd ibn Qays, although we consider him to be miserly.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “And what disease is worse than miserliness? Rather, your chief is ʿAmr ibn al-Jamūḥ.” ʿAmr had been the custodian of his people’s idols during the pre-Islamic period, and he would prepare wedding feasts for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whenever he married."],[491,"ʿAmr ibn Ḥurayth said: I prayed the Fajr prayer with the Prophet ﷺ, and he recited: “When the sun is wrapped up…” (Sūrat al-Takwīr 81:1). It is as though I can still hear his voice reciting: “So I swear by the retreating stars, those that run and disappear.” (Sūrat al-Takwīr 81:15–16). He also said: My mother or my father took me to him, and he supplicated to Allah to bless me with provision."],[492,"Ibn Shimāsah al-Mahrī said: We visited ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ while he was in the agony of death. He wept for a long time and turned his face toward the wall. His son kept saying: “Father, did not the Messenger of Allah ﷺ give you glad tidings of such-and-such? Did he not give you glad tidings of such-and-such?” ʿAmr then turned toward him and said: “The greatest thing we possess is the testimony that there is no god except Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah. I have passed through three stages in my life. There was a time when no one hated the Messenger of Allah ﷺ more than I did, and nothing was dearer to me than being able to overpower and kill him. Had I died in that state, I would certainly have been among the people of the Fire. Then Allah placed Islam in my heart, so I came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, ‘Extend your right hand so that I may pledge allegiance to you.’ He extended his hand, but I withdrew mine. He asked, ‘What is the matter, O ʿAmr?’ I said, ‘I wish to make a condition.’ He said, ‘What condition?’ I replied, ‘That I be forgiven.’ He said, ‘Do you not know that Islam wipes away everything that came before it, that Hijrah wipes away everything that came before it, and that Ḥajj wipes away everything that came before it?’ After that, no one was more beloved to me than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, nor was anyone greater in my eyes. Out of reverence for him, I could not look at him directly for long. If I were asked to describe him, I would not be able to do so, because I never looked at him fully. Had I died in that state, I would have hoped to be among the people of Paradise. Then we assumed positions of authority, and I do not know what my condition before Allah will be because of them. Therefore, when I die, let no professional mourner and no fire accompany my funeral. When you bury me, pour the earth over me gently, then remain around my grave for the time it takes to slaughter a camel and distribute its meat, so that I may find comfort in your presence while I consider how I will answer the messengers of my Lord.”"],[493,"ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent for me and said: “Put on your clothes and take your weapon, then come to me.” I came to him while he was performing ablution. He looked at me carefully, then lowered his gaze and said: “I intend to appoint you over an army. May Allah keep you safe, grant you victory and spoils, and give you a generous share of wealth.” I replied: “O Messenger of Allah, I did not accept Islam for the sake of wealth. Rather, I accepted Islam out of love for Islam and so that I could be with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O ʿAmr, how excellent is righteous wealth for a righteous man.”"],[494,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The two sons of al-ʿĀṣ are believers: ʿAmr and Hishām.”"],[495,"Abū Nawfal ibn Abī ʿAqrab said: ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ became intensely distressed when death approached him. When his son, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, saw this, he said: “O Abū ʿAbdullāh, why are you so distressed when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to bring you close to himself and appoint you to positions of responsibility?” ʿAmr replied: “My son, that is true, and I will tell you about it. By Allah, I do not know whether that was because of love for me or in order to win my affection. However, I bear witness concerning two men that they departed this world while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ truly loved them: the son of Sumayyah (ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir) and the son of Umm ʿAbd (ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd).” Then he placed his hand beneath his chin, as though restrained by shackles, and repeatedly supplicated: “O Allah, You commanded us, but we neglected; You forbade us, but we committed those deeds. Nothing can encompass us except Your forgiveness.” He continued repeating these words until he passed away."],[496,"Muṭarrif said: ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn said to me: “I will narrate to you a ḥadīth by which Allah may benefit you. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ combined Ḥajj and ʿUmrah, and he did not forbid doing so until he passed away. Nor was any verse of the Qur’an revealed prohibiting it. The angels used to greet me with salām until I underwent cauterization. Then the greetings ceased. When I abandoned cauterization, the greetings returned.”"],[497,"Anas ibn Mālik said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Busaysah as a scout to find out what had happened to the caravan of Abū Sufyān. He returned while no one was in the house except myself and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Anas said: I do not know whether he excluded some of his wives. The scout informed him of the news. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out and addressed the people, saying: “We have an objective to pursue, so whoever has his riding mount ready should ride with us.” Some men began asking his permission to go back for their riding animals, which were in the upper part of Madinah. He replied: “No, only those whose mounts are already present may come with us.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions set out until they reached Badr before the polytheists. When the polytheists arrived, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Let none of you advance toward anything until I am there before him.” As the enemy approached, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Rise to a Paradise whose width is that of the heavens and the earth.” ʿUmayr ibn al-Ḥumām al-Anṣārī said: “O Messenger of Allah, a Paradise whose width is that of the heavens and the earth?” He replied: “Yes.” ʿUmayr exclaimed: “Bakhin! Bakhin!” (an expression of admiration and delight). The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “What makes you say, ‘Bakhin! Bakhin!’?” He replied: “No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, except that I hope to be among its people.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Indeed, you are among its people.” ʿUmayr then took out some dates from his quiver and began eating them. Then he said: “If I were to live until I finish eating these dates, it would indeed be a long life.” He threw away the dates he was holding, then fought until he was killed."],[498,"Qurrah ibn Iyās said: I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and sought his permission to place my hand inside the opening of his garment while he was supplicating for me. Nothing prevented me from touching him while he was making duʿāʾ for me. I found on the upper part of his shoulder something like a small raised swelling."],[499,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated that Qays ibn Saʿd would stand before the Prophet ﷺ in the position of a chief of the guard serving a ruler."],[500,"Khalīfah ibn Ḥuṣayn narrated that his grandfather Qays accepted Islam, and the Prophet ﷺ instructed him: “Perform a ritual bath using water mixed with lote-tree leaves (sidr).” The Prophet ﷺ also said concerning Qays: “This is the leader of the people of the desert (Ahl al-Wabar).”"],[501,"Kaʿb ibn Mālik said—in the account of his remaining behind from the expedition of Tabūk and Allah’s acceptance of his repentance: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to me with his face shining with joy and said: “Rejoice with the best day that has come to you since the day your mother gave birth to you.” I asked: “Is this glad tiding from you, O Messenger of Allah, or from Allah?” He replied: “No, rather it is from Allah.” Then Allah revealed to His Messenger ﷺ: “Allah has certainly turned in mercy to the Prophet, the Muhājirīn, and the Anṣār…” until His statement: “…and be with the truthful.” (Sūrat al-Tawbah 9:117–119). Kaʿb said: “By Allah, after Allah guided me to Islam, He never bestowed upon me a blessing greater in my sight than my being truthful with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and not lying to him, lest I perish as those who lied perished.” The remainder of the narration follows."],[502,"Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah narrated that the Prophet ﷺ noticed Kaʿb’s absence and asked about him. They said: “He is ill.” So the Prophet ﷺ walked until he came to visit him. When he entered, he said: “Receive glad tidings, O Kaʿb.” Kaʿb’s mother said: “Congratulations to you for Paradise, O Kaʿb.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Who is this who presumes to speak on behalf of Allah?” It was said: “She is my mother, O Messenger of Allah.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “And how do you know, O mother of Kaʿb? Perhaps Kaʿb spoke about matters that did not concern him, or withheld what would not have diminished him to give.”"],[503,"Buraydah ibn al-Ḥuṣayb narrated that after Māʿiz ibn Mālik had been stoned, the Prophet ﷺ said: “Seek Allah’s forgiveness for Māʿiz ibn Mālik.” The people said: “May Allah forgive Māʿiz ibn Mālik.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “He has certainly repented with such a repentance that, if it were divided among an entire nation, it would have been sufficient for them all.”"],[504,"Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān said: “There is no person whom trials (fitan) may reach except that I fear for him—except Muḥammad ibn Maslamah, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say to him: ‘The tribulation will not harm you.’”"],[505,"Maḥmūd ibn al-Rabīʿ said: “I still remember the mouthful of water that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sprayed from a bucket in our home.” Another narration recorded by al-Bukhārī (77) adds: “I was five years old at the time.”"],[506,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: I was playing with some boys when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came. I hid behind a door. He came over, gently tapped me with his hand, and said: “Go and call Muʿāwiyah for me.” I went and returned, saying: “He is eating.” The Prophet ﷺ said again: “Go and call Muʿāwiyah for me.” I returned once more and said: “He is eating.” The Prophet ﷺ then said: “May Allah not satisfy his stomach.”"],[507,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent for Muʿāwiyah, who was his scribe."],[508,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī ʿUmayrah, who was one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said concerning Muʿāwiyah: “O Allah, make him one who guides others and is himself rightly guided, and guide people through him.”"],[509,"Ibn Abī Mulaykah narrated: Muʿāwiyah performed the Witr prayer after ʿIshāʾ with one rakʿah, while a freed slave of Ibn ʿAbbās was present. The man went to Ibn ʿAbbās and informed him. Ibn ʿAbbās replied: “Leave him, for he accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”"],[510,"Al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah said: No one asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ more about the Dajjāl than I did. So he said to me: “My son, why do you concern yourself so much with him? He will certainly not harm you.”"],[511,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd said: I witnessed a statement made by al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad, and to have been the one who said it would have been dearer to me than possessing the greatest wealth. He came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was supplicating against the polytheists and said: “We will not say as the people of Mūsā said: ‘So go, you and your Lord, and fight; indeed, we will remain sitting here.’ (Sūrat al-Māʾidah 5:24). Rather, we will fight on your right, on your left, before you, and behind you.” I saw the face of the Prophet ﷺ become radiant with joy because of what he had said."],[512,"Al-Miqdād said: I, along with two companions, came while we had become extremely weakened from hunger until our hearing and sight had nearly failed us. We presented ourselves to the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asking for help, but none of them was able to take us in. Then we came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who took us to his household. There were three goats there. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Milk these goats and divide the milk among us.” So we would milk them, each of us would drink his share, and we would set aside the Prophet’s ﷺ portion. He would come during the night and greet us with a salām that was quiet enough not to awaken anyone asleep but loud enough for one who was awake to hear. He would then go to the mosque and pray before returning to drink his portion of milk. One night Satan came to me after I had already drunk my own share and whispered: “Muḥammad goes to the Anṣār, and they present him with food and drink. He has no need for this small portion.” So I drank the Prophet’s ﷺ share as well. When it had settled in my stomach and I realized there was no way to undo what I had done, Satan filled me with regret, saying: “Woe to you! You have drunk the drink of Muḥammad! He will come, find nothing, and supplicate against you. Then you will be ruined in both this world and the Hereafter.” I had only a single cloak. If I covered my feet, my head became exposed, and if I covered my head, my feet were uncovered. Sleep would not come to me, while my two companions slept peacefully because they had done nothing wrong. The Prophet ﷺ came as he usually did, greeted us, prayed in the mosque, and then came to his drink. He uncovered it and found nothing. He raised his head toward the sky. I thought: “Now he will supplicate against me, and I will be destroyed.” Instead, he said: “O Allah, feed whoever feeds me, and give drink to whoever gives me drink.” I immediately wrapped my cloak tightly around myself, took a knife, and went to the goats intending to slaughter the fattest one for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. To my surprise, every one of the goats was full of milk. I brought a vessel belonging to the family of Muḥammad ﷺ—a vessel from which they never expected to be able to milk anything—and milked until it was filled and foam rose to the top. I brought it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He asked: “Have you drunk your milk tonight?” I replied: “O Messenger of Allah, please drink.” He drank and then handed it to me. I said: “O Messenger of Allah, drink some more.” He drank again and handed it back. When I knew that the Prophet ﷺ had drunk his fill and that I had received the blessing of his supplication, I laughed so hard that I fell to the ground. The Prophet ﷺ said: “This is one of your deeds, O Miqdād!” I then informed him of everything that had happened. The Prophet ﷺ said: “This is nothing but a mercy from Allah. Why did you not inform me so that we could have awakened your two companions and they too could have shared in it?” I replied: “By the One who sent you with the truth, so long as I have received this blessing together with you, I do not mind who else among the people receives it.”"],[513,"Khālid ibn Maʿdān narrated: Al-Miqdām ibn Maʿdīkarib, ʿAmr ibn al-Aswad, and a man from Banū Asad of Qinnasrīn came as a delegation to Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān. Muʿāwiyah said to al-Miqdām: “Have you heard that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī has passed away?” Al-Miqdām immediately said: “Indeed we belong to Allah, and indeed to Him we shall return.” A man then asked him: “Do you really consider it such a great loss?” Al-Miqdām replied: “Why should I not regard it as a great loss, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed him in his lap and said: ‘This one is from me, and Ḥusayn is from ʿAlī.’” The man from Banū Asad remarked: “He was merely a burning coal that Allah has extinguished.” Al-Miqdām responded: “As for me, I will not leave today until I upset you and make you hear what you dislike.” He then turned to Muʿāwiyah and said: “If I speak the truth, then acknowledge it; and if I lie, then reject it.” Muʿāwiyah replied: “Very well.” Al-Miqdām asked: “I adjure you by Allah, do you know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade wearing gold?” He answered: “Yes.” He asked: “I adjure you by Allah, do you know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade wearing silk?” He answered: “Yes.” He asked: “I adjure you by Allah, do you know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade wearing the skins of predatory animals and riding upon them?” He answered: “Yes.” Al-Miqdām then said: “By Allah, I have seen all of these things in your house, O Muʿāwiyah.” Muʿāwiyah replied: “I knew that I would not escape you, O Miqdām.” Khālid ibn Maʿdān said: Muʿāwiyah ordered that al-Miqdām be given more than his two companions, and he allotted two hundred (dirhams) to his son. Al-Miqdām distributed all of it among his companions. The man from Banū Asad, however, did not give anyone anything from what he received. When this reached Muʿāwiyah, he said: “Al-Miqdām is a generous man whose hand is open in giving, whereas the man from Banū Asad is one who carefully holds on to what belongs to him.”"],[514,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī narrated—in the story of the delegation of ʿAbd al-Qays—that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to al-Ashajj of ʿAbd al-Qays: “Indeed, you possess two qualities that Allah loves: forbearance and deliberateness.”"],[515,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to al-Ashajj of ʿAbd al-Qays: “Indeed, you possess two qualities that Allah loves: forbearance and deliberateness.”"],[516,"Al-Ashajj of Banū ʿAṣr narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “Indeed, you possess two characteristics that Allah loves.” I asked: “What are they?” He replied: “Forbearance and modesty.” I then asked: “Were these qualities something I acquired recently, or have they always been part of my nature?” He replied: “No, rather they have been part of your nature from the beginning.” I said: “All praise belongs to Allah, who created me with two qualities that He loves.”"],[517,"Ibn ʿUmar narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to al-Ashajj of ʿAbd al-Qays: “Indeed, you possess two qualities that Allah loves: forbearance and deliberateness.”"],[518,"Umm ʿĀṣim, an umm walad of Sinān ibn Salamah ibn al-Muḥabbaq al-Hudhalī, narrated: Nubayshah came to visit us. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had given him the name “Nubayshah al-Khayr.” On one occasion, he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while some prisoners of war were in his presence. Nubayshah said: “O Messenger of Allah, either show them favor by releasing them without ransom, or ransom them.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “You have advised what is good. From this day onward, you are Nubayshah al-Khayr (Nubayshah the Good).”"],[519,"Abū ʿAqīl narrated: His grandfather, ʿAbdullāh ibn Hishām, would take him to the marketplace or return with him from it to purchase food. On the way, Ibn al-Zubayr and Ibn ʿUmar would meet him and say: “Let us share with you, for the Prophet ﷺ supplicated for blessing for you.” So he would include them. Sometimes he would make such a profitable purchase that he would send an entire loaded riding animal full of goods back to his home."],[520,"ʿĀ’ishah narrated in the account of the beginning of revelation: Khadījah took the Prophet ﷺ to Waraqah ibn Nawfal ibn Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, the cousin of Khadījah. Waraqah had embraced Christianity during the pre-Islamic period. He used to write in Hebrew and would write from the Gospel in Hebrew as much as Allah willed. He had become an elderly blind man. Khadījah said to him: “O cousin, listen to your nephew.” Waraqah asked: “My nephew, what have you seen?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed him of what he had experienced. Waraqah said: “This is the same bearer of the divine revelation (al-Nāmūs) whom Allah sent to Mūsā. I wish I were a young man. I wish I could still be alive when your people drive you out.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “Will they really drive me out?” He replied: “Yes. No man has ever come with what you have brought except that he was opposed. If I live to see that day, I will support you with every possible support.” Soon afterward Waraqah passed away, and revelation paused for a period."],[521,"ʿĀ’ishah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Do not revile Waraqah, for I have seen that he has one garden—or two gardens—in Paradise.”"],[522,"ʿĀ’ishah narrated that Khadījah asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about Waraqah ibn Nawfal. The Prophet ﷺ replied: “I saw him in a dream wearing white garments. I believe that if he had been among the people of the Fire, he would not have been clothed in white.”"],[523,"Abū Umāmah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prepared an expedition, so I came to him and said: “O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah that I be granted martyrdom.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ instead supplicated: “O Allah, keep them safe and grant them spoils.” So we returned safely and obtained spoils. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prepared a second expedition. I again said: “O Messenger of Allah, supplicate that Allah grant me martyrdom.” He again said: “O Allah, keep them safe and grant them spoils.” So we returned safely and obtained spoils. Then he prepared a third expedition. I said: “O Messenger of Allah, I have come to you twice before this asking you to supplicate that Allah grant me martyrdom, but each time you prayed that we would return safely with spoils, and that is exactly what happened. So now, supplicate that Allah grant me martyrdom.” The Prophet ﷺ again supplicated: “O Allah, keep them safe and grant them spoils.”\nAgain we returned safely with spoils. Later I came to him and said: “O Messenger of Allah, instruct me to perform a deed.” He ﷺ said: “Hold fast to fasting, for nothing is equal to it.” Thereafter, Abū Umāmah, his wife, and even his servant were hardly ever seen except while fasting. It was said that whenever smoke was seen rising from their house during the daytime, people would say: “They must have guests.” For otherwise, they would be fasting.\nAfter some time, I returned and said: “O Messenger of Allah, you instructed us to fast, and I hope Allah has placed blessing in it for us. Now instruct me to perform another deed.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Know that whenever you prostrate to Allah, He raises you one rank because of it and erases one sin from you.”"],[524,"Abū Umāmah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me to the tribe of Bāhilah. When I came to them, they were eating. They welcomed me warmly, honored me, and said: “Come and eat.” I replied: “I have come to forbid you from this food. I am the messenger of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I have come to call you to believe in him.” They rejected me, rebuked me harshly, and sent me away. So I left while hungry and thirsty, overcome by severe exhaustion. I fell asleep, and in my dream I was given a drink of milk. I drank from it until I was completely satisfied, and my stomach became full. Then some of the people said to one another: “A man from among your finest and most honorable people came to you, and you turned him away. Go to him and offer him whatever food and drink he desires.” They came to me with food. I said: “I have no need of your food or drink, for Allah has fed me and given me drink. Just look at the condition I am in.” When they saw my condition, they believed in me and in the message I had brought from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[525,"A man from Thaqīf narrated: We asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for permission regarding three matters, but he did not grant us any concession. We said: “Our land is very cold.” So we asked him to grant us a concession regarding purification, but he did not permit it. We then asked him to allow us the use of al-dubbāʾ (gourds used as containers for fermented drinks), but he did not grant permission even for an hour. Then we asked him to return Abū Bakrah to us. He refused and said: “He is the freed servant of Allah and the freed servant of His Messenger.” Abū Bakrah had escaped to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the siege of Ṭā’if and accepted Islam."],[526,"Abū Bakrah said: Allah benefited me through a statement that I heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the days of the Battle of the Camel, after I had nearly joined those who were going to fight alongside the people of the Camel. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had been informed that the Persians had appointed the daughter of Kisrā (Khosrow) as their ruler, so he said: “A people who entrust their leadership to a woman will never prosper.”"],[527,"Abū Thaʿlabah al-Khushanī said: I said: “O Messenger of Allah, tell me what is lawful for me and what is unlawful.” The Prophet ﷺ looked upward and then downward before saying: “Nuwaybitah.” I asked: “O Messenger of Allah, is Nuwaybitah something good or something bad?” He replied: “Rather, Nuwaybitah is good. Do not eat the meat of domesticated donkeys, nor the meat of any beast possessing fangs.”"],[528,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: On the Day of Uḥud, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took up a sword and said: “Who will take this sword from me?” The Companions stretched out their hands, each saying: “I will! I will!” The Prophet ﷺ then asked: “Who will take it with its due right?” The people hesitated. Then Simāk ibn Kharashah, known as Abū Dujānah, said: “I will take it with its due right.” The Prophet ﷺ gave it to him, and he struck down the heads of the polytheists with it."],[529,"Jābir ibn Samurah narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered the funeral prayer for the son of al-Daḥdāḥ. Afterward, an unsaddled horse was brought to him. A man held it while the Prophet ﷺ mounted it. The horse began moving briskly, and we ran behind it. Then one of the people narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “How many clusters of dates are hanging in Paradise for Ibn al-Daḥdāḥ!” Or, as Shuʿbah narrated: “…for Abū al-Daḥdāḥ.”"],[530,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: A man said: “O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so owns a date palm, and it prevents me from completing the wall around my garden. Ask him to give it to me so that I may complete my wall.” The Prophet ﷺ said to the owner: “Give it to him, and in exchange you will have a date palm in Paradise.” But the man refused. Then Abū al-Daḥdāḥ went to him and said: “Sell me your date palm in exchange for my entire orchard.” The man agreed. Abū al-Daḥdāḥ then came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah, I have purchased the date palm in exchange for my orchard.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Then give it to him, for I have granted you its reward.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said repeatedly: “How many abundant clusters of dates await Abū al-Daḥdāḥ in Paradise!” Abū al-Daḥdāḥ went to his wife and said: “O Umm al-Daḥdāḥ, come out of the orchard, for I have sold it in exchange for a date palm in Paradise.” She replied: “What a profitable bargain!” —or words to that effect."],[531,"Abū al-Dardāʾ narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I do not wish to find myself disputing over any of you at the Pond (al-Ḥawḍ), saying, ‘This one is among my Companions,’ only to be told: ‘You do not know what they introduced after you.’” Abū al-Dardāʾ said: “O Prophet of Allah, supplicate to Allah that He does not make me among them.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “You are not among them.”"],[532,"Abū al-Dardāʾ said: I asked: “O Messenger of Allah, I have heard that you said: ‘There will be people who will disbelieve after having believed.’” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Yes, but you are not among them.” Abū al-Dardāʾ passed away before the assassination of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him)."],[533,"ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ṣāmit narrated that Abū Dharr said: We left our tribe of Ghifār, who used to violate the sanctity of the sacred months. My brother Unays, our mother, and I traveled until we stayed with one of our maternal uncles. He treated us generously and honored us. Then some people spoke against us to him. I said to him: “Whatever kindness you showed us before has now been spoiled, and there can be no close relationship between us after this.” So we loaded our belongings onto our riding animals and departed. Our uncle covered his face with his garment and wept as we left. We eventually camped near Makkah. My brother Unays entered into a contest concerning our camels against another man. They both went to an arbitrator, who judged in favor of Unays, so he returned with our camels and another set equal to them. Abū Dharr continued: “My nephew, I had already been praying to Allah for three years before I met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” I asked: “To whom did you pray?” He replied: “To Allah.” I asked: “In what direction?” He said: “I prayed wherever my Lord directed me. I would pray during the night until its end, then lie down as though I were a garment spread upon the ground until the sun rose.” One day Unays said: “I have some business in Makkah. Stay here until I return.” He went to Makkah and returned after some delay. I asked: “What happened?” He replied: “I met a man in Makkah who follows your religion. He claims that Allah has sent him.”\nI asked: “What are the people saying about him?” He replied: “They say he is a poet, a soothsayer, and a magician.” Unays himself was an accomplished poet, and he said: “I have heard the speech of soothsayers, and this is nothing like it. I compared his words with every form of poetry, and no one who understands poetry could ever claim they are poetry. By Allah, he is truthful, and they are the liars.” So I said: “Stay here while I go and see for myself.”\nI entered Makkah quietly and asked one of its people: “Where is the one whom you call al-Ṣābiʾ?” The man pointed at me and shouted: “There is al-Ṣābiʾ!” Immediately the people of the valley attacked me with clods of earth and bones until I fell unconscious. When I regained consciousness, I looked as though I had been covered in red dye because of all the blood. I went to Zamzam, washed the blood from myself, and drank from its water. By Allah, my nephew, I remained there for thirty days and nights without any food except the water of Zamzam. I became so well nourished that the folds of my stomach became full, and I never felt the slightest pang of hunger.\nOne bright moonlit night, while the people of Makkah were asleep and no one was circumambulating the Kaʿbah, two women came invoking Isāf and Nāʾilah. As they passed by me, I said mockingly: “Marry one of them to the other!” They ignored me and continued. When they passed again, I spoke more harshly about their idols. The two women left wailing and saying: “If only some of our people were here!” On their way they met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr, who were coming down toward the Kaʿbah.\nThe Prophet ﷺ asked: “What has happened?” They replied: “Al-Ṣābiʾ is between the Kaʿbah and its coverings.” He asked: “What did he say to you?” They answered: “He spoke words too offensive to repeat.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then came, kissed the Black Stone, circumambulated the Kaʿbah with Abū Bakr, and prayed. When he finished his prayer, I was the first person to greet him with the greeting of Islam. I said: “Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah.”\nHe replied: “And upon you be the mercy of Allah.” Then he asked: “Who are you?” I replied: “I am from Ghifār.” He lowered his hand and placed his fingers upon his forehead. I thought to myself that perhaps he disliked my belonging to Ghifār. I reached for his hand, but Abū Bakr gently restrained me, knowing the Prophet ﷺ better than I did. The Prophet ﷺ then asked: “How long have you been here?” I replied: “For thirty days and nights.” He asked: “Who has been feeding you?” I answered: “I have had no food except the water of Zamzam. I became so well nourished that the folds of my stomach filled out, and I never felt any hunger.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Indeed, it is blessed. It is a food that nourishes.” Abū Bakr then said: “O Messenger of Allah, allow me to host him tonight.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abū Bakr, and I went together. Abū Bakr opened his house and served us raisins from Ṭā’if. That was the first food I ate in Makkah.\nAfter remaining there for some time, I returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: “I have been directed toward a land filled with date palms, and I believe it is Yathrib. Will you convey my message to your people? Perhaps Allah will benefit them through you and reward you because of them.” I returned to my brother Unays. He asked: “What did you do?” I replied: “I accepted Islam and believed.” He said: “I have no desire for any religion other than yours. I too have accepted Islam and believed.” Then we went to our mother. She said: “I also have no desire for any religion other than yours. I too have accepted Islam and believed.” We then returned to our tribe of Ghifār. Half of them embraced Islam immediately. Their leader in prayer was Īmāʾ ibn Raḥḍah al-Ghifārī, who was also their chief. The other half said: “When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reaches Madinah, we will embrace Islam.” When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived in Madinah, the remainder accepted Islam.\nThen the tribe of Aslam came and said: “O Messenger of Allah, our brothers have embraced Islam, and we accept Islam upon the same faith they accepted.” So they accepted Islam. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then supplicated: “May Allah forgive Ghifār, and may Allah grant safety to Aslam.”"],[534,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated: When news reached Abū Dharr that the Prophet ﷺ had been sent, he said to his brother: “Travel to this valley and find out about this man who claims to be a prophet, to whom revelation comes from heaven. Listen to what he says, then return and inform me.” His brother traveled to Makkah, listened to the Prophet ﷺ, and then returned. Abū Dharr asked: “What did you find?” He replied: “I saw him commanding the noblest manners, and his speech is not poetry.” Abū Dharr said: “You have not fully satisfied what I wanted to know.” So he prepared provisions, took a leather water-skin filled with water, and traveled to Makkah. He entered the Sacred Mosque looking for the Prophet ﷺ, but he did not know who he was and disliked asking anyone about him. When part of the night had passed, ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib saw him and realized that he was a stranger. When ʿAlī saw him, he followed him, but neither of them asked the other any questions until morning. The next day Abū Dharr carried his water-skin and provisions back to the Mosque. He remained there all day without seeing the Prophet ﷺ. When evening came, he returned to the place where he had spent the previous night. ʿAlī passed by him again and said: “Has the time not come for this man to know where he should stay?” He took him home with him, and again neither of them asked the other anything. When the third day came, ʿAlī did the same. Then he asked: “Will you not tell me what has brought you here?” Abū Dharr replied: “If you promise to guide me, I will tell you.” ʿAlī gave him his promise, so Abū Dharr informed him. ʿAlī said: “It is indeed the truth. He is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Tomorrow morning, follow me. If I notice anything that makes me fear for your safety, I will stop as though I were pouring out water. When I continue walking, then follow me until you enter where I enter.” The next morning Abū Dharr did exactly that. He followed ʿAlī until he entered upon the Prophet ﷺ, then Abū Dharr entered after him. He listened to the Prophet ﷺ, and immediately accepted Islam. The Prophet ﷺ then said to him: “Return to your people and inform them until my command reaches you.” Abū Dharr replied: “By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I will proclaim it openly among them.” He then went to the Sacred Mosque and cried out at the top of his voice: “I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah.” The people immediately attacked him and beat him until he collapsed. Then al-ʿAbbās came, threw himself over him to protect him, and said: “Woe to you! Do you not know that he is from the tribe of Ghifār, and that your trade route to Syria passes through their territory?” So they left him alone. The following day, Abū Dharr returned and proclaimed the testimony of faith again. They attacked him once more, but al-ʿAbbās again shielded him and rescued him."],[535,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Rabāḥ narrated: We came as a delegation to Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān. In the lengthy narration concerning the conquest of Makkah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever enters the house of Abū Sufyān shall be safe.”"],[536,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās narrated: During the year of the Conquest of Makkah, al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib brought Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and Abū Sufyān accepted Islam at Marr al-Ẓahrān. Al-ʿAbbās said: “O Messenger of Allah, Abū Sufyān is a man who likes distinction and honor. If you would grant him something.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Yes. Whoever enters the house of Abū Sufyān is safe, and whoever closes the door of his own house is safe.”"],[537,"Umm Salamah narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon Abū Salamah after his eyes had become fixed in death. He closed his eyes and said: “When the soul is taken, the eyesight follows it.” Some of his family began to cry loudly. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Do not supplicate against yourselves except with good, for the angels say ‘Āmīn’ to whatever you say.” He then supplicated: “O Allah, forgive Abū Salamah, raise his rank among those who are rightly guided, appoint a successor after him to care for those he has left behind, forgive him and us, O Lord of the worlds, make his grave spacious for him, and illuminate it for him.”"],[538,"Abū al-Ṭufayl said: “I lived through the last eight years of the life of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I was born in the year of the Battle of Uḥud.”"],[539,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: On the Day of Uḥud, the people retreated from around the Prophet ﷺ, while Abū Ṭalḥah stood directly in front of him, shielding him with a leather shield. Abū Ṭalḥah was an exceptionally skilled archer with great strength in drawing the bow. On that day he broke two or three bows because of the intensity with which he shot arrows. Whenever someone carrying a quiver of arrows passed by, the Prophet ﷺ would say: “Pour them out for Abū Ṭalḥah.” Whenever the Prophet ﷺ would raise himself to look toward the enemy, Abū Ṭalḥah would say: “O Prophet of Allah! May my father and mother be sacrificed for you. Do not expose yourself. May no arrow from the enemy strike you. My neck is before yours.” Anas said: I saw ʿĀ’ishah bint Abī Bakr and Umm Sulaym with their garments tucked up, so that I could see the anklets on their legs. They carried water skins on their backs, brought them to the fighters, poured water into their mouths, then returned to refill them, and came back again and again to give water to the Muslims. Anas also said: The sword fell from the hand of Abū Ṭalḥah two or three times because of the drowsiness that overtook him."],[540,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: A son of Abū Ṭalḥah and Umm Sulaym passed away. Umm Sulaym said to her family: “Do not tell Abū Ṭalḥah about his son until I am the one who tells him.” When Abū Ṭalḥah returned home, she served him dinner. He ate and drank. Then she adorned herself for him more beautifully than she ever had before, and they were intimate with one another. When she saw that he had eaten, was at ease, and had been intimate with her, she said: “O Abū Ṭalḥah, if some people lent something to another household, and later the owners came to reclaim what they had lent, would the borrowers have the right to withhold it?” He replied: “No.” She then said: “Then seek Allah’s reward with patience for your son.” He became upset and said: “You left me until I had satisfied my desire, and only then did you tell me about my son!” He went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him of what had happened. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “May Allah bless the two of you in the remainder of this night.” As a result, Umm Sulaym conceived. Later, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on a journey, and Umm Sulaym accompanied him. Whenever the Prophet ﷺ returned from a journey, he would not enter Madinah during the night. As they approached Madinah, Umm Sulaym went into labor. Abū Ṭalḥah remained with her while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ continued on. Abū Ṭalḥah supplicated: “O my Lord, You know how much I love to leave with Your Prophet when he leaves and return with him when he returns, but now I have been delayed by what You see.” Umm Sulaym then said: “O Abū Ṭalḥah, I no longer feel what I was feeling. Let us go.” So they continued their journey. When they reached Madinah, her labor resumed, and she gave birth to a son. My mother said to me: “O Anas, do not let anyone nurse him until you take him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the morning.” The next morning I carried the baby to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I found him holding a branding iron. When he saw me, he asked: “Has Umm Sulaym given birth?” I replied: “Yes.” He set the branding iron aside. I placed the child in his lap. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked for some ʿajwah dates from Madinah. He chewed one until it became soft, then placed it into the infant’s mouth. The child began moving his lips to taste it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Look at how much the Anṣār love dates.” He then wiped the child’s face and named him ʿAbdullāh."],[541,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: Abū Ṭalḥah owned more date-palms than any of the Anṣār in Madinah. The dearest of all his wealth to him was a garden called Bayruḥāʾ, which faced the Prophet’s Mosque. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would enter it and drink from its pleasant water. When the following verse was revealed: “You will never attain righteousness until you spend from that which you love.” (Āl ʿImrān 3:92) Abū Ṭalḥah stood before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah, Allah, the Most Blessed and Exalted, says: ‘You will never attain righteousness until you spend from that which you love.’ The dearest of all my wealth to me is Bayruḥāʾ. I give it in charity for the sake of Allah, hoping for its reward and treasure with Allah. So dispose of it, O Messenger of Allah, wherever Allah shows you.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Excellent! That is profitable wealth. That is profitable wealth. I have heard what you have said, and I think you should distribute it among your close relatives.” Abū Ṭalḥah replied: “I will do so, O Messenger of Allah.” He then distributed the garden among his relatives and paternal cousins."],[542,"Abū Hurayrah narrated: A man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah, I have been overcome by hunger.” The Prophet ﷺ sent word to his wives, but they had nothing to offer. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Who will host this man tonight? May Allah have mercy upon him.” A man from the Anṣār stood and said: “I will, O Messenger of Allah.” He went home and said to his wife: “This is the guest of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Do not withhold anything from him.” She replied: “By Allah, I have nothing except the food prepared for the children.” He said: “When the children ask for dinner, put them to sleep. Then come and extinguish the lamp, and tonight we shall go to bed hungry.” She did exactly that. The following morning the man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, marveled at—or laughed at—the actions of so-and-so and his wife.” Then Allah revealed: “They give preference to others over themselves, even though they themselves are in need.”\n(Sūrat al-Ḥashr 59:9)"],[543,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: Abū Ṭalḥah recited Sūrat al-Tawbah until he reached the verse: “Go forth, whether light or heavy.” (Sūrat al-Tawbah 9:41) He then said: “I see that my Lord is calling me to go forth, whether I am young or old. Prepare me for battle.” His sons said: “You fought alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until he passed away. You fought alongside Abū Bakr until he died, and you fought alongside ʿUmar. We will fight on your behalf.”\nBut he insisted: “Prepare me.” So they prepared him, and he sailed by sea. During the journey he passed away. They could not find an island on which to bury him until seven days later, yet his body had not changed."],[544,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The voice of Abū Ṭalḥah is more terrifying to the polytheists than an entire military company.”"],[545,"Shaqīq ibn Salamah narrated: I was sitting with Abū Masʿūd, Abū Mūsā, and ʿAmmār. Abū Masʿūd said to ʿAmmār: “Among your companions there is no one about whom I could criticize except you. Since you accompanied the Prophet ﷺ, I have not seen anything I disapprove of more than your haste in this matter.” ʿAmmār replied: “O Abū Masʿūd, and I have not seen anything from you or from your companion, since you accompanied the Prophet ﷺ, that I disapprove of more than your delay in this matter.” Abū Masʿūd, who was a wealthy man, then said: “Young man, bring two sets of clothing.” He gave one suit of clothes to Abū Mūsā and the other to ʿAmmār, then said: “Wear them when you go to the Friday prayer.”"],[546,"Abū Mūsā narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: “If only you had seen me while I was listening to your recitation last night! You have indeed been given one of the melodious voices of the family of Dāwūd.”"],[547,"Buraydah ibn al-Ḥuṣayb narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Indeed, ʿAbdullāh ibn Qays—meaning al-Ashʿarī—has been given one of the melodious voices of the family of Dāwūd.”"],[548,"Abū Hurayrah narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the mosque and heard a man reciting the Qur’an. He asked: “Who is this?” It was said: “ʿAbdullāh ibn Qays.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “This man has indeed been given one of the melodious voices of the family of Dāwūd.”"],[549,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated: The Prophet ﷺ heard Abū Mūsā reciting the Qur’an and said: “This man has indeed been given one of the melodious voices of the family of Dāwūd.”"],[550,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib narrated: The Prophet ﷺ heard Abū Mūsā reciting the Qur’an and said: “It is as though this man’s voice is one of the melodious voices of Dāwūd (peace be upon him).”"],[551,"Abū Hurayrah narrated: I used to invite my mother to Islam while she was still a polytheist. One day I invited her again, but she said hurtful things about the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that I disliked. So I went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ weeping and said: “O Messenger of Allah, I have been inviting my mother to Islam, but she refuses. Today she spoke offensively about you. Supplicate to Allah to guide the mother of Abū Hurayrah.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated: “O Allah, guide the mother of Abū Hurayrah.” I left overjoyed because of the Prophet’s supplication. When I reached my house, I found the door closed. My mother heard my footsteps and called out: “Stay where you are, O Abū Hurayrah.” I heard the sound of water. She bathed, put on her garment, quickly covered herself with her head covering, opened the door, and said: “O Abū Hurayrah, I testify that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah, and I testify that Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger.” I returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ weeping with joy and said: “O Messenger of Allah, rejoice! Allah has answered your supplication and guided the mother of Abū Hurayrah.” The Prophet ﷺ praised and thanked Allah. Then I said: “O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah to make me and my mother beloved to the believing servants of Allah, and make the believers beloved to us.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Allah, make this servant of Yours—meaning Abū Hurayrah—and his mother beloved to Your believing servants, and make the believers beloved to them.” Abū Hurayrah said: “There has never been a believer who hears of me or sees me except that he loves me.”"],[552,"Abū Hurayrah narrated: I said: “O Messenger of Allah, who will be the happiest of people to receive your intercession on the Day of Resurrection?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “O Abū Hurayrah, I thought no one would ask me about this ḥadīth before you because of the eagerness I have seen in you to learn ḥadīth. The happiest people with my intercession on the Day of Resurrection will be those who sincerely say: ‘There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah,’ from the depths of their hearts.”"],[553,"Abū Hurayrah said: People claim that Abū Hurayrah narrates too many ḥadīths from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The truth is that I was a poor man who remained constantly with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ simply to satisfy my hunger. The Emigrants (Muhājirūn) were occupied with trade in the marketplaces, while the Anṣār were busy tending their farms and wealth. Because of this, I witnessed from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ matters that they did not witness. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever spreads out his cloak until I finish speaking, then gathers it to himself, will never forget anything he hears from me.” Abū Hurayrah said: I spread out the cloak I was wearing. By the One Who sent him with the truth, I never forgot anything that I heard from him afterward."],[554,"Abū Hurayrah said: I said: “O Messenger of Allah, I hear many ḥadīths from you, but I forget them.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Spread out your cloak.” So I spread it out. The Prophet ﷺ made a scooping motion with his hands over it, then said: “Gather it to yourself.” So I gathered it to myself. After that, I never forgot anything."],[555,"Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn narrated: We were with Abū Hurayrah, who was wearing two red-dyed linen garments. He blew his nose and then said: “Amazing! Amazing! Abū Hurayrah is now blowing his nose into linen!” Then he said: “I remember a time when I would collapse unconscious between the pulpit of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the room of ʿĀ’ishah. Someone would come and place his foot on my neck, thinking that I was insane. But I was not insane—I was only overcome by hunger.”"],[556,"Abū Hurayrah narrated: When I was on my way to the Prophet ﷺ, I recited these lines of poetry: “O what a long and difficult night it has been, Yet it has rescued me from the land of disbelief.” A servant of mine had run away during the journey. When I arrived, I pledged allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ. While I was sitting with him, my servant suddenly appeared. The Prophet ﷺ said to me: “O Abū Hurayrah, here is your servant.” I replied: “He is free for the sake of Allah.” So I emancipated him."],[557,"Abū Hurayrah narrated: The Prophet ﷺ asked me: “From which tribe are you?” I replied: “From Daws.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “I did not think there was anyone of goodness among the people of Daws.”"],[558,"Abū ʿUthmān narrated: I stayed as a guest with Abū Hurayrah for seven nights. He, his wife, and his servant divided the night into three parts. One would stand in prayer, then wake the next person, who would pray, then wake the third, and so on throughout the night. I also heard Abū Hurayrah say: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ distributed dates among his Companions, and my share was seven dates, one of which was a dried, inferior date.”"],[559,"Saʿīd al-Maqburī narrated that Abū Hurayrah said: People say: “Abū Hurayrah narrates too many ḥadīths.” So I met a man and asked him: “What did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recite in the ʿIshāʾ prayer last night?” He replied: “I do not know.” I asked: “Were you not present?” He said: “Yes.” I replied: “But I know. He recited Sūrah such-and-such and Sūrah such-and-such.”"],[560,"ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated that ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: “Are you not amazed at Abū Hurayrah? He came and sat beside my room, narrating ḥadīths from the Prophet ﷺ while making sure I could hear him. I was engaged in glorifying Allah, and he left before I had finished my remembrance. Had I caught up with him, I would have corrected him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not narrate ḥadīths rapidly the way you people do.”"],[561,"Abū al-Sawwār narrated from his maternal uncle, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with some people following him, so I joined them. Then the people hurried forward, while others slowed behind. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came by me and struck me lightly with something he was carrying—either a palm stalk, a small stick, a tooth-stick (siwāk), or something similar. By Allah, it did not hurt me. That night I thought to myself: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ only struck me because of something Allah knew about me.” I intended to go to him the next morning. Then Jibrīl (Gabriel), peace be upon him, came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “You are a shepherd, so do not break the horns of your flock.” After the dawn prayer—or when morning came—the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Allah, there are people who follow me, though I do not always wish for them to do so. O Allah, whoever I have struck or spoken harshly to, make it for him an expiation and a reward.” Or, according to another wording: “…a forgiveness and a mercy.”"],[562,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib narrated: A man from Banū al-Nabīt, a tribe of the Anṣār, came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: “I testify that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that you are His servant and Messenger.” He then advanced into battle and fought until he was killed. The Prophet ﷺ said: “He performed only a little, yet he was given a great reward.” In another narration: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ wearing armor and asked: “O Messenger of Allah, should I fight first or accept Islam first?” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Accept Islam first, then fight.” So the man embraced Islam, fought, and was killed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “He did little, but he received a great reward.”"],[563,"Abū Juḥayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on a journey when he heard a mu’adhdhin say: “I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “He has cast away all rivals (to Allah).” Then the man said: “I bear witness that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “He has come out of the Fire.” He then said: “You will find him to be either a shepherd tending isolated goats or a keeper of dogs.”"],[564,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said to Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: “Shall I not give you glad tidings? I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘The leading women of the people of Paradise are four: Maryam bint ʿImrān, Fāṭimah the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Khadījah bint Khuwaylid, and Āsiyah, the wife of Pharaoh.’”"],[565,"Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “It is sufficient for you to know that among the women of the worlds are: Maryam bint ʿImrān, Khadījah bint Khuwaylid, Fāṭimah the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and Āsiyah, the wife of Pharaoh.”"],[566,"Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ drew four lines on the ground and asked: “Do you know what these are?” They replied: “Allah and His Messenger know best.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The best women of the people of Paradise are: Khadījah bint Khuwaylid, Fāṭimah bint Muḥammad, Āsiyah bint Muzāḥim, the wife of Pharaoh, and Maryam bint ʿImrān.”"],[567,"ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “The best of the women of her time was Maryam, and the best of the women of her time was Khadījah bint Khuwaylid.”"],[568,"Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: Jibrīl came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah, here is Khadījah coming to you carrying a vessel containing stew, food, or drink. When she arrives, convey to her greetings of peace from her Lord and from me, and give her the glad tidings of a house in Paradise in which there will be neither noise nor fatigue.”"],[569,"Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: Jibrīl came to the Prophet ﷺ while Khadījah was with him and said: “Indeed, Allah sends His greetings of peace to Khadījah.” She replied: “Allah is As-Salām (the Source of Peace). Peace be upon Jibrīl, and peace, the mercy of Allah, and His blessings be upon you.”"],[570,"Ismāʿīl narrated: I asked ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā: “Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ give Khadījah the glad tidings of a house in Paradise?” He replied: “Yes. He gave her the glad tidings of a house in Paradise made of qaṣab, in which there will be neither noise nor fatigue.”"],[571,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave Khadījah bint Khuwaylid the glad tidings of a house in Paradise."],[572,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I have been commanded to give Khadījah the glad tidings of a house in Paradise made of qaṣab, in which there will be neither noise nor fatigue.”"],[573,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: I was never as jealous of any of the wives of the Prophet ﷺ as I was of Khadījah, even though she had passed away before he married me. This was because I would often hear him mention her. Allah had commanded him to give her the glad tidings of a house made of hollow pearl in Paradise. Whenever he slaughtered a sheep, he would send portions of it as gifts to Khadījah’s close friends, giving them as much as was sufficient for them."],[574,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: I was never jealous of any of the wives of the Prophet ﷺ as much as I was of Khadījah, although I had never even seen her. The Prophet ﷺ would frequently mention her. Sometimes he would slaughter a sheep, cut it into portions, and send those portions to the friends of Khadījah. At times I would say to him: “It is as though there was never any woman in the world except Khadījah!” He would reply: “She was such and such, and such and such, and I had children from her.”"],[575,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: I was never as jealous, on account of the Prophet ﷺ, of any of his wives as I was of Khadījah, because of how frequently he mentioned her, even though I never saw her."],[576,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated: Hālah bint Khuwaylid, the sister of Khadījah, sought permission to enter upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When he recognized her manner of asking permission, which reminded him of Khadījah, he was visibly moved and said: “O Allah, Hālah!” I became jealous and said: “Why do you keep mentioning an old woman from among the elderly women of Quraysh, whose gums had become reddish with age? She passed away long ago, and Allah has given you someone better than her.”"],[577,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: The Prophet ﷺ did not marry any other woman during the lifetime of Khadījah. He only married after she had passed away."],[578,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: One day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O ʿĀ’ish, this is Jibrīl sending you greetings of peace.” I replied: “And upon him be peace, and the mercy of Allah, and His blessings.” She then commented, referring to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: “You see what I do not see.”"],[579,"Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “The superiority of ʿĀ’ishah over other women is like the superiority of tharīd over all other foods.”"],[580,"Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Many men attained perfection, but among women none attained perfection except Maryam bint ʿImrān and Āsiyah, the wife of Pharaoh. And the superiority of ʿĀ’ishah over other women is like the superiority of tharīd over all other foods.”"],[581,"The Mother of the Believers, ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The superiority of ʿĀ’ishah over other women is like the superiority of tharīd over all other foods.”"],[582,"Qurrah al-Muzanī (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The superiority of ʿĀ’ishah over other women is like the superiority of tharīd over all other foods.”"],[583,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Indeed, ʿĀ’ishah excels other women just as tharīd excels all other foods.”"],[584,"ʿUrwah narrated: People used to wait until the day when the Prophet ﷺ was with ʿĀ’ishah before sending their gifts. ʿĀ’ishah said: My co-wives gathered at the house of Umm Salamah and said: “O Umm Salamah, by Allah, the people deliberately wait until the day of ʿĀ’ishah to present their gifts. We desire good just as ʿĀ’ishah desires it. So ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to instruct the people to send their gifts to him wherever he may be, regardless of whose house he is in.” Umm Salamah mentioned this to the Prophet ﷺ. He turned away from her without replying. When it was her turn again, she mentioned it a second time, and he again turned away. When she mentioned it a third time, he said: “O Umm Salamah, do not hurt me concerning ʿĀ’ishah. For by Allah, revelation never came down to me while I was under the covering of any one of you except her.”"],[585,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: During his final illness, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would repeatedly ask: “Where will I be today? Where will I be tomorrow?” He did so out of longing for the day allotted to ʿĀ’ishah. She said: When my day came, Allah took his soul while he was resting between my chest and my neck. 10388-1 — Ibn Abī Mulaykah narrated: Before the death of ʿĀ’ishah, Ibn ʿAbbās sought permission to visit her while she was gravely ill. She said: “I fear that he will praise me.” It was said to her: “He is the cousin of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and one of the leading Muslims.”\nShe replied: “Allow him to enter.” When he entered, he asked: “How are you feeling?” She replied: “I am well, if I have feared Allah.” He said: “Then you are upon goodness, if Allah wills. You are the wife of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He never married any virgin besides you, and your innocence was declared from above the heavens.” After Ibn al-Zubayr entered later, ʿĀ’ishah said: “Ibn ʿAbbās came and praised me, but I wished that I had been forgotten entirely.” Narrated by al-Bukhārī (4753). A narration recorded by Aḥmad (1950), Ibn Ḥibbān (7108), and al-Ḥākim with a ḥasan chain adds: When Ibn ʿAbbās came seeking permission, ʿĀ’ishah initially said: “I have no need for him.” ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr said: “Ibn ʿAbbās is among the righteous of your family. He has come to visit you.” She allowed him to enter. He said: “O my mother, receive glad tidings. By Allah, nothing remains between you and meeting Muḥammad ﷺ and your loved ones except that your soul departs your body. You were the most beloved of the wives of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ loved only what was pure and good. Your necklace was lost at al-Abwāʾ, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ searched for it until the people found no water. Allah then revealed the concession of tayammum because of you and as a blessing through you for this Ummah. Then came the incident involving Misṭaḥ, and Allah revealed your innocence from above the seven heavens. There is no mosque in which Allah is remembered except that the verses declaring your innocence are recited therein night and day.”\nʿĀ’ishah replied: “O Ibn ʿAbbās, leave me and your praise of me. By Allah, I wish that I had been completely forgotten.”"],[586,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I was shown you in a dream for three consecutive nights. An angel came to me carrying you wrapped in a piece of silk and said, ‘This is your wife.’ I uncovered your face and saw that it was you. I then said, ‘If this is from Allah, He will bring it to pass.’”"],[587,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, shortly before his death, while he was resting against my chest. I listened closely as he said: “O Allah, forgive me, have mercy upon me, and join me with the Highest Companion.”"],[588,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “I know when you are pleased with me and when you are upset with me.” I asked: “How do you know?” He replied: “When you are pleased with me, you say: ‘No, by the Lord of Muḥammad.’ But when you are upset, you say: ‘No, by the Lord of Ibrāhīm.’” I said: “Yes, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah. I only avoid mentioning your name.”"],[589,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated: I borrowed a necklace from Asmāʾ, but it was lost. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent some of his Companions to search for it. While they were searching, the time for prayer came, and since they had no water, they prayed without performing ablution. When they returned to the Prophet ﷺ, they complained to him about what had happened. Thereupon, Allah revealed the verse concerning tayammum (dry ablution). Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr then said: “May Allah reward you with goodness! By Allah, whenever any matter has befallen you, Allah has always made for you a way out and has made it a blessing for the Muslims.”\n10392-1 — Abū Maryam ʿAbdullāh ibn Ziyād al-Asadī narrated: When Ṭalḥah, al-Zubayr, and ʿĀ’ishah set out for Baṣrah, ʿAlī sent ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir and al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī to Kūfah. They arrived, ascended the pulpit, with al-Ḥasan standing on its upper part while ʿAmmār stood below him. The people gathered around them, and I heard ʿAmmār say: “ʿĀ’ishah has gone to Baṣrah. By Allah, she is indeed the wife of your Prophet ﷺ in this world and in the Hereafter. However, Allah, the Most Blessed and Exalted, is testing you to determine whether you will obey Him or her.” Narrated by al-Bukhārī (7100).\nAnother wording in al-Bukhārī (3772) states: “When ʿAlī sent ʿAmmār and al-Ḥasan to Kūfah to call the people to join him, ʿAmmār addressed them.”"],[590,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: I asked: “O Messenger of Allah, if you came upon a valley in which there was a tree that had already been grazed and another tree that had not been grazed, upon which would you let your camel graze?” He replied: “Upon the one that has not been grazed.” By this, she meant that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not marry any virgin besides her."],[591,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ married me when I was six years old, consummated the marriage when I was nine years old, and he passed away when I was eighteen years old."],[592,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: The Prophet ﷺ married me when I was six years old. We later came to Madinah and settled among Banū al-Ḥārith ibn Khazraj. I became ill, and my hair fell out. Later it grew back until it reached my earlobes. One day my mother, Umm Rūmān, came to me while I was playing on a swing with my friends. She called me, and I came to her, not knowing what she wanted. She took me by the hand until she brought me to the doorway of the house. I was breathing heavily until I calmed down. She then took some water and wiped my face and head with it before bringing me inside. There were some women from the Anṣār in the house, and they said: “May this be with goodness and blessing, and may it be a fortunate occasion.” My mother entrusted me to them, and they prepared me. Then, before I realized it, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to me in the morning, and they handed me over to him. At that time, I was nine years old."],[593,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intended to travel, he would draw lots among his wives. On one occasion, the lot fell to ʿĀ’ishah and Ḥafṣah, so both accompanied him. At night, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would ride beside ʿĀ’ishah and converse with her. Ḥafṣah said to ʿĀ’ishah: “Would you like to ride my camel tonight while I ride yours, so that each of us may see something different?” ʿĀ’ishah replied: “Yes.” So ʿĀ’ishah rode Ḥafṣah’s camel, and Ḥafṣah rode ʿĀ’ishah’s camel. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to the camel that normally belonged to ʿĀ’ishah, but Ḥafṣah was riding it. He greeted her and rode beside her, speaking with her until they stopped for the night. When they halted, ʿĀ’ishah missed him and became jealous. She began placing her feet among the idhkhir grass and saying: “O my Lord! Send against me a scorpion or a snake to sting me. Your Messenger is beside someone else, and I cannot say anything to him.”"],[594,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: The wives of the Prophet ﷺ sent Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, to speak to him. She then mentioned the lengthy account, in which the Prophet ﷺ said to Fāṭimah: “My dear daughter, do you not love what I love?” She replied: “Yes.” He then said: “Then love this one.” Meaning, he instructed Fāṭimah to love ʿĀ’ishah."],[595,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: I would drink from a vessel while I was menstruating, then hand it to the Prophet ﷺ, and he would place his mouth exactly where mine had been and drink from it. I would also eat meat from a bone while I was menstruating, then hand it to the Prophet ﷺ, and he would place his mouth on the very spot where I had eaten.\n10398-1 — ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: My mother wanted to help me gain weight before I entered the household of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Nothing she tried had any effect until she fed me fresh cucumbers with ripe dates.\nAfter that, I gained weight beautifully.\nAnother wording states: “Cucumbers with dates.” Authentic: Narrated by Ibn Mājah (3324) and Abū Dāwūd (3903)."],[596,"Shahr ibn Ḥawshab narrated that he entered upon Asmāʾ bint Yazīd ibn al-Sakan, one of the women of Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal, one day. She offered him food, but he said, “I do not desire it.” She then said: I was the one who prepared ʿĀ’ishah for her marriage to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. After adorning her, I invited the Prophet ﷺ to meet her. He came and sat beside her. A large cup of milk was brought to him. He drank from it, then handed it to ʿĀ’ishah. She lowered her head out of shyness. I scolded her and said: “Take it from the hand of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” So she took it and drank a little. Then the Prophet ﷺ said: “Pass it to your companion.” I said: “O Messenger of Allah, rather, take it again, drink from it, then hand it back to me yourself.” So he took it, drank from it again, and handed it to me. I sat down, placed the cup on my knees, and carefully turned it around until I drank from the exact place where the Prophet ﷺ had placed his lips.\nHe then said to the women who were with me: “Pass it among yourselves.” They replied: “We do not desire it.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Do not combine hunger with lying.”"],[597,"Qays narrated: When ʿĀ’ishah set out for Baṣrah and drew near a certain place, she heard the barking of dogs. She asked: “What is this place? What is the name of this place?” They replied: “Al-Ḥawʾab.” She said: “I think I should return.” They said: “No.” She repeated: “I think I should return, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say to his wives: ‘Which one of you will be barked at by the dogs of al-Ḥawʾab?’” Some people continued speaking to her until she eventually continued on toward Baṣrah. Another narration states: When ʿĀ’ishah reached the waters of Banū ʿĀmir at night, the dogs barked. She asked: “Which water is this?” They answered: “The water of al-Ḥawʾab.” She said: “I do not think I should do anything except return.” Some of those accompanying her said: “Rather, continue onward. The Muslims will see you, and Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, may reconcile the people through you.” She then said: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ once said: ‘What will happen when one of you is barked at by the dogs of al-Ḥawʾab?’”"],[598,"Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Which one of you will be the woman with the shaggy camel, around whom many people will be killed, yet she herself will survive after nearly perishing?”"],[599,"ʿUrwah narrated: The Prophet ﷺ proposed marriage to ʿĀ’ishah through Abū Bakr. Abū Bakr said: “But I am your brother.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “You are my brother in the religion of Allah and His Book, but she is lawful for me to marry.”"],[600,"ʿUrwah narrated: Khadījah passed away three years before the Prophet ﷺ emigrated. He remained for about two years, or close to that, and then married ʿĀ’ishah when she was six years old. He consummated the marriage with her when she was nine years old.\n10403-1 — ʿAmr ibn Ghālib narrated: A man spoke offensively about ʿĀ’ishah in the presence of ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir. ʿAmmār said: “Be gone, you disgraced and rejected man! Would you insult the beloved of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?”\nHasan: Narrated by al-Tirmidhī (3888) and al-Ṭabarānī in al-Muʿjam al-Kabīr (23/40)."],[601,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned Fāṭimah. I spoke, and he said to me: “Would it not please you to be my wife in this world and in the Hereafter?” I replied: “Indeed, yes, by Allah.” He said: “Then you are my wife in this world and in the Hereafter.”"],[602,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, said: Some Abyssinians were performing with their spears in the mosque. The Prophet ﷺ said to me: “O Ḥumayrāʾ, would you like to watch them?” I replied: “Yes.” He stood at the doorway, and I came to him. I rested my chin upon his shoulder and leaned my face against his cheek while watching them. Among what they were saying that day was: “Abū al-Qāsim is noble and good.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “Have you seen enough?” I replied: “O Messenger of Allah, do not hurry.” So he remained standing for me. After some time he again asked: “Have you seen enough?” I said: “Do not hurry, O Messenger of Allah.” ʿĀ’ishah then explained: “It was not that I particularly wished to keep watching them. Rather, I wanted women to know the position I held with him and my standing with him.”"],[603,"Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entrusted a man to Ḥafṣah, the daughter of ʿUmar, and said: “Keep watch over him.” Ḥafṣah became distracted, and the man left. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned and asked: “O Ḥafṣah, what happened to the man?” She replied: “I became distracted, O Messenger of Allah, and he left.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “May Allah cut off your hand.” Ḥafṣah raised her hands like this. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw her, he asked: “What is the matter with you, O Ḥafṣah?” She replied: “O Messenger of Allah, you said such-and-such to me.” He then said: “Lower your hands, for I have asked Allah that if I ever supplicate against any person from my Ummah, He should make that supplication a means of forgiveness for him.”"],[604,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The first of you to join me will be the one with the longest hand.” She said: We began measuring our hands against one another to see which of us had the longest. It turned out that Zaynab was the one with the “longest hand,” because she used to work with her own hands and give generously in charity."],[605,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā narrated: ʿUmar performed the funeral prayer over Zaynab bint Jaḥsh with four takbīrs. He then asked the wives of the Prophet ﷺ: “Who should descend into her grave?” They replied: “The one who used to enter upon her during her lifetime.” He also said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say: “The first of you to join me will be the one with the longest hand.” So they would compare the lengths of their hands. However, the intended meaning was that she worked with her own hands and spent what she earned in the cause of Allah."],[606,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: The Prophet ﷺ said to his wives: “The one among you with the longest hand will be the first to follow me.” After the Prophet ﷺ passed away, whenever we gathered in the home of one of us, we would stretch our hands against the wall to measure which was the longest. We continued doing this until Zaynab bint Jaḥsh passed away. Although she was a short woman—and certainly did not have the longest physical arm—we then realized that the Prophet ﷺ had meant the one who gave the most charity. Zaynab was a woman who worked skillfully with her own hands. She tanned leather, stitched and sewed, and then gave the proceeds in charity for the sake of Allah."],[607,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated: Some of the wives of the Prophet ﷺ asked him: “Which one of us will be the first to join you?” He replied: “The one with the longest hand.” They took a reed and measured the lengths of their arms. Sawdah appeared to have the longest arm. Later, however, we understood that the “longest hand” referred to generosity in charity. The wife intended by this description was Zaynab bint Jaḥsh, who was the first of the Prophet’s wives to pass away after him and was known for her abundant charity."],[608,"Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: When Zaynab’s waiting period (ʿiddah) had ended, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Zayd: “Go and mention me to her (propose marriage on my behalf).” Zayd said: I went to her while she was preparing dough. When I saw her, she became so honored in my heart because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had mentioned her that I could not even look directly at her. I turned my back toward her and said: “O Zaynab, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has sent me to speak to you regarding marriage.” She replied: “I will not decide anything until I seek my Lord’s guidance.” She then stood and went to her place of prayer. Thereupon, the Qur’an was revealed, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon her without needing anyone’s permission, for Allah Himself had joined them in marriage. Anas continued: I witnessed the wedding feast where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ served us bread and meat until the day had advanced. The people eventually left, but a few men remained sitting in the house talking after the meal. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ left, and I followed him. He visited the apartments of his wives, greeting each of them, and they asked: “O Messenger of Allah, how did you find your new family?” Later he returned, and I do not know whether someone informed him that the guests had finally left or whether he learned it himself. He entered the house, and as I tried to follow him inside, he lowered the curtain between himself and me. At that moment, the verse of ḥijāb was revealed, and the people were instructed with the etiquette that Allah prescribed."],[609,"Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: Ṣafiyyah learned that Ḥafṣah had said to her: “You are the daughter of a Jew.” Ṣafiyyah began weeping. The Prophet ﷺ entered and asked: “Why are you crying?” She replied: “Ḥafṣah said to me, ‘You are the daughter of a Jew.’” The Prophet ﷺ said: “You are indeed the descendant of a Prophet, your uncle was a Prophet, and you are married to a Prophet. So what reason does she have to boast over you?” He then said: “Fear Allah, O Ḥafṣah.”"],[610,"Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: Ṣafiyyah was traveling with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during one of his journeys, and it was her appointed day with him. She fell behind during the journey. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to meet her and found her weeping, saying: “You placed me on a slow camel.” The Prophet ﷺ gently wiped the tears from her eyes with his hands and tried to comfort her, but she continued crying. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ became upset and left her. When they arrived, Ṣafiyyah went to ʿĀ’ishah and said: “I will give you my day with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ if you can reconcile him with me.” ʿĀ’ishah took her head covering, which had been dyed with wars and saffron, sprinkled it with a little water, and came to sit near the head of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He asked: “What brings you here?” She replied: “That is the bounty of Allah; He gives it to whom He wills.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ let the matter pass and became pleased with Ṣafiyyah. After that, he went to Zaynab and said: “Ṣafiyyah’s camel has become exhausted. Would it harm you to give her one of your camels?” Zaynab replied: “Would you take my camel and give it to that Jewish woman?” Because of this remark, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ avoided Zaynab for three months and did not visit her. Zaynab stopped adorning herself and neglected preparing her home. She moved her bed to the back of the house, believing that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would no longer come to her. Then one day she suddenly heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entering her house. He returned the bed to its usual place. Zaynab said: “O Messenger of Allah, my servant girl has become pure from her menstruation today; she is at your service.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then entered upon her and was once again pleased with her."],[611,"Abū ʿUthmān narrated: I was informed that Jibrīl (Gabriel) came to the Prophet ﷺ while Umm Salamah was with him. Jibrīl spoke with the Prophet ﷺ for some time. The Prophet ﷺ then asked Umm Salamah: “Who do you think this is?” She replied: “It is Diḥyah.” After he left, she said: “By Allah, I thought it was no one other than Diḥyah, until I later heard the Prophet ﷺ delivering a sermon in which he informed the people that it had actually been Jibrīl.” Abū ʿUthmān was asked: “From whom did you hear this narration?” He replied: “From Usāmah ibn Zayd.”"],[612,"Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, was asked: “O Mother of the Believers, tell us about the private life of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” She replied: “His private conduct and his public conduct were the same.” Then she regretted having said it and thought: “I have disclosed one of the Messenger of Allah’s private matters.” When the Prophet ﷺ came home, she informed him of what she had said. He replied: “You have done well.”"],[613,"Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: When Abū Salamah was nearing death, Umm Salamah said: “To whom will you leave me?” He supplicated: “O Allah, You are better for Umm Salamah than Abū Salamah.” After Abū Salamah passed away, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ proposed marriage to her. She replied: “I am an elderly woman.” He said: “I am older than you. As for your children, Allah and His Messenger will care for them. And as for your jealousy, I hope Allah will remove it from you.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then married her and sent to her two handmills and a water jar."],[614,"Umm Ḥabībah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, narrated that she had been married to ʿUbaydullāh ibn Jaḥsh, who died in the land of Abyssinia. The Negus (al-Najāshī), the king of Abyssinia, performed her marriage to the Prophet ﷺ on his behalf and gave her a dowry of four thousand dirhams on behalf of the Prophet ﷺ. He then sent her to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with Sharḥabīl ibn Ḥasanah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not send anything separately to her as a dowry, whereas the usual dowry he gave his wives was four hundred dirhams. Abū Dāwūd said: “Ḥasanah was the name of Sharḥabīl’s mother.”"],[615,"Zayd al-Aṣamm narrated: Maymūnah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, became seriously ill while in Makkah, and none of her nephews were present with her. She said: “Take me out of Makkah, for I will not die here. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed me that I would not die in Makkah.” They carried her until they reached Sarif, beneath the tree where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had consummated his marriage with her, at the place where the tent had once been erected. There she passed away. When we placed her in her grave, I took my cloak and placed it beneath her cheek inside the grave. Ibn ʿAbbās removed it and threw it away."],[616,"Ṣafiyyah bint Shaybah narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ married Maymūnah while he was not in the state of iḥrām, and he consummated the marriage with her at Sarif. Maymūnah’s grave is also located at Sarif."],[617,"Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The believing sisters are: Maymūnah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ; Umm al-Faḍl bint al-Ḥārith; Salmā, the wife of Ḥamzah; and Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays. They are maternal sisters.”"],[618,"Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: A man was accused of having an improper relationship with Māriyah, the mother of the Prophet’s son. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to ʿAlī: “Go and strike off his neck.” ʿAlī found the man cooling himself inside a well. He said to him: “Come out.” The man extended his hand, and ʿAlī helped him out. When he saw him, he discovered that the man had been castrated and possessed no male organ. Therefore, ʿAlī refrained from harming him. He returned to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah, he has been castrated; he has no male organ.”"],[619,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: People repeatedly spread accusations concerning Māriyah, the mother of Ibrāhīm, because a Coptic man—who was her cousin—used to visit her regularly. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “Take this sword and go. If you find him with her, kill him.” I said: “O Messenger of Allah, when you send me on a command, should I carry it out without hesitation like a heated piece of iron, or does the one who witnesses the situation see what the absent person cannot?” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Rather, the one who witnesses sees what the absent person cannot.” So I went carrying the sword. I found the man there and drew my sword. When he saw me approaching, he feared that I intended to kill him. He climbed a palm tree, then threw himself onto his back and uncovered himself. I saw that he was completely impotent and lacked male genitalia. So I sheathed my sword. I returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him. He said: “All praise is due to Allah, who turns harm away from us, the people of this household.”"],[620,"Abū Dharr reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “You will indeed conquer Egypt, a land in which the qīrāṭ is commonly mentioned. When you conquer it, treat its people well, for they have a covenant and ties of kinship—or he said: a covenant and ties through marriage. Then, when you see two men disputing there over the space of a single brick, leave it.”"],[621,"ʿĀ’ishah said: “The wives of the Prophet ﷺ were with him, and not one of them was absent. Fāṭimah approached, walking in a manner that did not differ at all from the walk of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When he saw her, he welcomed her and said: ‘Welcome, my daughter.’ “Then he seated her on his right or his left and whispered something privately to her, whereupon she wept intensely. When he saw her distress, he whispered to her a second time, and she laughed. “I said to her, ‘The Messenger of Allah ﷺ singled you out from among his wives to speak privately with you, and then you weep?’ “When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ got up, I asked her, ‘What did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say to you?’ “She replied, ‘I would not disclose the secret of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.’ “When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, I said to her, ‘I adjure you by the right I have over you to tell me what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to you.’ “She said, ʿNow, yes. The first time he whispered to me, he informed me that Jibrīl used to review the Qurʾān with him once or twice every year, but that he had reviewed it with him twice that year, and he said: “I do not think except that my appointed time has drawn near. So fear Allah and be patient, for what an excellent predecessor I am for you.” “Thus I wept as you saw me weep. When he saw my distress, he whispered to me a second time and said: “O Fāṭimah, are you not pleased to be the leader of the women of the believers—or the leader of the women of this Ummah?” “So I laughed as you saw me laugh.”"],[622,"ʿĀ’ishah said: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ fell ill, and Fāṭimah came and leaned closely over the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He whispered to her, and she wept. Then she leaned over him again, and he whispered to her, whereupon she laughed. “After the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, I asked her about it. “She said: ‘When I leaned over him, he informed me that he would die from that illness, so I wept. Then I leaned over him again, and he informed me that I would be the quickest of his household to join him and that I am the leader of the women of Paradise, except Maryam bint ʿImrān. So I raised my head and laughed.’”"],[623,"Umm Salamah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called Fāṭimah and spoke privately with her, whereupon she wept. Then he spoke to her again, and she laughed. Umm Salamah said: “After the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, I asked her about her weeping and her laughter. “She said: ‘The Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed me that he was going to die, and then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed me that I am the leader of the women of Paradise after Maryam bint ʿImrān, so I laughed.’”"],[624,"Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say while he was delivering a sermon from the pulpit: “The family of Hishām ibn al-Mughīrah have asked my permission to marry their daughter to ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib. I will not permit it. I will not permit it. I will not permit it—unless Ibn Abī Ṭālib wishes first to divorce my daughter and then marry theirs. For indeed, Fāṭimah is a part of me. Whatever disturbs her disturbs me, and whatever harms her harms me.”"],[625,"Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib sought the hand of the daughter of Abū Jahl while he was already married to Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When Fāṭimah heard of this, she went to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “Your people are saying that you do not become angry for the sake of your daughters, and now ʿAlī intends to marry the daughter of Abū Jahl.” Al-Miswar said: The Prophet ﷺ stood and, after praising Allah, said: “As for what follows: I married my daughter to Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ. He spoke to me truthfully and fulfilled what he promised me. Fāṭimah bint Muḥammad is a part of me, and I dislike that she should be put through such a trial. By Allah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah and the daughter of the enemy of Allah shall never be joined in marriage to one man.” After hearing this, ʿAlī abandoned the proposal."],[626,"Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib proposed to the daughter of Abū Jahl while married to Fāṭimah. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressing the people from this very pulpit while I had already reached puberty. He said: “Indeed, Fāṭimah is a part of me, and I fear that she may be put to trial in her religion.” He then mentioned one of his sons-in-law from Banū ʿAbd Shams and praised him, saying: “He spoke to me truthfully and fulfilled his promise to me. I do not make lawful what Allah has forbidden, nor do I forbid what Allah has made lawful. But by Allah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah and the daughter of the enemy of Allah will never be together under one man.”"],[627,"ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: When the Prophet ﷺ heard that ʿAlī had mentioned marrying the daughter of Abū Jahl, he said: “Fāṭimah is only a part of me. Whatever hurts her hurts me, and whatever causes her hardship causes me hardship.”"],[628,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) said: When I married Fāṭimah, I said: “O Messenger of Allah, arrange for me to begin married life with my wife.” He replied: “Give her something as a bridal gift.” I said: “I do not possess anything.” He asked: “Where is your Ḥuṭamiyyah coat of mail?” I replied: “I still have it.” He said: “Then give it to her.”"],[629,"Abū Umāmah ibn Sahl narrated: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ completed the Battle of Badr, he sent two men to Madinah with the glad tidings of victory. He sent Zayd ibn Ḥārithah to the lower part of the city and ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah to the upper part to announce the victory Allah had granted His Messenger ﷺ. When Zayd arrived, his son Usāmah had just finished leveling the earth over the grave of Ruqayyah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[630,"Umm ʿAṭiyyah al-Anṣāriyyah (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated: When the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, he came to us and said: “Wash her three times, or five times, or more if you think it necessary, using water mixed with lote-tree leaves. In the final washing, add camphor—or some camphor. When you have finished, let me know.” She said: When we had completed the washing, we informed him. He then gave us his waist-wrapper (izār) and said: “Wrap her body with this next to her skin.” Another narration explicitly states: “When Zaynab, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, passed away…”"],[631,"Asmāʾ (may Allah be pleased with her) said: I prepared the provisions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the house of Abū Bakr when he intended to migrate to Madinah. We could not find anything with which to tie the food bag or the water skin. So I said to Abū Bakr: “By Allah, I cannot find anything to tie them with except my waist belt.” He replied: “Tear it into two pieces. Tie the water skin with one piece and the food bag with the other.” So I did that. For this reason I became known as Dhāt al-Niṭāqayn (The Woman of the Two Waist Belts)."],[632,"Jamrah bint ʿAbdullāh al-Yarbūʿiyyah narrated: My father took me to the Prophet ﷺ after the camels had been returned to my father. He said: “O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah to bless this daughter of mine.” The Prophet ﷺ seated me in his lap, placed his hand upon my head, and supplicated that Allah grant me blessing."],[633,"Umm Khālid bint Abī al-Aswad narrated: She entered upon the Prophet ﷺ. He asked: “Who is this?” They replied: “Umm Khālid, the daughter of al-Aswad ibn ʿAbd Yaghūth.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “All praise is due to Allah, who brings forth the living from the dead.” Meaning: He brings forth the believing person from a disbelieving parent."],[634,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: “All praise belongs to Allah whose hearing encompasses every voice.” Khawlah bint Thaʿlabah came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ complaining about her husband. Although I was nearby, much of their conversation was too quiet for me to hear. Then Allah, the Most High, revealed: “Allah has certainly heard the speech of the woman who argues with you concerning her husband and complains to Allah. And Allah hears your dialogue. Indeed, Allah is All-Hearing, All-Seeing.” (Sūrat al-Mujādilah 58:1)"],[635,"Khawlah bint Thaʿlabah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: “By Allah, the opening verses of Sūrat al-Mujādilah were revealed concerning me and Aws ibn al-Ṣāmit.” She said: I was married to him. He had become an elderly man whose temperament had grown harsh and irritable. One day we argued, and in anger he said: “You are to me like the back of my mother.” He then went out and sat with his people for some time before returning home. When he wished to approach me intimately, I refused and said: “No, by the One in whose Hand is the soul of Khawlah! You shall not touch me after what you have said until Allah and His Messenger judge between us.” He attempted to force himself upon me, but I resisted him and overcame him because he was an elderly, weak man. I then borrowed some clothes from one of my neighbors and went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I sat before him and complained of my husband’s treatment and harsh character. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ repeatedly advised me: “O Khawlah, he is your cousin and an elderly man, so fear Allah regarding him.” She said: Before I left, revelation descended upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He experienced the state that always accompanied revelation. When it ended, he said: “O Khawlah, Allah has revealed verses concerning you and your husband.” He then recited the opening verses of Sūrat al-Mujādilah until the verse: ”…and for the disbelievers is a painful punishment.” (58:4) Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Command him to free a slave.” I replied: “O Messenger of Allah, he cannot afford that.” He said: “Then let him fast for two consecutive months.” I replied: “O Messenger of Allah, he is an old man and is unable to fast.” He said: “Then let him feed sixty poor people a wasq of dates.” I replied: “By Allah, he does not possess enough food for that either.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Then we shall help him with a basket of dates.” I said: “And I will help him with another basket.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “You have done well and acted excellently. Go and give the expiation on his behalf, and continue to treat your cousin kindly.” She said: So I did. ʿAbdullāh said: My father related that Saʿd explained: “A ʿaraq is a large basket used for dates.”"],[636,"ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ narrated: Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) said to me: “Shall I show you a woman from the people of Paradise?” I replied: “Yes.” He said: “This black woman came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: ‘I suffer from epileptic seizures, and during them parts of my body become uncovered. So supplicate to Allah for me.’ The Prophet ﷺ replied: ‘If you wish, remain patient and Paradise will be yours. Or, if you wish, I will supplicate to Allah to cure you.’ She said: ‘I will remain patient.’ Then she added: ‘But I become uncovered during the seizures, so supplicate to Allah that I will not become uncovered.’ So the Prophet ﷺ supplicated for her."],[637,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated: Hind bint ʿUtbah said: “O Messenger of Allah, there was once no household on the face of the earth whose humiliation I desired more than the household of yours. But today, there is no household on earth whose honor and success I desire more than yours.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “And even more so, by the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad.” She then said: “O Messenger of Allah, Abū Sufyān is a very stingy man. Is there any sin upon me if I take some of his wealth to feed my family?” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “There is no sin, provided that you take only what is reasonable.”"],[638,"Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: After the death of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abū Bakr said to ʿUmar: “Let us go and visit Umm Ayman, just as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to visit her.” When they arrived, she began to weep. They asked: “Why are you crying? What is with Allah is better for His Messenger ﷺ.” She replied: “I am not crying because I do not know that what Allah has prepared for the Messenger ﷺ is better. Rather, I am crying because revelation from heaven has now ceased.” Her words moved both Abū Bakr and ʿUmar to tears, and they wept together with her."],[639,"Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to visit Umm Ayman, and I accompanied him. She offered him a vessel containing a drink. I do not know whether he happened to be fasting or simply did not wish to drink it. When he declined, she began speaking to him insistently and reproachfully."],[640,"Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to Qubāʾ, he would visit Umm Ḥarām bint Milḥān, who would prepare food for him. She was married to ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit. One day, after she had served him food, she sat searching his hair, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fell asleep. He then awoke smiling. She asked: “What has made you smile, O Messenger of Allah?” He replied: “People from my Ummah were shown to me fighting in the cause of Allah, sailing upon this sea like kings seated upon their thrones—or like kings reclining upon couches.” She said: “O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah to make me one of them.” So he supplicated for her. Then he rested his head again and slept. When he awoke smiling a second time, she asked the same question, and he gave the same reply. Again she requested: “Pray that Allah makes me one of them.” He replied: “You will be among the first of them.” Anas said: During the caliphate of Muʿāwiyah, she sailed with the Muslim naval expedition. When she disembarked, she was thrown from her riding animal and died from her injuries."],[641,"Umm Ḥarām bint Milḥān (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated: The Prophet ﷺ once slept near me. When he awoke, he was smiling. I asked: “O Messenger of Allah, what has made you smile?” He replied: “People from my nation were shown to me sailing upon this green sea, like kings reclining upon thrones.” I said: “Supplicate to Allah to make me one of them.” So he supplicated for me. Then he slept again. When he awoke, the same thing happened again. I asked the same question, and he gave the same answer. I again requested: “Supplicate to Allah to make me one of them.” He replied: “You will be among the first.” She later went out with her husband, ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, on the first naval expedition undertaken by the Muslims during the time of Muʿāwiyah. When they returned from their campaign and arrived in Syria, an animal was brought for her to ride. It threw her to the ground, and she passed away."],[642,"Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: The sister of al-Rabīʿ—who was the mother of Ḥārithah—injured another person, and the dispute was brought before the Prophet ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ruled: “Retaliation (qiṣāṣ), retaliation.” Umm al-Rabīʿ said: “O Messenger of Allah, will so-and-so really have retaliation carried out against her? By Allah, she will not be subjected to retaliation!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “Subḥān Allah, O Umm al-Rabīʿ! Retaliation is prescribed in the Book of Allah.” She continued insisting: “No, by Allah! Retaliation will never be carried out against her.” She continued until the injured family agreed to accept blood money (diyah) instead. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “Indeed, among the servants of Allah are those who, if they swear an oath by Allah, He fulfills it for them.”"],[643,"Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: The Prophet ﷺ would not visit any woman apart from his wives except Umm Sulaym. He used to visit her regularly. When he was asked about this, he replied: “I feel compassion for her because her brother was killed while fighting alongside me.”"],[644,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I was shown Paradise, and I saw the wife of Abū Ṭalḥah. Then I heard the sound of footsteps ahead of me, and it was Bilāl.”"],[645,"Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “I entered Paradise and heard a rustling sound. I asked, ‘Who is this?’ They replied, ‘This is al-Ghumayṣāʾ bint Milḥān, the mother of Anas ibn Mālik.’”"],[646,"Umm Sulaym (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated: The Prophet ﷺ would come to her house and take his midday rest there. She would spread a leather mat for him, and he would rest upon it. The Prophet ﷺ perspired a great deal, so she would collect his perspiration and place it into her perfume and perfume bottles. The Prophet ﷺ asked: “O Umm Sulaym, what is this?” She replied: “It is your perspiration. I mix it with my perfume.”"],[647,"Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: The Prophet ﷺ used to visit Umm Sulaym, and she would honor him by preparing food or gifts that she had made especially for him."],[648,"Umm Hāniʾ bint Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated: During the year of the Conquest of Makkah, I went to visit the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I found him bathing, while his daughter Fāṭimah was screening him with a cloth. I greeted him with salām. He asked: “Who is this?” I replied: “Umm Hāniʾ bint Abī Ṭālib.” He said: “Welcome, O Umm Hāniʾ.” After finishing his bath, he stood and prayed eight rakʿahs, wrapped in a single garment. When he had finished, I said: “O Messenger of Allah, my brother ʿAlī claims that he intends to kill a man to whom I have granted protection—so-and-so son of Hubayrah.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “We grant protection to whomever you have granted protection, O Umm Hāniʾ.” Umm Hāniʾ said: This took place during the forenoon (ḍuḥā)."],[649,"Umm Waraqah bint Nawfal (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated: When the Prophet ﷺ set out for the Battle of Badr, I said: “O Messenger of Allah, allow me to accompany you so that I may care for your wounded. Perhaps Allah will grant me martyrdom.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Remain in your home, for Allah will indeed grant you martyrdom.” Thereafter, she became known as “the Martyr (al-Shahīdah).” She had read the Qur’an, and she sought permission from the Prophet ﷺ to appoint a mu’adhdhin for her household. He granted her permission. She had also arranged that a male servant and a female servant would be freed after her death. One night, however, the two servants attacked her, wrapped a blanket around her until she died, and then fled. The following morning, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb stood before the people and announced: “Whoever has any information about these two people, or has seen them, should bring them forward.” They were eventually captured, and ʿUmar ordered that they be crucified. They became the first people to be crucified in Madinah.\nAnother narration states: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to visit Umm Waraqah in her home. He appointed a mu’adhdhin to call the adhān for her and instructed her to lead the members of her household in prayer. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: “I personally saw her mu’adhdhin as an elderly man.”"],[650,"ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: A poor woman came to me carrying her two daughters. I gave her three dates. She gave one date to each of her daughters and raised the third date to her mouth intending to eat it herself. Then her two daughters asked her for it, so she split the date that she had intended to eat between them. I was greatly impressed by what she had done, so I mentioned it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: “Indeed, because of that, Allah has made Paradise obligatory for her,” or he said: ”…Allah has freed her from the Fire because of it.”"],[651,"Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: A woman came to ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), and ʿĀ’ishah gave her three dates. The woman gave one date to each of her two children and kept one date for herself. After the two children had eaten their dates, they looked at their mother. She then took the date she had kept for herself, split it into two halves, and gave each child half of it. When the Prophet ﷺ came, ʿĀ’ishah informed him of what had happened. He said: “Why does that amaze you? Allah showed mercy to her because of the mercy she showed to her two children.”"],[652,"ʿĀmir ibn Shahr said: I heard two statements: one from the Prophet ﷺ and another from the Negus. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “Look to Quraysh and take heed of what they say, but leave what they do.” I was once sitting with the Negus when his son returned from the school and recited a passage from the Gospel. I recognized it—or understood it—and laughed. The Negus said: “What are you laughing at? Are you laughing at the Book of Allah, the Exalted? By Allah, among what Allah revealed to Jesus, son of Mary, was that a curse descends upon the earth when its rulers are children.”"],[653,"ʿĀ’ishah narrated that the Prophet ﷺ entered upon her and said: “Were it not that Quraysh might become arrogant, I would have informed them of what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has prepared for them.”"],[654,"ʿĀ’ishah said: The Prophet ﷺ entered upon me saying: “O ʿĀ’ishah, your people will be the quickest of my nation to join me.” When he sat down, I said: “O Messenger of Allah, may Allah make me your ransom! You entered while saying words that frightened me.” He asked: “What were they?” She said: “You claim that your people will be the quickest of your nation to join you.” He replied: “Yes.” She asked: “Why will that be?” He said: “Death will seize them readily, and their nation will begrudge them their position.” She said: “What will the people be like after that—or at that time?” He replied: “They will be like swarming locusts, whose strong devour their weak, until the Hour comes upon them.” Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said that a man explained dabā as locusts whose wings have not yet grown."],[655,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Allah, You caused the first of Quraysh to taste punishment, so cause the last of them to taste generosity and bestowal.”"],[656,"Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “A man from Quraysh possesses twice the strength of a man from outside Quraysh.” Al-Zuhrī was asked: “What did he mean by that?” He replied: “Nobility and soundness of judgment.”"],[657,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The best women ever to ride camels are the righteous women of Quraysh”—one narrator said, “the righteous women of Quraysh,” while the other said, “the women of Quraysh”—“for they are the most compassionate toward an orphan in his childhood and the most careful in protecting a husband’s wealth.”"],[658,"Abū Hurayrah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “The women of Quraysh are the best women ever to ride camels. They are the most compassionate toward a child and the most careful in safeguarding a husband’s wealth.” Abū Hurayrah then added: “Maryam bint ʿImrān never rode a camel.”"],[659,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Prophet ﷺ proposed marriage to Umm Hāniʾ bint Abī Ṭālib. She said: “O Messenger of Allah, I have grown old and I have children.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The best women ever to ride camels are the women of Quraysh, for they are the most compassionate toward a child during his youth.”"],[660,"Umm Hāniʾ bint Abī Ṭālib said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ proposed marriage to me. I said: “It is not that I lack desire to marry you, but I do not wish to marry while my children are still young.” He asked: “Is there any other reason?” I replied: “No.” He then said: “The best women ever to ride camels are the women of Quraysh. They are the most compassionate toward a young child and the most careful in safeguarding a husband’s wealth.”"],[661,"Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “The best women ever to ride camels are the righteous women of Quraysh. They are the most careful in safeguarding a husband’s wealth and the most compassionate toward a young child.”"],[662,"Jarīr ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “The freed people of Quraysh and the emancipated people of Thaqīf are allies of one another in this world and the Hereafter, and the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār are allies of one another in this world and the Hereafter.”"],[663,"Jābir said: The following verse was revealed concerning us: “When two groups among you were about to lose courage, although Allah was their Protector.” [Sūrat Āl ʿImrān 3:122] They were Banū Salimah and Banū Ḥārithah. We would not have wished that it had not been revealed, because Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: “Although Allah was their Protector.”"],[664,"ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Faḍl narrated that he heard Anas ibn Mālik say: I grieved deeply over those who were killed at al-Ḥarrah. Zayd ibn Arqam heard of the severity of my grief and wrote to me, mentioning that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “O Allah, forgive the Anṣār and the children of the Anṣār.” Ibn al-Faḍl was uncertain whether he also said: “And the children of the children of the Anṣār.” One of those who was present asked Anas about Zayd ibn Arqam, and he replied: “He is the one concerning whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘This is the man whose sound hearing Allah testified to.’”"],[665,"Abū Usayd al-Anṣārī testified that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The best households of the Anṣār are Banū al-Najjār, then Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal, then Banū al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj, then Banū Sāʿidah. Yet there is goodness in every household of the Anṣār.” Abū Salamah said that Abū Usayd remarked: “Would I falsely attribute something to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? Had I been lying, I would have begun with my own people, Banū Sāʿidah.” When this reached Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah, he felt upset and said: “We have been placed behind the others and made the last of the four. Saddle my donkey so that I may go to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”\nHis nephew Sahl spoke to him and said: “Will you go to object to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when the Messenger of Allah knows best? Is it not sufficient for you that you are the fourth of four?” Saʿd turned back and said: “Allah and His Messenger know best.” He then ordered that the saddle be removed from his donkey."],[666,"Abū Usayd narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “The best households of the Anṣār are Banū al-Najjār, then Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal, then Banū al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj, then Banū Sāʿidah. Yet there is goodness in every household of the Anṣār.” Saʿd said: “It appears to me that the Prophet ﷺ has preferred others over us.” It was said to him: “He has preferred you over many others.”"],[667,"Abū Ḥumayd al-Sāʿidī narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “The best household among the Anṣār is the household of Banū al-Najjār, then Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal, then the household of Banū al-Ḥārith, then Banū Sāʿidah. Yet there is goodness in every household of the Anṣār.” Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah caught up with us, and Abū Usayd said to him: “Have you not seen that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ ranked the Anṣār and placed us last?” Saʿd then caught up with the Prophet ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah, the households of the Anṣār were ranked, and we were placed last.” He replied: “Is it not sufficient for you that you are among the best?”"],[668,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Shall I tell you which are the best households of the Anṣār?” They replied: “Yes, O Messenger of Allah.” He said: “Banū al-Najjār, then those who come after them, Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal; then those who come after them, Banū al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj; then those who come after them, Banū Sāʿidah.” He then gestured with his hand, closing his fingers and then spreading them as one who shoots with his hand, and said: “Yet there is goodness in every household of the Anṣār.”"],[669,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The Anṣār are my innermost people and the keepers of my trust. The people will increase in number while they will decrease, so accept the good deeds of those among them who do good, and pardon those among them who do wrong.”"],[670,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: Abū Bakr and al-ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) passed by a gathering of the Anṣār while they were weeping. They asked: “What is making you weep?” They replied: “We remembered the gatherings of the Prophet ﷺ among us.” The two of them entered upon the Prophet ﷺ and informed him of this. The Prophet ﷺ then came out with the edge of a cloak tied around his head. He ascended the pulpit, and he did not ascend it again after that day. He praised and glorified Allah, then said: “I enjoin you to treat the Anṣār well, for they are my innermost people and the keepers of my trust. They have fulfilled what was required of them, while what is due to them remains. Therefore, accept the good deeds of those among them who do good and overlook those among them who do wrong.”"],[671,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: A woman came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ accompanied by her young child. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke to her and said: “By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, you are the most beloved people to me.” He said it twice."],[672,"Anas narrated that the Prophet ﷺ saw children and women returning from a wedding. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ stood upright and said: “By Allah, you are among the most beloved people to me. By Allah, you are among the most beloved people to me.” He meant the Anṣār."],[673,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sought forgiveness for the Anṣār. He said: “I believe that he also said: ‘And for the descendants of the Anṣār and the freed servants of the Anṣār.’ I have no doubt about it.”"],[674,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ distributed the gifts he had given to Quraysh and the Arab tribes, while the Anṣār received none of them, this group of the Anṣār felt something in their hearts. Discussion among them increased until one of them said: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has rejoined his own people.” Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah entered upon him and said: “O Messenger of Allah, this group has felt upset with you because of what you did with this spoils of war. You distributed it among your own people and gave great gifts to the Arab tribes, while this group of the Anṣār received nothing.” He asked: “Where do you stand regarding this, O Saʿd?” Saʿd replied: “O Messenger of Allah, I am only a man from among my people.” He said: “Gather your people for me in this enclosure.” Saʿd went out and gathered the Anṣār in that enclosure. Some men from the Muhājirūn came, and he allowed them to enter. Others came afterward, but he turned them away. When they had assembled, Saʿd came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “This group of the Anṣār has gathered for you.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to them, praised Allah, and extolled Him as He deserves. He then said: “O company of the Anṣār, what is this talk that has reached me concerning you, and this resentment that you have felt in your hearts? Did I not come to you while you were astray, and Allah guided you; poor, and Allah enriched you; and enemies of one another, and Allah united your hearts?” They replied: “Rather, Allah and His Messenger are more gracious and more excellent.” He said: “Will you not answer me, O company of the Anṣār?” They replied: “With what should we answer you, O Messenger of Allah? All favor and grace belong to Allah and His Messenger.” He said: “By Allah, had you wished, you could have said—and you would have spoken truthfully and been believed: ‘You came to us rejected, and we believed you; abandoned, and we supported you; driven out, and we sheltered you; and destitute, and we comforted you.’ O company of the Anṣār, did you feel resentment over some insignificant worldly possessions by which I sought to incline certain people toward Islam, while I entrusted you to the strength of your Islam? Are you not pleased, O company of the Anṣār, that the people should return with sheep and camels while you return with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to your homes? By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad, were it not for the Hijrah, I would have been a man from among the Anṣār. If the people were to travel through one mountain pass and the Anṣār through another, I would take the pass of the Anṣār. O Allah, have mercy upon the Anṣār, the children of the Anṣār, and the children of the children of the Anṣār.” The people wept until their beards became wet, and they said: “We are pleased with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as our allotted share and portion.” Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ departed, and they dispersed."],[675,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī said: Some of the Anṣār gathered and said: “Others have been preferred over us.” This reached the Prophet ﷺ, so he gathered them and addressed them, saying: “O company of the Anṣār, were you not humiliated, and Allah gave you honor?” They replied: “Allah and His Messenger have spoken the truth.” He said: “Were you not astray, and Allah guided you?” They replied: “Allah and His Messenger have spoken the truth.” He said: “Were you not poor, and Allah enriched you?” They replied: “Allah and His Messenger have spoken the truth.” He then said: “Will you not answer me? Will you not say: ‘You came to us as one driven out, and we sheltered you; and you came to us in fear, and we gave you security’? Are you not pleased that the people should depart with sheep and cattle, while you depart with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and bring him into your homes? If the people were to travel through one valley or mountain pass and you travelled through another valley or mountain pass, I would travel through your valley or your mountain pass. Were it not for the Hijrah, I would have been a man from among the Anṣār. After me, you will encounter others being favored over you, so remain patient until you meet me at the Reservoir.”"],[676,"Anas said: Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr al-Ashhalī, one of the chiefs, came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ after some food had already been distributed. He mentioned to him a household from Banū Ẓafar of the Anṣār who were in need. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: “Usayd, you waited until everything in our hands was gone! When you hear that something has come to us, remind me about that household.” Later, food arrived from Khaybar consisting of barley and dates. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ distributed it among the people. He gave generously to the Anṣār and gave generously to that household. Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr thanked him and said: “May Allah reward you, O Prophet of Allah, with the finest reward,” or he said: “with goodness.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “And you, company of the Anṣār—may Allah reward you with the finest reward,” or he said: “with goodness. As far as I have known you, you have been chaste and patient. After me, you will see others preferred over you in authority and distribution, so remain patient until you meet me at the Reservoir.”"],[677,"Ubayy ibn Kaʿb narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Were it not for the Hijrah, I would have been a man from among the Anṣār. If the people were to travel through one valley or mountain pass, I would be with the Anṣār.”"],[678,"Abū Qatādah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say from the pulpit concerning the Anṣār: “Indeed, the people are my outer garment, while the Anṣār are my inner garment. If the people were to travel through one valley and the Anṣār through another mountain pass, I would follow the mountain pass of the Anṣār. Were it not for the Hijrah, I would have been a man from among the Anṣār. Whoever is given authority over the Anṣār should treat their righteous people well and overlook the wrongdoings of those among them who do wrong. Whoever frightens them has frightened the one who is between these two.” He pointed to himself."],[679,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The Anṣār are my inner garment, while the people are my outer garment.”"],[680,"Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “Whoever loves the Anṣār, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, loves him; and whoever hates the Anṣār, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, hates him.”"],[681,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Were it not for the Hijrah, I would have been a man from among the Anṣār.”"],[682,"Anas said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “O company of the Anṣār, your appointed meeting place is my Reservoir. Its vessels are more numerous than the stars in the sky—or as numerous as the stars in the sky—and its width is like the distance between me and Ṣanʿāʾ—or between me and ʿUmān.”"],[683,"Anas narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out one day with his head wrapped. The Anṣār, along with their women and children, came to meet him. When he saw the faces of the Anṣār, he said: “By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I truly love you.” He also said: “The Anṣār have fulfilled what was required of them, while what is required of you remains. Therefore, treat their righteous people well and overlook the wrongdoings of those among them who do wrong.”"],[684,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever loves the Anṣār, Allah loves him; and whoever hates the Anṣār, Allah hates him.”"],[685,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “No one who believes in Allah and the Last Day hates the Anṣār.”"],[686,"Al-Ḥārith ibn Ziyād al-Sāʿidī al-Anṣārī narrated that he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the Day of the Trench while he was accepting the people’s pledges to migrate. He said: “O Messenger of Allah, accept this man’s pledge.” The Prophet ﷺ asked: “Who is he?” He replied: “He is my cousin, Ḥawṭ ibn Yazīd—or Yazīd ibn Ḥawṭ.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I will not accept your pledge to migrate, for the people migrate to you, while you do not migrate to them. By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad, no man continues to love the Anṣār until he meets Allah, Blessed and Exalted, except that he will meet Allah while Allah loves him; and no man continues to hate the Anṣār until he meets Allah except that he will meet Allah while Allah hates him.”"],[687,"Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever loves the Anṣār loves them because of his love for me, and whoever hates the Anṣār hates them because of his hatred for me.”"],[688,"A man from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ narrated that the Prophet ﷺ stood on that day and delivered a sermon. He praised and glorified Allah and sought forgiveness for the martyrs who had been killed at Uḥud. He then said: “O company of the Muhājirūn, you will continue to increase, while the Anṣār will not increase. The Anṣār are my trusted repository to which I took refuge. Therefore, honor the noble among them and overlook the wrongdoings of those among them who do wrong. They have fulfilled what was required of them, while what is due to them remains.”"],[689,"Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The Anṣār are my innermost people and the keepers of my trust. The people will increase while they will decrease, so accept the good deeds of those among them who do good and overlook the wrongdoings of those among them who do wrong.”"],[690,"Rifāʿah ibn Rāfiʿ narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Allah, forgive the Anṣār, the descendants of the Anṣār, the descendants of their descendants, their freed servants, and their neighbors.”"],[691,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Allah, forgive the Anṣār, the children of the Anṣār, and the children of the children of the Anṣār.”"],[692,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated that Thābit ibn Qays delivered an address when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived. He said: “We shall protect you from whatever we protect ourselves and our children from. What, then, shall we receive, O Messenger of Allah?” He replied: “You shall have Paradise.” They said: “We are satisfied.”"],[693,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh said: My father ordered that a dish of khazīrah be prepared. When it was ready, I carried it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I came to him while he was in his home, and he asked: “What is this, O Jābir? Is it meat?” I replied: “No. It is only khazīrah.” He ordered that it be accepted. When I returned to my father, he asked: “Did you see the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” I replied: “Yes.” He asked: “Did he say anything?” I replied: “Yes. He said, ‘What is this, O Jābir? Is it meat?’” My father said: “Perhaps the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is craving meat.” He went to a domesticated sheep that he owned, slaughtered it, ordered that it be roasted, and then instructed me to take it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I reached him while he was still in that same gathering. He asked: “What is this, O Jābir?” I said: “O Messenger of Allah, when I returned to my father, he asked whether I had seen you. I said that I had. He asked whether you had said anything, and I told him that you had asked, ‘What is this? Is it meat?’ My father then said, ‘Perhaps the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is craving meat.’ So he went to a domesticated sheep that he owned, slaughtered it, had it roasted, and then instructed me to bring it to you.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “May Allah reward the Anṣār on our behalf with goodness, especially ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥarām and Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah.”"],[694,"Abū Ṭalḥah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “Convey my greetings of peace to your people, for throughout the time I have known them, they have been chaste and patient.”"],[695,"Anas ibn Mālik said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us and said: “Indeed, every prophet leaves behind an inheritance and a cherished estate. My inheritance and cherished estate are the Anṣār, so honor my trust concerning them.”"],[696,"ʿĀ’ishah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “No harm will come to a woman who settles between two households of the Anṣār—or who settles between her parents.”"],[697,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh said: I bear witness that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever frightens this community of the Anṣār has frightened what lies between these two.” He then placed his hands upon his two sides, referring to himself."],[698,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “There is no true life except the life of the Hereafter, so set right the Anṣār and the Muhājirūn.”"],[699,"Anas narrated: On the Day of the Trench, the Anṣār were chanting: We are the ones who pledged allegiance to Muḥammad, To strive in battle for as long as we live. The Prophet ﷺ answered them: “O Allah, there is no true life except the life of the Hereafter, so honor the Anṣār and the Muhājirūn.”"],[700,"Anas narrated: The Muhājirūn and the Anṣār were digging the trench around Madinah and carrying the earth upon their backs while chanting: We are the ones who pledged allegiance to Muḥammad, To remain upon Islam for as long as we live. The Prophet ﷺ responded to them, saying: “O Allah, there is no goodness except the goodness of the Hereafter, So bless the Anṣār and the Muhājirūn.”"],[701,"Sahl ibn Saʿd narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to us while we were digging the trench and carrying the earth upon our shoulders. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Allah, there is no true life except the life of the Hereafter, so forgive the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār.”"],[702,"Anas narrated: The Prophet ﷺ summoned the Anṣār so that he could assign lands or revenues to them in Bahrain. They said: “No, by Allah, not unless you grant the same to our brothers from Quraysh.” He replied: “That will be theirs for as long as Allah wills.” They continued speaking to him about it, so he said: “After me, you will see others being preferred over you, so remain patient until you meet me.”"],[703,"Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr narrated that a man from the Anṣār said: “O Messenger of Allah, will you not appoint me to a position as you appointed so-and-so?” He replied: “After me, you will encounter others being preferred over you, so remain patient until you meet me at the Reservoir.”"],[704,"Zayd ibn Arqam narrated: The Anṣār said: “Every prophet has followers, and we have followed you, so supplicate to Allah that He make those who follow us from among us.” The Prophet ﷺ supplicated for that. The narrator said: I mentioned this wish to Abū Laylā, and he said: “Zayd did indeed report that.”"],[705,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Were it not for the Hijrah, I would have been a man from among the Anṣār. If the people travelled through one valley and the Anṣār travelled through another valley or mountain pass, I would take the valley or mountain pass of the Anṣār.”"],[706,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Zayd narrated: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Ḥunayn, he distributed the spoils and gave gifts to those whose hearts were being inclined toward Islam. It reached him that the Anṣār wished to receive what the other people had received. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ therefore stood and addressed them. He praised and glorified Allah, then said: “O company of the Anṣār, did I not find you astray, and Allah guided you through me; poor, and Allah enriched you through me; and divided, and Allah united you through me?” They replied: “Allah and His Messenger have bestowed the greater favor.” He said: “Will you not answer me?” They replied: “Allah and His Messenger have bestowed the greater favor.” He said: “Indeed, had you wished, you could have said such-and-such, and the matter was such-and-such.” He then enumerated a number of things, though ʿAmr stated that he did not remember them. The Prophet ﷺ continued: “Are you not satisfied that the people should depart with sheep and camels, while you depart with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to your dwellings? The Anṣār are my inner garment, while the people are my outer garment. Were it not for the Hijrah, I would have been a man from among the Anṣār. If the people travelled through one valley and mountain pass, I would travel through the valley and mountain pass of the Anṣār. After me, you will encounter others being favored over you, so remain patient until you meet me at the Reservoir.”"],[707,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: When Allah granted His Messenger ﷺ the wealth of Hawāzin as spoils, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ began giving some men from Quraysh one hundred camels each. Some people from the Anṣār said: “May Allah forgive the Messenger of Allah ﷺ! He gives to Quraysh and leaves us, even though our swords are still dripping with their blood!” Anas said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was informed of what they had said. He sent for the Anṣār and gathered them in a tent made of leather, allowing no one else to join them. When they had assembled, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to them and asked: “What is this statement that has reached me concerning you?” Their people of understanding replied: “As for those among us who possess sound judgment, O Messenger of Allah, they said nothing. However, some of our younger men said: ‘May Allah forgive the Messenger of Allah ﷺ! He gives to Quraysh and leaves the Anṣār, even though our swords are still dripping with their blood.’” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I give to men who have only recently left disbelief. Are you not satisfied that the people should depart with wealth, while you return to your dwellings with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? By Allah, that with which you return is better than that with which they return.” They replied: “Yes, O Messenger of Allah, we are satisfied.” He then said to them: “After me, you will witness severe favoritism toward others. Remain patient until you meet Allah and His Messenger ﷺ at the Reservoir.” Anas said: “But we did not remain patient.”"],[708,"Anas narrated: On the day of the Conquest of Makkah, when the Prophet ﷺ gave gifts to Quraysh, the Anṣār said: “By Allah, this is truly astonishing! Our swords are dripping with the blood of Quraysh, yet our spoils are being returned to them.” This reached the Prophet ﷺ, so he summoned the Anṣār and asked: “What is this that has reached me concerning you?” They were not people who told lies, so they replied: “It is exactly as it reached you.” He said: “Are you not satisfied that the people should return to their homes with the spoils, while you return to your homes with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? If the Anṣār travelled through one valley or mountain pass, I would travel through the valley or mountain pass of the Anṣār.”"],[709,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: On the Day of Ḥunayn, Hawāzin, Ghaṭafān, and the other tribes advanced with their livestock, women, and children. The Prophet ﷺ had ten thousand men with him that day, together with those who had been granted amnesty at the Conquest of Makkah. They turned away from him until he was left alone. That day he called out twice, keeping the two calls distinct. He turned to his right and said: “O company of the Anṣār!” They replied: “At your service, O Messenger of Allah. Be of good cheer; we are with you.” He then turned to his left and said: “O company of the Anṣār!” They replied: “At your service, O Messenger of Allah. Be of good cheer; we are with you.” He was riding a white mule. He dismounted and said: “I am the servant of Allah and His Messenger.” The polytheists were then defeated, and the Muslims obtained abundant spoils that day. The Prophet ﷺ distributed them among the Muhājirūn and those who had been granted amnesty, but he gave nothing to the Anṣār. The Anṣār said: “When hardship comes, we are the ones who are called, but the spoils are given to others.” When this reached him, he gathered them inside a tent and said: “O company of the Anṣār, what is this statement that has reached me concerning you?” They remained silent. He said: “O company of the Anṣār, are you not pleased that the people should depart with worldly possessions while you depart with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, bringing him back to your homes?” They replied: “Yes.” The Prophet ﷺ then said: “If the people travelled through one valley and the Anṣār through another mountain pass, I would take the mountain pass of the Anṣār.” Hishām asked: “O Abū Ḥamzah, were you present for that?” Anas replied: “Where could I have been absent from it?”"],[710,"Abū Hurayrah narrated: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ seeking food. The Prophet ﷺ sent word to his wives, but they replied: “We have nothing except water.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Who will take this man in, or host him?” A man from the Anṣār said: “I will.” He took the guest home and said to his wife: “Honor the guest of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” She replied: “We have nothing except the food meant for my children.” He said: “Prepare the food, light your lamp, and put the children to sleep when they ask for their evening meal.” She prepared the food, lit the lamp, and put the children to sleep. She then stood as though adjusting the lamp and extinguished it. The husband and wife pretended to the guest that they were eating, although they spent the night hungry. The following morning, the man went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: “Allah laughed last night—or was amazed—at what the two of you did.” Allah then revealed: “They give preference to others over themselves, even though they may be in need. Whoever is protected from the stinginess of his own soul—it is they who are the successful.” [Sūrat al-Ḥashr 59:9]"],[711,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated: During the illness from which he died, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out wearing a cloak and with a dark-colored band wrapped around his head. He sat upon the pulpit, praised and glorified Allah, and then said: “As for what follows: the people will increase in number, while the Anṣār will decrease, until they become among the people like salt in food. Whoever among you is placed in authority over a matter through which he may harm some people and benefit others should accept the good deeds of the righteous among the Anṣār and overlook the wrongdoings of those among them who do wrong.” This was the last gathering in which the Prophet ﷺ sat upon the pulpit."],[712,"Anas ibn Mālik said: I travelled with Jarīr ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Bajalī, and he would serve me. I said to him: “Do not do that.” He replied: “I saw how the Anṣār served the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so I swore that whenever I accompanied any one of them, I would serve him.” Ibn al-Muthannā and Ibn Bashshār added in their narrations that Jarīr was older than Anas. Ibn Bashshār said: “He was more advanced in age than Anas.”"],[713,"Ghaylān ibn Jarīr said: I asked Anas: “Regarding the name ‘the Anṣār,’ did you give yourselves this name, or did Allah name you?” He replied: “Rather, Allah named us.”"],[714,"Abū Mūsā narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I recognize the voices of the travelling party of the Ashʿarīs as they recite the Qur’an when they enter at night. I recognize the locations of their dwellings at night from the sound of their Qur’an recitation, even though I may not have seen where they settled during the day. Among them is Ḥakīm, who, when he encounters cavalry—or the enemy—says to them: ‘My companions command you to wait for them.’”"],[715,"Abū Mūsā narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “When the Ashʿarīs run short of provisions during a military campaign, or when the food of their families becomes scarce in Madinah, they gather whatever they possess into a single cloth and then divide it equally among themselves using one vessel. They are from me, and I am from them.”"],[716,"Anas narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “A people will come to you whose hearts are more tender than yours.” The Ashʿarīs then arrived, among them Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī. As they approached Madinah, they chanted: Tomorrow we shall meet the beloved ones— Muḥammad and his party."],[717,"Abū Dharr narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “May Allah forgive Ghifār, and may Allah keep Aslam safe.”"],[718,"Khafāf ibn Īmāʾ al-Ghifārī narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said during a prayer: “O Allah, curse Banū Liḥyān, Riʿl, Dhakwān, and ʿUṣayyah; they disobeyed Allah and His Messenger. May Allah forgive Ghifār, and may Allah keep Aslam safe.”"],[719,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said from the pulpit: “May Allah forgive Ghifār, may Allah keep Aslam safe, and ʿUṣayyah disobeyed Allah and His Messenger.”"],[720,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said from the pulpit: “May Allah keep Aslam safe, and may Allah forgive Ghifār.”"],[721,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “May Allah keep Aslam safe, and may Allah forgive Ghifār.”"],[722,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “May Allah keep Aslam safe, and may Allah forgive Ghifār.”"],[723,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “May Allah keep Aslam safe, and may Allah forgive Ghifār. By Allah, I did not say this of my own accord; rather, Allah said it.”"],[724,"ʿĀ’ishah narrated: Umm Sunbulah brought some milk as a gift for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, but she did not find him there. ʿĀ’ishah said to her: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has forbidden us from eating the food of the desert Arabs.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then entered with Abū Bakr and asked: “What do you have with you, O Umm Sunbulah?” She replied: “Milk that I brought as a gift for you, O Messenger of Allah.” He said: “Pour some, O Umm Sunbulah.” She poured it, and he said: “Give it to Abū Bakr.” She did so. He then said: “Pour some, O Umm Sunbulah, and give it to ʿĀ’ishah.” She gave it to her, and ʿĀ’ishah drank. He then said: “Pour some, O Umm Sunbulah.” She poured it and handed it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he drank. While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was drinking the milk of Aslam, ʿĀ’ishah said: “May my father and mother be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah. I had been told that you forbade the food of the desert Arabs.” He replied: “O ʿĀ’ishah, they are not desert Arabs in that sense. They are the people of our countryside, and we are the people of their town. When they are called, they respond. Therefore, they are not among the desert Arabs.”"],[725,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ said: I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and addressed him: “O Messenger of Allah.” He said: “You are the people of our countryside, and we are the people of your town.”"],[726,"Abū Hurayrah narrated: Al-Ṭufayl and his companions came and said: “O Messenger of Allah, Daws has persisted in disbelief and refused to accept Islam, so supplicate to Allah against them.” The people said: “Daws is ruined.” But the Prophet ﷺ said: “O Allah, guide Daws and bring them to me.”"],[727,"ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim said: I came to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, and he said to me: “The first charitable payment that brought joy and honor to the face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the faces of his Companions was the charity of Ṭayyiʾ, which you brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”"],[728,"Abū Hurayrah said: I shall always love Banū Tamīm because of three things that I heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: “They will be the strongest of my nation against the Antichrist.” Their charitable contributions were once brought, and the Prophet ﷺ said: “These are the charitable contributions of our people.” A female captive from among them was also in the possession of ʿĀ’ishah, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Set her free, for she is among the descendants of Ishmael.”"],[729,"Abū Hurayrah said: I heard three qualities concerning Banū Tamīm from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and because of them I shall always continue to love them. He then narrated the report with the same meaning, except that he said: “They are the fiercest people in fighting during the great battles.” He did not mention the Antichrist."],[730,"A man from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ narrated: Banū Tamīm were mentioned in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and a man said: “This clan of Tamīm has been slow to accept this matter.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked toward Muzaynah and said: “These people are no slower than they are.” On another occasion, a man said: “These people of Tamīm have been slow in sending their charitable contributions.” Then red and black livestock belonging to Banū Tamīm arrived, and the Prophet ﷺ said: “These are the livestock of my people.” On another day, a man spoke about Banū Tamīm in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: “Say nothing but good about Banū Tamīm, for they will be the people whose spears reach furthest against the Antichrist.”"],[731,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Quraysh, the Anṣār, Juhaynah, Muzaynah, Aslam, Ashjaʿ, and Ghifār are my allies. They have no protector or ally besides Allah and His Messenger.”"],[732,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad, Ghifār, Aslam, Muzaynah, and those from Juhaynah—or he said: Juhaynah and those from Muzaynah—will be better in the sight of Allah on the Day of Resurrection than Asad, Ṭayyiʾ, and Ghaṭafān.”"],[733,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Aslam, Ghifār, and some of Muzaynah and Juhaynah—or some of Juhaynah and Muzaynah—are better in the sight of Allah”—the narrator said, “I believe he said: on the Day of Resurrection”—“than Asad, Ghaṭafān, Hawāzin, and Tamīm.”"],[734,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakrah narrated from his father that al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis said to the Prophet ﷺ: “Only the robbers of the pilgrims from Aslam, Ghifār, Muzaynah—and, I believe, Juhaynah—have pledged allegiance to you.” Ibn Abī Yaʿqūb was uncertain whether Juhaynah was mentioned. The Prophet ﷺ said: “What do you think if Aslam, Ghifār, Muzaynah—and, I believe, Juhaynah—are better than Banū Tamīm, Banū ʿĀmir, Asad, and Ghaṭafān, while the latter have failed and lost?” He replied: “Yes.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, they are indeed better than them.”"],[735,"Abū Ayyūb narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The Anṣār, Muzaynah, Juhaynah, Ghifār, Ashjaʿ, and those from Banū ʿAbdullāh are my allies above all other people, while Allah and His Messenger are their Protectors.”"],[736,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “What an excellent people al-Azd are! Their speech is wholesome, their oaths are faithfully kept, and their hearts are pure.”"],[737,"Jābir narrated: The people said: “O Messenger of Allah, the arrows of Thaqīf have caused us severe suffering, so supplicate to Allah against them.” He said: “O Allah, guide Thaqīf.”"],[738,"Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb narrated: A delegation from Bajīlah came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Provide clothing for the Bajalīs, and begin with the Aḥmasīs.” A man from Qays remained behind and said: “I will wait and see what the Messenger ﷺ says to them.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then supplicated for them five times, saying: “O Allah, bestow Your blessings upon them,” or: “O Allah, bless them.” Mukhāriq was uncertain which wording was used."],[739,"Buraydah ibn al-Ḥuṣayb narrated: ʿUyaynah ibn Badr, al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis, and ʿAlqamah ibn ʿUlāthah gathered in the presence of the Prophet ﷺ and spoke about the fortunes and worldly standing of the tribes. The Prophet ﷺ said: “If you remain silent, I will tell you. The fortune of Banū ʿĀmir is like a red or dark-colored camel feeding from the tips of trees”—the narrator said, “I believe he also said: in a meadow”—“while Ghaṭafān is like a rough hillock that drives people away from it.” Al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis asked: “Then what is the fortune of Banū Tamīm?” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Had you remained silent.”"],[740,"Abū Juḥayfah al-Suwāʾī narrated: A delegation from Banū ʿĀmir came to the Prophet ﷺ, and I was among them. We found him at al-Abṭaḥ inside a red tent. We greeted him with peace, and he asked: “Who are you?” We replied: “Banū ʿĀmir ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿah.” He said: “Welcome to you. You are from me, and I am from you.” When the time for prayer arrived, Bilāl stood and gave the call to prayer, turning from side to side while calling it. A vessel containing water was then brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He performed ablution, and some water remained from his ablution. We eagerly used whatever remained to perform ablution ourselves.\nBilāl then gave the call to commence the prayer, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in two rakʿahs. When the time for the ʿAṣr prayer arrived, Bilāl again stood and called the adhān, turning from side to side, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in two rakʿahs."],[741,"Abū al-Qamūṣ Zayd ibn ʿAlī narrated: One of the members of the delegation of ʿAbd al-Qays who came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told me: Among the gifts we presented to him was a small basket—or a waterskin—containing Taʿḍūḍ or Barnī dates. He asked: “What is this?” We replied: “It is a gift.” The narrator said: I believe he looked at one of the dates, then returned it to its place and said: “Deliver it to the family of Muḥammad.” The people asked him several questions until they asked about beverages. He said: “Do not drink from gourds, glazed earthenware jars, hollowed palm trunks, or pitch-coated vessels. Drink from lawful vessels whose mouths can be tied closed.” One of us asked: “O Messenger of Allah, how do you know what gourds, glazed jars, hollowed trunks, and pitch-coated vessels are?” He replied: “Would I not know what they are? Which fortress of Hajar is the strongest?” We replied: “Al-Mushaqqar.” He said: “By Allah, I entered it and took possession of its key.” The narrator said: I had forgotten part of his statement, but ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Jarwah reminded me that he also said: “I stood at the spring of al-Zārah.” He then said: “O Allah, forgive ʿAbd al-Qays, for they accepted Islam willingly and without compulsion, neither humiliated nor deprived through bloodshed or loss, whereas some of our people do not accept Islam until they are humiliated and suffer loss.” He then turned his face in supplication toward the direction of the qiblah until he was fully facing it and said: “Indeed, the best people of the East are ʿAbd al-Qays.”"],[742,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd said: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicating for this clan of al-Nakhaʿ—or he said: praising them—until I wished that I were a man from among them."],[743,"ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasah al-Sulamī narrated: One day, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was inspecting horses while ʿUyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn ibn Badr al-Fazārī was with him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: “I am more knowledgeable about horses than you are.” ʿUyaynah replied: “And I am more knowledgeable about men than you are.” The Prophet ﷺ asked: “How is that?” He replied: “The best men are those from Najd who carry their swords upon their shoulders, place their spears across the backs of their horses, and wear striped cloaks.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “You have spoken falsely. Rather, the best men are the men of Yemen. Faith is Yemeni among Lakhm, Judhām, and ʿĀmilah. Those of Ḥimyar who have passed away are better than those who remain. Ḥaḍramawt is better than Banū al-Ḥārith. One tribe is better than another, and one tribe is worse than another. By Allah, I would not care if both clans of al-Ḥārith were destroyed. May Allah curse the four kings: Jamdāʾ, Makhūsāʾ, Mishraḥāʾ, and Abḍaʿah, and their sister al-ʿAmarradah.” He then said: “My Lord, Mighty and Majestic, commanded me to curse Quraysh twice, so I cursed them; and He commanded me to invoke blessings upon them twice, so I invoked blessings upon them twice.” He then said: “ʿUṣayyah disobeyed Allah and His Messenger, apart from Qays, Juʿdah, and ʿUṣayyah.” He then said: “Aslam, Ghifār, Muzaynah, and their allied groups from Juhaynah will be better in the sight of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, on the Day of Resurrection than Banū Asad, Tamīm, Ghaṭafān, and Hawāzin.” He then said: “The two worst tribes among the Arabs are Najrān and Banū Taghlib, and the tribe with the greatest number of people in Paradise will be Madhḥij.”"]]);
